-291629", "text": "Countries are banning social media in hopes of helping with the youth mental health crisis. [Anna Barclay/Getty Images News via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/in-this-photo-illustration-a-14-year-old-teenage-boy-looks-news-photo/2289305748?phrase=social%20media%20bans&adppopup=true) Can social media bans protect young people\u2019s mental health? Early research suggests that bans aren\u2019t the answer Published: October 16, 2026 8:21am EDT https://theconversation.com/can-social-media-bans-protect-young-peoples-mental-health-early-research-suggests-that-bans-arent-the-answer-291629 https://theconversation.com/can-social-media-bans-protect-young-peoples-mental-health-early-research-suggests-that-bans-arent-the-answer-291629 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The year 2026 has been an expensive one for social media platforms. Since March, YouTube and Meta, the parent company of Instagram and Facebook, have settled or paid out in multiple lawsuits to the tune of more than US$17 billion for allegedly [acting negligently](https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/verdict-reached-landmark-social-media-addiction-trial-rcna263421), deliberately designing their platforms [to hook kids](https://www.npr.org/2026/08/26/nx-s1-5944781/meta-settlement-child-safety-lawsuit) and [misleading the public](https://www.courthousenews.com/facebook-faced-with-massive-penalties-after-new-mexico-privacy-verdict/) about safety and privacy. If these suits are any indication of how states and the public are starting to view social media platforms, more may follow. On top of these lawsuits, social media platforms are facing a loss of business as [bans for minors](https://theconversation.com/is-australias-social-media-ban-for-under-16s-working-285518) are accelerating and spreading worldwide. [Australia was one of the first](https://theconversation.com/australias-social-media-ban-is-now-in-force-other-countries-are-closely-watching-what-happens-271407) with a ban on kids under 16 on social media starting in December 2025, [followed soon after by the U.K.](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/fact-sheet-new-rules-to-protect-children-online/fact-sheet-new-rules-to-protect-children-online) with a similar prohibition. The current list of countries looking to ban minors from these platforms tops [more than 20](https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/australia-europe-countries-move-curb-childrens-social-media-access-2026-09-17/), from Greece to the United Arab Emirates to Canada. The proposed and enacted bans around the world vary in terms of [age restriction](https://social-media-age-tracker.onrender.com/), parental consent rules and [age verification methods](https://facephi.com/observatory/en/age-verification-laws-by-country/). Different countries are looking at restricting different platforms, but most include a mix of Snapchat, YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Facebook and X. These bans [aren\u2019t necessarily for mental health reasons only](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/28/technology/social-media-bans-meta.html) \u2013 some are motivated by political concerns about foreign-owned platforms, and others aim to protect young people\u2019s privacy. But nearly every government banning social media platforms for young people [mentions their mental health as a reason](https://builtin.com/articles/social-media-bans-children). The question is, are these bans the solution to the [mental health problems young people are facing](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/adolescent-mental-health)? I [research digital media and marketing](https://www.heinz.cmu.edu/faculty-research/profiles/lightman-ari) and measure the impact of new technologies, and I\u2019m skeptical about whether prohibiting access to these platforms will actually improve young people\u2019s mental health. Mental health concerns Since Jonathan Haidt\u2019s 2024 book \u201c[The Anxious Generation](https://www.anxiousgeneration.com/)\u201d critics of social media have increasingly focused on young people\u2019s mental health. Haidt argues that social media disrupts kids\u2019 social and neurological development, contributing to rising anxiety and depression through [sleep loss](https://theconversation.com/social-media-before-bedtime-wreaks-havoc-on-our-sleep-a-sleep-researcher-explains-why-screens-alone-arent-the-main-culprit-251453), fragmented attention, [addiction](https://theconversation.com/it-is-hijacking-my-brain-a-team-of-experts-found-ways-to-help-young-people-addicted-to-social-media-to-cut-the-craving-219954), loneliness, social comparison and [perfectionism](https://theconversation.com/social-media-teenage-girls-with-perfectionist-tendencies-need-to-take-extra-care-heres-how-95479). A 2026 study found that young people between 14 and 20 are more than [100% more likely to experience mental illness](https://doi.org/10.1503/cmaj.250926) than 14- to 20-year-olds 50 years ago. Increased use of social media and screen time is compounding an already precarious time for minors. In 2021 it was estimated that 20.5% of young people [experienced elevated symptoms of anxiety](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2021.2482), and 25.2% of youth reported elevated symptoms of depression globally. Many factors contribute to this, including [better screening and awareness of kids\u2019 mental health problems](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2025.4605), as well as societal disruptions as a [result of the COVID-19 pandemic](https://theconversation.com/students-are-returning-to-school-with-anxiety-grief-and-gaps-in-social-skills-will-there-be-enough-school-mental-health-resources-165279). As the world sheltered in place and moved online for work, school and social interactions, there was an [increase in isolation and loneliness](https://www.apa.org/news/press/releases/2022/05/covid-19-increase-loneliness). Not all bad In light of these concerns, [some researchers argue](https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2026-721073) that bans on social media platforms are like putting an age restriction on buying alcohol or cigarettes. But unlike these addictive and potentially harmful substances, there are [benefits to social media](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40124-025-00357-7), especially for teens who feel disconnected from their local communities and [want to build relationships](https://theconversation.com/screen-time-guidelines-for-kids-and-adolescents-have-shifted-as-research-paints-a-more-nuanced-picture-281300) with others who feel similarly. Social media platforms allow for global connectivity and community-building with like-minded groups. They also provide a place for [self-expression and civic engagement](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40124-025-00357-7). In a Pew survey of U.S. teens, 80% said [social media helps them stay connected](https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2022/11/16/connection-creativity-and-drama-teen-life-on-social-media-in-2022/) to what\u2019s happening in their friends\u2019 lives. Even as states and countries are moving to ban young people from social media, there isn\u2019t clear evidence that spending time on these platforms is the main cause of their mental health problems. Show me the money One place where the comparison with tobacco is actually quite apt is in relation to the money from lawsuit settlements. In 1998, states [settled a lawsuit against the major tobacco companies](https://www.naag.org/our-work/naag-center-for-tobacco-and-public-health/the-master-settlement-agreement/) to recover smoking-related healthcare costs. Part of the settlement was allocated to programs aimed at reducing youth smoking. In 2026, total state funding for these programs amounts to [just 3.4% of the $21.7 billion](https://www.tobaccofreekids.org/state-report) in revenue states will collect from tobacco settlement payments and tobacco taxes. Similarly, some money from an [August 2026 Meta settlement with a group of states](https://www.npr.org/2026/08/26/nx-s1-5944781/meta-settlement-child-safety-lawsuit) for $17 billion is slated for [state-based programs focusing on mental health infrastructure](https://apnews.com/article/meta-trial-instagram-settlement-97d342f2a33d835eda2356c5e1af9e37), school and community programs, and public health campaigns. However, there is no oversight or direction as to how states should use the funds, nor is there a mechanism to report how that money has improved kids\u2019 mental health care, safety and well-being. Is it even possible? Early evidence from the Australian social media ban shows that kids are finding work-arounds: Roughly 61% of kids ages 12\u201315 [continue to access platforms using false IDs](https://mollyrosefoundation.org/more-than-60-of-australian-children-still-using-social-media-despite-ban-for-under-16s-research-shows/), altered appearances or virtual private networks, or VPNs, even though [millions of accounts were disabled](https://www.cato.org/blog/policymakers-should-stop-going-after-vpns-enforce-internet-age-restrictions). In fact, this has become so common that U.K. lawmakers are debating restricting consumer VPN access. Some countries, such as Australia, require the platforms themselves to develop ways of verifying a user\u2019s age. Some minors have [bypassed scans](https://www.bitdefender.com/en-us/blog/hotforsecurity/kids-bypass-age-verification) from [biometric age estimation technology](https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2025/12/age-assurance-methods-explained) by using photo manipulation software, substituting celebrity photos, [using clever makeup](https://fortune.com/2026/05/05/gen-alpha-using-makeup-to-pass-age-verification-tech-online/) and even utilizing older friends and parents to pass the \u201cselfie tests\u201d on their behalf. Others simply manipulate their birth years during registration or rely on [existing adult-verified accounts](https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-63204605) managed by family members. And all of these age-verification methods, whether developed by a government or a social media platform, rely on [collecting sensitive data to estimate age](https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/mar/02/ban-children-social-media-biometic-data-surveilled) accurately, which creates privacy concerns. Australia is currently [seeking to raise fines](https://www.npr.org/2026/06/29/nx-s1-5874576/australia-fines-child-social-media-accounts) on social media platforms that fail to comply with its requirements. The Australian safety commissioner, charged with enforcing the ban, issued a compliance update that showed 7 in 10 children who held accounts in restricted platforms [remained on those platforms](https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/most-australian-teens-still-social-media-three-months-after-under-16-ban-began-2026-07-31/), and more than 80% of Australian teens ages 10-15 still use social media frequently. Beyond concerns about enacting and enforcing these restrictions, some policymakers also worry that total bans could push some kids to [migrate to even riskier online spaces](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copsyc.2022.101339). Age verification software has proved to be unreliable and raises new concerns about young people\u2019s privacy online. [STR/NurPhoto via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/an-age-verification-login-screen-is-seen-on-a-mobile-device-news-photo/1244386242?phrase=kids%20social%20media%20age%20verification&adppopup=true) What could work Based on the evidence, I\u2019m not convinced that banning social media platforms will be an effective solution to the mental health crisis among young people today. So what could work? Supporting young people\u2019s mental health is a complex endeavor that will require a combination of people, organizations and resources coming together. I believe kids\u2019 mental health would be better served by governments enforcing [safety by default standards](https://hateaid.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/safety-by-design-report-pathways-to-safer-social-media-platforms-hateaid.pdf) on social media platforms rather than outright bans. This means that the highest security and privacy options are in place by default so that minors still on a platform are protected. For instance, accounts would be unsearchable by default, so users would have to intentionally make a profile change to be found by people they don\u2019t know in real life. Safety by default standards also place limits on the manipulation, algorithmic restraint and other addictive mechanisms that may lead to psychological harm in vulnerable populations such as young people. Educating kids on digital literacy and online safety as well as ensuring [some screen-free time](https://www.commonsensemedia.org/articles/parents-ultimate-guide-to-social-media) are also going to help support kids\u2019 mental health. It\u2019s also important for parents and other caregivers to recognize changes in behavior as a result of [addiction to social platforms and screens](https://theconversation.com/how-much-time-do-kids-spend-on-devices-playing-games-watching-videos-texting-and-using-the-phone-210118). And when adults start seeing signs of a problem, they need clear, easy and affordable ways to connect their kids to mental health support, which is not available in most places right now. And finally, the United Nations recommends [bringing kids into the conversation](https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/05/1167608) about what\u2019s best for them online. Obviously, this does not mean putting 12-year-olds in charge of regulatory agencies. But feedback from young people about what they care about, what they get out of being online and what makes it hard for them to look away from their screens could inform changes to social media platforms and education programs that could help young people engage online more safely and maintain a healthy balance. [Facebook](https://theconversation.com/topics/facebook-108) [Social media](https://theconversation.com/topics/social-media-109) [Mental health](https://theconversation.com/topics/mental-health-343) [YouTube](https://theconversation.com/topics/youtube-1950) [Young people](https://theconversation.com/topics/young-people-4225) [Instagram](https://theconversation.com/topics/instagram-4276) [Teens](https://theconversation.com/topics/teens-22776) [Chronic illnesses](https://theconversation.com/topics/chronic-illnesses-30104) [Meta](https://theconversation.com/topics/meta-112207) [kids mental health](https://theconversation.com/topics/kids-mental-health-140904) [Mind and body](https://theconversation.com/topics/mind-and-body-153894) [Social media ban](https://theconversation.com/topics/social-media-ban-159730) [TC global health](https://theconversation.com/topics/tc-global-health-189737) [Ari Lightman, Carnegie Mellon University](https://theconversation.com/can-social-media-bans-protect-young-peoples-mental-health-early-research-suggests-that-bans-arent-the-answer-291629) Author [Ari Lightman](https://theconversation.com/profiles/ari-lightman-887183) Professor of Digital Media and Marketing, Carnegie Mellon University Disclosure statement Ari Lightman is affiliated with Jewish Healthcare Foundation Partners [Carnegie Mellon University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/carnegie-mellon-university-970) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.7ncgegxq3](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.7ncgegxq3)", "content_hash": "c681fe120a2a2a6cfe9ab443b5f3d84d5bbb314f22d20113341dbd61187f9f96", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:18Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "940", "title": "Americans want electric cars \u2013 they just can\u2019t find\u00a0them", "url": "https://theconversation.com/americans-want-electric-cars-they-just-cant-find-them-293901", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/americans-want-electric-cars-they-just-cant-find-them-293901", "text": "Prices for used electric vehicles are climbing because new models are \u2013 and are likely to remain \u2013 in short supply. [Justin Sullivan/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/used-electric-vehicles-are-displayed-on-the-sales-lot-at-news-photo/2269134484) Americans want electric cars \u2013 they just can\u2019t find them Published: October 16, 2026 8:21am EDT https://theconversation.com/americans-want-electric-cars-they-just-cant-find-them-293901 https://theconversation.com/americans-want-electric-cars-they-just-cant-find-them-293901 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Within three months of the U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran on Feb. 28, 2026, the average price of gasoline for American drivers [climbed from US$2.94 to $4.35 a gallon](https://www.eia.gov/petroleum/gasdiesel/). If there was ever a moment for Americans to switch to electric vehicles, this was it. From March to May 2026, in fact, [sales of hybrid electric vehicles were 18%](https://www.anl.gov/esia/light-duty-electric-drive-vehicles-monthly-sales-updates) higher than over the same months in 2025. But sales of fully electric vehicles [were 16% lower from March to May 2026](https://www.anl.gov/esia/light-duty-electric-drive-vehicles-monthly-sales-updates) compared with the same months in 2025. Some observers took that as evidence that [high gas prices alone](https://grist.org/solutions/why-hybrids-not-evs-are-winning-over-u-s-consumers/) [wouldn\u2019t push Americans](https://www.autoweek.com/news/a71255747/americans-not-buying-evs-despite-high-gas-prices/) toward [fully electric cars](https://www.edmunds.com/car-news/ev-consideration-high-gas-prices.html), but that conclusion misses half the story. I study [car buyers and their preferences](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=DY2D56IAAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao), and there is an important distinction between what consumers want and what they can actually buy. Sales reveal what cars people actually bought, but not necessarily what they actually wanted. When the supply of some kinds of cars can\u2019t keep up with demand, prices rise, and that\u2019s exactly what happened for electric vehicles in 2026. An industry in retreat In 2026, hybrids and all-electric vehicles faced the same jump in demand from the oil crisis. While there were plenty of hybrids available to meet that demand, the same was not true for EVs. Hybrid production had been growing for years, led by Toyota, which [sells about half of the hybrids bought in the United States](https://www.automotiveworld.com/news/iran-war-drives-us-hybrid-sales-up-37-as-evs-lag-behind/). As a result, even as sales surged, [hybrid inventories on dealer lots were 19% higher in March 2026](https://www.spglobal.com/automotive-insights/en/blogs/2026/04/march-2026-new-vehicle-inventory-trends) than they had been a year earlier. However, EV production had been in retreat since the [tax and spending law](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1/text) President Donald Trump signed in July 2025 [ended federal tax credits](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/10/trump-big-beautiful-bill-ends-7500-ev-tax-credit-time-to-buy-vehicle.html) for EVs. Buyers had been able to get up to $7,500 for new EVs and $4,000 for used ones, but that ended after Sept. 30, 2025. Automakers responded by cutting back their EV production. General Motors [laid off more than 3,400 workers](https://www.wardsauto.com/news/gm-layoffs-ultium-cells-factory-zero/804171/) at its EV and battery plants and shifted its Tennessee plant to make [batteries for energy storage](https://whbl.com/2026/08/12/battery-cell-production-at-ohio-plant-to-restart-for-gm-evs/), not cars. Ford [wrote off $19.5 billion from its EV business](https://fortune.com/2025/12/15/ford-scrapping-ev-f150-lightning-19-billion-pivot/), ended production of the all-electric F-150 Lightning and [converted a Tennessee EV plant](https://tennesseelookout.com/2026/02/18/ford-changed-direction-on-plans-for-its-west-tennessee-plant-what-happens-now/) to build gas-powered trucks. Buyers, for their part, rushed to beat the tax credit deadline, pushing EVs to 11.3% of new vehicle sales in September 2025. But by November 2025, after the tax credits ended, [their share dropped to 4.3%](https://www.anl.gov/esia/light-duty-electric-drive-vehicles-monthly-sales-updates). As 2026 progressed and gas prices began to rise, so did EV demand. New EV inventories declined, shrinking from about [130 days of supply](https://electriccarsreport.com/2026/03/us-ev-market-splits-in-q1-as-new-sales-drop-28-while-used-demand-surges/) in the first quarter of 2026 to [78 days](https://www.coxautoinc.com/insights/ev-market-monitor-august-2026/) by August \u2013 just two days above the supply level of gas-burning cars. Discounts on new EV models shrank too. My analysis of [online listings from marketcheck.com](https://www.marketcheck.com/) shows the median new EV was listed 9.8% below sticker price in February. By April, the median discount was 6.6%, and 4.7% in June. That\u2019s a sign that dealers saw rising demand, not inventory to clear. A run on used electric vehicles Before the war, prices for used electric vehicles were still drifting down in the wake of the expired used EV federal tax credit. After the war began, they [climbed 13% through late June 2026](https://www.marketcheck.com). For a typical used electric vehicle, that\u2019s about $3,500 more. Other data sources point in the same direction. Recurrent, a [company that reports on batteries and used EVs](https://www.recurrentauto.com/about), found that the price of a [used 2023 Chevrolet Bolt rose almost 20%](https://web.archive.org/web/20260923101630/https://www.recurrentauto.com/research/used-electric-vehicle-buying-report) from January to June 2026. And inventories of used electric vehicles dropped too, with [only 31 days of supply in May](https://www.coxautoinc.com/insights/ev-market-monitor-may-2026/), 23% below the year before. A costly misreading None of this means the electric vehicle market is healthy. High prices and uncertain access to charging stations [are real stumbling blocks for people who are thinking about buying an EV](http://doi.org/10.1088/2516-1083/ada199). But the data doesn\u2019t support the claim that electric vehicle sales stalled in 2026 because Americans don\u2019t want them. Rather, the sales stalled because there weren\u2019t enough EVs available to buy \u2013 and there were plenty of hybrids as an alternative. The distinction matters because automakers have treated a change in the policy landscape as a change in consumer demand, leading to shortages with real costs. The average new car now sells for about $50,000 and the average new electric vehicle for about $55,000, according to [Kelley Blue Book](https://www.coxautoinc.com/insights/august-2026-atp-report/), a company that tracks car prices. The current situation leaves drivers exposed to the volatility of oil prices with fewer alternatives. The U.S. is the [world\u2019s largest oil producer](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=67844), yet a war in the Persian Gulf raised gas prices significantly in just three months. Oil trades on a global market, so [American drivers pay global prices](https://theconversation.com/whats-in-the-price-of-a-gallon-of-gas-281494). Seven months into the war, the pressure on drivers is still [going full throttle](https://theconversation.com/why-fuel-prices-may-go-higher-still-292853). Oil was still [above $100 a barrel](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/prices.php) in mid-October, and [diesel hit a record $6.52 a gallon](https://abc7.com/post/trump-says-us-get-diesel-russia-relaxing-pressure-moscow-ease-prices-before-midterms/19925563/) on Sept. 22. Electric vehicle sales haven\u2019t caught up. [Argonne\u2019s data](https://www.anl.gov/esia/light-duty-electric-drive-vehicles-monthly-sales-updates) show 86,000 of them sold in August, fewer than the 101,000 sold in May, while hybrid sales stayed above 216,000 a month all summer. A look ahead Rather than move to increase electric vehicle supply and make them more affordable, federal policy continues to hinder the U.S. electric vehicle sector. The [fuel economy rule released Sept. 28, 2026](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/30/2026-19964/the-safer-affordable-fuel-efficient-safe-vehicles-rule-iii-for-model-years-2022-to-2031-passenger), loosens efficiency requirements for gas-powered vehicles and in 2028 ends a system that let automakers that beat the standards \u2013 mostly EV makers \u2013 [earn credits for exceeding them](https://www.autobodynews.com/news/ev-makers-anticipate-billions-in-revenue-loss-as-fuel-economy-credit-program-halted) and sell those to automakers that fell short. That means automakers will have fewer regulatory incentives to produce EVs going forward. Regulators say [buyers will save $1,289](https://www.nhtsa.gov/sites/nhtsa.gov/files/2026-09/CAFE-SAFE-MY-2022-2031-Final-RIA_1.pdf) on the purchase price of a new gas-powered vehicle from reduced engineering and manufacturing costs to make less efficient engines. But the same analysis estimates drivers will [spend about $1,600 more on gas](https://rollcall.com/2026/09/28/trump-administration-cuts-mileage-rules-for-cars-light-trucks/) over the life of the car, and that\u2019s using fuel price forecasts well below today\u2019s prices. [Economists say](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aef0464) the [administration\u2019s math does not properly account for](https://downloads.regulations.gov/NHTSA-2025-0491-6064/attachment_4.pdf) those fuel costs. Chinese EV manufacturing is growing, but the U.S. market is mostly closed to those products and the companies that make them, and may get even more closed. [Kevin Frayer/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/workers-from-chinese-electric-vehicle-company-nio-inspect-news-photo/2194796297) The U.S. has also effectively shut out Chinese automakers, even though nearly three-quarters of the world\u2019s electric cars [are built in China](https://www.iea.org/reports/global-ev-outlook-2026/executive-summary). In 2024, Chinese car buyers could choose from over 130 EV models priced under $40,000; American buyers had [just four EV models under that threshold](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adz0541). Beyond [tariffs of more than 100%](https://www.reuters.com/business/us-locks-steep-china-tariff-hikes-many-start-sept-27-2024-09-13/) on EVs made in China, a [Commerce Department rule](https://www.bis.gov/press-release/commerce-finalizes-rule-secure-connected-vehicle-supply-chains-foreign-adversary-threats) bars cars from Chinese-linked automakers starting with model year 2027, [even if they are built in the U.S](https://theautowire.com/2026/09/20/chinese-automakers-us-factories-connected-vehicle-ban/). A [bill pending in Congress](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/4429) would [widen the ban](https://theautowire.com/2026/09/27/connected-vehicle-security-act-waivers-congress/), blocking Chinese investment in U.S. auto factories altogether. As long as gas stays expensive, the gap between the electric vehicles Americans want and the electric vehicles dealers can sell them is likely to remain. Decisions such as ending the tax credit, rolling back fuel economy standards and banning Chinese technology all make it harder to build them, which means Americans will continue to have fewer affordable electric vehicles to choose from. [Supply and demand](https://theconversation.com/topics/supply-and-demand-983) [Gas prices](https://theconversation.com/topics/gas-prices-7587) [Trump administration](https://theconversation.com/topics/trump-administration-33294) [Hybrid cars](https://theconversation.com/topics/hybrid-cars-41360) [US-Iran conflict](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-iran-conflict-73960) [Electric vehicle policies](https://theconversation.com/topics/electric-vehicle-policies-124395) [Electric vehicles (EVs)](https://theconversation.com/topics/electric-vehicles-evs-145458) [Iran war](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-war-185765) [John Paul Helveston, George Washington University](https://theconversation.com/americans-want-electric-cars-they-just-cant-find-them-293901) Author [John Paul Helveston](https://theconversation.com/profiles/john-paul-helveston-2411672) Associate Professor of Engineering Management and Systems Engineering, George Washington University Disclosure statement John Paul Helveston receives funding from the U.S. Department of Energy, U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology, and the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.nvfqmhjwq](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.nvfqmhjwq)", "content_hash": "5bb624dd25cd85264ce833e0800398a1233bf2abd0d2d76b94f600622d39c73b", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:19Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "941", "title": "For centuries, bird migration was a mystery \u2013 a tech revolution is changing that, and could help save these amazing\u00a0creatures", "url": "https://theconversation.com/for-centuries-bird-migration-was-a-mystery-a-tech-revolution-is-changing-that-and-could-help-save-these-amazing-creatures-294345", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/for-centuries-bird-migration-was-a-mystery-a-tech-revolution-is-changing-that-and-could-help-save-these-amazing-creatures-294345", "text": "Noisy tricolored blackbirds can be found in fields and wetlands in the Pacific coast states, but they are losing habitat. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) For centuries, bird migration was a mystery \u2013 a tech revolution is changing that, and could help save these amazing creatures Published: October 16, 2026 8:21am EDT https://theconversation.com/for-centuries-bird-migration-was-a-mystery-a-tech-revolution-is-changing-that-and-could-help-save-these-amazing-creatures-294345 https://theconversation.com/for-centuries-bird-migration-was-a-mystery-a-tech-revolution-is-changing-that-and-could-help-save-these-amazing-creatures-294345 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article For [more than 2,000 years](https://penelope.uchicago.edu/aristotle/histanimals8.html), scientists have scratched their heads over one of the most basic mysteries of the natural world: Where do birds go? [Billions of birds](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-018-0666-4) appear and disappear with the seasons every year. Early naturalists offered explanations for the disappearances that today seem fantastical: Birds [transformed into other species](https://penelope.uchicago.edu/aristotle/histanimals8.html), one theory went. Others suggested they [hibernated underwater](https://www.allaboutbirds.org/news/swallow-debate-america-mud-winter-hibernate/) or even [migrated to the moon](https://thereader.mitpress.mit.edu/when-birds-migrated-to-the-moon/). The truth, that these small animals were traveling thousands of miles across the planet, must once have seemed just as improbable. Then, in the early 1800s, dramatic evidence emerged supporting the theory of migration. White storks began appearing in Germany with African arrows embedded in their bodies. These birds became known as [pfeilst\u00f6rche, or \u201carrow storks](https://www.audubon.org/magazine/what-gruesome-stork-taught-us-about-bird-migration).\u201d They provided compelling evidence that birds disappearing from Europe were traveling all the way to Africa and back. When storks began showing up with African arrows in their backs, early ornithologists made an important discovery: Birds migrate. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) Almost two centuries later, scientists are still asking the same question, but our ability to answer it has been transformed. For most of ornithological history, scientists understood migration through brief glimpses of a bird, a specimen collected somewhere else, or the rare instance of a banded bird recovered hundreds or thousands of miles from where it had been marked. From these fragments of intelligence, scientists reconstructed journeys that took place largely beyond their view. Today, it\u2019s possible to see the journeys themselves, almost in real time. A century of following birds Bird banding was [the first tool](https://www.nature.com/scitable/knowledge/library/bird-banding-83032042/) used to attempt a systematic approach to connect these fragments of information. Since the early 1900s, researchers have [placed uniquely numbered metal bands](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4-HxZFJF8Y) on birds and hoped someone, somewhere, would encounter them again. Of [more than 79 million birds banded, only about 6.5 million have been recovered](https://www.pwrc.usgs.gov/BBL/Bander_Portal/login/howmany.php). That generally told scientists just two things: where a bird was banded and where it was encountered again. Everything between them remained invisible. Carefully attaching a band to a bird\u2019s leg allowed scientists to see where that bird ended up, but it couldn\u2019t show its entire journey. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) Over the past quarter-century or so, the tools used to determine where birds go have changed dramatically. Tracking devices can now be carried by increasingly smaller birds, [recording their movements](https://doi.org/10.1038/srep11069) and communicating with land-based receivers or satellites orbiting the earth. Testing [stable isotopes](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00442-005-0071-y) in feathers and other tissues can reveal where a bird originated. [Genetic testing](https://doi.org/10.1111/mec.12977) can distinguish populations. At the same time, the extraordinary [growth of bird-watching and citizen science](https://www.fws.gov/sites/default/files/documents/2024-11/2022-birding-in-the-us-demographic-and-economic-analysis.pdf) has created another revolution. Millions of observations by bird-watchers submitted to [eBird](https://ebird.org/home), an online database to record the birds they see, [show the distribution and abundance of birds](https://science.ebird.org/status-and-trends) across a continent throughout the year in ways early ornithologists could scarcely have imagined. Collectively, these methods open an extraordinary new window into migratory birds and one of nature\u2019s most remarkable behaviors: migration. Burrowing owls breed in the Western U.S., raising their young in burrows often taken over from ground squirrels or prairie dogs. In winter, they migrate to Southwestern states, Mexico or Central America. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) Discovering unknown migrations More than [70% of North American bird species](https://ace-eco.org/vol19/iss2/art13/) are migratory. Yet scientists have known surprisingly little about where specific populations go. A species like the [American robin](https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/American_Robin/overview) may breed across thousands of miles of North America, with different breeding populations migrating to entirely different places for the winter. Birds breeding in one part of the continent may travel one route and encounter one set of environmental conditions, while members of the same species experience a completely different set of conditions along another route. The yellow-billed cuckoo has three main flyways but many other routes between the U.S. and South America. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) These connections between where a bird breeds and where it spends the winter is called [migratory connectivity](https://repository.si.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/51a5d839-0ecc-4f44-ab3a-1371ef88261a/content). They are critical for understanding everything from the consequences of climate change on birds to how avian pathogens and diseases spread, to a species\u2019 basic ecology. We are [ornithologists](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=qi-EePYAAAAJ&hl=en) and [conservation scientists](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=NbFDyzcAAAAJ&hl=en) who have spent almost 15 years along with a team of contributors compiling this rapidly expanding knowledge on bird movement into [The Atlas of North America\u2019s Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds). The atlas, being published on Nov. 3, 2026, by Princeton University Press, covers the latest information on seasonal movements for nearly 600 species and combines a century of bird-banding data with eBird observations, tracking studies, stable isotope testing, genetics and other sources of information. What emerges is a picture of migratory birds that is both more complicated and more remarkable than the simple arrows pointing between breeding and wintering ranges on a map. Consider the [yellow-billed cuckoo](https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Yellow-billed_Cuckoo/overview). These birds breed over a wide range of the eastern two-thirds of the U.S. but migrate to a relatively small wintering area in the [Gran Chaco region](https://www.worldwildlife.org/places/gran-chaco/) of northern Argentina, Paraguay and southern Bolivia. The peregrine falcon travels as far north as Alaska in summer, then some individuals return as far south as South America. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) In contrast, [peregrine falcons](https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Peregrine_Falcon/overview) that breed in Alaska migrate almost directly south, dispersing across the Americas to wintering grounds that extend as far as southern South America. Discoveries like these are accumulating rapidly. Collectively, they amount to a renaissance in our understanding of animal movement never before imagined. Living masterpieces The more clearly scientists can follow individual birds and populations across the planet, the more extraordinary these species can seem. Migratory birds such as the [arctic tern](https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Arctic_Tern/overview) and [northern wheatear](https://doi.org/10.1098/rsbl.2011.1223) cross oceans, deserts and mountain ranges. Some, like the [bar-tailed godwits, fly thousands of miles without stopping](https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2008.1142). Others, like the American redstart, return year after year to [remarkably precise breeding](https://doi.org/10.1139/z96-254) and [wintering](https://doi.org/10.1093/auk/118.1.92) locations. In winter, large flocks of sandhill cranes can be found in places like Bosque del Apache, N.M., and Anahuac National Wildlife Refuge, Texas. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) They navigate landscapes and respond to winds, weather, food and environmental conditions across enormous geographic scales. The Atlas of North America\u2019s Migratory Birds\u2019 combines illustrations and maps to show where bird populations go when they migrate. [Princeton University Press](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) We see these species and their migrations as among evolution\u2019s great living masterpieces. That\u2019s why we combined science with natural history and artist [John Anderton\u2019s](https://alchetron.com/John-C-Anderton) extraordinary illustrations of the birds themselves. The maps allow us to see where birds travel, and the art brings the species to life. Together, they make visible something that humans could only imagine for most of our history. A map for conservation North America has [lost about 3 billion birds](https://www.birds.cornell.edu/home/bring-birds-back/) during the past half-century, and many migratory species continue to decline. To understand why a population is declining and help save it, researchers need to understand the species\u2019 entire annual cycle: its critical stopover habitats, wintering grounds and threats along its migration routes. For much of the history of ornithology, scientists studied birds primarily during their breeding season. That made practical sense. [Birds are conspicuous then](https://afonet.org/2022/07/peterson-reference-guide-to-bird-behavior/) \u2013 singing, defending territories, building nests and raising young. Male buff-breasted sandpipers perform a courtship dance on the Arctic tundra to attract their mates. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) But a migratory bird may spend only a few months of its life in its breeding grounds. Conditions such as weather, food availability and habitat quality encountered during migration can [determine whether an individual survives to breed again](https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.1854). Drought thousands of miles away can affect a bird\u2019s condition months later. [American redstarts](https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/American_Redstart/overview) depart [later after dry winters](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.282.5395.1884) and [produce fewer young](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2660976/). Declining horseshoe crab populations in Delaware Bay have reduced food availability for migrating [red knots](https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Red_Knot/overview), contributing to [lower survival](https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2003.2663). Rusty blackbirds favor wetlands. The males have feathers with rusty edges in winter, but they turn glossy black during breeding season. [John Anderton/The Atlas of North America's Migratory Birds](https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691163185/the-atlas-of-north-americas-migratory-birds?srsltid=AU7gw4WXEPB1-KOQTviczsSsMvq80f-4jIOIdb5BrdYHRmgzxpsWFQsn) The power of people All of this knowledge has accumulated because people value birds and have devoted enormous time and effort to understanding them. For more than a century, thousands of bird-banders have woken up before the crack of dawn to set traps, carefully place bands on birds\u2019 legs and release them, often knowing the chances of ever learning what happened to those individuals were exceedingly small. More recently, tracking technology has allowed researchers to [follow individual birds across continents and oceans](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0603715103). Those tracks exist because scientists, students, technicians and others have spent countless hours attaching and recovering tags, and contributing and sharing their data. In this animation, Cornell\u2019s Bird Lab traced the routes of 15 species using tracking signals. From tiny sensors to networks of humans with binoculars, efforts to understand migrations are helping to show where cruicial habitat can be preserved. Today, millions of bird-watchers contribute observations that, thanks to advances in data science, allow us to [watch migration unfold](https://science.ebird.org/status-and-trends/abundance-animations) across an entire continent. We have spent much of our careers [documenting the decline of birds](https://doi.org/10.1038/s44358-026-00177-7), and we know the challenges facing migratory species are formidable. But we also have hope for these creatures\u2019 survival. Bird migrations face threats, including from habitat loss and climate change. Scientists today know more about where migratory birds go, how populations are connected and what they encounter along the way than at any previous point in history. That knowledge is helping to identify where conservation efforts can make a difference for the future. [Climate change](https://theconversation.com/topics/climate-change-27) [Conservation](https://theconversation.com/topics/conservation-72) [Migration](https://theconversation.com/topics/migration-412) [Biology](https://theconversation.com/topics/biology-521) [Birds](https://theconversation.com/topics/birds-728) [Ecology](https://theconversation.com/topics/ecology-748) [Breeding](https://theconversation.com/topics/breeding-3074) [North America](https://theconversation.com/topics/north-america-5506) [Birdwatching](https://theconversation.com/topics/birdwatching-7493) [Long read](https://theconversation.com/topics/long-read-11601) [Tracking](https://theconversation.com/topics/tracking-19220) [Atlas](https://theconversation.com/topics/atlas-23508) [Maps](https://theconversation.com/topics/maps-37082) [Illustration](https://theconversation.com/topics/illustration-57787) [eBird](https://theconversation.com/topics/ebird-69392) [Birding](https://theconversation.com/topics/birding-87801) [Peter P. Marra, Amy L. Scarpignato, Georgetown University](https://theconversation.com/for-centuries-bird-migration-was-a-mystery-a-tech-revolution-is-changing-that-and-could-help-save-these-amazing-creatures-294345) Authors [Peter P. Marra](https://theconversation.com/profiles/peter-p-marra-2692554) Dean of The Earth Commons, Professor of Biology and the Environment, Georgetown University [Amy L. Scarpignato](https://theconversation.com/profiles/amy-l-scarpignato-2771327) Senior Species Recovery Specialist, Road to Recovery, The Earth Commons, Georgetown University Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.5kddy4je4](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.5kddy4je4)", "content_hash": "182afc90b79865767e17f38d52f8e79503c677c1e04a9acc56d430d75c6105d9", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:20Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "942", "title": "Starlink slowdown underscores Ukraine\u2019s reliance on the US in the battle for the\u00a0skies", "url": "https://theconversation.com/starlink-slowdown-underscores-ukraines-reliance-on-the-us-in-the-battle-for-the-skies-294446", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/starlink-slowdown-underscores-ukraines-reliance-on-the-us-in-the-battle-for-the-skies-294446", "text": "The aftermath of a Russian missile strike on a nine-story residential building in Kyiv on Oct. 14, 2026. [Danylo Antoniuk/Ukrinform/Future Publishing via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/rescuer-on-an-aerial-platform-extinguishes-a-fire-that-news-photo/2299726863?phrase=ukraine%20Russia&adppopup=true) Starlink slowdown underscores Ukraine\u2019s reliance on the US in the battle for the skies Published: October 16, 2026 8:22am EDT https://theconversation.com/starlink-slowdown-underscores-ukraines-reliance-on-the-us-in-the-battle-for-the-skies-294446 https://theconversation.com/starlink-slowdown-underscores-ukraines-reliance-on-the-us-in-the-battle-for-the-skies-294446 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article If Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy needed a reminder that Ukraine\u2019s war with Russia may well be decided in the skies by the nation with the most advanced weaponry, then it was delivered this week \u2013 [seemingly](https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/order-limit-starshield-ukraine-came-inside-white-house-sources-say-2026-10-15/) by the United States. On the night of Oct. 13-14, 2026, the guidance system Ukraine\u2019s military uses to blunt Russia\u2019s aerial attacks suddenly [stopped working normally](https://theconversation.com/is-the-us-limiting-starlink-in-ukraine-the-risks-could-be-catastrophic-294496). The system, called Starshield, is funded by the U.S. government and run by Elon Musk\u2019s low Earth orbit telecommunications company Starlink, part of SpaceX. Ukraine uses the system to counter strikes and to target Russian military forces and logistics in occupied Ukrainian territory. But new speed restrictions, imposed without notice remotely by code, contributed to multiple mission failures. Zelenskyy [described the move as a \u201cbig mistake](https://thehill.com/policy/international/6148494-zelensky-trump-musk-starlink-limitations/).\u201d Others, including Musk, [pointed the finger at the U.S. government](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8wy9veedgygo). Amid the blame game, the restrictions were just as suddenly removed: By Oct. 15, Starlink services had [apparently been restored to full capacity](https://www.axios.com/2026/10/15/ukraine-starlink-restored-strikes-russia-trump-musk). As a [scholar of geopolitics](https://spia.vt.edu/people/Faculty/toal.html), I see the incident as a significant moment in a series of setbacks Ukraine has faced in recent months, as shifting military technologies and targeting alter the balance of power in the conflict. Technological dominance has been tilting toward Moscow of late, exposing Ukraine\u2019s vulnerability to [Russia\u2019s relentlessly modernizing war machine](https://www.economist.com/briefing/2026/09/17/russia-has-entered-a-new-phase-of-total-war). What was unexpected, though, was the power flex by computer code against Ukraine\u2019s innovative defensive systems, [apparently at the behest](https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/order-limit-starshield-ukraine-came-inside-white-house-sources-say-2026-10-15/) of the U.S. government. A history of military innovation The latest developments in Ukraine belong to a broader history of technological networks and geopolitics intersecting in ways that drive military innovation. As far back as 1904, British geographer and politician [Halford Mackinder](https://www.britannica.com/topic/The-Geographical-Pivot-of-History) observed that the new railway networks crossing Eurasia were changing the strategic geography of land power by reducing the time needed to move military forces across vast territories. His larger insight was that pioneering technological networks reconfigure how distance, mobility and military power relate to one another. Mackinder\u2019s land-power anxiety was compounded less than a decade later by the development of airpower. Bomb-delivering aircraft rendered countries vulnerable to attacks from above, fears made real during World War I. This prompted the development of air defense and radar systems, which became [ever more elaborate](https://www.ll.mit.edu/about/history/sage-semi-automatic-ground-environment-air-defense-system) in the early Cold War. The development of hypersonic intercontinental missile technologies rendered these defenses obsolete, with the superpowers eventually accepting the dangerous condition of mutually assured destruction. Fast-forward to today. Relatively cheap, unmanned aerial systems connected to low Earth orbit satellite networks have birthed a new frontier of difficult-to-defend-against innovations in aerial warfare. And Russia\u2019s war against Ukraine has dramatically accelerated this revolution. \u201c[Close the sky](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/16/close-the-sky-over-ukraine-zelenskyy-pleads-us-congress)\u201d was an early, [unheeded](https://www.reuters.com/business/aerospace-defense/nato-meets-ukraine-calls-no-fly-zone-hinder-russia-2022-03-04/) plea from Zelenskyy to Ukraine\u2019s allies to establish a no-fly zone over the country when Russia first invaded the country in February 2022. Relations between Presidents Volodymyr Zelenskyy and Donald Trump have deteriorated. [Brendan Smialowski/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/ukraines-president-volodymyr-zelensky-meets-with-us-news-photo/2296063659?phrase=Zelensky%20trump&adppopup=true) But Ukraine did receive significant help from the U.S. in the form of satellite-based intelligence sharing. Starlink, which [established its low Earth orbit internet service](https://www.space.com/spacex-starlink-satellites.html) in May 2019, provided terminals and service to Ukraine for free after Russian strikes disrupted local internet networks. But by late 2022, Starlink [wanted payment for its services to Ukraine](https://www.reuters.com/technology/musk-says-spacex-cannot-fund-ukraines-starlink-internet-indefinitely-2022-10-14/). The U.S. Department of Defense and the [government of Poland](https://www.the-independent.com/news/world/europe/poland-elon-musk-starlink-putin-russia-war-b3066514.html) stepped in to fund what later became distinct defense and civilian systems. \u2018The battle for the sky\u2019 A combination of networked technological systems and cheaper drones has transformed how the Russia-Ukraine war is fought. Where previously tanks and massed armed formations fought across relatively delineated front lines, now drones from either side battle in the sky. These drones come in many different sizes and shapes. Some are low-altitude, short-range drones designed for a \u201c[kill zone](https://www.reuters.com/graphics/UKRAINE-CRISIS/KILL-ZONE/znpnojmknvl/)\u201d that ranges from 15 to 30 kilometers or more. Others are higher-altitude, midrange drones designed to attack behind enemy lines. Finally, there are far-range options designed to travel long distances to hit strategic targets, which have expanded from military sites to logistical hubs, warehouses and data centers. The Ukraine war is now largely, as Zelenskyy [declared this summer](https://www.ft.com/content/6ca8de40-15e4-470e-a258-6dd2d8d9d83e?syn-25a6b1a6=1), a \u201cbattle for the sky.\u201d But that battle relies on a vast infrastructure that encompasses [global supply chains running through China](https://www.csis.org/analysis/drone-supply-chain-war-identifying-chokepoints-making-drone), [funding systems](https://www.kielinstitut.de/research/topics/war-against-ukraine/ukraine-support-tracker/) that go through Washington, D.C., and Brussels, and [drone assemblage factories](https://www.foxnews.com/world/a-new-kind-war-inside-ukraines-hidden-factories-mass-producing-combat-drones) traversing Russia and Ukraine. There is also a vast telecommunications network that guides and controls drone systems. Parts of this are relatively localized radar towers and ground systems. But more are reliant on communication networks like Starlink, which presently comprises a constellation of [11,000 low Earth orbit satellites](https://www.space.com/spacex-starlink-satellites.html). The capacity of remote pilots to steer mid- and long-range drones varies. [Ukraine\u2019s long-range drone attacks](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/24/ukraine-russia-war-long-range-air-war-arms-race) into Russia operate independently of Starlink. Most rely on internal terrain matching systems and automated target lock algorithms on their final approach. Russia\u2019s competitive advantage In this battle for the sky, the pace of innovations is relentless. And since the summer, Russia has been gaining a relative advantage for two reasons. The first is Russia\u2019s increasing production and use of ballistic missiles against Ukrainian cities, targeting energy systems, data centers, bridges and civilian apartment complexes. Ukraine is mostly defenseless against the speed of these missiles because it lacks Patriot anti-missile systems. U.S. satellites usually provide alerts, [but these too stopped a few days ago](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/13/allies-did-not-warn-kyiv-of-russian-ballistic-missile-strikes-says-zelenskyy). Secondly, Russia is increasing use of jet-powered Geran drones, [based on Iranian designs](https://theconversation.com/russias-drone-pipeline-how-iran-helps-moscow-produce-an-ever-evolving-unmanned-fleet-272016), which are much faster than the previous generation and, consequently, more difficult to shoot down. Russia is also rapidly building out its own low Earth orbit [system of communications called Rassvet](https://www.kyivpost.com/post/86955). A Ukrainian soldier monitors the routes flown by Russian drones on April 1, 2026. [Ivan Antypenko/Global Images Ukraine via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/soldier-from-the-electronic-intelligence-unit-of-the-505th-news-photo/2272528480?phrase=Starlink%20ukraine&adppopup=true) More Ukrainians are dying as a result of these technological shifts. The logistical systems that keep Ukrainian front-line cities, which now includes Kyiv, functioning are being pushed to the brink. As a result, the interference with Starshield and missile attack alerts came at a moment of intensified vulnerability and exposure for Ukrainians. In deference to a unilaterally declared \u201cenergy ceasefire\u201d by Trump on Oct. 11, Kyiv did not attack Russian oil-refining facilities for three days. Russia, by contrast, continued its intense attacks on Ukrainian power systems. In the face of this imbalance, Zelenskyy vowed to [restart Ukrainian attacks](https://www.ft.com/content/4d79b52f-1f0d-4739-82ab-7ecfb4251326?syn-25a6b1a6=1), come what may. The implications beyond Ukraine This latest power flex involving Starlink has wider implications, too. SpaceX\u2019s ongoing campaign to [occupy low Earth orbit with 42,000 of its satellites](https://doi-org.ezproxy.lib.vt.edu/10.1080/21622671.2025.2594491) is being facilitated by the Trump administration. But the power this concentrates in the hands of one corporation and person \u2013 SpaceX and Musk \u2013 has raised alarm bells. Major countries and corporations are rushing to develop their own proprietary communications constellations. As a consequence, low Earth orbit is getting more crowded and [geopolitically contested](https://www.csis.org/analysis/maintaining-space-edge-strategic-reforms-us-dominance-low-earth-orbit). This latest incident underscores the potentially existential stakes for some countries of their reliance on private corporations for low Earth orbit telecommunications. No state, after all, wants its defensive systems to be at the mercy of unpredictable actors. The whole world is watching \u2013 and learning. [Russia](https://theconversation.com/topics/russia-1376) [Drones](https://theconversation.com/topics/drones-2513) [Military technology](https://theconversation.com/topics/military-technology-3937) [Eastern Europe](https://theconversation.com/topics/eastern-europe-7657) [Ukraine](https://theconversation.com/topics/ukraine-8201) [Donald Trump](https://theconversation.com/topics/donald-trump-10206) [Elon Musk](https://theconversation.com/topics/elon-musk-13147) [Trump administration](https://theconversation.com/topics/trump-administration-33294) [Starlink](https://theconversation.com/topics/starlink-96246) [Ukraine invasion 2022](https://theconversation.com/topics/ukraine-invasion-2022-117045) [Gerard Toal, Virginia Tech](https://theconversation.com/starlink-slowdown-underscores-ukraines-reliance-on-the-us-in-the-battle-for-the-skies-294446) Author [Gerard Toal](https://theconversation.com/profiles/gerard-toal-507842) Professor of Government and International Affairs, Virginia Tech Disclosure statement Gerard Toal received funding for research from the US National Science Foundation in the past. He is on the advisory board of KonKoop, a project of the Center for East European and International Studies (ZOIS) in Berlin. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.pcmxwuvr3](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.pcmxwuvr3)", "content_hash": "2639f93e8294c96626a49aa64756747f7c8acdffd5dd4b07e609bf3e7758d68c", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:21Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "943", "title": "Sexual violence concepts launched online are   spilling into the real world \u2013 harming women and young men and overwhelming counselors", "url": "https://theconversation.com/sexual-violence-concepts-launched-online-are-spilling-into-the-real-world-harming-women-and-young-men-and-overwhelming-counselors-287049", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/sexual-violence-concepts-launched-online-are-spilling-into-the-real-world-harming-women-and-young-men-and-overwhelming-counselors-287049", "text": "Sexual violence concepts launched online are spilling into the real world \u2013 harming women and young men and overwhelming counselors Published: October 16, 2026 8:23am EDT The online forums and sites that make up the manosphere host a common thread: They mobilize sexual violence against women. [Denis Novikov/iStock via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/illustration/addiction-to-media-and-gadgets-concep-royalty-free-illustration/1345707700?adppopup=true) https://theconversation.com/sexual-violence-concepts-launched-online-are-spilling-into-the-real-world-harming-women-and-young-men-and-overwhelming-counselors-287049 https://theconversation.com/sexual-violence-concepts-launched-online-are-spilling-into-the-real-world-harming-women-and-young-men-and-overwhelming-counselors-287049 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Andrew Tate, a [self-described misogynist](https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-64125045) and an online influencer with millions of followers, [faces charges](https://www.cnn.com/2026/08/27/us/tate-brothers-prison-lifestyle?source=sub_web_wall-exc) in several countries of allegedly causing physical harm, including rape and sex-trafficking women and girls. Tate is a prominent figure [in the manosphere](https://www.unwomen.org/en/articles/explainer/what-is-the-manosphere-and-why-should-we-care), a loosely connected network of online forums and sites united by beliefs in male supremacy. This messaging has increasingly moved to mainstream platforms, as social media algorithms on sites like YouTube and TikTok steer young men and boys toward manosphere content sometimes [within minutes](https://www.dcu.ie/antibullyingcentre/recommending-toxicity) of opening an account. Tate\u2019s behaviors are an extreme example of manosphere ideologies spilling into the physical world, [harming women as well as the men and boys](https://www.anrows.org.au/resources/guide-to-the-manosphere/) taken in by the messaging. The growth of the manosphere has been [attributed to](https://doi.org/10.1080/14680777.2022.2029925) [backlash toward the #MeToo Movement](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11199-024-01526-6), which encouraged women to speak up about sexual violence. There is also evidence that increased isolation and time spent online during the [COVID-19 pandemic](https://theconversation.com/youd-better-start-paying-attention-to-the-manosphere-youre-living-in-it-279547) contributed to many men finding these communities. [Nearly half of U.S. women](https://www.cdc.gov/nisvs/media/pdfs/sexualviolence-brief.pdf) experience unwanted sexual contact in their lifetimes, and [1 in 5 experience attempted rape or rape](https://www.cdc.gov/nisvs/media/pdfs/sexualviolence-brief.pdf). Some countries are taking initiative to curb sexually violent content online. For instance, the U.K. in 2026 implemented several guidelines for social media companies to [reduce the spread](https://www.gov.uk/government/news/tech-companies-must-go-above-and-beyond-to-protect-women-and-girls-from-online-abuse-or-face-further-action) of misogynistic and sexually violent content [to young men and boys](https://theconversation.com/dont-blame-toxic-masculinity-for-online-misogyny-the-manosphere-is-hurting-men-too-254802). But U.S. federal and state legislation is not keeping up. As [social scientists](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=tDyjU-oAAAAJ&hl=en) in [social work](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=YNQCrTMAAAAJ&hl=en) [and psychology](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=tSrJRyIAAAAJ&hl=en), we study the ways sexual violence and masculinities intersect with digital technologies. [The growth](https://fortune.com/2026/08/15/manosphere-multibillion-dollar-industry-adin-ross-looksmaxxing-report/) and [cultural salience](https://theconversation.com/youd-better-start-paying-attention-to-the-manosphere-youre-living-in-it-279547) of manosphere communities have made them increasingly relevant to the work we do. A [violence prevention specialist](https://menengage.org/resources/10-ways-to-engage-men-and-boys-to-counter-backlash) we talked to in October 2026 said he is struggling to engage men and boys and to keep up with a changing landscape that [includes online messaging](https://theconversation.com/we-analysed-the-tiktok-history-of-142-men-heres-what-it-taught-us-about-the-manosphere-282156). For every Andrew Tate there are [many more people launching misinformation](https://www.worcester.ac.uk/about/news/academic-blog/health-and-wellbeing-blogs/under-the-influence.aspx) and hateful content about women through [topics that seem less directly tied to it](https://doi.org/10.1177/17416590251351446), like working out, playing video games or just talking. These conversations connect with peoples\u2019 day-to-day lives, which makes it easy to then slip in disparaging comments about women. Masculinity educator Ben Hurst said he\u2019s been inundated with requests to talk about the rise in online misogyny influencer culture. People who counsel sexual violence survivors are also struggling with the added burden of online influence, said Kim Foster, a police department senior victim assistance specialist we also interviewed in October. The women Foster works with are often \u201cexpecting to be belittled,\u201d she said. \u201cThat\u2019s what they\u2019re seeing online.\u201d The network Our team of experts [reviewed 105 studies](https://doi.org/10.1080/00224499.2026.2670470) on manosphere communities, including research on nine contemporary social media sites: Reddit, Twitter/X, Twitch, Facebook, TikTok, 4chan, Telegram, Instagram and YouTube. We found that they collectively hosted 89 unique manosphere communities, not counting YouTube channels. Seventy-five of those communities were on Reddit. Our studies also included data from 195 independent websites and blogs. These communities use discussions of sexual violence to [help recruit and indoctrinate](https://doi.org/10.1080/00224499.2026.2670470) new members. They are united by the idea that feminism has gone too far and women\u2019s rights should be rolled back. Male supremacists assert that men are [owed sexual access to women](https://doi.org/10.1177/0959353519857754) and frame themselves as victims when women exercise their right to [decline sex or a relationship](https://doi.org/10.1080/14680777.2025.2512233), or seek to [hold men accountable](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11199-024-01526-6) for sexual harm. This content is not confined to niche communities. A 2024 survey of 16- to 24-year-olds in the U.K. found that 95% were [familiar with Tate](https://hopenothate.org.uk/2024/07/24/plugged-in-tate-misogyny-2/), and 41% of boys and men had a favorable view of him. Finding belonging In the context of [rising rates of loneliness](https://www.apa.org/pubs/reports/stress-in-america/2025/index.html#methodology) in the U.S., a [shared badge of victimhood](https://doi.org/10.1177/13675494251371661) helps recruit and engage men into manosphere communities, where they can find [belonging and shared purpose](https://doi.org/doi:10.7282/t3-44as-5910) as they cultivate antagonism between genders. Online forum quote used as an example in a scholarly paper by Catherine Patterson Sterling. [Sterling, C. P.](https://scholarworks.waldenu.edu/dissertations/15257/) Within these communities, men who are accused of sexual violence are lauded as [heroes or martyrs](https://doi.org/10.1086/701155) for the male supremacist cause. Online forum quote used as an example in a scholarly paper by Pierce Alexander Dignam et al. [Dignam, P. A. et al](https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/701155) Discrediting survivors Men of the manosphere use what researchers call the [DARVO strategy](https://dynamic.uoregon.edu/jjf/articles/freyd97r.pdf): deny, attack and reverse victim and offender \u2013 to frame #MeToo and anti-sexual violence activism as a [feminist conspiracy targeting men](https://doi.org/10.1558/jld.25747). \u201cI\u2019d rather be raped than falsely accused,\u201d [a Men\u2019s Rights forum user](https://commons.lib.jmu.edu/diss202029/39/) posted in 2018, showing one way that social media users in the manosphere [argue that men, not women](https://doi.org/10.5204/ijcjsd.v5i2.310), are the true victims of sexual violence. This positions women as a [shared enemy](https://doi.org/10.1177/1097184X17706401) for men to blame for [loneliness, frustration or anger](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11245-023-09921-6). Manosphere members claim that false rape allegations [happen all the time](https://doi.org/10.1177/09593535211033461) and point to [skewed statistics](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11199-024-01526-6) and cherry-picked stories. In reality, research consistently reports that a small minority \u2013 [roughly 5%](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-015-0666-2) \u2013 of sexual assault allegations investigated by police are proven to be false. Manosphere communities also judge what forms of sexual violence are worthy of taking seriously. Online forum quote used as an example in a scholarly paper by Yi Ting Chua et al. [Chua, Y. T. et al.](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10610-023-09555-9) A common trope: [\u201creal victims\u201d stay silent](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11199-024-01526-6). Online forum quote used as an example in a scholarly paper by Heather Hensman Kettrey et al. [Kettrey, H. H. et al](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11199-024-01526-6) Foster sees this affecting her work: \u201cI think we had really hoped that after the #MeToo Movement, people would be more confident coming forward, but I don\u2019t feel like victims expect to be believed and taken seriously,\u201d she told us. The #MeToo Movement encouraged women to speak up about sexual violence, but was met with an online backlash that has silenced some women. [Gareth Fuller/PA Images via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/rape-victim-is-interviewed-by-a-police-officer-in-an-news-photo/832726278) Defending their worldview offline Manosphere communities contain [many references to violence and rape](https://doi.org/10.17615/w7sa-qs12). Participants often call these protected speech or [innocent jokes](https://doi.org/10.1080/01639625.2022.2142864). But for some men, [they are a bridge](https://doi.org/10.1080/00224499.2026.2670470) [to further harm toward women](https://doi.org/10.1177/00104140241312095). Pick-up artist and seduction communities [teach members](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dcm.2020.100402) that \u201cno\u201d rarely actually means \u201cno.\u201d They teach that women deny sex as a game or to avoid being seen as a slut, a concept which they call \u201clast minute resistance.\u201d Users share detailed stories of sexual encounters \u2013 referred to as [field reports](https://ijoc.org/index.php/ijoc/article/view/20283) \u2013 to give advice or ask about overcoming last-minute resistance. Our research found that these stories often represent [clear disclosures](https://doi.org/10.1080/00224499.2026.2670470) of men sexually assaulting women. Online forum quote used as an example in a scholarly paper by David Wright. [Wright, D.](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dcm.2020.100402) The manosphere socializes boys and men into a worldview where entitlement, victimhood and male supremacy are normalized \u2013 making it more difficult for them to achieve [healthy, consent-based, gender-equitable relationships](https://doi.org/10.1080/09540253.2024.2315052). Online forum quote used as an example in a scholarly paper by Albina Laskovtsov. [Laskovtsov, A.](https://encompass.eku.edu/etd/662/) This isn\u2019t happening organically As social media algorithms [push more manosphere content](https://www.isdglobal.org/publication/algorithms-as-a-weapon-against-women-how-youtube-lures-boys-and-young-men-into-the-manosphere/), these types of beliefs surface in the real world. In a case involving a male teacher preying on a female student, for instance, the investigation found notes on tactics from a pick-up artist forum in the teacher\u2019s possession, Foster said. Australian researchers found in 2026 that [40% of teenage boys say they think women lie](https://theconversation.com/40-of-teenage-boys-believe-women-lie-about-domestic-and-sexual-violence-new-research-276978) about domestic violence or sexual assault. The [U.K.\u2019s Online Safety Act](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/online-safety-act-explainer/online-safety-act-explainer) calls algorithms that promote or normalize hatred and sexual violence against women an [online safety problem](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/online-safety/illegal-and-harmful-content/ofcom-tech-firms-must-up-their-game-to-tackle-online-harms-against-women-and-girls). The act advises tech companies to [remove creator payments](https://digiday.com/marketing/how-every-major-platform-pays-creators/) for misogynistic posts and diversify algorithmic content types to lessen the impact of [digital echo chambers](https://www.esafety.gov.au/young-people/echo-chambers-and-your-feed). But U.S. federal policy on social media does not call out the algorithmic spread of misogynistic content as a concern. The proposed [Kids Online Safety Act](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/1748/text) calls for online platforms to prevent and mitigate harmful material, such as physical violence or sexual exploitation of children, but [does not mention women](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/1748/text#id44feef84-d084-43a7-8595-cbdedf4d2e0e). State legislation proposed in California in 2026 would have fined large social media companies for algorithms spreading hate speech or violent content, [including promotion of violence against women](https://legiscan.com/CA/text/SB771/2025). But the bill was [vetoed by Gov. Gavin Newsom](https://www.politico.com/news/2025/10/13/newsom-siding-with-tech-giants-vetoes-online-hate-speech-bill-00606600). New York\u2019s [Stop Hiding Hate Act](https://ag.ny.gov/press-release/2025/attorney-general-james-announces-social-media-companies-must-start-reporting), enacted in 2025, requires large social media companies to report their content moderation policies and how they respond to hate speech. But [the law](https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2023/S895/amendment/B) doesn\u2019t address algorithm transparency or gender-related hate speech. Worsening trends Negative societal beliefs about sexual assault victims have long contributed to low rates of reporting, prosecution and jail time for perpetrators. About [1 in 3 survivors](https://rainn.org/facts-statistics-the-scope-of-the-problem/statistics-the-criminal-justice-system/) [report sexual assault](https://bjs.ojp.gov/library/publications/criminal-victimization-2024) to law enforcement. Meanwhile, a low percentage \u2013 [just a few percent](https://www.nbcnews.com/specials/sex-assault-convictions/) \u2013 of perpetrators [serve any prison time](https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2018/10/06/less-than-percent-rapes-lead-felony-convictions-least-percent-victims-face-emotional-physical-consequences/). Based on our research, we believe that manosphere messaging about sexual violence worsens these trends and allows those who wish to cause harm to act without feeling the threat of punishment. Until changes are made to the algorithms that spread this type of content on social media, these problems are likely to continue to grow. \u201cI don\u2019t know how to prevent this from happening without widespread systemic change,\u201d Foster told us. [Social media](https://theconversation.com/topics/social-media-109) [Loneliness](https://theconversation.com/topics/loneliness-2663) [Misogyny](https://theconversation.com/topics/misogyny-4045) [Masculinity](https://theconversation.com/topics/masculinity-4559) [Sexual assault](https://theconversation.com/topics/sexual-assault-4845) [Sexual violence](https://theconversation.com/topics/sexual-violence-9876) [#MeToo](https://theconversation.com/topics/metoo-45316) [Manosphere](https://theconversation.com/topics/manosphere-54638) [Social media harm](https://theconversation.com/topics/social-media-harm-159729) [Morgan E. PettyJohn, University of Texas at Arlington, Jacob Nason, Michigan State University, Kyla Cary, Illinois State](https://theconversation.com/sexual-violence-concepts-launched-online-are-spilling-into-the-real-world-harming-women-and-young-men-and-overwhelming-counselors-287049) Authors [Morgan E. PettyJohn](https://theconversation.com/profiles/morgan-e-pettyjohn-1526310) Assistant Professor of Social Work, University of Texas at Arlington [Jacob Nason](https://theconversation.com/profiles/jacob-nason-2727869) PhD Candidate in Social Work, Michigan State University [Kyla Cary](https://theconversation.com/profiles/kyla-cary-2727870) Assistant Professor of Psychology, Illinois State University Disclosure statement Morgan E. PettyJohn receives funding from the Kalman and Ida Wolens Foundation. Jacob Nason is currently volunteering on the campaigns of Michigan Democrats William Lawrence for the U.S. House (MI-07) and Abdul El-Sayed for the U.S. Senate. Kyla Cary does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Michigan State University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/michigan-state-university-1349) provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation US.[University of Texas Arlington](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-texas-at-arlington-718) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.pah6qp9nd](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.pah6qp9nd)", "content_hash": "3087557e1d7251523cd7c4662490afae6656ad6842926758b5e2d6ba04a388b3", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:23Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "944", "title": "Pennsylvania\u2019s aging prisons weren\u2019t built for today\u2019s extreme weather \u2013 leaving women and seniors especially at risk of illness and\u00a0death", "url": "https://theconversation.com/pennsylvanias-aging-prisons-werent-built-for-todays-extreme-weather-leaving-women-and-seniors-especially-at-risk-of-illness-and-death-293079", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/pennsylvanias-aging-prisons-werent-built-for-todays-extreme-weather-leaving-women-and-seniors-especially-at-risk-of-illness-and-death-293079", "text": "People in prison can\u2019t just switch on the air conditioning when it starts to swelter. [Mark Makela/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/exterior-view-of-chester-county-prison-from-which-convicted-news-photo/1657117984) Pennsylvania\u2019s aging prisons weren\u2019t built for today\u2019s extreme weather \u2013 leaving women and seniors especially at risk of illness and death Published: October 16, 2026 8:23am EDT https://theconversation.com/pennsylvanias-aging-prisons-werent-built-for-todays-extreme-weather-leaving-women-and-seniors-especially-at-risk-of-illness-and-death-293079 https://theconversation.com/pennsylvanias-aging-prisons-werent-built-for-todays-extreme-weather-leaving-women-and-seniors-especially-at-risk-of-illness-and-death-293079 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article When outdoor temperatures spike to 103 degrees Fahrenheit (39 degrees Celsius), or drop to -9 F (-23 C), like they did across Pennsylvania last year, people locked up in jails and prisons cannot simply turn on the air conditioning or find a public warming center. For several years, people in custody, advocates and local media [have sounded the alarm](https://www.spotlightpa.org/news/2024/07/pennsylvania-prisons-air-conditioning-heatwave) about the dangers posed by extreme temperatures in Pennsylvania\u2019s state prisons. Conditions inside Pennsylvania\u2019s prisons have been described [as a sauna](https://www.spotlightpa.org/news/2024/07/pennsylvania-prisons-air-conditioning-heatwave) during a summer heat wave and bitterly cold in the winter, especially when heating systems have malfunctioned. Amy McBride, a woman in her early 60s incarcerated at MCI Muncy, [told the Prison Journalism Project](https://prisonjournalismproject.org/2025/02/25/extreme-cold-prison/), \u201cI tend to wear two to three layers of pants and four to six layers of shirts (in winter), sometimes with my jacket and coat over them.\u201d I am an epidemiologist who [studies the health of incarcerated people](https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=rhfnW0YAAAAJ&view_op=list_works&sortby=pubdate). My colleagues and I put numbers to these warnings in a recent study. We found that extreme hot and cold temperatures were associated with [higher rates of deaths, infirmary admissions and transfers to external medical facilities](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-78012-y) among imprisoned people in Pennsylvania over the past 20 years. While these temperature hazards are not unique to Pennsylvania, several factors make this issue a particular concern for the state. Pennsylvania has the nation\u2019s [sixth-largest prison population](https://bjs.ojp.gov/library/publications/prisoners-2023-statistical-tables) among U.S. states. Nearly 40,000 people are confined in Pennsylvania prisons. Furthermore, most of Pennsylvania\u2019s state prison facilities were built in the 1990s or earlier, when weather patterns were not so [extreme](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/dep/residents/climate-change/impacts). Some, including the state correctional institutions in [Cambridge Springs](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/cor/state-prisons/sci-cambridge-springs), [Dallas](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/cor/state-prisons/sci-dallas), [Laurel Highlands](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/cor/state-prisons/sci-laurel-highlands), [Muncy](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/cor/state-prisons/sci-muncy), [Waymart](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/cor/state-prisons/sci-waymart) and [Huntingdon](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/cor/state-prisons/sci-huntingdon), the oldest continuously running correctional facility in Pennsylvania, have partial or very limited air conditioning. This means they have air conditioning in some buildings or units, such as nursing care areas, and not others. Previous research also suggests that while hot temperatures [pose risks to incarcerated people across the country](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0281389), those incarcerated in the Northeast have experienced some of the greatest increases in heat-related mortality risk since 2001. Prison healthcare systems under strain Even under normal weather conditions, prison healthcare systems are highly strained. People in prison are more likely than the general population to have [chronic medical conditions such as heart disease](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2017.04.040), and the state\u2019s prison population [is getting older](https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/TR/Transcripts/2024_0522_0002_TSTMNY.pdf), which increases its medical needs and expenses. The average age at prison admission has increased from 31 in 2000 to [38 in 2023](https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/TR/Transcripts/2024_0522_0002_TSTMNY.pdf). The average cost for medications alone for adults over 50 in the state\u2019s prison is [US$3,357 per person per year](https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/TR/Transcripts/2024_0522_0002_TSTMNY.pdf). That\u2019s [53% more](https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/TR/Transcripts/2024_0522_0002_TSTMNY.pdf) than costs for younger incarcerated adults. The state prison system experiences [chronic staffing shortages](https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/TR/Transcripts/2024_0522_0002_TSTMNY.pdf) of corrections officers, nurses and physicians. Some facilities have experienced vacancy rates of medical staff of nearly 40%. Shortages of corrections officers result in greater restrictions on the movement of incarcerated people, who then have less access to health appointments, group therapy and other programming. This can be especially difficult for [older incarcerated people and those who have been incarcerated for long periods of time](https://prisonjournalismproject.org/2026/03/17/prison-staffing-problems-are-especially-hard-on-lifers/), because they have greater medical needs and are often more isolated from loved ones. Constitutional protections against cruel and unusual punishment [prohibit deliberate indifference to prisoners\u2019 medical needs](https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/USREPORTS-429/USREPORTS-429-97). But in addition to staffing shortages and other administrative challenges with healthcare delivery in prisons, incarcerated people also face financial barriers. In Pennsylvania, they must pay a [$5 copay](https://www.pa.gov/content/dam/copapwp-pagov/en/cor/documents/about-us/doc-policies/820%20Co-Payment%20for%20Medical%20Services.pdf) per visit to receive medical treatment that is determined by the Department of Correction to not be an emergency. This may sound low but is substantial in the context of wages that range from [23 cents to 50 cents per hour](https://www.prisonsociety.org/updates/doc-raises-wages-for-incarcerated-people). Incarcerated women may face higher heat risks One unexpected finding from our analysis was that women in Pennsylvania state prisons experienced greater increases in rates of infirmary visits and transfers to external medical facilities on and after extremely hot days than men. This was unexpected because [previous studies](https://www.doi.org/10.1097/EDE.0000000000000375) haven\u2019t consistently shown that women are more vulnerable to heat-related health risks. But these studies were conducted in the general population, not among people in prison. Incarcerated people experience what medical researchers call \u201c[accelerated aging](https://www.doi.org/10.1177/00221465211052568)\u201d because of the stressors they experience during and prior to incarceration. This means their biologic age is ahead of their chronological age. They are more likely to experience menopause, cancer and chronic disease [at a younger age](https://whyy.org/articles/in-prison-women-develop-diseases-earlier-and-often/) than would typically be expected. Hot flashes and night sweats are common symptoms of menopause that can become [life threatening](https://www.doi.org/10.1177/10783458261449636) during extreme heat events and when cooling, hydration and hormone therapy are limited or unavailable. Extreme heat can turn common symptoms of menopause into health hazards for women in prison. [Mindful Media/E+ Collection via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/close-up-shot-of-healthcare-workers-gloved-hands-royalty-free-image/1397838530) Preparing for a warmer climate Our research found that extreme heat and extreme cold increase the demands on an already strained system of prison operations and healthcare delivery. These demands will likely only get worse as extreme temperatures continue to intensify. We believe climate resilience planning will require improvements in prison conditions, improvements in prison healthcare systems, and policy efforts to reduce the number of people \u2013 particularly older adults \u2013 who are incarcerated. Read more of our stories about [Philadelphia and Pennsylvania](https://theconversation.com/us/philadelphia-pennsylvania-news), or sign up for our Philadelphia [newsletter on Substack](https://tcphilly.substack.com/). [Climate change](https://theconversation.com/topics/climate-change-27) [Weather](https://theconversation.com/topics/weather-357) [Menopause](https://theconversation.com/topics/menopause-563) [Prisons](https://theconversation.com/topics/prisons-889) [Incarceration](https://theconversation.com/topics/incarceration-1639) [Health care](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-care-2204) [Aging](https://theconversation.com/topics/aging-2802) [Philadelphia](https://theconversation.com/topics/philadelphia-13777) [Pennsylvania](https://theconversation.com/topics/pennsylvania-18738) [Pittsburgh](https://theconversation.com/topics/pittsburgh-39377) [Extreme heat](https://theconversation.com/topics/extreme-heat-41752) [Jaquelyn Jahn, Drexel University](https://theconversation.com/pennsylvanias-aging-prisons-werent-built-for-todays-extreme-weather-leaving-women-and-seniors-especially-at-risk-of-illness-and-death-293079) Author [Jaquelyn Jahn](https://theconversation.com/profiles/jaquelyn-jahn-2768054) Assistant Professor of Epidemiology, Drexel University Disclosure statement Jaquelyn Jahn receives funding from the National Institutes of Health, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Arnold Ventures, and the Robert Wood Johnson Foundation. Partners [Drexel University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/drexel-university-1074) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.yyxep5vs3](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.yyxep5vs3)", "content_hash": "db7455eaebe2a73016bca94aeeae563ae14d6d755253d6af95067d180e50d426", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:24Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "945", "title": "Fentanyl is fueling overdose deaths in Colorado \u2013 what voters need to know about Proposition 132\u2019s attempt to stiffen\u00a0penalties", "url": "https://theconversation.com/fentanyl-is-fueling-overdose-deaths-in-colorado-what-voters-need-to-know-about-proposition-132s-attempt-to-stiffen-penalties-292870", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/fentanyl-is-fueling-overdose-deaths-in-colorado-what-voters-need-to-know-about-proposition-132s-attempt-to-stiffen-penalties-292870", "text": "Fentanyl-related overdose deaths are a concern in Colorado. [KatarzynaBialasiewicz/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/drug-grams-and-tabs-royalty-free-image/482857483?phrase=group%20of%20people%20fentanyl&searchscope=image%2Cfilm) Fentanyl is fueling overdose deaths in Colorado \u2013 what voters need to know about Proposition 132\u2019s attempt to stiffen penalties Published: October 16, 2026 8:23am EDT https://theconversation.com/fentanyl-is-fueling-overdose-deaths-in-colorado-what-voters-need-to-know-about-proposition-132s-attempt-to-stiffen-penalties-292870 https://theconversation.com/fentanyl-is-fueling-overdose-deaths-in-colorado-what-voters-need-to-know-about-proposition-132s-attempt-to-stiffen-penalties-292870 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Colorado continues to face an increase in overdose deaths while other states have seen a downturn. Illicitly manufactured fentanyl, a potent opioid, emerged in the Colorado drug market around 2018. That was nearly five years after it began tormenting people who use street drugs in [Northeastern states](http://dx.doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm6614a2). By 2019, fentanyl replaced other drugs as the [driving force behind opioid overdoses in Colorado](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nvss/vsrr/drug-overdose-data.htm), and it has [held its grip](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nvss/vsrr/drug-overdose-data.htm) ever since. But while [overdose deaths in the U.S.](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nvss/vsrr/drug-overdose-data.htm) are currently experiencing a historic decline of almost 14%, Colorado is [one of eight states where rates are not dropping](https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/5878575-drug-overdose-deaths-declined-last-year-except-in-these-8-states/). In fact, Colorado has had an increase of [nearly 12% in overdose deaths](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nvss/vsrr/drug-overdose-data.htm) since April 2025. Overdoses are top of mind for many everyday Coloradans, healthcare professionals, elected officials and [public health experts like me](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=WaGmzxoAAAAJ&hl=en). [Proposition 132](https://ballotpedia.org/Colorado_Proposition_132,_Penalties_for_Fentanyl_Sale_and_Possession_Initiative_(2026)) attempts to address these concerns by stiffening legal penalties. A conservative policy organization called Advance Colorado collected the signatures to get the initiative on the November ballot. The proposed change to Colorado law would make possession of any amount of fentanyl a felony, require a mandatory minimum eight-year prison sentence for distribution of any quantity of fentanyl, and eliminate some existing opportunities for reduced penalties. Before Coloradans head to the polls, it is worth examining the state\u2019s overdose trends and considering whether Proposition 132 is likely to solve the problem. A 2022 law already increased penalties Let\u2019s start by looking at Colorado\u2019s past attempts to stop drug overdoses through legislation. In recent years, the state has pursued some public health interventions but has leaned heavily on increasing criminal penalties for using and possessing fentanyl. The 2022 [Fentanyl Accountability and Prevention Act (HB22-1326)](https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/hb22-1326), for instance, represented a major expansion of criminal penalties. This law rolled back a [2019 law](https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/hb19-1263) that had decriminalized small amounts of drugs. Specifically, HB22-1326 made the knowing possession of more than 1 gram and up to 4 grams of a [substance containing fentanyl a Level 4 drug felony](https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/hb22-1326) punishable by up to 180 days in jail and two years of probation. Unlike Proposition 132, the 2022 fentanyl act directed funding for the distribution of naloxone, an overdose reversal medication, and fentanyl test strips that help people identify fentanyl in their drugs before using them. Both [naloxone](https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.f174) and [fentanyl test strips](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.10077) decrease the risk of fatal overdose. The law did not direct funding toward evidence-based treatments such as methadone or buprenorphine for [opioid use disorder](https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/medications-opioid-use-disorder#buprenorphine). [Supporters argued](https://patch.com/colorado/across-co/fentanyl-accountability-prevention-bill-moves-governors-desk) that the legislation was necessary to prevent overdose deaths and get people treatment. I led a research team that included addiction treatment providers and substance use\u2013focused epidemiologists to study the effects of HB22-1326. Although more people were arrested and faced the tougher penalties, we found [no increase in people beginning or staying on buprenorphine](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.drugpo.2025.105037). We also found that fewer people were starting and staying on methadone following the implementation of the law. Importantly, we found that the law did not change [the trajectory of overdose deaths](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2025.2654) compared to what was expected. Taken together, we found no substantial evidence that increasing criminal penalties, even with some increased funding for naloxone and overdose prevention, \u201csaved lives\u201d as proponents had hoped. In addition to increased criminalization, Colorado has invested in prevention programs and treatment support for users of illicit drugs. [KatarzynaBialasiewicz/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/top-view-on-difficult-youth-talking-with-therapist-royalty-free-image/1007209056) Colorado is already [uniquely punitive toward people who use illicit drugs](https://www.networkforphl.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/State-Fentanyl-Penalty-Enhancements_A-Comprehensive-Legal-Review-2.pdf), with some of the harshest penalties for drug possession and distribution in the U.S. Colorado\u2019s public health response has developed alongside, not in place of, a substantial criminal-legal response. What decreases overdose deaths? Even with [efforts to provide additional access to harm-reduction protections](https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/HB24-1037), escalating criminal penalties have not produced the treatment gains or decreases in overdose deaths in Colorado that proponents promised. So, let\u2019s look at a place in the state where overdose deaths are actually falling to see what is being done differently there. Larimer County, home of Colorado State University and part of Rocky Mountain National Park, offers the clearest contrast to the rest of the state. Accidental overdose deaths [have declined year after year since 2022](https://cohealthviz.dphe.state.co.us/t/HealthInformaticsPublic/views/Drug_Overdose_Dashboard_All_Years_No_Race_FINAL/Story1), down nearly 42% from 2021. What\u2019s going on in Larimer County? For one, the Larimer County Jail is [one of less than half](https://doi.org/10.1186/s40352-026-00434-z) of the Colorado county jails that offers [a comprehensive treatment program](https://cdn.theconversation.com/static_files/files/4355/JUST-NOCO-NCHA-Annual-Report-2024_FINAL_%281%29.pdf?1791917542) that includes medications for opioid use disorder. The sheriff\u2019s office, in collaboration with several community-based organizations, has also flooded the community with naloxone through the [Reversal Project](https://www.larimer.gov/spotlights/2024/01/29/grant-fuels-battle-against-opioid-crisis-repeat-offenses). Furthermore, [using opioid abatement funds](https://www.larimer.gov/bocc/regional-opioid-abatement-council#docaccess-d2eae7271383f0388efc8934636fbb8d), the county has expanded youth and family support services, co-responder units and other harm-reduction services. The county has also steadily [increased the number of people on buprenorphine](https://cohealthviz.dphe.state.co.us/t/PSDVIP-MHPPUBLIC/views/BuprenorphineDashboard/BupeNaive?%3Aembed=y&%3AisGuestRedirectFromVizportal=y&%3Aorigin=card_share_link). Naloxone can quickly reverse an opioid overdose. [Marvin Samuel Tolentino Pineda/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/naloxone-kit-with-a-couple-of-vials-at-pharmacy-royalty-free-image/1361636220) No single intervention can be credited with Larimer County\u2019s decline in accidental overdoes deaths. Criminal enforcement remains one component of the county\u2019s response. Yet, what stands out is its emphasis on evidence-based interventions with an emphasis on expanding treatment access and harm-reduction services that save lives and give people the stability and opportunity to build healthier lives. A polysubstance problem In addition, Proposition 132 has a major flaw. It targets one drug: fentanyl. But the [available data suggests](https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/data-research/facts-stats/sudors-dashboard-fatal-overdose-data.html) most people take fentanyl together with stimulants such as methamphetamine. Both drugs can be fatal. Between 2020 and 2024, nearly 40% of [overdose deaths in Colorado](https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/data-research/facts-stats/sudors-dashboard-fatal-overdose-data.html) involved both opioids and stimulants. National figures follow a similar pattern. Nationally, 43% of overdose deaths in 2024 [involved both opioids and stimulants](https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/data-research/facts-stats/sudors-dashboard-fatal-overdose-data.html). Targeting one substance, as Proposition 132 does, ignores the fact that the state has a true \u201cpolysubstance\u201d epidemic and will end up making people who unknowingly possess fentanyl susceptible to harsher penalties set forth by Proposition 132. Since Colorado increased fentanyl penalties in 2022, the promised treatment gains examined in our research have not materialized. The state\u2019s number of overdoses has continued to climb. Those facts do not mean that criminal penalties alone caused Colorado\u2019s worsening trend. But they do raise an important question as voters consider Proposition 132: What evidence suggests that increasing those penalties further will address the rise in overdose deaths? [Colorado](https://theconversation.com/topics/colorado-638) [Methamphetamines](https://theconversation.com/topics/methamphetamines-1070) [Overdose](https://theconversation.com/topics/overdose-1942) [Methadone](https://theconversation.com/topics/methadone-3236) [Overdose deaths](https://theconversation.com/topics/overdose-deaths-7973) [Fentanyl](https://theconversation.com/topics/fentanyl-28070) [Buprenorphine](https://theconversation.com/topics/buprenorphine-29079) [Colorado ballot](https://theconversation.com/topics/colorado-ballot-151622) [Joshua Barocas, University of Colorado Anschutz](https://theconversation.com/fentanyl-is-fueling-overdose-deaths-in-colorado-what-voters-need-to-know-about-proposition-132s-attempt-to-stiffen-penalties-292870) Author [Joshua Barocas](https://theconversation.com/profiles/joshua-barocas-2330479) Professor of Medicine, University of Colorado Anschutz Disclosure statement Joshua Barocas receives funding from the National Institutes on Drug Abuse and has previously received funding from the Colorado Department of Human Services. Partners [University of Colorado Anschutz](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-colorado-anschutz-4838) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.v3v976n7m](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.v3v976n7m)", "content_hash": "74f2a06a1c06a83619d5220e6d61f4d4c635316c70e004a7e9640398cd8afeaf", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:25Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "946", "title": "Treating depression during pregnancy may benefit baby\u2019s brain development \u2013 new\u00a0research", "url": "https://theconversation.com/treating-depression-during-pregnancy-may-benefit-babys-brain-development-new-research-291260", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/treating-depression-during-pregnancy-may-benefit-babys-brain-development-new-research-291260", "text": "Treating depression during pregnancy may benefit baby\u2019s brain development \u2013 new research Published: October 16, 2026 8:23am EDT Depression affects 1 in 5 women during pregnancy. [SDI Productions/E+ via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/pregnant-woman-receiving-emotional-support-during-royalty-free-image/2229743179) https://theconversation.com/treating-depression-during-pregnancy-may-benefit-babys-brain-development-new-research-291260 https://theconversation.com/treating-depression-during-pregnancy-may-benefit-babys-brain-development-new-research-291260 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Treating pregnant women\u2019s depression with a short course of talk therapy doesn\u2019t just improve their health \u2013 it can also shape [how their babies\u2019 brains develop before birth](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-78276-4). That\u2019s the finding of a new study we co-authored, published in the journal Nature Communications. The study is part of [an ongoing research project](https://careprojectdenver.org/) that explores how a form of brief interpersonal psychotherapy called MOMCare, which is specially tailored to [treating prenatal depression](https://doi.org/10.1002/da.22405), affects pregnant women with depression and their babies. MOMCare consists of eight 50-minute sessions focused on building interpersonal skills, reducing conflict and strengthening social support. We enrolled 234 pregnant women who reported having symptoms of depression during their routine prenatal care appointments. Mothers joined the research project during the first or second trimester and were randomly assigned to one of two groups. Women in one group received MOMCare. Women in the other group received education about mental health, strategies for managing depression symptoms and referrals to mental health support services, such as therapy, in the community. Both groups continued getting their usual medical care, which could also include referrals to therapy or medication. In a previous publication, we found that 36% of moms enrolled in our study were diagnosed with depression in pregnancy before getting MOMCare and [just 6% after it](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2023.0702), showing the intervention was effective. Women who received the MOMCare intervention were also more likely to [carry their baby to term](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eclinm.2024.102601), which is linked with shorter hospital stays after birth and improvements in many aspects of child health and development, including [better school performance](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2020.2027). In the current study, to investigate how this form of talk therapy affects a baby\u2019s developing brain, we measured the volume of different brain areas using an imaging technique called [magnetic resonance imaging](https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diagnostics/4876-magnetic-resonance-imaging-mri), or MRI, within a few weeks of birth. We found that a brain area called the [hippocampus](https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/hippocampus), which plays a role in learning, memory and [emotional regulation](https://theconversation.com/what-is-emotional-regulation-a-child-psychologist-explains-280443), was 5% smaller by volume in infants whose mothers received MOMCare compared to mothers in the other group. Brain imaging showed a difference in the brains of newborns whose moms got talk therapy compared to those whose moms who did not. [travelism/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/asian-baby-boy-develops-fine-motor-skills-playing-royalty-free-image/2232215754) Six months later, we brought the babies back into the lab to examine their emotional responses to unfamiliar experiences, such as encountering a noisy toy robot that danced and moved unpredictably. According to earlier studies, babies who respond more negatively to these tasks have higher levels of [depression and anxiety](https://doi.org/10.1037/abn0000769) in adolescence. In our study, we found that babies with a smaller hippocampal volume at birth tended to respond less negatively than those with larger volume. It may seem counterintuitive that a smaller brain volume is associated with stronger emotional regulation, particularly since in adults smaller brain volume is [often associated with negative effects](https://doi.org/10.1038/nn958). However, in infancy the [brain is growing](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dcn.2024.101392), and faster growth may not always be advantageous. For example, one hypothesis proposes that [stress can accelerate early brain development](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cobeha.2015.11.018). Why it matters Depression affects up to [1 in 5 women during pregnancy](https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2021.00615). It also raises the risk of [pregnancy complications](https://doi.org/10.1001/archgenpsychiatry.2010.111) such as high blood pressure and preterm birth. And having depression during pregnancy raises the risk of [mental illness in the child](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lanepe.2026.101595). Pregnancy offers a unique opportunity to simultaneously improve the health of both mother and baby. This is because women often have more contact with the healthcare system during pregnancy than at other times in their lives. Treating a woman\u2019s depression during her pregnancy benefits [two generations](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0954579418000470), improving the health of the mother and her developing baby. Even though both MOMCare and the control condition in our study provided more support than pregnant women experiencing symptoms of depression often receive, we still saw a big difference between the groups. This suggests that a targeted intervention, even a noninvasive and relatively low-cost one such as MOMCare, can make a big difference. Our study is the first to show that treating a woman\u2019s depression during pregnancy may influence infant brain development. It aligns with a small but growing number of randomized clinical trials showing that reducing maternal depression has benefits for babies, including improved [emotional development](https://doi.org/10.3368/jhr.1222-12693R3) and changes in [stress-related hormones](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssmmh.2022.100082). These findings, if confirmed, could explain the biological underpinnings behind why treating prenatal depression is so critical. Further, they support public health initiatives to increase [accessible mental health support](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cct.2025.107988) during pregnancy. What\u2019s still unknown It\u2019s not clear how lessening mom\u2019s depression in pregnancy leads to changes in the baby\u2019s brain. It\u2019s possible, for example, that [stress-related hormones](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.psyneuen.2016.10.005) or [inflammation associated with depression](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0033291719001909) could influence brain growth. Another open question is how the brain changes we observed might affect babies\u2019 development long term. We hope to address this by continuing to follow mothers and babies through early childhood. By tracking conditions such as depression and anxiety as the children get older, we hope to determine whether treating mothers\u2019 depression in pregnancy has lasting benefits for their children\u2019s mental health and well-being. The [Research Brief](https://theconversation.com/us/topics/research-brief-83231) is a short take on interesting academic work. [Child development](https://theconversation.com/topics/child-development-305) [Pregnancy](https://theconversation.com/topics/pregnancy-624) [Depression](https://theconversation.com/topics/depression-669) [Maternal health](https://theconversation.com/topics/maternal-health-1545) [Child health](https://theconversation.com/topics/child-health-5935) [Brain development](https://theconversation.com/topics/brain-development-6528) [Quick reads](https://theconversation.com/topics/quick-reads-37963) [New research](https://theconversation.com/topics/new-research-53456) [Research Brief](https://theconversation.com/topics/research-brief-83231) [perinatal depression](https://theconversation.com/topics/perinatal-depression-124064) [Elysia Poggi Davis, University of Denver, Benjamin L. Hankin, University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign, Catherine Demers,](https://theconversation.com/treating-depression-during-pregnancy-may-benefit-babys-brain-development-new-research-291260) Authors [Elysia Poggi Davis](https://theconversation.com/profiles/elysia-poggi-davis-2760759) Professor of Psychology, University of Denver [Benjamin L. Hankin](https://theconversation.com/profiles/benjamin-l-hankin-2768542) Professor of Psychology, University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign [Catherine Demers](https://theconversation.com/profiles/catherine-demers-2768546) Assistant Professor of Psychiatry, University of Colorado Anschutz Disclosure statement Elysia Davis receives funding from the National Institutes of Health Benjamin L. Hankin receives funding from the National Institutes of Health. Catherine Demers receives funding from the National Institutes of Health Partners [University of Denver](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-denver-812), [University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-illinois-urbana-champaign-1266), [University of Colorado Anschutz](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-colorado-anschutz-4838), and [University of Illinois System](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-illinois-system-6797) provide funding as members of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.da4aekg3u](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.da4aekg3u)", "content_hash": "6dfdce367ede33a8f66f97d83310fcb25e55361b2dd20b7e56180d0bf013c323", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:26Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "947", "title": "The Conversation U.S. weekly news\u00a0quiz", "url": "https://theconversation.com/the-conversation-u-s-weekly-news-quiz-189437", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/the-conversation-u-s-weekly-news-quiz-189437", "text": "The Conversation U.S. weekly news quiz Published: August 25, 2022 3:17pm EDT \u2022Updated: July 17, 2026 11:08am EDT https://theconversation.com/the-conversation-u-s-weekly-news-quiz-189437 https://theconversation.com/the-conversation-u-s-weekly-news-quiz-189437 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article We hope you\u2019ve enjoyed this quiz from our quizmaster, Fritz Holznagel, a past winner of the Jeopardy! Tournament of Champions. If you have feedback on the quiz, please send us an email at us.quiz@theconversation.com. [The Conversation US](https://theconversation.com/topics/the-conversation-us-24225) [Quiz](https://theconversation.com/topics/quiz-37137) [Fritz Holznagel, The Conversation](https://theconversation.com/the-conversation-u-s-weekly-news-quiz-189437) Author [Fritz Holznagel](https://theconversation.com/profiles/fritz-holznagel-327164) Quizmaster, The Conversation Disclosure statement Fritz Holznagel is a professional writer in Somerville, Mass. and fun fact fanatic. He is the 1995 winner of the Jeopardy! Tournament of Champions and co-founder of the biographical database Who2. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.4g6g3n9hq](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.4g6g3n9hq)", "content_hash": "c999f408fc738a278eee3dc612d219dbbdaee5493be81f1f56c5fdc919446bf8", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:27Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "948", "title": "Belonging and belief: Why evangelical churches draw many migrants in the\u00a0US", "url": "https://theconversation.com/belonging-and-belief-why-evangelical-churches-draw-many-migrants-in-the-us-286504", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/belonging-and-belief-why-evangelical-churches-draw-many-migrants-in-the-us-286504", "text": "People pray during a service in a storefront church in the Little Havana neighborhood of Miami, Fla. [Joe Raedle/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/louis-enrique-originally-from-nicaragua-prays-during-a-news-photo/76043617?phrase=evangelical%20church%20immigrant%20u.s.&adppopup=true) Belonging and belief: Why evangelical churches draw many migrants in the US Published: October 16, 2026 8:24am EDT https://theconversation.com/belonging-and-belief-why-evangelical-churches-draw-many-migrants-in-the-us-286504 https://theconversation.com/belonging-and-belief-why-evangelical-churches-draw-many-migrants-in-the-us-286504 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article In October 2024, right before the presidential election, I had coffee with Kim, a Congolese asylum-seeker living in southern Maine. Our conversation quickly turned to politics, and I cautiously asked about her views, although she could not vote. During his campaign, Donald Trump [had vowed mass deportations](https://www.npr.org/2024/10/30/nx-s1-5170339/trumps-mass-deportation-plan-of-undocumented-migrants-offers-few-specifics), and I assumed immigration policies would be among her top priorities. But instead, she quickly turned to matters of faith, invoking her strongly held beliefs. Kim \u2013 a pseudonym, like all names used in this article \u2013 was an evangelical Christian whose life revolved around her church: a nondenominational congregation led by other Congolese migrants. \u201cDemocrats are not people of God,\u201d she declared. People in her community were \u201ctraditional,\u201d she added, and generally wanted politicians who were, too. Congolese Christians, she said, usually believed marriage should only occur between a man and a woman, that sex and gender are chosen by God, and that life is sacred from conception. Her pronouncement underscores an important but often overlooked reality. Popular commentary in the U.S. often portrays evangelicals as one homogeneous, overwhelmingly white group. In reality, they are diverse, from political views and geography to race: [3 in 10 U.S. evangelicals are not](https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2025/02/26/age-race-education-and-other-demographic-traits-of-us-religious-groups/) white. And [a growing percentage](https://www.christianitytoday.com/2024/02/evangelical-immigration-reform-lifeway-safety-threat-econom/) are migrants \u2013 a term I use for people who have come to the U.S., regardless of their legal status. Belonging and belief Overall, 12% of evangelicals in the U.S. were born outside the country, [according to a 2024 study](https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2025/02/26/age-race-education-and-other-demographic-traits-of-us-religious-groups/#immigration-and-region-of-birth) conducted by the Pew Research Center. Another 7% have at least one parent born outside the country. These figures are likely underestimates, since undocumented migrants generally avoid surveys for fear of deportation. As an [anthropologist of religion](https://www.johannarichlin.com/), I\u2019ve studied the growing number of migrants drawn to evangelical faith \u2013 and learned how significantly faith shapes their lives in the United States. In turn, these migrants are [shaping America\u2019s religious landscape](https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/rowman--littlefield-handbook-of-contemporary-christianity-in-the-united-states-9781538196434/). Parishioners pray during a service at New Life Fellowship Church, which has many members from Liberia, in Euless, Texas, in 2014. [Joe Raedle/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/keabeh-goyah-whose-son-is-a-doctor-in-liberia-prays-with-news-photo/456691916?phrase=african%20immigrants%20church%20u.s.&adppopup=true) While writing my 2022 book, \u201c[In the Hands of God](https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691194981/in-the-hands-of-god?srsltid=AfmBOooJMOdCYZBsIP6VCmLVMnZ1SEBZA7uAl2xB81__agiw2ZDFkihU),\u201d I spent over a year attending evangelical churches around Washington, D.C., that catered to Brazilian migrants. I interviewed about 80 churchgoers and surveyed dozens more. Congregants described how belief and belonging helped them overcome challenges such as loneliness, \u201cfeeling stuck\u201d due to their legal precariousness and being exploited at work. I heard stories of employers holding migrants\u2019 passports hostage, or refusing to pay construction workers seriously injured on the job. Migrants often told me that their churches\u2019 close-knit communities, and evangelical Christianity\u2019s emphasis on fostering an intimate relationship with God, [gave them a sense of empowerment](https://www.jstor.org/stable/26854758). Through prayer, worship, Bible study and fasting, they tried to discern and implement God\u2019s plan for their lives. The three congregations that I attended offered multiple prayer, study and worship opportunities for migrants throughout the week, both at church and at congregants\u2019 homes. Many pastors were migrants themselves and shared from the pulpit their stories of overcoming adversity. Gl\u00e1ucia, an undocumented migrant from Brazil, described leaving herself \u201cin the hands of God\u201d as she faced uncertainty about her future. \u201cI love living here,\u201d she explained. \u201cBut if God wants me to leave, I\u2019m under God\u2019s will. Wherever God directs me, I will go. Therefore, I don\u2019t get worried, because we can\u2019t become worried about what comes tomorrow. Because tomorrow belongs to God. \"The most I can do,\u201d she added, \u201cwhich I always try to do, is pray to God to give direction\u201d to political leaders. Members of the First Brazilian Church of Boston pray during a Sunday evening service in 2013. [Kelvin Ma/MediaNews Group/Boston Herald via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/members-of-the-first-brazilian-church-of-boston-pray-during-news-photo/1290418098?phrase=brazilian%20immigrants%20church&adppopup=true) Growing communities Some people I met identified as evangelical before coming to the U.S., but a significant number were converts. Even among people who had attended evangelical churches in Brazil, many insisted that they truly \u201cmet Jesus\u201d in the United States and reflected that their faith back home had been \u201clukewarm.\u201d Angela, an undocumented migrant, knew many evangelicals in Brazil. It wasn\u2019t until arriving in the U.S., though, that she converted from Catholicism. She told me, \u201cWhen you are here, without your family, without any friends, you feel alone, and look for something to hold on to,\u201d she told me. According to large-scale surveys, [most immigrants in the U.S. are Christian](https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2013/05/17/the-religious-affiliation-of-us-immigrants/), and [the majority of Christian migrants are Catholic](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3629734/). Over the past several decades, however, a growing percentage of people in Latin America, long a Catholic stronghold, [have been embracing evangelical churches](https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2014/11/13/religion-in-latin-america/). That has influenced the religious makeup of migrants to the United States. Others convert to evangelical Christianity [once they arrive](https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691194981/in-the-hands-of-god?srsltid=AU7gw4VwLjQ6Py74sgZV5QzvMGf1gY-dNFjzbKwU3O2dQ6RbECgptcja), as my research highlights. People attend Sunday services at the Church of Grace to Fujianese in New York in 2010. [Najlah Feanny/Corbis via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/throngs-of-fujianese-people-file-through-a-doorway-under-news-photo/2175809015?phrase=immigrants%20church&adppopup=true) Most of the migrants I met reported intense psychological pain as they endured family separation, workplace exploitation and legal uncertainty, which prompted them to search for meaning and comfort in religious spaces. Migrant evangelical churches, they found, [spoke directly to their distress](https://www.jstor.org/stable/26854758). Their style of worship and theological teachings provided concrete tools, including practices such as prayer, fasting and sharing personal testimony, that relieved migrants\u2019 suffering. Research suggests it is also common for migrants from other parts of the world, [including Taiwan](https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691164663/getting-saved-in-america) [and China](https://doi.org/10.2307/3711910), to turn to evangelical, charismatic and Pentecostal churches to address difficulties [they confront in the United States](https://www.rutgersuniversitypress.org/a-place-to-be/9780813544939). Changing face of Christianity Evangelical churches play a major role in these migrants\u2019 experiences of the U.S. In turn, these migrants are playing a significant role in American Christianity, as I explain in \u201c[The Handbook of Contemporary Christianity in the United States](https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/rowman--littlefield-handbook-of-contemporary-christianity-in-the-united-states-9781538196434/).\u201d Over the past few decades, Americans have become [less Christian and less religious](https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2025/02/26/decline-of-christianity-in-the-us-has-slowed-may-have-leveled-off/#trends-in-the-religiousness-of-christians). In 2007, 78% of Americans called themselves Christians; today, that number is 63%. Migrants, however, are [more likely to have a religious affiliation](https://www.pewresearch.org/2024/08/19/geographic-spotlights-a-closer-look-at-4-migration-stories/): 87%, compared with 70% of all people in the United States. In Maine, for example, where I live, journalists have noted how [African migrants are reinvigorating churches](https://www.mainepublic.org/arts-and-culture/2024-06-13/african-immigration-to-maine-fuels-boom-in-new-churches-bringing-distinct-forms-of-christianity). And many [thriving migrant churches are evangelical](https://www.patheos.com/blogs/anxiousbench/2021/09/the-2020-census-immigration-and-the-future-of-american-evangelicalism/). Looking toward the future, I believe these trends will continue to transform U.S. religion, which may affect politics as well. Many migrants I met, including green-card holders and asylum-seekers, hoped to eventually gain citizenship and be able to vote. Many attended church alongside their own children, born in the U.S. \u2013 future voters whose attitudes may be shaped by their parents\u2019 experiences of both migration and faith. People opposed to Arizona SB 1070, an immigration enforcement law, attend a prayer service at a Baptist church in Phoenix on April 25, 2010. [John Moore/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/opponents-of-arizonas-new-immigration-enforcement-law-news-photo/98662032?phrase=pray%20immigration%20church%20u.s.&adppopup=true) I learned that evangelical migrants\u2019 identity as \u201cpeople of God\u201d was central to their political views, as it is for their white, U.S.-born counterparts. Some of them believed Trump would restore the United States to its presumed destiny as an explicitly Christian nation. His policies about abortion, marriage and gender seemed to outweigh concerns about the risks his immigration policies posed to them and their families. Since the start of his second term, with its waves of [sometimes violent](https://www.npr.org/2026/01/16/nx-s1-5675286/violent-incidents-involving-ice-raise-questions-about-their-training-and-use-of-force) and aggressive [ICE raids](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/25/us/ice-surge-immigration-arrests.html), detentions and deportations, I have wondered how they would assess the administration today. Regardless of their political views, it is clear that migrants will continue to look to evangelical faith to cope with and make sense of their experiences in the United States. My research underscores the significant role evangelical faith plays in the life of growing numbers of migrants and the far-reaching impacts of such faith for U.S. society. [Immigration](https://theconversation.com/topics/immigration-411) [Christianity](https://theconversation.com/topics/christianity-2092) [Donald Trump](https://theconversation.com/topics/donald-trump-10206) [Immigrants](https://theconversation.com/topics/immigrants-10993) [Religion and politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/religion-and-politics-15754) [Evangelicals](https://theconversation.com/topics/evangelicals-17912) [Latinos](https://theconversation.com/topics/latinos-26978) [Religion and society](https://theconversation.com/topics/religion-and-society-72761) [Johanna Bard Richlin, University of Maine](https://theconversation.com/belonging-and-belief-why-evangelical-churches-draw-many-migrants-in-the-us-286504) Author [Johanna Bard Richlin](https://theconversation.com/profiles/johanna-bard-richlin-1481530) Associate Professor of Anthropology, University of Maine Disclosure statement Johanna Bard Richlin does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Maine](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-maine-2120) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.4gpuwsjdn](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.4gpuwsjdn)", "content_hash": "01a219e781c627b84f36de861b5aea2f5dbc70af83b1a075c0a1c1c34e17a452", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:29Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "949", "title": "Remote work made these colleagues more open with each other, not\u00a0less", "url": "https://theconversation.com/remote-work-made-these-colleagues-more-open-with-each-other-not-less-291728", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/remote-work-made-these-colleagues-more-open-with-each-other-not-less-291728", "text": "Many employees see having the freedom to work from home as a benefit. [DigitalVision/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/black-entrepreneur-wearing-headphones-on-video-royalty-free-image/1369954837?adppopup=true) Remote work made these colleagues more open with each other, not less Published: October 16, 2026 8:24am EDT https://theconversation.com/remote-work-made-these-colleagues-more-open-with-each-other-not-less-291728 https://theconversation.com/remote-work-made-these-colleagues-more-open-with-each-other-not-less-291728 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article If you need to participate in video meetings for work, you\u2019ve probably experienced more than one disruption, whether it\u2019s kids darting around or home renovation noise. Meetings that use Zoom and similar software are a fixture of remote work, which [surged during the COVID-19 pandemic](https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2023/acs/acs-52.html) and remains prevalent. As of August 2026, [36% of American workers](https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cpseea42.htm) in management, professional and related occupations did at least some of their work from home, according to the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. I\u2019m a management scholar who studies [how, when and where people work](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=3I0eCFcAAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao). Before the pandemic began, I was studying a professional services company. That made it possible for me to follow how its staff transitioned from primarily working in person to mostly remote through 2024, which gave me more insight into how remote work has changed people\u2019s lives. The study included 275 interviews and about 330 hours of observation. Most people in the groups I followed ended up working from home four or five days a week, joining meetings by video. What I found was the opposite of what I expected. Learning more about colleagues Rather than pulling colleagues apart, remote work brought them closer and made them more willing to be open with one another. The findings were peer-reviewed and will be [published in an upcoming issue](https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2024.19818) of the management journal Organization Science. Before the shift to remote work, I assumed \u2013 consistent with the predictions made by many prominent [researchers of workplace relationships](https://hbr.org/2021/04/what-psychological-safety-looks-like-in-a-hybrid-workplace) \u2013 that moving people out of the office would break down social connections. But I was wrong. In the office, before the shift to remote work, I found that people primarily viewed one another as work colleagues rather than whole people. Reflecting this relationship, workers sometimes said they felt uncomfortable sharing with one another aspects of their lives that might limit their ability to do work. For example, one worker I interviewed, Patrick, was afraid to tell his colleagues he could not work late one night because he was helping his mom. All the names I\u2019m mentioning are pseudonyms to protect the privacy of the people involved and their employer. \u201cEven though you didn\u2019t sleep last night, you\u2019re expected to show up and smile and be fine,\u201d said Tamara, an exhausted parent with two young children, when she was still commuting regularly to the office. Surprisingly, people told me that they learned a lot more about one another once they began to work remotely instead of being together in an office. During my research, I heard about children yelling in the background and pets wandering in front of cameras. A spouse arriving home from work stopped to say hello on screen. In team chat channels, people posted photos of their children, their animals and the meals they had cooked. \u201cEveryone has noises in the background, or they have something going on or whatever, and so we get to know each other quicker and on a deeper level than we would otherwise,\u201d Tamara told me. In some cases, this increased sense of closeness happened to people who had known each other for years prior to switching to remote work. When you\u2019re a remote employee, you may get more quality time with your pets. [Moment/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/woman-working-from-home-with-candlestick-chart-on-royalty-free-image/2210727461?adppopup=true) Growing psychological safety [Psychological safety](https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-orgpsych-031413-091305) is the sense that you can take an interpersonal risk at work, such as admitting a mistake, asking for help or raising a problem, without being judged negatively. Decades of management research tie psychological safety to important outcomes for both workers and organizations, including [learning, creativity and speaking up](https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-orgpsych-120920-055217). Over time, working remotely seemed to improve psychological safety for many of these co-workers. People became \u201cless afraid to talk about the interface of their personal and professional lives,\u201d Leah said. Before, she added, no one would have volunteered that a breakup was affecting them and that they needed a day off to decompress. After the company went remote, people told me they felt more comfortable sharing their own challenges as they tried to juggle their work and the challenges they faced in their personal lives. Supporting work and nonwork activities And as people started sharing these problems with their co-workers, they also told me they felt as if they could then better support one another. When one worker\u2019s father fell ill, his colleague, Brooke, took over the tasks the two had planned to do together. \u201cI\u2019m just going to take this over for you right now,\u201d Brooke told him. \u201cAnd then, whenever you can, come back and swoop in.\u201d The colleague was able to dedicate time to caring for his father. And importantly, because help circulated this way, Brooke said, a task no longer \u201cstopped dead in the water\u201d when one person could not get to it. This deeper collaboration did not seem to come at the expense of getting tasks completed. As an employee I\u2019m calling Charles explained, having co-workers sharing details about their personal lives has \u201chelped us to work more effectively in a lot of ways.\u201d Crucially, these improvements were experienced by people with many different living arrangements \u2013 young and old, those with and without kids or partners, men and women. And these benefits were positively reinforcing. For instance, sharing more meant learning more about others, which further reinforced psychological safety. My findings suggest that remote work can be a benefit that employers and employees may overlook. Rather than treating time out of the office as a cost to social connection among employees, they might treat it as a way to grow personally and get to know co-workers better. [Workplace](https://theconversation.com/topics/workplace-2524) [Employees](https://theconversation.com/topics/employees-4287) [Colleagues](https://theconversation.com/topics/colleagues-30039) [Remote work](https://theconversation.com/topics/remote-work-35265) [Psychological safety](https://theconversation.com/topics/psychological-safety-63536) [Zoom](https://theconversation.com/topics/zoom-84280) [Vanessa M. Conzon, Boston College](https://theconversation.com/remote-work-made-these-colleagues-more-open-with-each-other-not-less-291728) Author [Vanessa M. Conzon](https://theconversation.com/profiles/vanessa-m-conzon-2374332) Assistant Professor of Management and Organizations, Boston College Disclosure statement Vanessa M. Conzon does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.6t3qkxdp5](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.6t3qkxdp5)", "content_hash": "1d2cbb3cd7e160b1821bab52e832097ecb29f3ee1483d096017d2d25bac1f8c7", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:30Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "950", "title": "Leap seconds have helped keep clocks synchronized with Earth\u2019s rotation, but their time is now over \u2013 here\u2019s\u00a0why", "url": "https://theconversation.com/leap-seconds-have-helped-keep-clocks-synchronized-with-earths-rotation-but-their-time-is-now-over-heres-why-291017", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/leap-seconds-have-helped-keep-clocks-synchronized-with-earths-rotation-but-their-time-is-now-over-heres-why-291017", "text": "Humans haven\u2019t always counted time down to the second. [mrs/Moment via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/clock-royalty-free-image/2184408356?phrase=clock&searchscope=image,film&adppopup=true) Leap seconds have helped keep clocks synchronized with Earth\u2019s rotation, but their time is now over \u2013 here\u2019s why Published: October 8, 2026 8:11am EDT \u2022Updated: October 15, 2026 5:29pm EDT https://theconversation.com/leap-seconds-have-helped-keep-clocks-synchronized-with-earths-rotation-but-their-time-is-now-over-heres-why-291017 https://theconversation.com/leap-seconds-have-helped-keep-clocks-synchronized-with-earths-rotation-but-their-time-is-now-over-heres-why-291017 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article You\u2019re probably used to the month of February having an extra day every four years, during the [leap year](https://theconversation.com/why-does-a-leap-year-have-366-days-218330). Timekeepers added that extra leap day because Earth completes one orbit around the Sun in about 365 and one-quarter days. If we didn\u2019t add leap days, the equinoxes and solstices that mark the beginning of seasons would occur later, slowly shifting the days on which each season began. For millennia, a day has been defined as one full rotation of the Earth. But the Earth\u2019s rotation [isn\u2019t completely uniform](https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/news/feature-articles/when-day-day), and in order to be more precise, modern timekeeping systems have stopped using the Earth\u2019s rotation as their basis. This switch created a discrepancy between how a single second used to be defined, and how it is defined today. Over the course of several years, modern time systems can become a few seconds out of sync with the Earth\u2019s movement. To solve this issue, timekeepers have used what\u2019s called a leap second. Just as leap years keep our calendars in alignment with Earth\u2019s orbit around the Sun, leap seconds have kept our timekeeping aligned with Earth\u2019s daily rotation. But on Oct. 15, 2026, an international organization governing timekeeping [decided to stop](https://theconversation.com/experts-have-voted-to-stop-adding-extra-seconds-to-clock-time-the-outcome-favours-big-tech-294297) adding leap seconds. As a [space scientist](https://dornsife.usc.edu/profile/vahe-peroomian/), I know how measuring time is fundamental to physics research. The precision of physicists\u2019 measurements has gotten better as the way we measure time has changed. History of seconds The systems humans use to measure time have evolved over the past few centuries. The origin of the 24-hour day can be traced to the second millennium B.C.E., when [ancient Egyptians](https://www.britannica.com/topic/24-hour-clock) divided day and night into 12 parts each. Because the length of day and night varies during the year, the length of each hour of daylight and each hour of night did as well. The pendulum clock design patented by Christiaan Huygens in the 17th century. [Christiaan Huygens](https://www.aps.org/apsnews/2017/06/huygens-patents-first-pendulum-clock), [CC BY](http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/) The [Greek astronomer Hipparchus](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Hipparchus-Greek-astronomer), who lived in the second century B.C.E., later adopted a system using 24 equal hours for astronomical calculations. The division of an hour into 60 minutes of 60 seconds each also has its roots in astronomy. Division by 60, or [a base-60 system](https://theconversation.com/what-was-the-first-thing-scientists-discovered-a-historian-makes-the-case-for-babylonian-astronomy-244463), traces back to the Sumerian and Babylonian [civilizations of Mesopotamia](https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20260320-the-ancient-reason-there-are-60-minutes-in-an-hour-and-60-seconds-in-a-minute). The number 60 is particularly convenient because it can be divided up evenly into smaller parts. The Greek astronomer [Claudius Ptolemy](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Ptolemy) adopted the base-60 system while measuring the motions of planets around 150 C.E. He divided each angular degree into 60 parts, and each of these into 60 smaller parts. In Latin, these were later called partes minutae primae, or \u201cfirst small parts,\u201d and partes minutae secundae: \u201csecond small parts.\u201d This system eventually led to the words \u201cminute\u201d and \u201csecond.\u201d Claudius Ptolmey was an astronomer who adopted a 24-hour system to measure time for astronomical observations. [Justus van Gent or Pedro Berruguete (1476)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemy#/media/File:Ptolemy_1476_with_armillary_sphere_model.jpg) Centuries later, as mechanical clocks became increasingly precise, these astronomical subdivisions were adopted for timekeeping as well. Based on the systems devised by Ptolemy and his successors, a mean solar day was divided into 24 hours, times 60 minutes per hour, times 60 seconds per minute: 86,400 seconds. However, actually measuring time down to the second only became possible after Christiaan Huygens [invented the pendulum clock in 1657](https://www.aps.org/apsnews/2017/06/huygens-patents-first-pendulum-clock). In a pendulum clock, a suspended weight swings back and forth at a steady rate. As long as you take the differences in the Earth\u2019s gravitational pull into account, all pendulums of the same length complete a swing in the same length of time. This metric provided a standardized way to measure time. The leap day and the leap second In the spirit of the pendulum clock, scientists spent three more centuries developing systems that would measure time precisely and in a standardized way so that all clocks around the world could tell the same time. During this process, the definition of a second changed several times. They finally found a solution in the cesium atom. In 1967, the [General Conference on Weights and Measures](https://www.bipm.org/en/committees/cg/cgpm) \u2013 an intergovernmental organization that sets measurement standards and has over 100 member nations today \u2013 adopted a new definition based on the [cesium-133 atom](https://www.nist.gov/atomic-clocks/how-atomic-clocks-work/beams-atoms-first-atomic-clocks). Cesium-133 atoms behave like [extraordinarily precise clocks](https://theconversation.com/using-atomic-nuclei-could-allow-scientists-to-read-time-more-precisely-than-ever-what-this-research-could-mean-for-future-clocks-272017). When the atom switches between two particular energy states, it produces radiation that oscillates, just like a pendulum, at a remarkably steady rate. Scientists defined 1 second as exactly 9,192,631,770 of these oscillations. Atomic clocks exploit a property of atoms to define a second that\u2019s standard everywhere. [Coordinated Universal Time](https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/time-realization/utcnist-time-scale), UTC, is [based on atomic clocks](https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/how-utcnist-related-coordinated-universal-time-utc-international) that use cesium-133. As a result, [UT1](https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/UT), the time scale based on Earth\u2019s rotation, can drift relative to UTC. This divergence reflects both short-term irregularities in how fast Earth rotates, and a very gradual, long-term slowdown of Earth\u2019s rotation, [caused primarily by](https://theconversation.com/the-moon-is-getting-slightly-farther-away-from-the-earth-each-year-a-physicist-explains-why-262106) the [Moon\u2019s gravitational pull](https://www.britannica.com/science/tidal-friction) on Earth\u2019s oceans. The difference between the 86,400 atomic seconds metric and a day measured by Earth\u2019s rotation is less than [4 milliseconds](https://www.timeanddate.com/time/earth-rotation.html). This discrepancy may seem small, but it can accumulate to a significant fraction of a second over the course of a year. Since 1972, timekeepers have added 27 positive leap seconds to UTC, at very irregular intervals. Their aim has been to keep the discrepancy between UTC and UT1 to less than 0.9 seconds. They added the [last leap second at the end of 2016](https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/time-realization/leap-seconds). The end of the leap second Adding a second to clocks every so often can disrupt high-precision computer systems. Many computer operating systems assume that a day is 86,400 seconds long and that time advances in a predictable, monotonically increasing sequence. Adding a leap second violates both of those assumptions. On days when a leap second is added, clocks jump by one second on the designated day. That day is then 86,401 seconds long. In 2012, for example, [Reddit experienced an outage](https://www.wired.com/2012/07/leap-second-glitch-explained/) after a leap second was added on June 30 of that year. Other systems, including the Linux kernel and some Java-based applications, [were also affected](https://observer.com/2012/07/june-30-leap-second-bug-briefly-knocks-out-reddit-foursquare/). Because of incidents like these, many high-profile technology companies, including Google and Meta, use \u201c[smearing](https://medium.com/@vishalkamath853/how-google-handles-leap-seconds-877da8252620),\u201d where they spread the extra second in gradual increments over a long time period, rather than inserting it as a one-second step. This endeavor is not easy, and these companies strongly advocated for [abolishing the leap second](https://engineering.fb.com/2022/07/25/production-engineering/its-time-to-leave-the-leap-second-in-the-past/). In 2022, the General Conference on Weights and Measures decided the maximum difference between UTC and UT1 should be increased in, or before, 2035. They planned to agree upon a new maximum value at their 2026 meeting. One factor accelerating their decision is that the Earth has experienced a period of unusually rapid rotation [since about 2015](https://www.timeanddate.com/time/earth-faster-rotation.html). During the 2020 calendar year, timekeepers recorded 28 days where the Earth completed its full rotation before the clock hit 86,400 atomic seconds. The shortest day measured in the modern atomic-clock era occurred on June 29, 2022, when the Earth completed its rotation [1.59 milliseconds](https://www.timeanddate.com/news/astronomy/shortest-day-2022) faster than 86,400 atomic seconds. If Earth keeps up this faster rotation, timekeepers would have eventually needed to introduce the first-ever negative leap second. There\u2019s no large-scale precedent for how computer systems would handle one. So, the General Conference on Weights and Measures met on Oct. 13-15, 2026. It passed a resolution that would make UTC a continuous time scale, without the abrupt addition of leap seconds, beginning on May 20, 2027. The resolution will allow the difference between UTC and UT1 to grow as large as 3,600 seconds, or one hour. This is akin to replacing the leap second with a \u201cleap hour.\u201d The resolution does not specify a schedule for inserting discrete one-hour corrections. A leap hour would not be necessary for centuries. Will I notice the change? The clocks on your smartphones, laptops and other electronic devices seldom, if ever, display seconds. Because of this, the addition of leap seconds has typically gone unnoticed by all but the [metrologists responsible](https://careercenter.ies.org/career/metrologist) for various timekeeping systems. As UTC and UT1 are allowed to diverge, astronomers, Earth scientists and other users of Earth-orientation data will need to account for the growing offset when relating their observations and measurements to civil time. Navigating using the positions of the Sun, the Moon, and the stars requires knowing the precise time when observations are made. Celestial navigators will need to account for the growing difference between UTC and UT1 when determining their precise position on Earth. When you use a compass for navigation, you must take into account the difference between the location of Earth\u2019s magnetic and geographic poles. You can do so by factoring in the [magnetic declination](https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-declination) of your location, or the angle between the direction the compass is pointing and true north. Software on smartphones can do this automatically. A similar approach already exists for converting between UTC, UT1 and other time scales in use today. Keeping track of the differences between these time scales, especially the drift between UTC and UT1, will now become a more important part of high-precision timekeeping. The leap second is now a thing of the past. Given the importance that people attach to the regular change of seasons, though, the leap year is not going anywhere. This is an updated version of an article originally published on Oct. 8, 2026. [Astronomy](https://theconversation.com/topics/astronomy-50) [Time](https://theconversation.com/topics/time-68) [Physics](https://theconversation.com/topics/physics-154) [History](https://theconversation.com/topics/history-180) [History of science](https://theconversation.com/topics/history-of-science-7649) [Leap second](https://theconversation.com/topics/leap-second-18331) [Leap year](https://theconversation.com/topics/leap-year-24431) [Vahe Peroomian, USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences](https://theconversation.com/leap-seconds-have-helped-keep-clocks-synchronized-with-earths-rotation-but-their-time-is-now-over-heres-why-291017) Author [Vahe Peroomian](https://theconversation.com/profiles/vahe-peroomian-749331) Professor of Physics and Astronomy, USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences Disclosure statement Vahe Peroomian has previously received funding from the National Science Foundation (NSF) and from the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) for work on space weather and geomagnetic storms. Partners [USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences](https://theconversation.com/institutions/usc-dornsife-college-of-letters-arts-and-sciences-2669) and [University of Southern California](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-southern-california-1265) provide funding as members of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) Languages [Fran\u00e7ais](https://theconversation.com/les-secondes-intercalaires-synchronisent-nos-horloges-avec-la-rotation-de-la-terre-depuis-des-decennies-mais-elles-pourraient-disparaitre-294266) English DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.33fgvmjre](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.33fgvmjre)", "content_hash": "283ae5b0948a2496a030c3fb989e1652284a2ad76f8abd26b5e0bfa2b176d730", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:31Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "951", "title": "In Alberta, far\u2011right populism fuelled by conspiracy theories and alienation drive bid to separate from\u00a0Canada", "url": "https://theconversation.com/in-alberta-far-right-populism-fuelled-by-conspiracy-theories-and-alienation-drive-bid-to-separate-from-canada-294374", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/in-alberta-far-right-populism-fuelled-by-conspiracy-theories-and-alienation-drive-bid-to-separate-from-canada-294374", "text": "[Vadim Gouida/Shutterstock](https://www.shutterstock.com/image-photo/edmonton-canada-august-29-2026-lone-2838015519?trackingId=d34157c0-f210-41f9-b140-fc598f97b0a7&listId=searchResults) In Alberta, far\u2011right populism fuelled by conspiracy theories and alienation drive bid to separate from Canada Published: October 15, 2026 11:02am EDT https://theconversation.com/in-alberta-far-right-populism-fuelled-by-conspiracy-theories-and-alienation-drive-bid-to-separate-from-canada-294374 https://theconversation.com/in-alberta-far-right-populism-fuelled-by-conspiracy-theories-and-alienation-drive-bid-to-separate-from-canada-294374 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article In the western Canadian province of Alberta, a political battle is playing out on people\u2019s front lawns. Separatists are flying the royal blue Alberta flag, others the red and white maple leaf of Canada to show their support for a united Canada. It\u2019s all because a populist right-wing political movement wants Alberta to break away from the rest of the country. On October 19, Albertans will vote on whether to have a binding referendum on independence. \u201cPublic opinion polls show quite consistently over the last two years that about 70% to 75% of Albertans want to remain in Canada,\u201d explains Jared Wesley, a professor of political science at the University of Alberta to [The Conversation Weekly](https://theconversation.com/topics/the-conversation-weekly-98901) podcast. \u201cSo there\u2019s a lot of confusion around the process and why we\u2019re holding this vote in the first place.\u201d Separatist sentiment is driven in part by a longstanding sense of western alienation from eastern Canada, fuelled by grievances that the province puts more into the federal budget than it gets back. But the current wave of separatism in Alberta also has links to protests against Covid vaccine mandates and conspiracy theories. While most separatists want Alberta to become its own country, some even want the province to be the 51st US state. Donald Trump\u2019s aggressive trade war with Canada and his attacks on Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney loom large over the poll. Some Albertans who may otherwise have voted in favour of independence to send a message to Ottawa, Wesley says, \u201care really withholding that support because they worry about what message that sends to the United States.\u201d Read more: [Albertans disapprove of Donald Trump \u2014 and his brand is weakening separatism, too](https://theconversation.com/albertans-disapprove-of-donald-trump-and-his-brand-is-weakening-separatism-too-292573) The referendum was called by the Alberta premier, Danielle Smith, leader of the province\u2019s United Conservative Party. Despite her support for Alberta remaining in Canada, Smith faced pressure from separatists within her party to hold a referendum. Wesley says Smith\u2019s decision bears similarities with the decision taken by then UK prime minister David Cameron to hold the 2016 Brexit referendum on leaving the European Union: \u201cMore or less, David Cameron called the bluff of his internal Brexiteers that were putting pressure on him, and called a nationwide referendum thinking that he would win it. A lot of the same pressures are at play for Danielle Smith.\u201d There are [ten questions on the ballot](https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/calgary/guide-to-the-10-questions-in-alberta-s-oct-19-referendum-9.7361550), ranging from whether to introduce stricter rules on access to public services for immigrants, to proposals for judicial and constitutional reform. The final question reads: \u201cShould Alberta remain a province of Canada, or should the government of Alberta commence the legal process required under the Canadian Constitution to hold a binding provincial referendum on whether or not Alberta should separate from Canada?\u201d Wesley says turnout will be key to how the referendum plays out: \u201cVery few polls show a reasonable prospect for this first referendum passing. However, in a low turnout situation, the distance between remain and continue the conversation \u2026 narrows considerably.\u201d Disclosure statement Jared Wesley receives funding from the Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council. Credits This episode of [The Conversation Weekly](https://pod.link/1550643487) was written and produced by Gemma Ware and Grace Jennings-Edquist. Mixing by Dan Semo and theme music by Neeta Sarl. Newsclips in this episode from [cpac](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HAPaLE2OFvE), [BBC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rrcgKLveCgo) and [Global News](https://globalnews.ca/video/11646995/alberta-separatist-supporters-say-conversation-about-leaving-canada-long-overdue). Listen to The Conversation Weekly via any of the apps listed above, download it directly via our [RSS feed](https://feeds.captivate.fm/the-conversation-weekly/) or find out [how else to listen here](https://theconversation.com/how-to-listen-to-the-conversations-podcasts-154131). A transcript of this episode is available via the Apple Podcasts or Spotify apps. [Canada](https://theconversation.com/topics/canada-2257) [Podcast](https://theconversation.com/topics/podcast-3738) [Audio](https://theconversation.com/topics/audio-3759) [Canadian politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/canadian-politics-7958) [US-Canada Relationship](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-canada-relationship-41262) [Western alienation](https://theconversation.com/topics/western-alienation-79233) [The Conversation Weekly](https://theconversation.com/topics/the-conversation-weekly-98901) [Danielle Smith](https://theconversation.com/topics/danielle-smith-124113) [Alberta separatism](https://theconversation.com/topics/alberta-separatism-133315) [Gemma Ware, The Conversation, Jared Wesley](https://theconversation.com/in-alberta-far-right-populism-fuelled-by-conspiracy-theories-and-alienation-drive-bid-to-separate-from-canada-294374) Author [Gemma Ware](https://theconversation.com/uk/team#gemma-ware) Head of Audio, The Conversation UK Interviewed [Jared Wesley](https://theconversation.com/profiles/jared-wesley-722961) Professor, Political Science, University of Alberta Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AB.yfwvhxkje](https://doi.org/10.64628/AB.yfwvhxkje)", "content_hash": "931f648ea3012d3f2ed0521b4f9cdfcbe81d596b8cf1e9d992d4fab42271704a", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:32Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "952", "title": "Young voters fear mass shootings like the one in Oregon, but that fear divides them on gun\u00a0control", "url": "https://theconversation.com/young-voters-fear-mass-shootings-like-the-one-in-oregon-but-that-fear-divides-them-on-gun-control-294620", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/young-voters-fear-mass-shootings-like-the-one-in-oregon-but-that-fear-divides-them-on-gun-control-294620", "text": "People leave a family meeting area following a shooting at Silver Rail Elementary School on Oct. 15, 2026, in Bend, Ore. [AP Photo/Joe Kline](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Oregon%20School%20Shooting/83d35196352f423ca913c945621f97bc/photo) Young voters fear mass shootings like the one in Oregon, but that fear divides them on gun control Published: October 16, 2026 2:02pm EDT https://theconversation.com/young-voters-fear-mass-shootings-like-the-one-in-oregon-but-that-fear-divides-them-on-gun-control-294620 https://theconversation.com/young-voters-fear-mass-shootings-like-the-one-in-oregon-but-that-fear-divides-them-on-gun-control-294620 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article A gunman shot and killed three children and a teacher at a [school in Bend, Oregon](https://www.cnn.com/2026/10/16/us/live-news/bend-oregon-school-shooting), on Oct. 15, 2026, the [52nd school shooting this year](https://www.cnn.com/us/school-shootings-fast-facts-dg), according to CNN. The children\u2019s ages were 5, 8 and 11; a 7-year-old and another teacher were taken to the hospital with critical injuries. The 16-year-old shooting suspect was shot and killed by a bystander. Though Oregon has [stricter gun laws than most states](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/15/us/oregon-gun-control-laws.html), some of those laws, such as a measure requiring tougher background checks and requiring a person to have a permit in order to buy a gun, are stalled in court. The Bend shooting \u2013 the latest in a long line of modern school shootings dating back to [the late 1980s](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/law/stockton-massacre/) \u2013 comes just weeks before the midterm elections, in which voters will choose members of Congress and state lawmakers who can shape gun laws. Nearly [50 million Americans](https://circle.tufts.edu/latest-research/49-million-young-people-will-be-eligible-vote-2026-midterms) ages 18 to 29 are eligible to vote in the upcoming midterm elections, and [about 60% say they plan to](https://now.tufts.edu/2026/09/22/young-voters-flex-their-power). If they follow through, it would break midterm turnout records for voters under 30. [Gun control](https://now.tufts.edu/2026/09/22/young-voters-flex-their-power) is an issue these young voters care about more than the public at large does, and it\u2019s easy to understand why: In their lifetime, mass shootings have been [a recurring part of American life](https://doi.org/10.1111/ssqu.13148). Directly experiencing a mass shooting is rare, yet some young people have survived two before turning 25. When a gunman opened fire at Brown University in December 2025, [at least two students](https://www.thecut.com/article/brown-university-shooting-parkland-survivor.html) on campus had already experienced a school shooting before they got to college. The same thing happened in April 2025, when students who lived through [the 2018 Parkland, Florida, shooting](https://www.cnn.com/2018/02/15/us/florida-shooting-victims-school) at Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School found themselves caught in another shooting at [Florida State University](https://www.cnn.com/2025/04/19/us/florida-state-university-shooting-what-we-know). As [one of them](https://www.local10.com/news/local/2025/04/17/parkland-school-massacre-survivor-was-at-fsu-during-fatal-shooting/) put it: \u201cThis is America.\u201d Researchers have called [today\u2019s young adults](https://doi.org/10.1111/ssqu.13148) the \u201cmassacre generation.\u201d They\u2019re the first group to grow up entirely in the [post-9/11, post-Columbine High School shooting era](https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/apr/19/columbine-parkland-how-mass-shootings-changed-us-schools), with routine [lockdown drills](https://nces.ed.gov/fastfacts/display.asp?id=954), heightened [security in schools](https://www.cnbc.com/2022/07/06/the-school-security-industry-was-valued-at-3point1-billion-in-2021.html) and nonstop [media coverage of mass violence](https://www.universityofcalifornia.edu/news/media-exposure-mass-violence-can-fuel-cycle-distress-study-shows). As criminologists who study the impacts of [victimization and gun violence](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=cbfYVuoAAAAJ&hl=en) among [young people](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=X52GgbIAAAAJ&hl=en), we wanted to know how growing up with the threat of mass shootings has shaped young adults\u2019 views on gun legislation. In [a recent study](https://doi.org/10.1111/ssqu.70087), we surveyed nearly 1,700 young adults, ages 18 to 29, across the country to find out. We found that fear of mass shootings pulls this generation apart along political and gender lines: It pushes some toward wanting stricter gun laws and others in the opposite direction. Police tape is placed outside Silver Rail Elementary school, where a shooting took place on Oct. 15, 2026, in Bend, Ore. [AP Photo/Joe Kline](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/APTOPIX%20Oregon%20School%20Shooting/9d43596a655348c9b084f117f1f97771/photo) A debate that repeats after every shooting Mass shootings account for [about 1% of gun deaths](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2026/04/28/what-the-data-says-about-gun-deaths-in-the-us/) in the U.S. Still, they profoundly shape how safe millions of Americans feel. In a 2019 [survey by the American Psychological Association](https://www.apa.org/news/press/releases/stress/2019/stress-america-2019.pdf), nearly 80% of Americans said the possibility of a mass shooting stressed them out, and a third said they [avoided certain places because of it](https://www.apa.org/news/press/releases/2019/08/fear-mass-shooting). The fear has become so common that clinicians now have [screening tools](https://doi.org/10.1080/07481187.2023.2214889) to assess and treat people with anxiety over mass violence. Young adults have also grown up watching the same [heated gun debates](https://www.sacbee.com/opinion/article313732975.html) play out after every tragedy. [Gun control advocates](https://www.everytown.org/issues/mass-shootings/) call for [stricter firearm restrictions](https://doi.org/10.1111/ssqu.12857) to prevent [the next mass shooting](https://www.wired.com/story/the-looser-a-states-gun-laws-the-more-mass-shootings-it-has/), whereas [gun rights advocates](https://tpusa.com/live/mass-shootings-are-not-a-reason-for-gun-control/) push back with the \u201cgood guy with a gun\u201d argument, a phrase [made popular by the National Rifle Association](https://www.npr.org/2012/12/21/167824766/nra-only-thing-that-stops-a-bad-guy-with-a-gun-is-a-good-guy-with-a-gun). Supporters of the latter view say more armed citizens are needed to stop active shooters, so more people should be able to buy and carry guns with fewer restrictions, such as [carrying in public without a permit or on college campuses](https://doi.org/10.1111/ssqu.70087). Researchers have known for a long time that [more guns don\u2019t necessarily mean less violence](https://doi.org/10.1177/00027162231164865). A [2021 study](https://doi.org/10.1080/07418825.2020.1789693) found that mass shootings were more common in states with higher rates of gun ownership, and gun homicides were more common in states with looser concealed carry laws. Yet even after [decades of mass shootings](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/390806181_NIJ_Final_Report_A_Comprehensive_Assessment_of_Deadly_Mass_Shootings_1980-2018), federal gun policy has [barely budged](https://www.bu.edu/articles/2022/the-long-failed-history-of-gun-control-legislation/). Even where individual states and local governments have passed assault weapons bans, including [Connecticut](https://portal.ct.gov/ag/press-releases/2026-press-releases/attorney-general-statement-on-scotus-certification-in-challenge-to-connecticut-assault-weapon-ban) and [Cook County, Illinois](https://www.jurist.org/news/2026/07/us-supreme-court-agrees-to-hear-challenges-to-assault-weapons-bans/), they\u2019ve faced [Second Amendment challenges that are now before the Supreme Court](https://theconversation.com/why-the-assault-weapons-debate-is-a-culture-debate-291639). Young people have become some of the [loudest voices in this debate](https://time.com/7304031/march-for-our-lives-pam-bondi-shootings-jaclyn-corin/), most notably through [March for Our Lives](https://marchforourlives.org/) \u2013 an organization started by Parkland survivors that focuses on ending gun violence \u2013 and [other youth-led groups](https://www.texastribune.org/2018/06/28/texas-gun-control-high-school-student-activists-santa-fe-mass-shooting/) across the country. And because pro-gun views tend to be more common among [older Americans](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2024/07/24/key-facts-about-americans-and-guns/), many political observers assume [stricter gun laws will follow](https://iop.harvard.edu/press-releases/harvard-iop-youth-poll-finds-stricter-gun-laws-ban-assault-weapons-favored-two) as young people [gain voting power](https://now.tufts.edu/2026/09/22/young-voters-flex-their-power). This fall, voters in a few states will weigh in on gun policy directly. In Massachusetts, voters will decide whether to [keep or repeal a 2024 law](https://ballotpedia.org/Massachusetts_Question_9,_Firearm_Regulations_Referendum_(2026)) that, among other things, requires gun owners to register their firearms with the state, expands safety training for people applying for a gun license and broadens the state\u2019s ban on assault-style weapons. In Arkansas, they\u2019ll decide whether to [change the state constitution](https://ballotpedia.org/Arkansas_Issue_2,_Right_to_Keep_and_Bear_Arms_Amendment_(2026)) to more strongly protect gun rights, including the right to own ammunition and gun parts. If young adults turn out in record numbers, their views could help determine outcomes like these. Students at the University of Texas at Austin hold a candlelight vigil in March 2026 for the victims of a mass shooting at a bar in downtown Austin. [Brandon Bell/Getty Images News via Getty](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/students-gather-for-a-candlelight-vigil-for-the-victims-of-news-photo/2264851721) Young adults are split So where do young adults stand? We\u2019ve found that a shared fear of mass shootings does not necessarily lead to shared views on guns. [Our survey asked](https://doi.org/10.1111/ssqu.70087) young adults to rate on a scale from 1 to 3 \u2013 from \u201cnot at all\u201d to \u201ca lot\u201d \u2013 how much they fear that they or someone they love will become a victim of a mass shooting. More than 60% reported moderate or high levels of fear, including about 17% who fell in the high-fear range. In general, about two-thirds of young adults said people should need a permit to carry a gun in public and that guns shouldn\u2019t be allowed on college campuses. About 6 in 10 disagreed that owning more guns makes people safer, and a similar share disagreed that gun control laws are unconstitutional. We found that young adults who worried more about mass shootings tended to be more supportive of gun control. But this pattern didn\u2019t hold for young men, Republicans or those who were politically conservative. For these groups, the opposite was true: The more they feared mass shootings, the less they supported gun restrictions. Young adults are divided on gun control, and those divisions are sharpest when fear runs high. In the coming years, the massacre generation is on track to become part of the [largest voting bloc in the country](https://doi.org/10.1111/polp.12340). Most young adults share a fear of mass shootings, but they disagree on what to do about them. And with each new mass shooting, that divide may only deepen. This is an updated version of [an article](https://theconversation.com/young-voters-fear-mass-shootings-but-are-divided-on-gun-control-272235) originally published on Oct. 15, 2026. [Gun reform](https://theconversation.com/topics/gun-reform-4580) [Gun violence](https://theconversation.com/topics/gun-violence-7990) [Mass shootings](https://theconversation.com/topics/mass-shootings-10655) [Mass violence](https://theconversation.com/topics/mass-violence-20963) [US gun control](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-gun-control-21136) [School shootings](https://theconversation.com/topics/school-shootings-40052) [Parkland school shooting](https://theconversation.com/topics/parkland-school-shooting-50173) [March for Our Lives](https://theconversation.com/topics/march-for-our-lives-51551) [Gen Z](https://theconversation.com/topics/gen-z-65413) [Columbine shooting](https://theconversation.com/topics/columbine-shooting-69027) [US midterms](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-midterms-129216) [2026 midterm elections](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-midterm-elections-178375) [Jillian Turanovic, University of Colorado Boulder, Kristin Lloyd, Clemson University](https://theconversation.com/young-voters-fear-mass-shootings-like-the-one-in-oregon-but-that-fear-divides-them-on-gun-control-294620) Authors [Jillian Turanovic](https://theconversation.com/profiles/jillian-turanovic-2548303) Professor of Sociology, University of Colorado Boulder [Kristin Lloyd](https://theconversation.com/profiles/kristin-lloyd-2767569) Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice, Clemson University Disclosure statement Jillian Turanovic has recently received research funding from the National Institute of Justice, the National Collaborative on Gun Violence Research, the Bureau of Justice Assistance, and Arnold Ventures, none of which are responsible for this content. Kristin Lloyd has recently received funding from the Bureau of Justice Assistance, who is not responsible for this content. Partners [Clemson University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/clemson-university-1819) and [University of Colorado](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-colorado-boulder-733) provide funding as members of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ngsxxkhfu](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ngsxxkhfu)", "content_hash": "cdd4e2e5dc88badd9dbc66d3cd240c457ff8fc8166c436226c0104729699bdb7", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:34Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "953", "title": "Social Security benefits will rise 3.6% in 2027, but that may not be enough to offset healthcare\u00a0costs", "url": "https://theconversation.com/social-security-benefits-will-rise-3-6-in-2027-but-that-may-not-be-enough-to-offset-healthcare-costs-293688", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/social-security-benefits-will-rise-3-6-in-2027-but-that-may-not-be-enough-to-offset-healthcare-costs-293688", "text": "Social Security benefits will rise 3.6% in 2027, but that may not be enough to offset healthcare costs Published: October 13, 2026 3:26pm EDT \u2022Updated: October 14, 2026 11:03am EDT Increasing Social Security benefits annually helps millions of older Americans afford necessities. [Jeffrey Greenberg/Universal Images Group via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/vero-beach-florida-publix-grocery-store-supermarket-female-news-photo/2268676973?phrase=older%20person%20paying%20in%20a%20store&adppopup=true) https://theconversation.com/social-security-benefits-will-rise-3-6-in-2027-but-that-may-not-be-enough-to-offset-healthcare-costs-293688 https://theconversation.com/social-security-benefits-will-rise-3-6-in-2027-but-that-may-not-be-enough-to-offset-healthcare-costs-293688 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Monthly checks for the nearly [76 million Americans who receive Social Security benefits](https://www.ssa.gov/policy/docs/quickfacts/stat_snapshot/) will [rise by 3.6%](https://www.ssa.gov/news/en/press/releases/2026-10-14.html) in 2027. The increase, announced on Oct. 14, 2026, is the largest since the one that took effect in 2023. This [cost-of-living adjustment](https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/latestCOLA.html), or COLA, is how the government tries to make sure that the value of Social Security benefits keeps up with inflation. Without it, the same payment would cover less of what recipients are spending on medical care, housing, groceries, clothing and other necessities. I am a [finance professor](https://umaine.edu/directory/ums_directory/pankaj-agrrawal-2/), and one of the topics I research is how [inflation affects retirees\u2019 investment choices](http://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5013829). And I believe the upcoming bump doesn\u2019t capture the inflation challenges that many people who receive Social Security benefits face. The percentage increase in Social Security benefits depends in part on the annual inflation rate as of September 2026. It [stood at 3.7% that month](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm), as the Bureau of Labor Statistics, a government agency, also announced on Oct. 14. But the size of the increase is only part of the issue. The index used to set the COLA reflects what working people buy, not retirees, and that is a mismatch at the heart of a long-running debate over how the adjustment should be measured. Calculating the COLA The government has [annually increased Social Security benefits](https://www.ssa.gov/policy/docs/ssb/v67n3/v67n3p73.html) to keep up with inflation since 1975. Congress previously had to pass a law each time it increased benefits. As a result, years could pass without an adjustment, despite climbing prices. The metric that the government uses is its [consumer price index for urban wage earners and clerical workers](https://www.ssa.gov/oact/STATS/cpiw.html), known as the CPI-W. The Bureau of Labor Statistics tracks changes in the prices of goods and services that working households typically buy to calculate this index. The Social Security Administration then compares the average CPI-W reading for July, August and September with the average for those same months in the prior year to set the COLA adjustment, which kicks in the following January. If the economy contracts and prices fall, however, [benefits stay the same](https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/colaseries.html). That happened in 2009, 2010 and 2015. Spending much of the bump on premiums One big problem with this calculation is that it understates healthcare inflation. Out-of-pocket health costs, including [Medicare premiums, deductibles and copayments](https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs) for medical care and prescription drugs, typically consume nearly 30% of Social Security benefits, according to research from the nonpartisan [Center for Retirement Research at Boston College](https://crr.bc.edu/retirees-are-worried-about-the-cost-of-healthcare-and-who-can-blame-them/). For example, the government deducts [Medicare Part B premiums](https://www.medicare.gov/publications/11579-medicare-costs.pdf) directly from many retirees\u2019 Social Security checks. The [standard monthly Part B premium rose by US$17.90](https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/2026-medicare-parts-b-premiums-deductibles) in 2026, from $185 to $202.90. That increase alone consumed roughly one-third of the average $56 monthly gain in benefits from the 2026 COLA. More broadly, this big jump in healthcare costs for people covered by Medicare, as [most Americans getting Social Security benefits are](https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/medicare-basics/working-past-65), helps explain why there is a long-running debate over how the program measures inflation. Finding other options Critics of the system the government uses to adjust benefits for inflation, including the [Senior Citizens League](https://seniorsleague.org/cola-watch/) and the [National Committee to Preserve Social Security and Medicare](https://www.ncpssm.org/documents/social-security-policy-papers/the-cpi-e-a-better-option-for-calculating-social-security-colas/), say the CPI-W reflects the spending habits of working people, who shell out less on medical care than retirees. Retirees also tend to spend less on clothing, education and transportation than people with jobs. There are other ways the government measures inflation, including the [consumer price index for Americans 62 and older](https://www.bls.gov/cpi/research-series/r-cpi-e-home.htm), known as the CPI-E, that could be more accurate. The Bureau of Labor Statistics began to calculate this experimental index in the 1980s, but the Social Security Administration has not used it to set benefits yet despite [some efforts in Congress](https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12675) to make the switch. The National Committee to Preserve Social Security and Medicare has researched how much more older people spend on medical care. People 65 and older [spend more than twice as much](https://www.ncpssm.org/documents/social-security-policy-papers/the-cpi-e-a-better-option-for-calculating-social-security-colas/) as younger Americans on out-of-pocket health and medical costs, the group estimates. Those 75 and older spend nearly three times as much, the committee estimated after reviewing data from 1982 through 2014. It argues that a CPI-E weighted more toward medical care, housing and recreation would yield slightly larger cost-of-living adjustments than the CPI-W. But an index tied to how older people generally spend their money does not always lead to a bigger cost-of-living adjustment. The CPI-E has [increased less than CPI-W in some years](https://crr.bc.edu/what-is-the-right-price-index-for-the-social-security-cola/). What\u2019s more, the CPI-E was not designed to set benefits and would need a larger survey sample before it could be used for that purpose. This article was originally published on Oct. 13, 2026. It has been updated with the latest cost-of-living adjustment for Social Security benefits. [Inflation](https://theconversation.com/topics/inflation-645) [Social security](https://theconversation.com/topics/social-security-1564) [Medicare](https://theconversation.com/topics/medicare-1897) [Safety net](https://theconversation.com/topics/safety-net-2185) [Older people](https://theconversation.com/topics/older-people-5646) [Cost of living](https://theconversation.com/topics/cost-of-living-6549) [Out of pocket costs](https://theconversation.com/topics/out-of-pocket-costs-29203) [Quick reads](https://theconversation.com/topics/quick-reads-37963) [US Bureau of Labor Statistics](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-bureau-of-labor-statistics-59788) [Consumer Price Index](https://theconversation.com/topics/consumer-price-index-91688) [Pankaj Agrrawal, University of Maine](https://theconversation.com/social-security-benefits-will-rise-3-6-in-2027-but-that-may-not-be-enough-to-offset-healthcare-costs-293688) Author [Pankaj Agrrawal](https://theconversation.com/profiles/pankaj-agrrawal-2769558) Professor of Finance, University of Maine Disclosure statement Pankaj Agrrawal does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Maine](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-maine-2120) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.thd4pnrmf](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.thd4pnrmf)", "content_hash": "0e4c11e2ad3b191c14940111bebfc1950702e5217a6891eb1ef3b68348892665", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:35Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "954", "title": "Is the world pricing the risk of a US diesel export\u00a0ban?", "url": "https://theconversation.com/is-the-world-pricing-the-risk-of-a-us-diesel-export-ban-293242", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/is-the-world-pricing-the-risk-of-a-us-diesel-export-ban-293242", "text": "One driver of higher U.S. diesel prices is a major drop in supply from Russia and the Middle East. [AP Photo/David Zalubowski)](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Gasoline%20Prices%20Colorado/56519097b55a490a814a1e9d2aa2df67/photo) Is the world pricing the risk of a US diesel export ban? Published: October 15, 2026 8:14am EDT https://theconversation.com/is-the-world-pricing-the-risk-of-a-us-diesel-export-ban-293242 https://theconversation.com/is-the-world-pricing-the-risk-of-a-us-diesel-export-ban-293242 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article A [spike in diesel prices](https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/energy-prices-midterm-voters-e360a372) is hammering broad sectors of the U.S. economy \u2013 especially agriculture and shipping \u2013 and becoming a flash point ahead of the November 2026 [midterm elections](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-09-23/diesel-price-spike-hits-farms-food-prices-battleground-states). This squeeze has prompted Republicans from hard-hit farm states to [call for a ban](https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/republicans-running-out-of-time-on-lowering-diesel-gas-prices-before-midterms-51b9d1fe) on U.S. diesel exports, with President Donald Trump reportedly [weighing the idea](https://www.wsj.com/business/energy-oil/trump-weighs-diesel-export-ban-as-fuel-prices-soar-6d1a3d2e) as well. He also [announced a deal](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/trump-big-announcement-coming-up-diesel-2026-10-09/) on Oct. 9 to supply Russian diesel to U.S. and global markets. Meanwhile, the G7 countries have agreed to release an additional 100 million barrels of diesel and crude oil from their [emergency reserves](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/europe-discussing-plan-diesel-stock-releases-after-us-pressure-source-says-2026-10-02/). What would happen if the U.S. did enact an export ban? It\u2019s a [net exporter](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=67184) of petroleum products, so such a move would split the U.S. and world markets for diesel. The resulting mismatch between domestic demand and supply could depress U.S. diesel prices in the short term. This would give some relief to American consumers, at least temporarily. But as a [professor of finance](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=U5EiP0cAAAAJ&hl=en) who studies commodities and market volatility, I also see a big adverse shock hitting the rest of the world under that scenario. The current political response to pricey diesel, however, shouldn\u2019t be a surprise. A recent paper of mine, co-authored with two other economists, asks more broadly when governments are more inclined to restrict commodity exports. We found that export bans are more likely when prices are high and [when politicians start weighing the preferences of commodity users](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jimonfin.2026.103552), like consumers, over those of commodity producers, like the oil industry. This could be what\u2019s happening right now, amid record energy prices and just a few weeks from Election Day. A global supply squeeze Diesel prices are hovering near all-time highs. The Energy Information Administration reports that [the average price](https://www.eia.gov/petroleum/gasdiesel/) of the U.S. benchmark, known as ultra-low sulfur diesel, has topped US$7 per gallon in some regions like the West Coast. Soaring energy prices are also continuing to push U.S. inflation up more broadly. Consumer prices in September jumped year-on-year by a [higher-than-expected 3.7%](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/cpi.pdf), with gas prices responsible for more than half of the most recent monthly increase. How did this happen? Before the conflict with Iran erupted in February 2026, key diesel exporters included Russia, Middle Eastern producers, the United States and China. Since then, those first two export sources [have withered](https://theconversation.com/trump-speculates-on-diesel-fuel-export-ban-as-high-prices-ripple-across-us-economy-292323). Russia, now in the fifth year of its war against Ukraine, [banned diesel exports](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/russia-extends-diesel-gasoline-export-bans-until-end-january-2027-2026-07-30/) in July 2026 after Ukraine launched an effective air campaign that has knocked out [more than half](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/ukraine-claims-its-strikes-have-taken-out-more-than-half-of-russias-oil-refining-capacity) of Russia\u2019s oil refinery capacity and destroyed many diesel storage-tank farms. That retaliatory campaign has pushed Russia from being a top diesel exporter to [importing refined oil products](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/south-korea-exports-eased-russia-fuel-crisis-caused-by-drone-strikes-ukraine-2026-10-06/), including diesel. The price of transporting oil has more than quintupled, as tanker owners charge record fees to travel through the Strait of Hormuz, hiking up the price of diesel. [Amirhosein Khorgooi/ISNA via AP](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Iran%20War%20Strait%20of%20Hormuz/9ad6b7af50c94709a88a38b78d36a591/photo) Meanwhile, in the Middle East, crude oil prices surged following missile attacks against Iran [at the end of February](https://www.oxfordeconomics.com/resource/a-conflict-driven-fuel-price-surge-is-raising-airfares-and-slowing-global-air-travel-demand/). The Strait of Hormuz, which was the [transit route](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=65504) for more than one-fifth of the global crude oil and natural gas output, essentially shut down amid fears over Iran targeting commercial ships sailing through. Since then, increased shipments through [Saudi and Emirati pipelines](https://www.iea.org/about/oil-security-and-emergency-response/strait-of-hormuz) and [actions by the U.S. Navy](https://www.wsj.com/world/middle-east/middle-east-oil-exports-rebound-as-irans-chokehold-on-hormuz-breaks-down-4ffde655) in the strait have helped restart petroleum flows. But the threat to Hormuz shipping remains real, as seen by recent strikes by [Iranian forces](https://understandingwar.org/research/middle-east/iran-update-october-9-2026/) on multiple tankers in early October. These shocks have changed the economics of petroleum shipments in two ways. First, the [price of transporting oil has more than quintupled](https://www.ft.com/content/5cad1f95-b06d-4ee1-b1c7-0244999337d4), as tanker owners charge record fees to travel through the strait. Those fees, of course, bleed into the price that diesel buyers pay. Second, in the strait, the content of tanker cargo has changed. Many ships are now loaded with [crude oil, rather than diesel](https://www.wsj.com/business/energy-oil/diesel-strait-of-hormuz-e1940e50), after Iranian strikes on Gulf countries earlier this year knocked out a substantial share of the Middle Eastern refining capacity. Insofar as there isn\u2019t much additional refinery capacity elsewhere in the world, even these restored crude oil flows can\u2019t bring diesel prices down to prewar levels. What an export ban would look like The effects of removing Russian and Middle Eastern diesel from the market are being felt worldwide. One result is that U.S. diesel exports to the rest of the world have surged due to heightened foreign demand for the product and the [high prices](https://www.ft.com/content/b06d00da-9df7-405b-9410-b5485c267397?syn-25a6b1a6=1) it can fetch abroad. Those export flows have been accompanied by the diesel price spike at home. That jump in domestic prices is what\u2019s driving discussions of a possible ban on U.S. diesel exports, and it reflects a dynamic that [my co-authors and I laid out](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jimonfin.2026.103552) in our study of commodity export restrictions and bans. While we focused on agricultural markets, our findings also apply to the current diesel crunch. Governments of commodity-exporting countries typically balance the interests of commodity producers and exporters, who prefer high prices, against those of domestic consumers, who like lower prices. When consumers\u2019 wishes matter relatively more \u2013 for example, as elections approach \u2013 the likelihood of a ban rises. American consumers never like high energy prices. Nowadays, after years of elevated inflation, they seem especially unhappy about them. Until the Iran war, Trump had been [able to tout](https://www.energy.gov/state-american-energy-promises-made-promises-kept) a [substantial fall](https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/05/business/2026-gas-prices-oil) in petroleum product prices after he returned to office in January 2025. That\u2019s no longer the case. Higher diesel prices are [being felt across the economy](https://theconversation.com/soaring-gas-prices-and-disrupted-supply-chains-will-ripple-out-to-increase-costs-in-every-store-and-sector-of-the-economy-278349). The fuel is used to heat homes, especially in the Northeast, and to power machinery, tractors, trucks, boats and trains. This is why farmers and transportation operators are especially hard hit. Furthermore, these businesses typically try to pass their cost increases on to their customers. So shoppers are shelling out more for groceries and any other goods that must be transported. Under the scenario of an export ban, the cost of U.S. diesel might fall in the short run, but world prices would spike. Soaring prices, however, would not be the only issue for the rest of the world. Our research shows that a major exporting country\u2019s ban would also [increase market uncertainty and volatility](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jimonfin.2026.103552). This is because restricting exports would effectively concentrate the rest of the world\u2019s production, making the remaining supply base more fragile and increasing the rest of the world\u2019s exposure to any subsequent output shocks. If other countries think that their diesel situation is already bad, it would be much worse under a U.S. export ban. And the G7 move to release more diesel into the global market indicates that the rest of the world might understand how U.S. politics could lead to that outcome. [Transport](https://theconversation.com/topics/transport-39) [Inflation](https://theconversation.com/topics/inflation-645) [Energy](https://theconversation.com/topics/energy-662) [Sanctions](https://theconversation.com/topics/sanctions-4538) [Diesel](https://theconversation.com/topics/diesel-5351) [Energy prices](https://theconversation.com/topics/energy-prices-7957) [G7](https://theconversation.com/topics/g7-17736) [Russia-Ukraine](https://theconversation.com/topics/russia-ukraine-72684) [Strait of Hormuz](https://theconversation.com/topics/strait-of-hormuz-73874) [US-Iran conflict](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-iran-conflict-73960) [US agriculture](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-agriculture-95098) [US trade policy](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-trade-policy-97249) [fuel prices](https://theconversation.com/topics/fuel-prices-126003) [Trump 2.0](https://theconversation.com/topics/trump-2-0-169566) [2026 midterm elections](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-midterm-elections-178375) [Michel Robe, University of Richmond](https://theconversation.com/is-the-world-pricing-the-risk-of-a-us-diesel-export-ban-293242) Author [Michel Robe](https://theconversation.com/profiles/michel-robe-2768419) Professor of Finance and Distinguished Chair, University of Richmond Disclosure statement Michel Robe does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Richmond](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-richmond-766) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.fytamg5qr](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.fytamg5qr)", "content_hash": "5467203eaa7d335b7cb38d0bf6c4c7fcc5050bbe3a047057daf6f358cd3423cf", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:36Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "955", "title": "Why the GOP\u2019s voter fraud messaging may\u00a0backfire", "url": "https://theconversation.com/why-the-gops-voter-fraud-messaging-may-backfire-292106", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/why-the-gops-voter-fraud-messaging-may-backfire-292106", "text": "A sticker on a lamppost warns of voter fraud on Nov. 7, 2022, in Anchorage, Alaska. [Spencer Platt/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/sticker-on-a-lamp-post-warns-of-voter-fraud-on-november-07-news-photo/1244593156?phrase=voter%20fraud&adppopup=true) Why the GOP\u2019s voter fraud messaging may backfire Published: October 15, 2026 8:14am EDT https://theconversation.com/why-the-gops-voter-fraud-messaging-may-backfire-292106 https://theconversation.com/why-the-gops-voter-fraud-messaging-may-backfire-292106 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article With Election Day 2026 just weeks away and control of Congress hanging in the balance, the results will almost certainly hinge on voter turnout, [just as they did](https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/feature/how-changes-in-turnout-and-vote-choice-powered-trumps-victory-in-2024/) in 2024. Given that genuinely undecided voters are scarce \u2014 [as little as 5%](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/16-undecided-voters-are-weighing-2026-election-options-rcna599899) to [7% of the electorate](https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/6105250-voters-america-wrong-direction-midterms-poll/), according to major polls \u2014 both Democrats and Republicans must look to mobilize their rank-and-file supporters. To do so, the Republican Party has bet strongly this year on [a strategy of convincing people](https://revealnews.org/podcast/voter-fraud-christian-adams-elections-rights/) that [voter fraud is rampant](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/07/us/politics/trump-mail-voting-elections-fact-check.html). [As a scholar of U.S. politics](https://polisci.richmond.edu/faculty/cmiller3/), I see this as a high-risk strategy for Republican candidates and American democracy. That\u2019s because reduced trust in election integrity can [reduce motivation to vote](https://statesunited.org/resources/when-americans-trust-elections/) \u2013 even if based on flawed information. But it\u2019s not at all clear which Americans are most likely to stay home in November. Voter fraud vs. reality [Voter fraud is not a real problem](https://www.americanbar.org/groups/crsj/resources/human-rights/2026-september/debunking-voter-fraud-myth-again/) in the United States. Study after study, by [public officials](https://www.senatoredmcbroom.com/senate-oversight-committee-releases-report-on-november-2020-general-election/) and [think tanks](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/analysis-heritage-foundations-database-undermines-claims-recent-voter), has shown that [voting by noncitizens and other ineligible voters](https://www.cato.org/testimony/restoring-election-integrity-oversight-voter-fraud-new-jersey) happens only at minuscule, statistically insignificant levels. Nonetheless, [rhetoric from the Trump administration](https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/americans-increased-belief-voter-fraud-180029721.html) about voter fraud has been pervasive. Consider the recent claim by Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin that [Nevada had nearly 16,000 noncitizens](https://www.dhs.gov/news/2026/07/17/dhs-secretary-markwayne-mullin-sends-letters-secretaries-state-warning-about-non) on its voter rolls \u2013 a number reduced to 185 when state authorities pressed for details, and [reduced to zero](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/24/us/politics/nevada-voters-homeland-security-trump.html) when the state investigated the names provided. There is comparable behavior from Congress. [My own research](https://doi.org/10.1080/27671127.2025.2478863) on political communication has shown that a substantial number of members of Congress, strongly skewing Republican, make frequent use of rhetoric that undermines democratic norms and delegitimizes political institutions. The strategy ties readily into President Donald Trump\u2019s [persistent false claims that he won the 2020 presidential election](https://www.propublica.org/article/big-lie-trump-stolen-election-inside-creation). It also ties into his [administration\u2019s strongly anti-immigrant agenda](https://www.whitehouse.gov/releases/2026/02/americans-overwhelmingly-support-deporting-criminal-illegals-local-cooperation-with-ice/), which spreads fear of [noncitizens corrupting U.S. politics](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/1-in-3-fears-immigrants-influence-us-elections-ap-norc-poll). How does this uncertain political environment influence voters? There is [clear data showing](https://yankelovichcenter.ucsd.edu/public-engagement/CTTE-Report-2025-2026_2.17.pdf) that voter confidence in elections has fallen across party lines: Only 65% of Republicans, 64% of Democrats and 57% of independent voters are very or somewhat confident that the 2026 vote count will be reliable. [Conventional wisdom suggests](https://statesunited.org/resources/when-americans-trust-elections/) such discouraging numbers would depress turnout. It\u2019s not as simple as that, however. A \u2018no voter fraud\u2019 sign is displayed in support of President Donald Trump at the Maricopa County Elections Department office on Nov. 4, 2020, in Phoenix. [Courtney Pedroza/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/no-voter-fraud-sign-is-displayed-by-a-protester-in-support-news-photo/1229466917?phrase=voter%20fraud&adppopup=true) What motivates voters Extensive research confirms the axiom that a sense of civic duty is the [strongest predictor of whether someone will vote](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11109-018-9459-3). It supersedes trust, partisanship and policy preferences. Any campaign\u2019s quest to mobilize additional voters, therefore, must find them among citizens without a strong sense of civic duty. But among these people, diminished trust in democratic processes can play out in counterintuitive ways. [Recent research on German voters](https://doi.org/10.1057/s41269-026-00442-9) is relevant to the U.S.: It shows that among more passive voters \u2013 those following cues from political elites \u2013 reduced trust in elections can depress motivation to vote. That\u2019s because there\u2019s no trustworthy result that voters are seeking to affirm. Conversely, among voters with greater skepticism about elite messaging, reduced trust can motivate increased turnout as an attempted corrective for a flawed system. What does this mean for potential voters in the U.S.? Among Republicans, it appears that the party\u2019s strategy carries a strong risk of backlash. [Findings from MIT\u2019s Election Lab](https://electionlab.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-10/voter-trust.pdf) confirm that cues from political elites are a powerful factor in voter behavior. Specifically, cues setting expectations of a disappointing or untrustworthy result will reduce motivation. Why vote if you don\u2019t expect it to help achieve the desired result? The same study finds Republican voters broadly inclined to believe GOP leaders\u2019 cues about voter fraud and to frame their expectations accordingly. And once they\u2019ve made that shift, they [cling firmly to the belief](https://electionlab.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-10/voter-trust.pdf), even in the face of contrary empirical evidence or messaging from within the party. In the current election cycle, 50% of [Republicans surveyed](https://yankelovichcenter.ucsd.edu/public-engagement/CTTE-Report-2025-2026_2.17.pdf) distrust mail-in voting, and 51% fear voting by noncitizens. They expect election results to be fraudulent. This likely won\u2019t mobilize them, as there\u2019s no positive result they can expect to affirm. The Republican Party\u2019s messaging has painted its voters into a corner. Democrats, meanwhile, are less inclined to believe claims of voter fraud. But they are [more inclined to be concerned](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/10/us/politics/department-of-justice-election-fraud-states.html) about attempts to undermine the midterms by political elites making such claims. Examples include the Trump administration\u2019s efforts to sow doubt about [voting by mail](https://theconversation.com/why-the-supreme-court-blocked-trump-and-the-postal-service-from-restricting-mail-in-voting-for-the-midterms-291319) and about alleged [noncitizen voting](https://www.npr.org/2026/09/25/nx-s1-5976804/supreme-court-trump-save-noncitizen-voting). These efforts increase motivation to vote among Democrats. Unlike GOP voters, they see the capacity to correct for the system\u2019s vulnerabilities \u2013 the potential and urgency to overwhelm attempts at voter suppression through higher turnout. An election integrity officer, in red, trains poll watchers at the Orange County Registrar of Voters in Santa Ana, Calif., on Nov. 1, 2022. Television monitors display what each employee is looking at on their desk for observers to watch. [Paul Bersebach/MediaNews Group/Orange County Register via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/karen-sewell-oc-gop-election-integrity-officer-in-red-news-photo/1439039405?phrase=election%20integrity&adppopup=true) Truth and trust Opponents of Trump and the GOP may find these diverging effects encouraging. No matter the results this November, however, we\u2019re left with a precarious dynamic. Increasing numbers of voters lack trust in elections and make their decisions to vote \u2013 or not \u2013 based on limited or flawed information. Fortunately, there are signs of hope. Although voters in general [are poorly informed](https://electionlab.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-10/voter-trust.pdf) about how elections are conducted, they can and do respond [to clear information](https://theconversation.com/election-officials-are-fighting-wavering-voter-trust-by-making-mundane-processes-visible-and-recruiting-citizens-to-help-292199) familiarizing them with election processes. I\u2019ve seen this myself over the years, working with nonpartisan efforts to register young adults and train them as poll watchers. And in the current election cycle, there have already been [widespread efforts](https://protectdemocracy.org/work/frequently-asked-questions-about-the-2026-midterm-elections/) to [broaden public awareness](https://karshinstitute.virginia.edu/news/emergency-powers-and-elections-whats-stake-2026) about the fact that state officials, not federal ones, [have authority over voter rolls](https://statedemocracy.law.wisc.edu/our-work/can-the-federal-government-force-states-to-hand-over-citizens-voter-information) and election security. Meanwhile, [fewer Americans are expected to vote by mail this year](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/voters-trust-local-election-officials-theyre-less-sure-about-the-mail) \u2013 whether due to fear of fraud or [fear of the Post Office](https://www.lwv.org/newsroom/news-clips/postmaster-generals-remarks-mail-ballots-stoke-fears-among-voting-rights). If they instead vote in person, a silver lining is that positive [personal experiences with poll workers](https://doi.org/10.1177/1065912908324870) are also associated with increased voter trust. Looking beyond November, what Americans can hope for is a cycle in which clear election outcomes provide an opportunity to inform voters of how and why the system works. That\u2019s the way to motivate more political engagement and voter turnout in the future. [US politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-politics-1015) [Republicans](https://theconversation.com/topics/republicans-1524) [Democrats](https://theconversation.com/topics/democrats-1525) [US Congress](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-congress-7016) [Trump administration](https://theconversation.com/topics/trump-administration-33294) [Voter fraud](https://theconversation.com/topics/voter-fraud-35372) [Election polls](https://theconversation.com/topics/election-polls-79425) [Poll workers](https://theconversation.com/topics/poll-workers-92962) [Voting on Election Day](https://theconversation.com/topics/voting-on-election-day-128841) [US midterms](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-midterms-129216) [US voters](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-voters-163461) [Noncitizens](https://theconversation.com/topics/noncitizens-173208) [Christopher Miller, University of Richmond](https://theconversation.com/why-the-gops-voter-fraud-messaging-may-backfire-292106) Author [Christopher Miller](https://theconversation.com/profiles/christopher-miller-1541575) Visiting Assistant Professor of Philosophy, Politics, Economics, and Law (PPEL), University of Richmond Disclosure statement Christopher Miller does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Richmond](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-richmond-766) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.qtcc6vury](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.qtcc6vury)", "content_hash": "7b4477c54c677f68140305b7fd413f676583b1fd698f6f28aae9576a49660593", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:37Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "956", "title": "Ketamine\u2019s role in alleged Cornell rape highlights flood of psychoactive drugs on\u00a0campus", "url": "https://theconversation.com/ketamines-role-in-alleged-cornell-rape-highlights-flood-of-psychoactive-drugs-on-campus-293512", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/ketamines-role-in-alleged-cornell-rape-highlights-flood-of-psychoactive-drugs-on-campus-293512", "text": "Ketamine and many other psychoactive substances can\u2019t be detected by most standard drug screens, so it\u2019s unclear how often they play a role in sexual assault. [D-Keine/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/ketamine-bottle-with-warning-label-royalty-free-image/2251150920) Ketamine\u2019s role in alleged Cornell rape highlights flood of psychoactive drugs on campus Published: October 15, 2026 8:14am EDT https://theconversation.com/ketamines-role-in-alleged-cornell-rape-highlights-flood-of-psychoactive-drugs-on-campus-293512 https://theconversation.com/ketamines-role-in-alleged-cornell-rape-highlights-flood-of-psychoactive-drugs-on-campus-293512 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Former Cornell student Jane Doe, who [filed a civil lawsuit in September 2026](https://www.cornellsun.com/article/2026/09/tccsuykuvb4z) alleging that seven fraternity members raped her at a party, said the young men who assaulted her pressured her into taking the drug ketamine. As an [emergency room physician, medical toxicologist](https://med.virginia.edu/emergency-medicine/faculty/full-time-faculty/christopher-p-holstege-md/) and the [head of student health services](https://studentaffairs.virginia.edu/staff/christopher-holstege) at the University of Virginia, I have had a front-row seat to the growing danger that psychoactive substances such as ketamine present to the health and safety of university students. [Ketamine and its emerging spin-off versions](https://med.virginia.edu/toxicology/wp-content/uploads/sites/268/2026/03/Mar26-Ketamine-analogues.pdf) are part of a [wave of new and evolving designer drugs](https://www.unodc.org/unodc/secured/wdr/wdr2013/World_Drug_Report_2013.pdf) entering the United States and other countries. How widely these substances are used remains unknown, since most hospitals are [unable to detect them](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.emc.2022.01.003) on standard urine drug screens. But [ketamine and other drugs](https://www.justice.gov/archive/ndic/pubs8/8872/index.htm#commission) are being increasingly reported as [agents used in sexual assault](https://doi.org/10.28933/ijprr-2021-07-1505) \u2013 either when people take these drugs voluntarily, are pressured to take them or are given them covertly. The increasing prevalence of ketamine and other drugs \u2013 and their use in sexual assault \u2013 is [playing out on college campuses across the U.S.](https://doi.org/10.1176/appi.focus.20180037) and putting students at risk. A growing supply Ketamine is a synthetic chemical that was [first synthesized in 1962](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/health-and-medicine/ketamine/). The U.S. Food and Drug Administration approved it as a [general anesthetic for people and animals](https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2016.00612) in 1970, and doctors and veterinarians used it for decades to induce anesthesia during surgeries and to relieve pain. People have [used ketamine recreationally](https://doi.org/10.1176/appi.books.9798894551944.lg03) since the 1970s. Getting high on it became more popular in the U.S. in the 1980s, when it was called \u201cSpecial K.\u201d It was frequently found in the club drug scenes and was associated with raves into the 1990s. Today, ketamine is sold on the street under a [variety of slang names](https://www.justice.gov/archive/ndic/pubs8/8872/8872p.pdf), such as K, Ket, and Vitamin K. The prevalence of ketamine and other psychoactive drugs in the U.S., including on college campuses, has spiked since the early 2010s. [Jeremy Bernfeld/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/outdoor-college-party-royalty-free-image/1751137399) In 2019, [the FDA approved a nasal spray version of ketamine](https://doi.org/10.1017/S2045796019000751), called esketamine, to treat depression that does not respond to other treatments. That drug\u2019s use is tightly regulated, but clinicians are increasingly prescribing ketamine \u201coff-label\u201d for a variety of psychiatric conditions, including [post-traumatic stress disorder](https://theconversation.com/ketamine-can-rapidly-reduce-symptoms-of-ptsd-and-depression-new-study-finds-216077). Cases of poisoning caused by ketamine in the U.S. [doubled between 2019 and 2023](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.drugalcdep.2025.112549), according to an analysis of calls to poison control centers nationwide, and are now the highest they\u2019ve ever been. This surge is in part due to the more widespread medical use of ketamine, which makes accessing it much easier. But [street versions of the drug](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2023.1423) are also prevalent as chemists in basement laboratories create novel ketamine analogs. Ketamine\u2019s effects on the body Ketamine\u2019s effects vary depending on the dose and the person. It can cause someone to dissociate, creating an altered state of consciousness, changing what\u2019s perceived through the senses and causing a sense of detachment from one\u2019s self and one\u2019s surroundings. The drug can also [cause coma at high doses](https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/illicit-drugs-and-intoxicants/ketamine-and-phencyclidine). Someone who orally consumes ketamine would typically begin to [feel its effects within 20 minutes](https://doi.org/10.1080/20961790.2017.1285219). If they inhale or inject it, the effects are almost immediate. The primary effects generally wear off in less than an hour, but a person\u2019s senses may remain dulled and their judgment and coordination impaired for several hours afterward. Ketamine can cause a slew of other side effects, including delirium, amnesia, increased salivation, abnormal eye movements, high blood pressure and potentially fatal breathing problems. Alcohol and other drugs can [interact with ketamine to worsen these effects](https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms23147800) significantly. Ketamine is generally sold illegally as either a colorless, odorless liquid or a white or off-white powder. Either form can be easily and covertly placed into an unsuspecting person\u2019s beverage or food. Ketamine can be snorted, and it can be smoked or vaped on products such as marijuana. Ketamine and other drugs in sexual assault Research is extremely spotty on how often a perpetrator of rape or sexual assault [uses a chemical agent](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.emc.2007.02.008) \u2013 whether ketamine, alcohol or another substance \u2013 to alter a victim\u2019s senses. Research suggests that most often both parties [use mind-altering substances voluntarily](https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/108860), although the effects of these substances can alter a person\u2019s ability to grant consent. Even so, there are numerous reports of perpetrators spiking a victim\u2019s drink or otherwise slipping a drug to them without their knowledge. According to one study, [4.2% of rape victims were drugged covertly](https://www.ojp.gov/library/publications/estimate-incidence-drug-facilitated-sexual-assault-us-final-report), though how often this actually happens is undoubtedly underreported. That\u2019s because it\u2019s very hard to detect drugs taken this way. Most of the substances used to facilitate sexual assaults are rapidly absorbed and metabolized by the body, rendering them difficult to detect in routine urine and blood drug screenings. Another issue is that many days may have elapsed between the victim ingesting the drugs and their subsequent treatment at a medical facility. Ketamine\u2019s increasing use in the clinic has helped fuel its popularity as a party drug. Victims of rape or sexual assault are often reluctant to report incidents because of a sense of embarrassment, guilt or perceived responsibility, or because they may know their attacker. Some may [not trust that their institution or local law enforcement](https://theconversation.com/handling-of-rape-allegations-at-cornell-shows-why-so-many-assaults-go-unreported-victims-dont-trust-the-institutions-that-are-supposed-to-help-them-293907) will support them. In my experience, the involvement of ketamine or other drugs invariably makes a bad situation worse \u2013 complicating consent, impairing judgment and reducing inhibitions. [Ketamine causes confusion and drowsiness](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.emc.2007.02.008), and its sedative, hypnotic and memory-erasing properties can diminish a survivor\u2019s recollection of events surrounding the sexual assault. The availability of these drugs in the U.S., including on college campuses, has risen sharply \u2013 particularly since the early 2010s, as a slew of new substances have emerged and [international drug control efforts have not kept up](https://doi.org/10.1111/bcp.14236). Without more active education and prevention efforts, as well as better detection methods for these drugs, students will continue to pay the price. [Depression](https://theconversation.com/topics/depression-669) [Rape](https://theconversation.com/topics/rape-1027) [Drugs](https://theconversation.com/topics/drugs-1588) [Sexual assault](https://theconversation.com/topics/sexual-assault-4845) [Vitamin K](https://theconversation.com/topics/vitamin-k-6886) [Ketamine](https://theconversation.com/topics/ketamine-8311) [College campuses](https://theconversation.com/topics/college-campuses-16771) [New psychoactive substances](https://theconversation.com/topics/new-psychoactive-substances-20378) [TC global health](https://theconversation.com/topics/tc-global-health-189737) [Cornell 7](https://theconversation.com/topics/cornell-7-194133) [Christopher P. Holstege, University of Virginia](https://theconversation.com/ketamines-role-in-alleged-cornell-rape-highlights-flood-of-psychoactive-drugs-on-campus-293512) Author [Christopher P. Holstege](https://theconversation.com/profiles/christopher-p-holstege-731398) Professor of Emergency Medicine and Pediatrics, University of Virginia Disclosure statement Christopher P. Holstege does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Virginia](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-virginia-752) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.6hx4nmnca](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.6hx4nmnca)", "content_hash": "d08421e3b7e1706510c0045e4cf5403f8b8f764d9a8d946237c1a225c02882f0", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:39Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "957", "title": "We blasted mobile homes with hurricane\u2011force winds to test how they fail \u2013 and found clues for making affordable housing\u00a0safer", "url": "https://theconversation.com/we-blasted-mobile-homes-with-hurricane-force-winds-to-test-how-they-fail-and-found-clues-for-making-affordable-housing-safer-292074", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/we-blasted-mobile-homes-with-hurricane-force-winds-to-test-how-they-fail-and-found-clues-for-making-affordable-housing-safer-292074", "text": "Hurricane-force winds from the Wall of Wind test facility rolled mobile homes, showing where their anchoring systems fail. [Margi Rentis/Florida International University](https://news.fiu.edu/2026/fius-wall-of-wind-tests-manufactured-home-across-various-u.s-installation-practices) We blasted mobile homes with hurricane\u2011force winds to test how they fail \u2013 and found clues for making affordable housing safer Published: October 9, 2026 8:06am EDT https://theconversation.com/we-blasted-mobile-homes-with-hurricane-force-winds-to-test-how-they-fail-and-found-clues-for-making-affordable-housing-safer-292074 https://theconversation.com/we-blasted-mobile-homes-with-hurricane-force-winds-to-test-how-they-fail-and-found-clues-for-making-affordable-housing-safer-292074 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article It\u2019s 9:15 in the morning. The wind is roaring at 110 miles per hour (177 kph); the sound is deafening. Dust swirls through the air. We are watching a single-wide manufactured home rock back and forth, as it is pounded by turbulent wind. Then, in an instant, the screws shear off and the entire unit tumbles backward, rolling two full revolutions before it stops. In any other situation, this would be terrifying. But here, we are conducting a [first-of-its-kind experiment](https://news.fiu.edu/2026/fius-wall-of-wind-tests-manufactured-home-across-various-u.s-installation-practices) at Florida International University\u2019s [Wall of Wind](https://theconversation.com/30-years-after-hurricane-andrew-devastated-florida-researchers-are-using-a-wall-of-wind-to-design-safer-homes-but-storms-are-getting-even-more-intense-183510), a test facility that can reproduce wind speeds the equivalent of a Category 5 hurricane. We are testing a full-scale manufactured home to measure the effects of hurricane-force winds and identify strategies that can improve the safety of all mobile homes as disaster risk rises. We want disaster-resilient and affordable housing \u2013 not just one, but both. How a mobile home rolled in high winds during testing in FIU\u2019s Wall of Wind. As [structural](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=PPvrXaEAAAAJ&hl=en) and [wind engineering](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=puvjTZUAAAAJ&hl=en) researchers, we have been working on this problem for a decade. Our [recent Wall of Wind experiment](https://news.fiu.edu/2026/fius-wall-of-wind-tests-manufactured-home-across-various-u.s-installation-practices) was the first time the federal design requirements for manufactured homes had ever been put to the test in a controlled environment with sensors and videography to capture the effects. The results of our work show that manufactured housing can be built safely. For that to happen, state and federal building codes need a serious update. Finding the weaknesses, and solutions To back up for a moment, one thing you should know is that the design and construction of manufactured homes are regulated by a [specific federal building code](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-24/subtitle-B/chapter-XX/part-3280) with wind engineering standards from 1988. The antiquated federal code lacks any guidance for building these homes to withstand earthquakes, wildfires or tornadoes. It also created differences in construction costs and performance between manufactured housing and site-built housing, which is regulated by a [different building code system](https://www.nist.gov/buildings-construction/understanding-building-codes) that is updated every three years. The cost differences are critical and what keeps manufactured homes affordable. The performance differences can be deadly. Over [22 million people live in manufactured homes](https://www.manufacturedhomes.com/blog/manufactured-housing-share-vs-site-built/) in the U.S., as they are the [most common type of unsubsidized affordable housing](https://www.urban.org/research/publication/building-climate-resilient-manufactured-housing-stock). Our research is investigating ways to enhance the performance of manufactured homes without compromising affordability. Hurricane Irma\u2019s winds pushed mobile homes off their pad at the Enchanted Shores manufactured home park in Naples, Fla., in September 2017. [Nicholas Kamm/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/an-overturned-trailer-is-seen-at-the-enchanted-shores-news-photo/846035164?adppopup=true) By integrating structural engineering laboratory experiments, post-disaster field investigations and community engagement, we are finding new solutions to increase building strength, reduce loads and optimize the use of construction materials. When we started, there was a dearth of research on factory-built housing and no clear public documentation on how these structures are constructed. So, we began examining [how mobile and manufactured](http://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)CF.1943-5509.0001486) homes [failed during disasters](http://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)NH.1527-6996.0000476). Through testing, we observed failures in how the homes were [anchored to the ground](http://doi.org/10.1061/JPCFEV.CFENG-5309) and in how the [roof connects to the walls](http://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)CF.1943-5509.0001486). At the University of Kansas, we have now performed hundreds of experiments on [load path connections](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engstruct.2026.123383) \u2013 measuring the stress on the connections between roof sheathing and rafters, roofs and walls, and walls and floors. We have also been testing new designs using thicker straps, screws and different hardware to enhance their strength. Changing a federal building code that has remained [stagnant for 32 years](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-24/subtitle-B/chapter-XX/part-3280) requires more than lab work. We also talk with residents, home and installation system manufacturers, installers, advocacy organizations, federal and state agencies and fellow researchers to learn from their perspectives what is and isn\u2019t working. We have worked with the Federal Emergency Management Agency to update its approach to using mobile and manufactured homes as post-disaster temporary housing so that when disaster victims need housing, they can continue to receive it quickly, with assurance it was installed following best practices. And we\u2019re translating our research into templates that will make it easier for state and local governments to adopt best practices for local installation requirements. Inside the Wall of Wind In spring 2026, we [tested three manufactured homes](https://news.fiu.edu/2026/fius-wall-of-wind-tests-manufactured-home-across-various-u.s-installation-practices) provided by FEMA. We examined seven installation systems commonly used in regions prone to hurricanes, floods or earthquakes. These tests are unique because the manufactured homes were installed by local experts in soil matching real-world conditions and tested at full scale against hurricane-force winds. A full-scale mobile home being tested at the Wall of Wind in June 2026. Margi Rentis/Florida International University We collected high-speed video imagery and tracked the movement of the manufactured home and stresses on its materials through time. The tests enabled us to document damage as it progressed, learning what fails first, second, and so forth that then ends with the unit rolling over. This damage progression is something that cannot be observed after the disaster or through testing small-scale models in wind tunnels. Our preliminary results reveal that installation practices allowed by some states do not meet the federal code minimum requirements, and that many commonly used installation systems in the U.S. do not meet modern wind engineering standards. There is good news too. The most robust installation systems performed effectively. Manufactured home residents can talk to their installers about meeting [Wind Zone III requirements](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-24/subtitle-B/chapter-XX/part-3285#subpart-E) \u2013 which may exceed the legal minimum requirement for where the home is going. Engineers Elaina Sutley and Arindam Chowdhury examine the anchoring system of a mobile home after a wind test. [Margi Rentis/Florida International University](https://news.fiu.edu/2026/fius-wall-of-wind-tests-manufactured-home-across-various-u.s-installation-practices) Our team also designed a wind load reduction device that attaches to the gutter system along the roof line. It reduces wind pressures on the roof, helping the roof shingles remain attached and intact under harsh wind conditions. And we collaborated with an industry team to test a new anchoring device that optimizes the use of the construction materials. The results demonstrate that manufactured homes can be designed and built to be both affordable and resilient to high winds. In fact, some already are; it just isn\u2019t the norm. The mobile homes we tested were built specifically for FEMA at three times the strength required by the federal building code. When installed with a robust anchoring system, the homes we tested performed well even at high wind speeds. Safe, affordable housing in the future The state of housing in the U.S. is on an unsustainable path, with [rising costs](https://www.census.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2025/acs-1-year-estimates.html), inadequate construction quality and [frequent extreme weather events](https://theconversation.com/two-key-ingredients-cause-extreme-storms-with-destructive-flooding-why-these-downpours-are-happening-more-often-254123). Transforming the way manufactured homes are built and installed can save lives and reduce losses for millions of people, while making prefabricated housing a valuable solution to the affordable housing crisis. Our results show it can be done. [Safety](https://theconversation.com/topics/safety-189) [Housing](https://theconversation.com/topics/housing-376) [Engineering](https://theconversation.com/topics/engineering-824) [Construction](https://theconversation.com/topics/construction-2700) [Affordable housing](https://theconversation.com/topics/affordable-housing-3413) [Extreme weather](https://theconversation.com/topics/extreme-weather-3799) [Hurricanes](https://theconversation.com/topics/hurricanes-9045) [Florida](https://theconversation.com/topics/florida-15445) [Tornado](https://theconversation.com/topics/tornado-22606) [Wind](https://theconversation.com/topics/wind-41574) [Local](https://theconversation.com/topics/local-56577) [Building standards](https://theconversation.com/topics/building-standards-83539) [Mobile homes](https://theconversation.com/topics/mobile-homes-98538) [Elaina Sutley, University of Kansas, Arindam Chowdhury, Florida International University](https://theconversation.com/we-blasted-mobile-homes-with-hurricane-force-winds-to-test-how-they-fail-and-found-clues-for-making-affordable-housing-safer-292074) Authors [Elaina Sutley](https://theconversation.com/profiles/elaina-sutley-2765144) Associate Professor of Engineering, University of Kansas [Arindam Chowdhury](https://theconversation.com/profiles/arindam-chowdhury-1347245) Professor of Civil Engineering, Florida International University Disclosure statement Elaina Sutley receives funding from the National Science Foundation, the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development, the State of Florida Division of Emergency Management, the State of California Office of Emergency Services, and the Applied Technology Council for her research on mobile and manufactured housing. Arindam Chowdhury receives funding from the National Science Foundation, DEM, FSG, and other agencies. He is affiliated with the American Association for Wind Engineering and other organizations. He is an associate editor for Natural Hazards Review journal. Partners [Florida International University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/florida-international-university-729) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.6tajugjxh](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.6tajugjxh)", "content_hash": "6bbaa6b46baddbf4165b14d324faad9cc6282ae7728262a00015b2ef23af918d", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:40Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "958", "title": "Yemen\u2019s Houthis are bringing their war to Saudi Arabia \u2013 and reframing the narrative of being an Iranian\u00a0proxy", "url": "https://theconversation.com/yemens-houthis-are-bringing-their-war-to-saudi-arabia-and-reframing-the-narrative-of-being-an-iranian-proxy-294208", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/yemens-houthis-are-bringing-their-war-to-saudi-arabia-and-reframing-the-narrative-of-being-an-iranian-proxy-294208", "text": "Supporters of Yemen\u2019s Houthis brandish their weapons during a protest against Saudi intervention in Yemen on Oct. 8, 2026. [Mohammed Huwais/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/supporters-of-the-houthi-authorities-brandish-their-weapons-news-photo/2298772403?phrase=houthi&adppopup=true) Yemen\u2019s Houthis are bringing their war to Saudi Arabia \u2013 and reframing the narrative of being an Iranian proxy Published: October 14, 2026 1:57pm EDT https://theconversation.com/yemens-houthis-are-bringing-their-war-to-saudi-arabia-and-reframing-the-narrative-of-being-an-iranian-proxy-294208 https://theconversation.com/yemens-houthis-are-bringing-their-war-to-saudi-arabia-and-reframing-the-narrative-of-being-an-iranian-proxy-294208 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article A [deadly Houthi attack](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/10/world/middleeast/riyadh-airport-attack-energy-congress.html) on Riyadh\u2019s international airport has highlighted how the Yemeni group is reframing its perceived \u201cIran proxy\u201d role and shifting its regional focus to its ongoing battle against Saudi Arabia. The Oct. 10 strike, in which 13 people were killed and hundreds injured, was the most violent episode on Saudi Arabian soil since the beginning of the war in Iran in February. It was followed by [further \u2013 albeit](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-13/saudi-arabia-intercepts-new-houthi-attacks-as-conflict-drags-on?cmpid=101326_morningamer&utm_campaign=morningamer&utm_medium=email&utm_source=newsletter&utm_term=261013&utm_content=10016) [less deadly](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/yemens-houthis-renew-warning-over-saudi-airports-after-deadly-riyadh-attack-2026-10-11/) \u2013 [missile barrages](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/13/world/middleeast/saudi-arabia-yemen-houthis-aramco-threat.html) in the days since. The violence, moreover, comes just as the [kingdom is hosting a major conference](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/13/business/saudi-arabia-houthi-oil.html) for oil producers. As a longtime [analyst of Yemeni politics](https://www.sciencespo.fr/ceri/en/directory/bonnefoy-laurent/), I see the latest attacks as another illustration of both the expanding regional stalemate across the Middle East and the need to understand how Yemen\u2019s decade-long war is having effects far beyond its territory. Houthi narratives The [Houthi movement](https://theconversation.com/who-are-yemens-houthis-106423) first emerged as a militarized political actor in Yemen in 2004 and has been locked in a multiyear civil war against the internationally recognized, Saudi-backed central government. Currently, the Houthis control about a third of the territory and two-thirds of the population. They are undoubtedly Yemen\u2019s most powerful armed force, despite lacking access to warplanes, and have developed an arsenal of long-range missiles and drones. All along, the Houthis have [frequently been portrayed](https://www.bloomsbury.com/uk/sectarianism-desectarianization-and-regional-politics-in-the-middle-east-9780755639212/) by their adversaries and many external observers as a proxy for Iran, the chief regional rival to Saudi Arabia. It\u2019s true that the movement has [long been broadly aligned with Tehran](https://academic.oup.com/ia/article-abstract/92/3/647/2326947) and frequently shared regional objections, [even if the relationship is complex and varied](https://sanaacenter.org/the-yemen-review/april-june-2026/28082). At the same time, the evolution of the Houthi movement remains intimately connected to Yemen\u2019s [domestic political context](https://hurstpublishers.com/book/tribes-and-politics-in-yemen/) and to a historical feud specific to the country \u2013 [something I and many other scholars](https://hurstpublishers.com/book/yemen-and-the-world/) have researched. A plume of smoke rises during a reported airstrike by the Saudi-led coalition on the Houthi-controlled Yemeni capital of Sanaa on Oct. 9, 2026. [AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/plume-of-smoke-rises-during-a-reported-airstrike-by-the-news-photo/2298776531?phrase=houthi&adppopup=true) What is particularly significant in the current context of an escalating fight with Saudi Arabia, however, is the way in which the Houthi leadership has sought to reframe its military engagement over the course of 2026 so that it does not appear to be directed by Iran. This shift is analytically important because it reveals how Houthi leaders [understand the political effectiveness](https://www.bloomsbury.com/uk/huthi-movement-in-yemen-9780755644285/) of particular narratives and how they seek to legitimize their actions. A belated return to war When the United States and Israel launched their attack on Iran on Feb. 28, many observers expected the Houthis to enter the conflict swiftly. Their previous [intervention in the Red Sea](https://orientxxi.info/the-houthis-in-yemen-gain-the-world-s-attention,6988) \u2013 between November 2023 and May 2025 \u2013 in support of Palestinians after Israel\u2019s war in Gaza had, to some extent, provided a blueprint for [disrupting maritime traffic](https://atlasinstitute.org/the-red-sea-shipping-crisis-2024-2025-houthi-attacks-and-global-trade-disruption/) through the Strait of Hormuz. The symbolic returns of their earlier campaign had been considerable. On the one hand, it demonstrated the Houthis\u2019 willingness to act in support of Gaza at a time when many Arab governments were [widely perceived as turning their backs](https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2026/6/29/why-the-arab-league-could-not-stop-israels-genocide) on the plight of Palestinians. It also revealed the movement\u2019s capacity to disrupt global supply chains and affect international trade far beyond Yemen. But the Houthis remained on the sidelines for several months after the initial U.S.-Israeli strikes on Iran. Their leaders appeared to disregard reports that Iranian decision-makers [had called on them](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/iran-tells-houthis-close-red-sea-gateway-if-us-hits-power-network-sources-say-2026-07-16/), in July 2026, to close the Strait of Bab el-Mandeb to maritime traffic. Assuming that the Houthis would automatically act at Tehran\u2019s command reflected a misreading of Yemeni history and of the identity markers that shape the movement\u2019s political legitimacy. Acting in support of Palestinians could be framed for a domestic and regional audience as an expression of Arab solidarity. But acting on behalf of Iran, a non-Arab Persian country composed almost entirely of [Twelver Shia Muslims](https://theconversation.com/twelver-shiism-a-branch-of-islam-that-serves-both-as-a-spiritual-and-political-force-in-iran-and-beyond-259853), was considerably more difficult to rationalize in Arab Yemen where Twelver Shia Muslims are absent and Sunnis are the majority. The Houthis\u2019 reentry into the regional conflict in August took place alongside a shift in their political narrative, which increasingly centered on their long-standing adversary, Saudi Arabia. Although the strategic consequences of their actions may ultimately benefit Iran in its own regional and international conflicts, the Houthis see their military campaign against Saudi Arabia chiefly as an act of national defense. An earlier airport bombing During the summer of 2026, an important episode went largely unnoticed in the Western media: the Houthis\u2019 attempt to [establish the reopening](https://sanaacenter.org/publications/perspectives-and-analyses/27981) of Sanaa International Airport through the surprise departure of a Tehran-bound flight and its subsequent return. The airport, located in the Houthi-controlled capital, had been closed since late 2024 following [Israeli strikes](https://www.newarab.com/news/israeli-strikes-shut-down-sanaa-airport-cause-500m-damages). Its continued closure meant that access to and exit from Houthi-controlled territory remained subject to restrictions imposed by the movement\u2019s adversaries. These included the authorities controlling Aden, the interim capital of the internationally recognized government. An airport official walks past the wreckage of a military transport aircraft destroyed by Saudi-led airstrikes at Sanaa\u2019s airport in 2015. [AP Photo/Hani Mohammed](https://newsroom.ap.org/home/search?query=saudi%20bombing%20yemen%202015&mediaType=photo) From the Houthis\u2019 perspective, their sovereignty consequently remained incomplete. The airport\u2019s closure not only restricted civilian movement but also limited their ability to send supporters abroad for training and potentially to receive military equipment. On July 13, the Saudi military\u2019s [subsequent bombing](https://www.reutersconnect.com/item/moment-iranian-plane-lands-in-yemens-hudaydah-after-strike-on-sanaa-airport-2/dGFnOnJldXRlcnMuY29tLDIwMjY6bmV3c21sX09XQU5BQ0FBVklERU8yMDI2MDcxMzQxOTY1NDY4) of the Sanaa runway, as the incoming aircraft from Tehran approached, provided the movement an opportunity to reignite its confrontation with Riyadh. After more than three years of a de facto ceasefire and difficult negotiations, Riyadh appeared, as in its dealings with Iran, [increasingly eager to extricate itself](https://www.crisisgroup.org/cmt/middle-east-north-africa/yemen-saudi-arabia/yemens-war-returning) from the region\u2019s protracted conflicts. Sensing an opening, the Houthi leadership responded by invoking a logic of tit-for-tat retaliation. The Houthis\u2019 tactics today are a kind of mirror to the [2015 launch of a Saudi-led bombing campaign](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-29319423) and blockade of the Houthi-controlled parts of Yemen that has led to years of protracted civil war. The Houthis now claim the right to impose reciprocal restrictions on Saudi Arabia, particularly by targeting its ports and oil-exporting infrastructure. Saudi\u2019s Houthi blowback The Houthis\u2019 new tactic serves two purposes. First, it allows the Houthis to [redirect responsibility](https://sanaacenter.org/publications/perspectives-and-analyses/28309) for the economic and humanitarian crisis in and around Sanaa toward the Saudi-enforced blockade rather than their own governance failures, mismanagement and corruption. Second, it provides a rationale for escalating the conflict across the region. Previously, the Houthi military leadership had [halted attacks](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/11/11/yemens-houthis-appear-to-pull-back-from-red-sea-shipping-attacks) on Israeli-linked shipping in the Red Sea following the formal Gaza ceasefire of October 2025, despite having repeatedly come under bombardment. It had likewise suspended attacks on American vessels following an [agreement with the Trump administration](https://www.reuters.com/world/trump-says-us-will-stop-bombing-houthis-after-agreement-struck-2025-05-06/) in May 2025. These decisions demonstrated a [degree of pragmatism](https://theconversation.com/america-is-the-mother-of-terrorism-why-the-houthis-new-slogan-is-important-for-understanding-the-middle-east-222865) beneath the movement\u2019s inflammatory rhetoric. That pragmatism appeared to persist even as the Houthis resumed their regional military engagement, with American diplomats reportedly [meeting Houthi representatives in Oman](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/us-officials-met-iran-backed-houthis-oman-over-weekend-sources-say-2026-09-16/) in September 2026. With Saudi Arabia, however, the Houthis felt less compunction. Riyadh was already facing repeated barrages of Iran missiles as a consequences of its alliance with the U.S. and facing an energy export squeeze. So with the Saudi airport incident, the Houthi leadership feels it [has trapped Saudi Arabia](https://theconversation.com/rapid-houthi-advances-in-yemen-pose-massive-strategic-risks-for-saudi-arabia-in-the-gulf-292070), with consequences extending to the global economy. This retaliatory strategy was bolstered by [successful Houthi moves](https://sanaacenter.org/publications/perspectives-and-analyses/28317) in early September to establish a degree of control over the Strait of Bab el-Mandeb, one of the world\u2019s most important maritime choke points. That gave the Houthis leverage over shipping crucial for Saudi Arabia in the Red Sea. Drones and ballistic missiles, as the attacks on the Riyadh airport illustrate, also give the movement the [capacity to strike targets](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/11/why-are-houthi-missiles-breaking-through-saudi-arabias-air-defences) across much of Saudi Arabia. The targeting of civilian infrastructure in Saudi Arabia, particularly airports, illustrates the retaliatory logic the Houthis have invoked to justify their actions. It\u2019s far from clear what the consequences of this increasingly aggressive Houthi campaign will be. The region seems poised for ongoing strife, and it\u2019s an open question whether it will draw back in the likes of the U.S., which has been reluctant to return to full-scale regional conflict. Yet none of that should obscure the plight of civilians in Yemen. Rather, it should serve as a reminder of the suffering of a population exhausted by a war that has lasted for more than a decade and has devastated the already meager infrastructure of [what was the poorest country](https://saqibooks.com/books/saqi/yemen-in-crisis/) in the Middle East even before the conflict started. [Oil](https://theconversation.com/topics/oil-23) [Diplomacy](https://theconversation.com/topics/diplomacy-79) [Politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/politics-245) [Foreign policy](https://theconversation.com/topics/foreign-policy-266) [Middle East](https://theconversation.com/topics/middle-east-361) [Energy](https://theconversation.com/topics/energy-662) [War](https://theconversation.com/topics/war-830) [Saudi Arabia](https://theconversation.com/topics/saudi-arabia-1591) [Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-1870) [Yemen](https://theconversation.com/topics/yemen-6632) [Houthis](https://theconversation.com/topics/houthis-63976) [US-Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-iran-70837) [Strait of Hormuz](https://theconversation.com/topics/strait-of-hormuz-73874) [Middle East politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/middle-east-politics-136981) [Bab el-Mandeb Strait](https://theconversation.com/topics/bab-el-mandeb-strait-151287) [Red Sea shipping](https://theconversation.com/topics/red-sea-shipping-151288) [Red Sea politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/red-sea-politics-153898) [Middle East tensions](https://theconversation.com/topics/middle-east-tensions-156327) [War in Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/war-in-iran-186317) [Strait of Hormuz blockade](https://theconversation.com/topics/strait-of-hormuz-blockade-187096) [Laurent Bonnefoy, Sciences Po](https://theconversation.com/yemens-houthis-are-bringing-their-war-to-saudi-arabia-and-reframing-the-narrative-of-being-an-iranian-proxy-294208) Author [Laurent Bonnefoy](https://theconversation.com/profiles/laurent-bonnefoy-2771004) CNRS research fellow, Center for International Studies, Sciences Po Disclosure statement Laurent Bonnefoy does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Sciences Po](https://theconversation.com/institutions/sciences-po-2196) provides funding as a member of The Conversation FR. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ehpcxqxp6](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ehpcxqxp6)", "content_hash": "0a4a77da575ae88832348ce705f53660fdc5724f971c75475c1be2e52642bc6e", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:41Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "959", "title": "Cystic fibrosis patients got their \u2018miracle drug\u2019 thanks to federal research funding \u2013 new Trump administration rules may cut future miracles\u00a0short", "url": "https://theconversation.com/cystic-fibrosis-patients-got-their-miracle-drug-thanks-to-federal-research-funding-new-trump-administration-rules-may-cut-future-miracles-short-293934", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/cystic-fibrosis-patients-got-their-miracle-drug-thanks-to-federal-research-funding-new-trump-administration-rules-may-cut-future-miracles-short-293934", "text": "Prior to effective treatments, it was difficult for children with cystic fibrosis to live full lives. [Chris Carlson/AP Photo](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Surf%20Therapy/b0988aeeab63430aaa4958bbef12c123/photo) Cystic fibrosis patients got their \u2018miracle drug\u2019 thanks to federal research funding \u2013 new Trump administration rules may cut future miracles short Published: October 14, 2026 3:50pm EDT https://theconversation.com/cystic-fibrosis-patients-got-their-miracle-drug-thanks-to-federal-research-funding-new-trump-administration-rules-may-cut-future-miracles-short-293934 https://theconversation.com/cystic-fibrosis-patients-got-their-miracle-drug-thanks-to-federal-research-funding-new-trump-administration-rules-may-cut-future-miracles-short-293934 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The hardest disease to care for when I was training [as a pediatrician](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=TLARkTcAAAAJ&hl=en) in the late 1970s was [cystic fibrosis](https://www.childrenshospital.org/conditions-treatments/cystic-fibrosis), a disease in which abnormally thick mucus secretions block the lungs\u2019 airways and make it progressively harder to breathe. Back then, there was no [treatment for the disease](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0012-3692(16)62421-2) itself. All we had was physical therapy to clear mucus from the lungs, and drugs to dilate the airways and aid breathing. We used high doses of antibiotics to treat chronic pneumonia \u2013 drugs that also generated dangerous antibiotic-resistant infections that not only made the disease progressively harder to treat but also posed a threat to other people. At the same time, we also had to manage co-occurring conditions, including diabetes, malnutrition and heart failure. Worse, these efforts did not work. In 1979 the [median age of death from cystic fibrosis was 15](https://doi.org/10.1001/archpedi.1996.02170360071012). While all young deaths are tragic, caring for children with a progressive, inevitably fatal disease that denied emerging adolescents the promise of adulthood was especially heartbreaking. Cystic fibrosis treatment changed dramatically after 2012, when the biotech company Vertex Pharmaceuticals launched the drug [Kalydeco (ivacaftor)](https://www.cff.org/node/1086), followed by additional cystic fibrosis drugs [Orkambi (lumacaftor/ivacaftor)](https://www.cff.org/press-releases/2015-07/cf-foundation-celebrates-fda-approval-orkambi-important-advance-cf-community) in 2015, [Symdeko (tezacaftor/ivacaftor)](https://www.cff.org/node/941) in 2018, [Trikafta (elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ivacaftor)](https://www.cff.org/press-releases/2019-10/cystic-fibrosis-foundation-statement-fda-approval-trikafta-first-triple) in 2019 and [Alyftrek (vanzacaftor/tezacaftor/deutivacaftor)](https://www.cff.org/news/2026-04/fda-approves-modulator-expansion-rare-cf-mutations) in 2024. Cystic fibrosis is a fatal disease without treatment. [Fernando Salazar/The Wichita Eagle via AP Photo](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Panorama%20Shadow%20Buddies/1cc484148f9b490fb08821f72f551362/photo) Now, the median survival age for a child with cystic fibrosis [born today in the United States is 66 years](https://www.cff.org/managing-cf/understanding-changes-life-expectancy). Mary Vought, the former wife of the director of the Office of Management and Budget, Russell Vought, stated that their daughter, who was diagnosed with cystic fibrosis, \u201ccan live likely a full life because of this [miracle drug](https://archive.li/7qAua),\u201d and that \u201cit is very, very, very hopeful.\u201d Cystic fibrosis treatments are not miracles; they required decades of research funded by a combination of public and private investments. The largest contributor to this work was the National Institutes of Health, which funded much of the pivotal research required to [understand the genetics](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.2475911) and [biology of the disease](https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM198112173052502). Only with this knowledge was it possible to begin searching for drugs to treat cystic fibrosis. Ironically, the federal funding that was so critical to developing these drugs is now [threatened by policies](https://theconversation.com/new-trump-administration-rules-on-federal-science-funding-could-make-american-research-slower-costlier-and-harder-to-share-286430) that were initially proposed by the Office of Management and Budget. Those policies, which would increase political control over federal research grants, limit peer review and curtail research on certain topics, were [temporarily blocked by Congress](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.zcu2wsm). But [several federal agencies](https://www.aau.edu/newsroom/despite-congressional-block-agencies-seek-to-implement-omb-led-grantmaking-changes) are still pursing [portions of this agenda](https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-02584-4), [including the NIH](https://grants.nih.gov/grants/guide/notice-files/NOT-OD-26-088.html). NIH laid the foundation for treatment My colleagues and I examined the role that federal funding from the National Institutes of Health has played in developing cystic fibrosis treatments. We found that NIH funding supported [more than 6,200 published research papers](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2023.0511) on the biology of cystic fibrosis in the 27 years before Kalydeco was approved in 2012. Some of these publications describe celebrated milestones in understanding the biology of CFTR \u2013 the gene that causes cystic fibrosis \u2013 or the disease process. Others describe the studies necessary to replicate, refute or refine these advances and ensure the necessary scientific rigor to [successfully discover and develop new drugs](https://doi.org/10.36687/inetwp133). Decades of scientific research laid the foundation for cystic fibrosis drugs. [Gregory Bull/AP Photo](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Rare%20Diseases%20Drugs/6045408abf264f1ba432f5ac06f5d7b7/photo) Overall, we identified [US$3.2 billion in NIH funding](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2023.0511) for basic research on cystic fibrosis from 1985 to 2012 and approximately $940 million in funding for applied research on potential drugs. This funding complemented at least [$200 million in investments](https://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3376673) from the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation for the discovery or development of these drugs as well as undisclosed spending by Vertex to develop and commercialize these products. The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation [acknowledged the essential role of the NIH](https://www.cff.org/press-releases/2025-02/response-to-nih-announcement-limit-funding) in a 2025 letter to Russell Vought and other Trump administration leaders, writing that \u201censuring that the National Institutes of Health (NIH) is fully functional is critical for furthering our knowledge of the biology of CF and advancing CF therapeutic development.\u201d What will it take to produce future miracles? Developing groundbreaking drugs requires a long-term, sustained commitment from the public, from the government and from industry. FDA-approved drugs are typically based on [20 to 30 years of basic research](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0177371) in the lab and [eight to nine years](https://doi.org/10.1038/d41573-021-00190-9) of human testing in the clinic. This work is supported, at least in part, by an average of [$1.4 billion in NIH funding](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2023.0511) and as much as a reported [$1.4 billion in industry funding](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhealeco.2016.01.012) per drug. Russell Vought and his family have benefited from the commitment that scientists, advocates, legislators and industry leaders have placed into developing treatments that now offer patients with cystic fibrosis a normal life. A similar commitment can help find cures for patients who don\u2019t respond to available drugs and to provide cures for cardiovascular disease, cancer, dementia and other conditions. This commitment to research is currently under threat from Trump administration proposals that would reduce NIH funding, research, training and global research collaboration. Protecting or expanding federal funding for basic research instead can help lead to innovative therapies for countless patients. [Research funding](https://theconversation.com/topics/research-funding-238) [Drug development](https://theconversation.com/topics/drug-development-1083) [Drugs](https://theconversation.com/topics/drugs-1588) [Respiratory disease](https://theconversation.com/topics/respiratory-disease-4531) [Cystic fibrosis](https://theconversation.com/topics/cystic-fibrosis-7438) [Basic research](https://theconversation.com/topics/basic-research-15729) [Office of Management and Budget](https://theconversation.com/topics/office-of-management-and-budget-36659) [National Institutes of Health (NIH)](https://theconversation.com/topics/national-institutes-of-health-nih-37219) [Federal research funding](https://theconversation.com/topics/federal-research-funding-41120) [Health research funding](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-research-funding-97090) [Fred D. Ledley, Bentley University](https://theconversation.com/cystic-fibrosis-patients-got-their-miracle-drug-thanks-to-federal-research-funding-new-trump-administration-rules-may-cut-future-miracles-short-293934) Author [Fred D. Ledley](https://theconversation.com/profiles/fred-d-ledley-1503077) Director, Center for Integration of Science and Industry, Bentley University Disclosure statement Fred D. Ledley has received research funding for related research from the National Biomedical Research Foundation, Institute for New Economic Thinking, West Health, and the National Pharmaceutical Council. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.cvpjde6nn](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.cvpjde6nn)", "content_hash": "9e197df95326f0dda919974c306d00cd99d7f5e2e887dd22405525642fb3075d", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:42Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "960", "title": "In newly independent US, Americans debated whether public executions taught a lesson or just showed off the state\u2019s power to\u00a0destroy", "url": "https://theconversation.com/in-newly-independent-us-americans-debated-whether-public-executions-taught-a-lesson-or-just-showed-off-the-states-power-to-destroy-294211", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/in-newly-independent-us-americans-debated-whether-public-executions-taught-a-lesson-or-just-showed-off-the-states-power-to-destroy-294211", "text": "A 1747 print of a public execution at the so-called Tyburn Tree, a gallows in London that served for hundreds of years as a site of hangings. [William Hogarth](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/399844) In newly independent US, Americans debated whether public executions taught a lesson or just showed off the state\u2019s power to destroy Published: October 14, 2026 8:23am EDT \u2022Updated: October 15, 2026 11:41am EDT https://theconversation.com/in-newly-independent-us-americans-debated-whether-public-executions-taught-a-lesson-or-just-showed-off-the-states-power-to-destroy-294211 https://theconversation.com/in-newly-independent-us-americans-debated-whether-public-executions-taught-a-lesson-or-just-showed-off-the-states-power-to-destroy-294211 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The proposed [livestream of Nidal Hasan\u2019s execution](https://www.cnn.com/2026/10/08/politics/pete-hegseth-vows-to-make-first-firing-squad-execution-in-decades-public) by a U.S. military firing squad gave an old question a new audience. Hasan, an Army psychiatrist convicted by a military court of killing 13 people at Fort Hood in Texas in 2009, is scheduled to be executed on Dec. 3, 2026. Explaining the plan to make his death public, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth said that \u201cpeople need to understand \u2026 [there\u2019s serious consequences for these types of things](https://edition.cnn.com/2026/10/09/politics/fort-hood-execution-hegseth-livestream).\u201d But on Oct. 15, 2026, President Donald Trump announced in a social media post that [Hasan\u2019s execution would not be livestreamed](https://www.politico.com/news/2026/10/15/trump-military-execution-livestream-canceled-01120035), after the plan drew [widespread public objections](https://www.politico.com/news/2026/10/09/hegseth-public-execution-backlash-01113728) from both Republicans and Democrats. In his Truth Social [post canceling the livestream](https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/117445309638266855), Trump wrote \u201cI think streaming the execution for all to see might have been a strong deterrent for the World to witness how America deals with Terrorists and SCUM like Hasan.\u201d And [as a historian of the early American republic](https://www.gilderlehrman.org/history-resources/videos/maurizio-valsania-first-among-men-george-washington-and-myth-american), I recognize that familiar expectation: that watching someone die will teach a useful lesson. Americans at the republic\u2019s founding debated that premise \u2013 and doubted what the lesson might be. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth said the execution of Nidal Hasan would be a public event; President Trump nixed the plan. The power of government On Aug. 17, 1797, New Yorkers gathered to watch John Young hang. An English musician and music publisher struggling with debt, [Young had shot and killed Robert Barwick](https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=coo.31924109046239&seq=1), a deputy sheriff taking him into custody. Barwick left a widow and nine children. [Young\u2019s conviction by a jury](https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/evans/N24300.0001.001/?view=toc) brought him to a scaffold surrounded by soldiers, before an immense crowd. [Governor John Jay](https://www.nga.org/governor/john-jay/), a leading Federalist who placed greater faith in firm government than in popular judgment, had authorized the military presence after the sheriff reported fears of a rescue. But Jay also wanted the execution to impress its witnesses with the power of the government carrying it out. Writing to [James Kent](https://history.nycourts.gov/figure/james-kent/), New York City\u2019s chief municipal legal officer, two days before the hanging, Jay stressed that the authority of law should prevail visibly. The occasion should be \u201cparticularly solemn and impressive,\u201d he said. The \u201c[authority of the law should be seen to bear down (on) all opposition](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511844997).\u201d Solemnity, however, proved difficult to arrange. The military escort played lively music as the procession made its way to the scaffold. One newspaper critic thought an uninformed passerby might mistake the spectacle for \u201c[the celebration of some Festival](https://infoweb-newsbank-com.tjportal.idm.oclc.org/apps/readex/doc?p=EANX&t=pubname%3A10D345D01D2428C8%21New-York%2BGazette/year%3A1797%211797/mody%3A0818%21August%2B18&year=1797&docref=image/v2%3A10D345D01D2428C8%40EANX-1197270C5729F268%402377631-1197270C79D1FC98%401&origin=image/v2%3A10D345D01D2428C8%40EANX-1197270C5729F268%402377631-1197270C71D89AD8%400).\u201d Soldiers formed a square around the gallows. The crowd remained orderly. Young addressed a few words to the minister, sang a psalm \u201c[and with great composure pulled his cap over his face](https://infoweb-newsbank-com.tjportal.idm.oclc.org/apps/readex/doc?p=EANX&t=pubname%3A109215B64EAFB460%21Greenleaf%2527s%2BNew%2BYork%2BJournal/year%3A1797%211797/mody%3A0819%210819&sort=YMD_date%3AA&fld-base-0=alltext&val-base-0=young&docref=image/v2%3A109215B64EAFB460%40EANX-1095B8A2EA913398%402377632-1095B8A604F62058%402-1095B8A8360C3268%40Friday%252C%2BAugust.%2B18&firsthit=yes),\u201d reported one newspaper. Then, before the assembled witnesses, he was hanged. Loss of government\u2019s dignity Whatever the crowd\u2019s reaction that day, the public execution had already become [an argument about the nature of government](https://global.oup.com/academic/product/rites-of-execution-9780195066630?cc=ch&lang=en&). The display did not impress everyone as evidence of governmental strength. Writing in the \u201cGreenleaf\u2019s New York Journal\u201d two weeks after the hanging, \u201cA CITIZEN\u201d accused Jay of \u201cletting down the dignity of civil authority.\u201d Calling out troops for the execution suggested, among other things, \u201c[a wantonness on the part of the Executive](https://infoweb-newsbank-com.tjportal.idm.oclc.org/apps/readex/doc?p=EANX&t=pubname%3A109215B64EAFB460%21Greenleaf%2527s%2BNew%2BYork%2BJournal/year%3A1797%211797/mody%3A0830%210830&sort=YMD_date%3AA&fld-base-0=alltext&val-base-0=executive&val-database-0=&fld-database-0=database&fld-nav-0=YMD_date&val-nav-0=&docref=image/v2%3A109215B64EAFB460%40EANX-1095B8BF32F07B48%402377643-1095B8C1EB7B7070%403-1095B8C3EFC92B68%40For%2Bthe%2BArgus.%2BTo%2BJohn%2BJay%252C%2BGovernor%2Bof%2Bthe%2BState%2Bof%2BNew-York&firsthit=yes).\u201d To this critic, the military presence advertised executive excess. The spectacle could undermine the very authority it was meant to affirm. That point mattered in a republic still deciding how its authority should differ from that of the European monarchies that Americans portrayed as corrupt. In the 1780s and 1790s, American reformers took particular aim at England\u2019s \u201c[sanguinary laws](https://msupress.org/9781611865370/executing-democracy/)\u201d \u2013 statutes prescribing death for offenses ranging from murder to relatively minor theft. By 1776, nearly 200 offenses carried a possible death sentence in England. These laws had accumulated piecemeal over generations, but American critics portrayed them as a single system with bloodshed at its center. England\u2019s punishments became a measure of what republican justice must overcome. Independence required Americans to reconsider how readily, and under what circumstances, [their own governments could take a life](https://www.hup.harvard.edu/books/9780674010833). The contrast was partly an exercise in national self-definition. Americans cast English severity as evidence of [an old, corrupt political order](https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/7313/to-begin-the-world-anew-by-bernard-bailyn/) and imagined their own laws as rational, proportionate and suited to free citizens. In practice, the distinction was harder to sustain. Nevertheless, the aspiration entered constitutional language. New Hampshire\u2019s 1784 Constitution declared a multitude of sanguinary laws \u201cboth impolitic and unjust.\u201d Punishment\u2019s proper object was \u201c[to reform, not to exterminate mankind](https://law.justia.com/constitution/new-hampshire/billofrights.html).\u201d Reform did not necessarily mean abolition. In 1796, New York reduced the number of crimes punishable by death but [retained capital punishment for murder](https://www.nycourts.gov/courts/ad4/library/pdfs/deathpenalty.pdf). Young went to the gallows the following year. Even if Jay favored milder criminal laws, he was able to see a carefully staged execution as a necessary assertion of public authority. No guarantee of lesson learned Other Americans pressed further. [Benjamin Rush](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Benjamin-Rush), the Philadelphia physician and signer of the Declaration of Independence, opposed executing even murderers. His argument went to the heart of the relationship between government and the governed. \u201cCapital punishments are the natural offspring of monarchical governments,\u201d [Rush wrote in 1792](https://press-pubs.uchicago.edu/founders/documents/amendVIIIs16.html). \u201cKings consider their subjects as their property.\u201d For Rush, a republic that put citizens to death still acted like a monarchy, treating citizens\u2019 lives as its own to dispose of. Calling the killing a lawful punishment did not make it less an act of revenge. A related debate concerned the audience. Even Americans who accepted capital punishment could doubt the wisdom of making it public. In 1787, Rush had already questioned what spectators actually learned from watching others suffer. Sympathy for the condemned could turn them against the government: \u201cWe secretly condemn the law which inflicts the punishment \u2013 [hence arises a want of respect for laws in general](https://collections.nlm.nih.gov/bookviewer?PID=nlm:nlmuid-2569014R-bk),\u201d he wrote in a pamphlet titled \u201cAn Enquiry into the Effects of Public Punishments upon Criminals and upon Society.\u201d Alternatively, repeated exposure to cruelty could harden spectators, encouraging the very violence that punishment was supposed to deter. \u201cHow often,\u201d Rush asked, \u201cdo we find pockets picked under a gallows, and [highway-robberies committed within sight of a gibbet](https://collections.nlm.nih.gov/bookviewer?PID=nlm:nlmuid-2569014R-bk)?\u201d The crowd could pity the condemned, admire his composure or simply enjoy an outing. Gathering witnesses did not guarantee that they absorbed the intended lesson. The complaint about festive music at Young\u2019s execution exposed that uncertainty: Officials could stage the spectacle, but they could not control its meaning. In this publication, Benjamin Rush asked whether \u2018we secretly condemn the law which inflicts the punishment.\u2019 [NIH](https://collections.nlm.nih.gov/bookviewer?PID=nlm:nlmuid-2569014R-bk) What harm does this prevent? Young\u2019s execution gives the proposed broadcast of Hasan\u2019s death a particular resonance. The crimes, jurisdictions and audiences differ, yet the ambition remains recognizable: to make one person\u2019s punishment teach everyone else a lesson \u2013 and demonstrate the government\u2019s strength. Cruelty concerns more than the pain inflicted. It also lies in [treating a person as fodder for exposure](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17438721241313176), humiliation and public instruction. The condemned person\u2019s body becomes a public demonstration of whom the state protects \u2013 and whom it has the power to destroy. But spectators may come for the theater of death itself, drawn by fascination with what human beings can do to one another. What officials present as instruction can become the marketing of guilty pleasures. Cesare Beccaria, [an Italian criminologist and economist](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Cesare-Beccaria) whose 1764 essay \u201c[On Crimes and Punishments](https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/voltaire-an-essay-on-crimes-and-punishments)\u201d helped shape American reform debates, argued that punishment could not undo a crime, only prevent future harm. Suffering beyond that purpose was unnecessary cruelty. For Beccaria, an execution\u2019s fleeting spectacle deterred less effectively than lifelong penal servitude. Beccaria\u2019s alternative was harsh, but his principle constrained the state: The power to inflict suffering did not establish its necessity. At Young\u2019s scaffold, the state could end his life without controlling the meaning of his death. A broadcast would vastly enlarge the audience, but reach is no proof of usefulness, and curiosity supplies no justification. Beccaria\u2019s question thus remains: What future harm does this additional spectacle prevent? If it prevents none, what justifies it \u2013 and what does it reveal about the government that stages it? This story was updated on Oct. 15, 2026, to reflect the cancellation of the livestream of Hasan\u2019s execution. [Capital punishment](https://theconversation.com/topics/capital-punishment-15335) [firing squads](https://theconversation.com/topics/firing-squads-15904) [Early Republic](https://theconversation.com/topics/early-republic-84295) [Hanging](https://theconversation.com/topics/hanging-89563) [John Jay](https://theconversation.com/topics/john-jay-93035) [Benjamin Rush](https://theconversation.com/topics/benjamin-rush-107060) [Maurizio Valsania, Universit\u00e0 di Torino](https://theconversation.com/in-newly-independent-us-americans-debated-whether-public-executions-taught-a-lesson-or-just-showed-off-the-states-power-to-destroy-294211) Author [Maurizio Valsania](https://theconversation.com/profiles/maurizio-valsania-1098422) Professor of American History, Universit\u00e0 di Torino Disclosure statement Maurizio Valsania does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.dsggj3kuh](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.dsggj3kuh)", "content_hash": "6b8ba83f8e9a4f9630ab6fa88d5398d0fa3ebd9dddfa31c31c5c96388361c21a", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:44Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "961", "title": "Text with us and get our best\u00a0stories", "url": "https://theconversation.com/text-with-us-and-get-our-best-stories-222576", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/text-with-us-and-get-our-best-stories-222576", "text": "[Subscribe](https://theconversation.com/columns/rigor-and-flair-notes-from-the-conversation-us-602.atom) [Rigor & Flair: Notes from The Conversation U.S. News about what we\u2019re doing and where we\u2019re going](https://theconversation.com/columns/rigor-and-flair-notes-from-the-conversation-us-602) Text with us and get our best stories Published: February 15, 2024 3:52pm EST [Katrina Aman, The Conversation](https://theconversation.com/text-with-us-and-get-our-best-stories-222576) Author [Katrina Aman](https://theconversation.com/us/team#katrina-aman) Audience Engagement Editor, The Conversation Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ksqdxxpwd](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ksqdxxpwd) https://theconversation.com/text-with-us-and-get-our-best-stories-222576 https://theconversation.com/text-with-us-and-get-our-best-stories-222576 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Several times a week, we\u2019ll send you a brief description and link to one story from The Conversation that explains, inspires or sparks your curiosity. Sometimes we\u2019ll send you an article that helps you understand something complicated, like Supreme Court rulings or Iran\u2019s strategic goals. Sometimes it will be a researcher who has made progress in solving one of humanity\u2019s problems. And sometimes it will just be something fascinating and fun. We\u2019ll also include our weekly news quiz on Fridays. There\u2019s no charge, and you can opt out anytime. And if you prefer social media, you can now follow us on your favorite platforms, too: [BlueSky](https://bsky.app/profile/us.theconversation.com) [Threads](https://www.threads.net/@theconversationdotcom) [Instagram](https://www.instagram.com/theconversationdotcom/) [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/conversationus) [Mastodon](https://newsie.social/@TheConversationUS) [Facebook](https://facebook.com/theConversationUS) [X](https://twitter.com/ConversationUS) [TikTok](https://tiktok.com/@TheConversation) [The Conversation US](https://theconversation.com/topics/the-conversation-us-24225) [Previous post Published: October 24, 2023 TCUS senior editor Kalpana Jain explores Indigenous communities in Indonesia \u2212](https://theconversation.com/tcus-senior-editor-kalpana-jain-explores-indigenous-communities-in-indonesia-and-learns-about-their-struggles-to-reclaim-land-216257) [Next post Published: May 23, 2025 Here\u2019s how we figured the number of guns illegally trafficked from the US across the b](https://theconversation.com/heres-how-we-figured-the-number-of-guns-illegally-trafficked-from-the-us-across-the-border-to-mexico-255074)", "content_hash": "01222bf9fa518d73d30bac55764fbc770c08cb18962076c2c65d248d82f41faf", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:45Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "962", "title": "The death of a Russian plague researcher, explained by a\u00a0microbiologist", "url": "https://theconversation.com/the-death-of-a-russian-plague-researcher-explained-by-a-microbiologist-293675", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/the-death-of-a-russian-plague-researcher-explained-by-a-microbiologist-293675", "text": "[The Irkutsk Anti-Plague Research Institute of Siberia and Far East via AP](https://photos.aap.com.au/search/20261006180053517460) The death of a Russian plague researcher, explained by a microbiologist Published: October 6, 2026 2:41am EDT https://theconversation.com/the-death-of-a-russian-plague-researcher-explained-by-a-microbiologist-293675 https://theconversation.com/the-death-of-a-russian-plague-researcher-explained-by-a-microbiologist-293675 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Conflicting information about [the death of a lab worker](https://www.reuters.com/business/healthcare-pharmaceuticals/dozens-quarantined-siberia-after-plague-institute-lab-worker-dies-2026-10-05/) at a Russian plague research institute has fuelled fears about the possibility of pneumonic plague spreading. Nearly [200 people are in quarantine](https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2026-101046) in Siberia after 28-year-old Darya Shipilova fell ill at work and [later died on October 2](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/05/russia-lab-worker-possibly-dies-of-plague-siberia-quarantine-measures-irkutsk). The exact circumstances of her death are unknown. A senior Russian official [posted on social media](https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-06/russia-pneumonia-death-sparks-speculation-of-plague-lab-leak/107231250) last week that Shipilova \u201chad died from the plague\u201d \u2013 before editing the post to add \u201cpossibly\u201d. Russia\u2019s [health watchdog](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/05/russia-lab-worker-possibly-dies-of-plague-siberia-quarantine-measures-irkutsk) has since stated she died from \u201cpneumonia of unknown etiology\u201d. Local media reports [suggested](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/05/russia-lab-worker-possibly-dies-of-plague-siberia-quarantine-measures-irkutsk) she was infected following an accident at the lab where she worked, the Irkutsk Anti-Plague Research Institute of Siberia and the Far East. But these have not been confirmed. So, what is plague, and how does it spread? Should we be worried? The incident has prompted questions about what the lab was studying. What is plague? Are there different types? Plague is [caused](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/plague) by the bacterium Yersinia pestis. The bacteria generally live in ground-dwelling rodents such as rats and prairie dogs, but can also infect humans. The same Y. pestis bacteria can cause three different types of plague. Bubonic plague is spread from animals to humans by fleas, and causes swelling (called buboes) in the lymph nodes. Other [symptoms include](https://www.cdc.gov/plague/signs-symptoms/index.html) fever, headache, chills and weakness. Bubonic plague can kill within a few days if antibiotics are not used. Between [30\u201360% of people](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/plague) who contract this form of plague will die. Septicemic plague occurs if bubonic plague is left untreated, or it can occur directly if the disease enters the bloodstream. Septicemic plague causes bleeding into the organs; the name refers to the serious blood infection, septicemia, that this form of the disease can cause. Pneumonic plague \u2013 the suspected but unconfirmed cause of Shipilova\u2019s death in Siberia \u2013 can occur when bubonic plague spreads to the lungs. But pneumonic plague can also spread from person to person via tiny respiratory droplets. Pneumonic plague is the rarest but most [severe form](https://doi.org/10.1093/pastj/gtj001). The fatality rate is [up to 100%](https://theconversation.com/a-person-in-the-us-has-died-from-pneumonic-plague-its-not-just-a-disease-of-history-261088) if left untreated. In addition to the other symptoms, pneumonic plague also includes severe [pneumonia](https://www.healthdirect.gov.au/pneumonia) \u2013 an infection of the lungs, in this case caused by bacteria. Isn\u2019t plague a medieval sickness? Bubonic plague was responsible for the death of tens of millions of people in the western Mediterranean between 541\u2013750CE (the [Plague of Justinian](https://origins.osu.edu/connecting-history/covid-justinianic-plague-lessons)) and [killed](https://origins.osu.edu/connecting-history/covid-black-death-plague-lessons) up to half of Europe\u2019s population in the middle ages (the Black Death). But there have been outbreaks since medieval times. A [third pandemic](https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2018.2429) spanning the end of the nineteenth century and beginning of the twentieth [killed up to 12 million people](https://www.crassh.cam.ac.uk/research/projects-centres/visual-representations-of-the-third-plague-pandemic/). Plague is [still endemic](https://www.cdc.gov/plague/maps-statistics/index.html) to Central Asia, Africa, the Americas and [Siberia](https://www.reuters.com/business/healthcare-pharmaceuticals/dozens-quarantined-siberia-after-plague-institute-lab-worker-dies-2026-10-05/), where the Russian lab worker died last week. This means it is present at a steady, consistent level in these regions. Cases typically occur in [rural and agricultural areas](https://theconversation.com/a-person-in-the-us-has-died-from-pneumonic-plague-its-not-just-a-disease-of-history-261088) which makes a potential lab leak in an urban area unusual. Can it be treated? Yes. A 10\u201314-day course of antibiotics (either oral or intravenous) [treats the plague](https://www.cdc.gov/plague/hcp/clinical-care/index.html) \u2013 as long as they are taken soon after infection. However, since 1995, several antibiotic-resistant strains have been [detected in the wild](https://doi.org/10.24171/j.phrp.2021.0288). In the 1980s, Soviet Union scientists also engineered [resistant strains](https://www.popularmechanics.com/science/a74033610/russia-plague-lab-worker-dies/) \u2013 this history forms the basis of [hypothetical scenarios](https://theconversation.com/the-threat-of-biological-warfare-is-real-a-new-book-describes-the-potential-devastation-288934) involving biological warfare. However, there is no evidence the strain alleged to have killed Shipilova could not be treated by antibiotics or that the lab is researching biological weapons. Read more: [The threat of biological warfare is real \u2013 a new book describes the potential devastation](https://theconversation.com/the-threat-of-biological-warfare-is-real-a-new-book-describes-the-potential-devastation-288934) How worried should we be? There has only been one documented major outbreak of the [pneumonic plague](https://academic.oup.com/past/article-abstract/190/1/147/1450013), in the 1910s in China. We still don\u2019t know much about the case in Siberia. But at this stage, there\u2019s little evidence to cause concern about potential for an epidemic (which is localised) or a pandemic (which involves multiple countries). In a [statement on Monday](https://www.newsweek.com/russias-response-to-suspected-plague-outbreak-under-scrutiny-12523852), the World Health Organization (WHO) said it was aware of reports a laboratory worker in Irkutsk had \u201cdied of severe pneumonia\u201d but no cause of death had been officially confirmed. The WHO statement also said: \u201cBased on unofficial information available, the public health risk to the general population appears to be low. The risk assessment will be updated once more information is available.\u201d To assess this risk, access to transparent information about how Shipilova fell ill and died will be essential. [Public health](https://theconversation.com/topics/public-health-136) [Pandemic](https://theconversation.com/topics/pandemic-1134) [Russia](https://theconversation.com/topics/russia-1376) [Siberia](https://theconversation.com/topics/siberia-1606) [Bubonic plague](https://theconversation.com/topics/bubonic-plague-1652) [Health](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-4159) [Pneumonic plague](https://theconversation.com/topics/pneumonic-plague-11669) [Infection risk](https://theconversation.com/topics/infection-risk-87057) [Health in the news](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-in-the-news-156027) [Thomas Jeffries, Western Sydney University](https://theconversation.com/the-death-of-a-russian-plague-researcher-explained-by-a-microbiologist-293675) Author [Thomas Jeffries](https://theconversation.com/profiles/thomas-jeffries-1511629) Senior Lecturer in Microbiology, Western Sydney University Disclosure statement Thomas Jeffries does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Western Sydney University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/western-sydney-university-1092) provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) Languages [Fran\u00e7ais](https://theconversation.com/la-peste-a-t-elle-tue-une-chercheuse-russe-ce-que-lon-peut-dire-a-ce-stade-293846) [Portuguese](https://theconversation.com/a-morte-de-uma-pesquisadora-russa-especializada-em-peste-explicada-por-um-microbiologista-293918) English DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.jva9wd9te](https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.jva9wd9te)", "content_hash": "ce4abe2ec0e7137755cf39264f3f25379b520e750158bf3f62ff462ac9509922", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:46Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "963", "title": "Nicotine pouches, vaping or cigarettes: Which nicotine product is actually the least\u00a0harmful?", "url": "https://theconversation.com/nicotine-pouches-vaping-or-cigarettes-which-nicotine-product-is-actually-the-least-harmful-290604", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/nicotine-pouches-vaping-or-cigarettes-which-nicotine-product-is-actually-the-least-harmful-290604", "text": "Evidence is clear that nicotine is harmful, but the level of harm varies depending on mode of delivery and the person using it. [Irina Piskova/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/varied-snus-pouches-and-electronic-cigarettes-on-a-royalty-free-image/2275014184?phrase=vaping%20pouches%20cigarettes%20&searchscope=image%2Cfilm&adppopup=true) Nicotine pouches, vaping or cigarettes: Which nicotine product is actually the least harmful? Published: October 13, 2026 8:18am EDT https://theconversation.com/nicotine-pouches-vaping-or-cigarettes-which-nicotine-product-is-actually-the-least-harmful-290604 https://theconversation.com/nicotine-pouches-vaping-or-cigarettes-which-nicotine-product-is-actually-the-least-harmful-290604 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article \u201cDo you smoke cigarettes?\u201d I asked my patient succinctly in a recent clinic appointment. He matter-of-factly responded, \u201cNot anymore, I use Zyn pouches now.\u201d As with any patient, I gauged his readiness \u2013 or reluctance, in some instances \u2013 to disrupt his use of nicotine. I asked about his interest in trying nicotine replacement therapy, a medical [treatment that provides low, controlled amounts of nicotine](https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/want-quit-smoking-fda-approved-and-fda-cleared-cessation-products-can-help). Initially, his response caught me by surprise. \u201cAren\u2019t I already using nicotine replacement products?\u201d he asked astutely. I gathered my thoughts and explained that while nicotine pouches are indeed a form of nicotine replacement, there are important caveats. Actual nicotine replacement therapy products are regulated by the [Food and Drug Administration](https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/want-quit-smoking-fda-approved-and-fda-cleared-cessation-products-can-help) to help people quit smoking. In contrast, I explained that Zyn and other nicotine pouches are [commercial tobacco products](https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco/nicotine-pouches/) that are regulated by the FDA but are not FDA-approved smoking cessation medications. My patient was surprised, because he had not realized there was a difference, which is a common misunderstanding. This encounter proved pivotal in the direction I would take in my subsequent clinical encounters. As an [addiction medicine physician](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=Gb7borIAAAAJ&hl=en), I often talk with patients who use nicotine in various forms. Understanding how these products differ and what we know about their relative risks can help people make more informed choices. Doing away with good/bad thinking One of the most important distinctions I make when I talk to patients is distinguishing between the harms caused by nicotine itself and the harms caused by the way it is delivered. Combustible tobacco products such as cigarettes expose users to [thousands of chemicals](https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/tobacco-products-ingredients-components/chemicals-every-puff-cigarette-smoke) produced by burning tobacco, while newer products such as vapes deliver nicotine without combustion. But the lack of combustion does not make the newer products inherently safe. [Nicotine remains addictive](https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/health-effects-tobacco-use/nicotine-why-tobacco-products-are-addictive), and [studies have raised concerns about its cardiovascular effects](https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms241914570), including changes in heart rate, blood pressure and underlying vascular function. The long-term health effects of newer nicotine products \u2013 particularly nicotine pouches \u2013 are not yet well established. As a result, discussions about which nicotine product is appropriate for a given person do not fall into categories of \u201cgood\u201d or \u201cbad.\u201d Rather, they involve nuanced conversations with patients to help them reach their goals and reduce overall harm. What\u2019s more, the health implications of a nicotine product depend not only on what is in it, but also on who is using it, how they use it and what it replaces. Relative harms vary significantly when talking about a current tobacco smoker who switches to an e-cigarette compared with a nonsmoker who starts vaping. The same distinction matters for nicotine pouches: Their lower exposure to many toxic chemicals does not make them risk-free, particularly for adolescents and other nonsmokers who have not previously used nicotine. Some noncigarette nicotine products deliver as much or more nicotine than cigarettes. Relative harms In 1965, 42.4% of the U.S. population smoked. By 2022, that [figure had dropped to 11.6%](https://www.lung.org/research/trends-in-lung-disease/tobacco-trends-brief/overall-smoking-trends). This dramatic decline did not happen because of a single intervention, but through decades of [accumulating scientific evidence](https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco-surgeon-general-reports/about/history.html), [public education campaigns](https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco/campaign/tips/about/impact/campaign-impact-results.html), [warning labels](https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/labeling-and-warning-statements-tobacco-products/cigarette-labeling-and-health-warning-requirements), restrictions on advertising, smoke-free policies, higher tobacco taxes and shifts in social norms. At the same time, the tobacco landscape has evolved, with noncombustible nicotine products such as e-cigarettes and nicotine pouches becoming increasingly prominent. In the U.S., 6.5% of adults [reported using e-cigarettes](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/products/databriefs/db524.htm) in 2023, while [past-year nicotine pouch use](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108393) rose from 3.4% in 2023 to 5.9% in 2024. This fast-growing trend makes it clear that the public is in need of a better understanding of the relative harms of nicotine products. To be clear, the major source of cigarette-related harm is [burning tobacco](https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/tobacco-products-ingredients-components/chemicals-every-puff-cigarette-smoke), not simply the presence of nicotine. Tobacco smoke contains thousands of chemicals, many of which are [toxic and carcinogenic](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41568-021-00423-4), culminating in [approximately 2.5 million cancer deaths](https://iarc2026conf.iarc.who.int/data/onglet27/module0/modalPreview.php?langue=fr&paramProjet=22269) per year worldwide. Patients also often land in the hospital with complications related to tobacco use, including [chronic obstructive pulmonary disease](https://theconversation.com/more-than-140-000-americans-die-from-copd-each-year-heres-why-survival-depends-on-more-than-avoiding-smoking-277475) and cardiovascular disease. While nicotine itself is the reason for dependence, the toxic mixture generated by burning tobacco leaves is what makes cigarette smoking so extraordinarily harmful. Cigarette combustion generates a toxic mixture of substances, many of which cause cancer. [Darya Komarova/Moment via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/close-up-of-a-hand-of-a-man-with-a-cigar-smoke-on-a-royalty-free-image/2066135673?phrase=burning%20tobacco&searchscope=image%2Cfilm&adppopup=true) Nicotine products compared So, where do vaping, [heated tobacco](https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco/other-tobacco-products/heated-tobacco-products.html) \u2013 also known as \u201cheat-not-burn products\u201d \u2013 cigarettes and oral nicotine fall on the spectrum of safety? Electronic cigarettes \u2013 or vapes \u2013 generally contain fewer and lower levels of many toxic chemicals compared with cigarette smoke. However, inhaled aerosols can still contain harmful substances such as propylene glycol, which is often found in [certain medicines, cosmetics and food products](https://wwwn.cdc.gov/tsp/substances/ToxSubstance.aspx?toxid=240). While propylene glycol is classified as \u201cgenerally safe\u201d by the FDA for use in food, that designation does not establish that it is safe to inhale. The lungs process substances differently from the digestive system, and the health effects of chronic inhalation of propylene glycol through e-cigarette aerosol remain incompletely understood. [Heated tobacco products](https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco/other-tobacco-products/heated-tobacco-products.html) such as processed tobacco sticks, loose-leaf chambers, tobacco capsules and pods, or carbon-tipped pods typically heat tobacco to lower temperatures as opposed to burning it. This can lead to lower exposure to harmful substances compared with conventional cigarettes. However, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention [considers any tobacco product harmful](https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco/other-tobacco-products/heated-tobacco-products.html), given that these products still deliver tobacco-derived nicotine and other toxins. An increasingly prominent product is the nicotine pouch, such as the brand-name product Zyn. Sales of nicotine pouches in the U.S. have increased substantially in recent years, [particularly since 2023](https://tobaccomonitoring.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Nicotine-Pouch-Sales-Data-Brief_8.10.2025.pdf). Unlike traditional smokeless tobacco, nicotine pouches contain no tobacco leaf; instead, they contain nicotine, flavorings and other ingredients. Users typically place a pouch between the upper lip and gum, where nicotine is absorbed through the tissues of the mouth. Nicotine pouches generally result in lower exposure to many toxic substances, given that there is no combustion or inhaled aerosols. However, they can deliver substantial amounts of nicotine. Depending on the product, a [single pouch may contain several milligrams of nicotine](https://doi.org/10.1007/s13318-021-00742-9) \u2013 comparable to or exceeding the amount of nicotine you would otherwise get from a cigarette. These products therefore [remain addictive](https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/health-effects-tobacco-use/nicotine-why-tobacco-products-are-addictive). With all nicotine products, the relevant comparison is not \u201csafe\u201d versus \u201cdangerous,\u201d but rather \u201cmore harmful\u201d versus \u201cless harmful. Heated tobacco products heat cigarettes without burning them, releasing fewer toxins \u2013 but harmful ones nonetheless. [Fabrice Coffrini/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/picture-taken-on-august-21-2018-in-neuchatel-at-the-news-photo/1032223640?phrase=heated%20tobacco%20products&adppopup=true) FDA-approved nicotine replacement therapy Nicotine replacement therapy, on the other hand, is an [FDA-approved medical treatment](https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/want-quit-smoking-fda-approved-and-fda-cleared-cessation-products-can-help) that delivers controlled, low amounts of nicotine without the many carcinogens and toxic chemicals found in tobacco smoke. These products come in the form of patches, gum, lozenges, nasal sprays and inhalers, intended to reduce cravings and withdrawal while helping people gradually move away from tobacco use. Unlike nicotine replacement therapies, which are FDA-approved and supported by strong [evidence](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2022.0395) of effectiveness for smoking cessation, e-cigarettes are not FDA-approved cessation treatments, despite evidence that they can help some people quit smoking. Evidence for nicotine pouches as a cessation aid remains limited, and they have not been FDA-approved for this purpose. Patterns of nicotine and tobacco use [also vary considerably across countries and populations](https://www.who.int/publications/b/81238), reflecting differences in culture, tobacco-control policies, product availability and patterns of use. Similarly, access to smoking-cessation treatment and harm-reduction resources [varies substantially from country to country](https://escholarship.org/uc/item/7x18m7f9). In the U.K., for example, cessation services are integrated into the country\u2019s National Health Service, while Australia provides [state-based \"Quit\u201d line services](https://www.quit.org.au/en) and permits regulated access to some nicotine vaping products through pharmacies. Many countries have limited or no comprehensive services focused on smoking cessation, and access to medications such as nicotine replacement therapy remains uneven. Putting nicotine risk in perspective For people who do not use nicotine, there is little reason to start. For people who smoke, quitting remains the best option. But if quitting is not immediately achievable, moving away from combustible tobacco may reduce exposure to harmful chemicals in cigarette smoke. The goal is not to label every nicotine product \u201cgood\u201d or \u201cbad,\u201d but to help people understand the trade-offs \u2013 and to make choices that move them toward less harm and, ultimately, away from nicotine dependence. [Addiction](https://theconversation.com/topics/addiction-421) [Cigarettes](https://theconversation.com/topics/cigarettes-704) [Global health](https://theconversation.com/topics/global-health-968) [Nicotine](https://theconversation.com/topics/nicotine-4806) [E-cigarettes](https://theconversation.com/topics/e-cigarettes-8718) [Vaping](https://theconversation.com/topics/vaping-9048) [Nicotine replacement therapy](https://theconversation.com/topics/nicotine-replacement-therapy-10769) [Nicotine pouches](https://theconversation.com/topics/nicotine-pouches-152967) [Emma Fenske, University of Colorado Anschutz, Oregon Health & Science University](https://theconversation.com/nicotine-pouches-vaping-or-cigarettes-which-nicotine-product-is-actually-the-least-harmful-290604) Author [Emma Fenske](https://theconversation.com/profiles/emma-fenske-2498956) Assistant Professor of Medicine, Addiction Medicine, University of Colorado Anschutz; Oregon Health & Science University Disclosure statement Emma Fenske does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Colorado Anschutz](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-colorado-anschutz-4838) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.rkn4m7gce](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.rkn4m7gce)", "content_hash": "1970683ea093de6b6acc9e7f696e27f8149c3f7c496cee18f18a5e9f4425993e", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:47Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "964", "title": "79 deaths a day: South Africa\u2019s diabetes crisis needs a new\u00a0strategy", "url": "https://theconversation.com/79-deaths-a-day-south-africas-diabetes-crisis-needs-a-new-strategy-293063", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/79-deaths-a-day-south-africas-diabetes-crisis-needs-a-new-strategy-293063", "text": "79 deaths a day: South Africa\u2019s diabetes crisis needs a new strategy Published: October 2, 2026 8:56am EDT Diabetes was the country\u2019s leading underlying natural cause of death in 2024. [CIDRAP/X](https://x.com/CIDRAP/status/2023499001455128858/photo/1) https://theconversation.com/79-deaths-a-day-south-africas-diabetes-crisis-needs-a-new-strategy-293063 https://theconversation.com/79-deaths-a-day-south-africas-diabetes-crisis-needs-a-new-strategy-293063 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Statistics South Africa (Stats SA) recently released its [report on deaths that occurred in 2024](https://www.statssa.gov.za/publications/P03093/P030932024.pdf). The headline findings contain a sobering, recurring truth: diabetes was once again the country\u2019s leading underlying natural cause of death. In 2024 alone, 28,767 people died from complications caused by diabetes, accounting for 6.1% of all deaths nationwide. That is an average of nearly 79 deaths every day. Diabetes also claimed the top spot in [2022](https://www.statssa.gov.za/publications/P03093/P030932022.pdf) and 2023. These numbers cannot be accepted as South Africa\u2019s new normal. Behind every data point is a human life, a grieving family and, all too often, years of encounters with a health system that missed opportunities to diagnose, treat and support people in time. As a public health specialist and chairperson of the non-profit organisation [Diabetes Alliance](https://www.diabetesalliance.org.za/), I view this three-year trend as an unacceptable tragedy. Diabetes is manageable. With timely support, people can lead long, healthy lives free from preventable complications. Acknowledging this crisis is the start, but recognition must give way to political action. The Department of Health must establish a national diabetes strategy with clear targets for prevention and care. The finance minister must resource it. And the Presidency must lead a coordinated, whole-of-government response. The broader health landscape is equally stark. Noncommunicable diseases accounted for 60.7% of all recorded deaths in 2024. The top three causes of natural death \u2013 diabetes, hypertensive diseases and cerebrovascular diseases \u2013 frequently overlap in the same individuals. Managing these interconnected conditions through isolated disease programmes makes little sense for primary care clinics or the patients navigating them. Reading beyond the numbers At first glance, the 2024 total is 800 deaths higher than the revised 2023 figure of 27,967 [(5.8% of recorded deaths)](https://www.statssa.gov.za/publications/P03093/P030932024.pdf). Stats SA compiles these mortality figures from death notification forms filed through the Department of Home Affairs. Medical practitioners record the cause of death, which Stats SA then classifies using international coding standards. Late death registrations and administrative backlogs are processed retroactively; this leads to revision or updating of prior years\u2019 totals. The updated count for 2022 stood at 33,126, higher than diabetes deaths recorded in 2023 and 2024. Diabetes has remained South Africa\u2019s single largest natural killer for three consecutive years. The diabetes burden is also deeply unequal: Women bear the heaviest loss: diabetes was the leading recorded natural cause among women, claiming 17,429 lives (7.9% of all female deaths) and 11,334 or 4.5% of all male deaths. Working-age adults are hard hit: it ranked first among people aged 45-64, accounting for 9,364 deaths (7.9% of deaths in this group). Geographic split: diabetes ranked as the top natural cause in five provinces, Western Cape, KwaZulu-Natal, Limpopo, Eastern Cape and Gauteng, while hypertensive diseases led in the remaining four provinces. These provincial rankings are not a measure of individual care quality or residency. They are a starting point for region-specific questions about access and system performance. The geographic split shows the need to investigate whether access to diagnosis, treatment and good quality care differs between and within provinces. A death certificate is only the final chapter Mortality statistics record the primary cause listed on a death notification, but remain blind to what happened along the way. They cannot tell us how many South Africans live with undiagnosed diabetes. They cannot show whether a patient had consistent access to insulin and testing supplies, whether their blood sugar was adequately controlled, or whether a simple follow-up could have prevented a fatal complication. Nor do they capture the full toll of diabetes when another condition is listed as the underlying cause. Those are the questions we must answer while patients are still alive. In my work on strengthening diabetes care, including the [Tshwane Insulin Project](https://www.up.ac.za/diabetes-research-centre/tshwane-insulin-programme-tip) \u2013 a nurse-led programme that helped people with type 2 diabetes start and adjust insulin treatment in primary care, with follow-up support at home \u2013 I have seen where care pathways break down, and where they succeed. Survival rarely hinges on high-tech interventions. It depends on the links in primary healthcare: a trained clinic nurse with the time to act a reliable supply chain for medicines and diagnostics routine follow-up mechanisms when a patient misses an appointment regular screening for kidney, eye and vascular (blood vessel) complications support systems that respect the economic reality of a person\u2019s daily life. We need real-time clinical data that helps a facility respond today, not a national death tally published two years later. Turning policy into frontline care South Africa already has a framework: the [National Strategic Plan for Non-Communicable Diseases](https://www.health.gov.za/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/NCD-NSP-FINAL-VERSION-20-SEPT-22-1.pdf) and its 90-60-50 targets. This cascade aims to ensure that 90% of adults know their blood pressure and blood glucose status, 60% of those with elevated levels receive treatment, and 50% of those receiving care achieve clinical control. The challenge is making policy work in practice. Tools like the [National Health Laboratory Service diabetes dashboard](https://www.nhls.ac.za/nhls-develops-national-diabetes-dashboard-to-strengthen-south-africas-response/) offer valuable insights into blood sugar levels control across districts. But to close the loop, South Africa urgently needs a national diabetes registry and integrated digital tracking to understand who enters care, who remains in care, and who is being left behind. From declaration to delivery After the 2025 Diabetes Summit, the Diabetes Alliance launched the [Johannesburg Declaration for Accelerated Action on Diabetes](https://www.diabetesalliance.org.za/johannesburg-declaration/). The declaration calls for coordinated national leadership, sustainable financing, decentralised primary care, and direct participation by people living with diabetes. It asks the Presidency to lead a multisectoral response and calls on the National Treasury and health sector to match policy commitments with financial resources. The latest mortality report gives this agenda even more urgency. There are three practical priorities: Connect early diagnosis to sustained care. Screening is only useful if a positive result leads to uninterrupted treatment, reliable medicines, blood glucose monitoring and clinical review. Measure quality of care: publish a picture of diagnosis, treatment, retention and complication rates by district and province. Address the conditions in which health choices are made. Food prices, transport barriers and economic hardship shape health outcomes. People living with diabetes must help define what quality care looks like. Their experience should guide national policy and daily clinic operations alike. [Public health](https://theconversation.com/topics/public-health-136) [Diabetes](https://theconversation.com/topics/diabetes-612) [Public policy](https://theconversation.com/topics/public-policy-1288) [Hypertension](https://theconversation.com/topics/hypertension-1363) [NCDs](https://theconversation.com/topics/ncds-1488) [Noncommunicable diseases](https://theconversation.com/topics/noncommunicable-diseases-4207) [South Africa](https://theconversation.com/topics/south-africa-5995) [Blood sugar](https://theconversation.com/topics/blood-sugar-6767) [Primary healthcare](https://theconversation.com/topics/primary-healthcare-126183) [TC global health](https://theconversation.com/topics/tc-global-health-189737) [Patrick Ngassa Piotie, University of Pretoria](https://theconversation.com/79-deaths-a-day-south-africas-diabetes-crisis-needs-a-new-strategy-293063) Author [Patrick Ngassa Piotie](https://theconversation.com/profiles/patrick-ngassa-piotie-1161629) Chairperson, Diabetes Alliance; Co-founder, Diabetes Research Centre, University of Pretoria Disclosure statement Patrick Ngassa Piotie is the Chairperson of the Diabetes Alliance in South Africa. Partners [University of Pretoria](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-pretoria-1645) provides funding as a partner of The Conversation AFRICA. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.ja3ypjsur](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.ja3ypjsur)", "content_hash": "5bc8a9149ea3c157cae8721665cb9852b85b396a69e5f9a7b6171fa7595e52d9", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:49Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "965", "title": "Global health research makes America richer and\u00a0healthier", "url": "https://theconversation.com/global-health-research-makes-america-richer-and-healthier-292430", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/global-health-research-makes-america-richer-and-healthier-292430", "text": "Global health research has proved to be a solid economic investment, in addition to helping make the world healthier. [Noko LTD/E+ via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/healthcare-professionals-discussing-patient-royalty-free-image/2287385477?phrase=global%20health%20research&searchscope=image,film&adppopup=true) Global health research makes America richer and healthier Published: October 1, 2026 8:40am EDT https://theconversation.com/global-health-research-makes-america-richer-and-healthier-292430 https://theconversation.com/global-health-research-makes-america-richer-and-healthier-292430 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Recently, a middle-aged woman came to see me in my ophthalmology clinic because her eyes felt gritty and uncomfortable. She had seen another doctor a month earlier who diagnosed her with eyelid inflammation and wrote her a prescription. As I examined her eyes, she mentioned in passing the prescription medication she\u2019d been using \u2013 ivermectin. This was the first I\u2019d heard of this drug being [used for itchy, uncomfortable eyes](https://theconversation.com/ivermectin-is-a-nobel-prize-winning-wonder-drug-but-not-for-covid-19-168449). I knew researchers had [studied and rejected ivermectin as a cure for COVID-19](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijantimicag.2021.106516), and that the medication is to treat [onchocerciasis](https://www.who.int/health-topics/onchocerciasis), a condition more commonly known as river blindness that is mostly seen in rural sub-Saharan Africa. River blindness is caused by a parasitic worm that\u2019s transmitted when people are bitten repeatedly by infected blackflies. A long neglected tropical disease, it\u2019s mostly known for causing severe skin disfigurement and permanent blindness. So why was this patient with itchy eyes in the U.S. using the same drug used to kill a blinding parasitic worm? This is just one instance where decades of investment and collaboration to fight a disease that affects the global poor had yielded an [unexpected benefit](https://doi.org/10.1038/ja.2017.11) \u2013 a treatment for eyelid inflammation, dry eyes and rosacea. This medicine is just one data point in a pattern worth billions of dollars. As a [population health researcher](https://src.isr.umich.edu/research/faculty-profiles/profiles/josh-ehrlich/), I see firsthand the benefits of investment in global health research. Global health research has a long history of producing treatments, knowledge and economic benefits that advantage wealthy countries in unexpected ways. While ivermectin is used to treat river blindness in C\u00f4te d'Ivoire, the same drug is used to treat itchy eyes and rosacea in the U.S. [Issouf Sanogo/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/christophe-koffi-a-nurse-mamadou-kone-gives-medecine-news-photo/83038610?adppopup=true) Economic investments paying off Global health research \u2013 whether it\u2019s vaccine and drug development, large-scale population data collection or comparative policy studies \u2013 demands considerable investment. The results consistently show this investment is one that makes good economic sense. A 2012 report from the U.S. National Institutes of Health estimated that every US$1 the agency invests in global health research will have a [return on investment of $2.21](https://www.fic.nih.gov/News/GlobalHealthMatters/may-june-2012/Pages/us-economic-impact-research.aspx) and that each research grant creates seven American jobs. This report has proved to be true. Between 2008 and 2024, U.S. governmental institutions like the NIH spent around $46 billion in three key global health areas \u2013 neglected diseases, emerging infectious diseases, and sexual and reproductive health \u2013 an investment that [catalyzed $104 billion in economic activity and created 600,000 jobs](https://www.ghtcoalition.org/resources-item/doing-well-by-doing-good-why-investing-in-global-health-r-d-benefits-the-united-states-and-the-world) in the U.S. Global health research is a profitable investment for high-income countries. Beyond the economics The benefits of global health research go far beyond economic gains. This work also improves health and medicine in the U.S. and worldwide. As I saw in my own clinic, research into a disease half a world away led to treatment for a patient in the U.S. \u2013 and this is not unusual. The nonprofit research organization Impact Global Health [tracks what it calls innovation spillover](https://www.impactglobalhealth.org/data/innovation-spillover-tracker). It details a host of global health research innovations that have led to health and economic benefits in the U.S. and other high-income countries. From COVID-19 tests to vaccines for HPV and rotavirus, to breast cancer risk reduction and pediatric cancer treatments, the direct benefits are impressive. One example is a research partnership from the 1980s between GlaxoSmithKline and the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research that [yielded a lifesaving malaria vaccine](https://www.path.org/our-impact/case-studies/developing-the-worlds-first-malaria-vaccine-rtss/). That vaccine now prevents up to [1 in 8 childhood deaths](http://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(26)00248-5) in malaria-affected areas of sub-Saharan Africa. On top of that, the technology behind that malaria vaccine is also the basis for the shingles and RSV vaccines used in the U.S. and globally. The shingles vaccine alone is expected to [prevent over 9 million cases of shingles](https://www.impactglobalhealth.org/insights/report-library/shingrix-vaccine) in the U.S. and save the healthcare system $55 billion by 2050. An even older example of this innovation spillover is the [tuberculosis vaccine](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis). Over 100 years ago, French scientists developed a vaccine for tuberculosis, a lung infection that still kills over 1 million people each year, 80% of them in low- and middle-income countries. As it happens, in 1990 the U.S. Food and Drug Administration [approved the medication used in the tuberculosis vaccine](https://www.mskcc.org/news/oldest-approved-immunotherapy-gets-new-explanation) as a treatment for bladder cancer. Researchers estimate this medication [will prevent more than 300,000 cancers](https://www.impactglobalhealth.org/insights/report-library/bcg-vaccine) from recurring and save nearly 100,000 lives and nearly $12 billion in the U.S. by 2050. Improving the healthcare system itself Beyond essential medications, global health research also produces vital and actionable knowledge about healthcare delivery. Studying the health of populations around the world can provide unique insights that researchers can use to improve care in the U.S. and strengthen its healthcare system. Most Americans, regardless of political beliefs, are [dissatisfied with the U.S. healthcare system](https://news.gallup.com/poll/654044/view-healthcare-quality-declines-year-low.aspx). Many other wealthy countries [spend less money to get better outcomes](https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/fund-reports/2024/sep/mirror-mirror-2024) \u2013 fewer premature deaths, longer health and life expectancies, more equitable access to care and so on. U.S. researchers have been studying those countries to understand what works about their systems and what doesn\u2019t to inform ways to improve American healthcare. One example of this is Washington state, which [borrowed key lessons from Germany\u2019s experience](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/13/well/long-term-care-costs.html) with long-term care to design the first publicly operated long-term care insurance program in the U.S. This program, [Washington Cares](https://wacaresfund.wa.gov/), is funded through a small payroll tax of less than 0.6% of wages and can be used to cover long-term care costs such as home care, installing ramps or grab bars, compensating informal caregivers, and nursing home expenses. Economists estimate that [unpaid caregivers in the U.S.](https://www.aarp.org/pri/topics/work-finances-retirement/economics-aging/longevity-economy-outlook/) provide care valued in the trillions of dollars per year. A robust long-term care system could enable caregivers to make larger contributions to the formal economy. This would be a boon for the U.S. economy as the [American population ages](https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2026/demo/P95-26-1.html). Looking forward [Proposed cuts to NIH\u2019s 2027 budget](https://www.statnews.com/2026/04/03/trump-budget-nih-5-billion-cut-in-2027/) threaten to undermine the benefits of global health research. In a time when many Americans feel [overwhelmed with the cost of living](https://theconversation.com/trump-speculates-on-diesel-fuel-export-ban-as-high-prices-ripple-across-us-economy-292323), it may be tempting to consider cutting [research aimed at helping people outside the U.S](https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefings-statements/2025/08/historic-pocket-rescission-package-eliminates-woke-weaponized-and-wasteful-spending/). But looking at the data, I believe the kind of global health research the NIH funds is giving Americans a big bang for their buck. Any investment that yields these kinds of financial returns, while also delivering new drugs, vaccines and useful knowledge, seems worth holding onto. Global health research is an area where the U.S. can do well by doing good. [Research funding](https://theconversation.com/topics/research-funding-238) [Health policy](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-policy-431) [Malaria](https://theconversation.com/topics/malaria-762) [Drug research](https://theconversation.com/topics/drug-research-7741) [River blindness](https://theconversation.com/topics/river-blindness-12691) [Shingles](https://theconversation.com/topics/shingles-29977) [Health disparities](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-disparities-29998) [Public health research](https://theconversation.com/topics/public-health-research-31317) [Health research](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-research-48964) [Global aid](https://theconversation.com/topics/global-aid-75630) [Health research funding](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-research-funding-97090) [TC global health](https://theconversation.com/topics/tc-global-health-189737) [Joshua R. Ehrlich, University of Michigan](https://theconversation.com/global-health-research-makes-america-richer-and-healthier-292430) Author [Joshua R. Ehrlich](https://theconversation.com/profiles/joshua-r-ehrlich-2646554) Associate professor, University of Michigan Disclosure statement Joshua R. Ehrlich receives funding from the U.S. National Institutes of Health and Gates Ventures. He has received an honorarium from AARP. Partners [University of Michigan](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-michigan-1290) provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.gnt56ems5](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.gnt56ems5)", "content_hash": "2962a5b50126e7c4e7f3a538994e09ea7ad761269741545af12bb89670f0d89f", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:50Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "966", "title": "Teenage girls may know how to prevent pregnancy \u2013 that doesn\u2019t mean they have the power to do\u00a0it", "url": "https://theconversation.com/teenage-girls-may-know-how-to-prevent-pregnancy-that-doesnt-mean-they-have-the-power-to-do-it-290007", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/teenage-girls-may-know-how-to-prevent-pregnancy-that-doesnt-mean-they-have-the-power-to-do-it-290007", "text": "Teenage girls may know how to prevent pregnancy \u2013 that doesn\u2019t mean they have the power to do it Published: September 20, 2026 1:03am EDT Decisions about sex, contraception and pregnancy are made within gendered relationships. [Kei Scampa/Pexels](https://images.pexels.com/photos/3013668/pexels-photo-3013668.jpeg?_gl=1*1iec2pr*_ga*MzU5MzMwNzAyLjE3ODM5NTgyOTc.*_ga_8JE65Q40S6*czE3ODk0NjQ0MjEkbzE1JGcxJHQxNzg5NDY1MzM3JGo1OSRsMCRoMA..) https://theconversation.com/teenage-girls-may-know-how-to-prevent-pregnancy-that-doesnt-mean-they-have-the-power-to-do-it-290007 https://theconversation.com/teenage-girls-may-know-how-to-prevent-pregnancy-that-doesnt-mean-they-have-the-power-to-do-it-290007 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Teenage pregnancy is often explained in simple terms. Girls are said to lack knowledge about contraception, take sexual risks or make poor choices. But is it that simple? For almost three decades, I have been [researching](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.1080/10130950.2003.9676019) how [gender and sexuality](https://www.routledge.com/Childhood-Sexuality-and-AIDS-Education-The-Price-of-Innocence/Bhana/p/book/9781138853003) come to matter in the [lives](https://www.routledge.com/Love-Sex-and-Teenage-Sexual-Cultures-in-South-Africa-16-turning-17/Bhana/p/book/9781138235274) of young people in South Africa. My collaborators and I are currently working on [The Teenage Pregnancy Project: Classroom, Cradles and Care](https://education.ukzn.ac.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/SARChI-Newsletter-2023-min.pdf) and related projects on [teenage fathers](https://researchportal.port.ac.uk/en/publications/inhlawulo-kin-and-custom-young-men-negotiating-fatherhood-and-res/) and caregivers. These projects seek to examine how pregnancy and parenting is experienced and understood by teenage fathers, mothers and their caregivers. The research is conducted mainly in KwaZulu-Natal province in South Africa with linked [projects in Zimbabwe](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17441692.2026.2697093). It involves interviews and group discussions including the use of visual methods to understand teenage pregnancy. [The research](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.1080/17441692.2026.2697093) findings tell a more complicated story than the usual explanation. It\u2019s that poverty, unequal relationships and institutional barriers narrow girls\u2019 choices about contraception, with dramatic consequences. Our findings matter because they shift attention to the socio-cultural and structural conditions that shape what choices are actually possible. Decisions about sex, contraception and pregnancy are made within gendered relationships and economic and institutional constraints that may limit what young women can do. Read more: [Pregnant teens in Kenya\u2019s low-income areas avoid health care because of fear and stigma](https://theconversation.com/pregnant-teens-in-kenyas-low-income-areas-avoid-health-care-because-of-fear-and-stigma-266263) Understanding teenage pregnancy in this way changes the question from \u201cWhy did she make that choice?\u201d to \u201cWhat made some choices easier, harder or impossible?\u201d Situating girls\u2019 choices within gendered, economic and institutional environments underlines the complexity of the interventions needed. These include [involving families](https://hsrcpress.ac.za/product/young-families/), schools and comprehensive [sexuality education](https://repository.hsrc.ac.za/handle/20.500.11910/3370). Relationships tied to money and survival Our Zimbabwe [study](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17441692.2026.2697093) involved 26 teenage mothers aged 16 to 19 from Shamva district in Mashonaland Central Province. All had experienced pregnancy while attending school. Our South African study drew on interviews with young mothers aged 15 to 18 and forms part of the Classrooms, Cradles and Care project. Together, the studies show what can happen both before pregnancy and after it becomes visible. In our Zimbabwean research we used the term \u201creproductive pipeline\u201d to describe how poverty, unequal gender relations, family expectations, sexual coercion and inadequate reproductive healthcare can combine to push girls towards pregnancy and early motherhood. The idea of a pipeline does not mean that girls have no agency. The girls we spoke to made decisions, resisted, negotiated, sought contraception and imagined different futures for themselves. But they did so within circumstances that could sharply narrow what was possible. Many of the girls in our South African study knew what they wanted for their futures. They wanted to finish school, find work and delay motherhood. Some knew how pregnancy could be prevented and actively sought contraception. But wanting to avoid pregnancy and having the power and resources to do so are not the same thing. One of the young women in our Zimbabwe study began a relationship while she was still at school. Her boyfriend, who was older than her, was already thinking about marriage. She was not. As she [put it](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17441692.2026.2697093#abstract): I was still too young and wanted to finish school, get a job \u2026 I did not know I would get pregnant. Her experience captured a central problem in how teenage pregnancy is discussed. People tend to ask why a girl made a particular choice. Less is asked about the conditions in which that choice had to be made. Poverty shaped intimate relationships in powerful ways. In Zimbabwe, Grace had lost both parents and was living with her elderly grandmother. Food was scarce. She [explained](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17441692.2026.2697093#d1e564) that she entered a relationship partly because her boyfriend sometimes gave her small amounts of money: If he could give me even 50 cents, at least I would buy whatever it could get me. I just needed the money. Another participant, Anesu, wanted to return to her grandmother\u2019s home. She was living with her sister and she could see that life was difficult. Unable to afford the bus fare, she entered a relationship hoping her boyfriend would give her the money. Instead, she said, he forced her to have sex and she became pregnant. Contraception does not guarantee control The studies\u2019 findings also challenge the assumption that information alone prevents pregnancy. Mutsai, in Zimbabwe, knew that condoms could prevent pregnancy. She even had condoms available. But her boyfriend refused to use them. Girls described relationships in which older or more powerful male partners influenced whether sex happened and whether contraception was used. Some talked about how age differences, male authority, economic dependency, fear and the threat of social shame constrained their capacity to act. Read more: [Sex for money: South African study reveals the harm that \u2018blessers\u2019 can do to young women](https://theconversation.com/sex-for-money-south-african-study-reveals-the-harm-that-blessers-can-do-to-young-women-267839) Healthcare can create another barrier. Zimbabwean participants described being unable to afford transport costs to reach services, contraceptive shortages and judgment from healthcare workers. One participant could not afford US$2 for a contraceptive implant. Another could not raise the US$1 needed to travel to a government hospital and US$1 to return home. One sought contraceptive pills at a clinic but said she was required to buy a pregnancy test first. What needs to change Based on our findings, we make a number of recommendations. The first is that reducing teenage pregnancy requires more than telling girls to abstain from sex or giving them information about contraception. Secondly, sexual and reproductive healthcare must be accessible, affordable, adolescent-friendly and non-judgmental. Thirdly, sexuality education needs to go beyond the biology of pregnancy prevention and create space to discuss love, relationships, consent, coercion, gender inequality, material dependence and the power differences that can make negotiating contraception difficult. Fourth, boys and men must be part of the response. Pregnancy prevention cannot remain girls\u2019 responsibility when male partners can refuse condoms, pressure girls into sex or exercise greater control over reproductive decisions \u2013 then escape much of the blame when pregnancy occurs. Fifth, reproductive health cannot be separated from poverty and material inequality. Sixth, schools, families and communities also need to support girls\u2019 educational aspirations after pregnancy rather than turning young mothers into warnings about failed girlhood. [Poverty](https://theconversation.com/topics/poverty-717) [Inequality](https://theconversation.com/topics/inequality-829) [Social determinants of health](https://theconversation.com/topics/social-determinants-of-health-3922) [South Africa](https://theconversation.com/topics/south-africa-5995) [Zimbabwe](https://theconversation.com/topics/zimbabwe-6565) [Teen pregnancy](https://theconversation.com/topics/teen-pregnancy-33031) [Women and girls](https://theconversation.com/topics/women-and-girls-38471) [TC global health](https://theconversation.com/topics/tc-global-health-189737) [Deevia Bhana, University of KwaZulu-Natal](https://theconversation.com/teenage-girls-may-know-how-to-prevent-pregnancy-that-doesnt-mean-they-have-the-power-to-do-it-290007) Author [Deevia Bhana](https://theconversation.com/profiles/deevia-bhana-836076) Professor Gender and Childhood Sexuality, University of KwaZulu-Natal Disclosure statement Deevia Bhana receives funding from the National Research Foundation of South Africa (Grant Numbers: SARCI15020511366) Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.6p7m9ukxv](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.6p7m9ukxv)", "content_hash": "c4c0e13d0445c09f62547f24b4f92430412bde0a7fb4ecb3eafa862e9f9398fc", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:51Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "967", "title": "I\u2019ve been studying election integrity for 26 years, and here\u2019s why I expect a normal election this November instead of\u00a0chaos", "url": "https://theconversation.com/ive-been-studying-election-integrity-for-26-years-and-heres-why-i-expect-a-normal-election-this-november-instead-of-chaos-294094", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/ive-been-studying-election-integrity-for-26-years-and-heres-why-i-expect-a-normal-election-this-november-instead-of-chaos-294094", "text": "Concern about election interference by the Trump administration sparked this rally in Washington, D.C., on Sept, 15, 2026. [Leigh Vogel/Getty Images for People's Action Institute](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/view-of-participants-and-signage-during-the-hands-off-our-news-photo/2295431041) I\u2019ve been studying election integrity for 26 years, and here\u2019s why I expect a normal election this November instead of chaos Published: October 13, 2026 10:28am EDT https://theconversation.com/ive-been-studying-election-integrity-for-26-years-and-heres-why-i-expect-a-normal-election-this-november-instead-of-chaos-294094 https://theconversation.com/ive-been-studying-election-integrity-for-26-years-and-heres-why-i-expect-a-normal-election-this-november-instead-of-chaos-294094 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Fear of [federal interference in the 2026 midterms](https://www.npr.org/2026/06/16/nx-s1-5702491/trump-voting-threat-midterm-election-dhs), from intimidation stoked by armed officials at the polls to seizure of ballots, has been growing in the United States. That\u2019s led state and local voting officials across the country to [draw up plans for scenarios](https://www.votebeat.org/national/2026/09/24/midterm-2026-election-preparation-federal-interference/) requiring them to summon law enforcement or prepare legal filings [so they can keep the polls open](https://www.governing.com/politics/election-officials-prepare-for-federal-intervention-in-the-midterms). In an interview with The Conversation\u2019s politics and legal affairs editor Naomi Schalit, [election law scholar Justin Levitt](https://www.lls.edu/faculty/justinlevitt/) takes the temperature down a bit. Levitt says that while voting officials\u2019 preparations mean they are doing their jobs, the anticipated election interference won\u2019t happen \u2013 in part because of that preparation. Schalit: Could we see widespread intimidation and challenging of voters at the polls? Levitt: It\u2019s extremely unlikely. I completely understand that [Americans are freaked out](https://www.npr.org/2026/09/25/nx-s1-5946736/voting-confusion-turnout). They are at an electric level of anxiety right now, not just about the world but about the coming elections. But I don\u2019t think the intimidation and challenges they\u2019re fearing or forecasting are going to manifest for a couple reasons. I have [run a nationwide election protection program](https://www.laloyolan.com/news/loyola-law-professor-justin-levitt-appointed-senior-advisor-for-president-biden/article_4fbb98fe-d785-5737-9483-0b4048affabb.html). It\u2019s real work to get people to dedicate a full day to helping others. It\u2019s that much harder to recruit a group of people to show up and look for fraud, [and fraud at scale doesn\u2019t really happen](https://apnews.com/article/us-elections-donald-trump-voting-fraud-db0a438023d8451c2854940504b48547). It\u2019s even harder to recruit people to show up to actively intimidate voters. Not that many people are in for it \u2013 there are keyboard warriors who love to act out online, but to actually show up is a totally different thing. And they\u2019re really risking either getting thrown out of the polling place or going to jail, [because intimidating voters is a crime](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/594). Residents check in to vote in the primary at a polling place at Morgan Elementary School on Aug. 4, 2026, in Shelby, Mich. [Scott Olson/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/residents-vote-at-a-polling-place-inside-the-gymnasium-at-news-photo/2289061687?phrase=signing%20in%20voters&adppopup=true) What about intimidation and challenges by government officials? On the government side, which is something people are also really freaked out about, we are a giant country and there are [almost 100,000 polling places](https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/polling-places). The logistics of an intimidation operation don\u2019t work countrywide. To go from Step A, I\u2019d like to intimidate people, to Step C, intimidate people everywhere? You\u2019re missing a pretty giant Step B. Here\u2019s what I believe will happen: The federal government will send a group of a dozen goons to a suburban polling place somewhere, and they\u2019ll have a camera crew with them. Bystanders will happily oblige them in filming. That clip will go viral and scare voters into not showing up at the polls because they think that there are individuals intimidating voters all over the place. If people are worried about intimidation, go outside and see for yourself that in your neighborhood things are very normal. Even if social media depicts pure chaos. Local and state election officials are preparing for [mayhem or interference by the federal government](https://www.votebeat.org/national/2026/09/24/midterm-2026-election-preparation-federal-interference/). Are their efforts just based on overwrought imaginations? No, they are being responsible. It\u2019s their responsibility to prepare for everything so that the voters don\u2019t have to worry about it. And they are as prepared as I have ever seen them. [They\u2019re preparing for a lot of truly outlandish scenarios](https://www.governing.com/politics/election-officials-prepare-for-federal-intervention-in-the-midterms), and some that are more realistic, so that they can be ready to make sure that the election is smooth and secure and feels very normal. Could ballots be seized by state or federal officials? [Ballot seizure is also very much on people\u2019s minds](https://www.wgcu.org/2026-09-09/ballot-seizures-seemed-impossible-but-judges-and-officials-are-prepping-for-them-now), and I think it\u2019s particularly salient because [the feds did it in Fulton County, Georgia](https://www.ajc.com/politics/2026/09/what-the-fulton-county-election-warehouse-raid-did-and-did-not-accomplish/), and because local sheriffs did it in Riverside, California. But there are two big reasons that\u2019s not going to happen this fall. First, the seizures in both Fulton County and Riverside were of old ballots. [Fulton County\u2019s ballots were 6 years old](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/04/us/politics/fulton-county-fbi-raid-trump-gabbard.html). California was more recent. [It was a 2026 seizure for the 2025 elections](https://statecourtreport.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/california-supreme-court-finds-sheriffs-seizure-650000-ballots-unlawful), but those elections were still over and results had already been tallied. That\u2019s very different from [an attempt to seize ballots while results are still pending](https://electionlawblog.org/wp-content/uploads/berstein-seizure.pdf), [which is flatly unconstitutional](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/legal-guardrails-against-rubber-stamping-search-warrants-elections). I just [worked on an article](http://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7227158) with the former chief of the public integrity section at the U.S. Department of Justice. We did a lot of research and we think there\u2019s never before been a [federal warrant to seize ballot material](https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/search-warrants-for-election-materials-get-their-day-in-court) when the results are pending. And that\u2019s because judges don\u2019t like to sign off on things that are illegal. These seizures, in Fulton County and in Riverside, California, surely took the judges in those districts by surprise. Judges see applications for search warrants all the time, all day, every day. But [warrants for election materials](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/legal-guardrails-against-rubber-stamping-search-warrants-elections) when the results are still pending are vanishingly rare. The warrants in Fulton and Riverside did us a favor because there are very few judges anywhere who are going to be surprised right now. [Those seizures were big news](https://www.courthousenews.com/democrats-sue-doj-over-midterm-ballot-seizure-plans/). [In Fulton County, the judge allowed it](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ym4YLiKogwU), but he said very, very clearly [this would be different](https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.gand.355452/gov.uscourts.gand.355452.108.0_1.pdf#page=42) if we were talking about a pending election where results weren\u2019t still in the door. [In California, the trial judge signed off on it](https://calmatters.org/politics/2026/04/chad-bianco-election-warrants/). But the California Supreme Court came back just about a month ago, [said this was absolutely, flatly illegal](https://calmatters.org/politics/2026/09/supreme-court-ruling-sheriff-ballot-seizure/), there is absolutely no reason why a sheriff needs to seize these ballots. It is unlawful under California state law in a way that mimics other states\u2019 laws too. Minnesota Secretary of State Steve Simon discusses the state\u2019s concerns about voter intimidation. If somehow ballots get seized during the election process before they are counted and certified, that would mean that the chain of custody was broken and they would no longer be valid. Is that correct? In the extraordinarily unlikely event that there\u2019s actually a seizure, we\u2019re in a little bit of terra incognita. It\u2019s not entirely clear what happens. There will be immediate efforts legally to get the ballots back. But the [election system is built with belts and suspenders](https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/elections-q-and-a-for-lawmakers). If there\u2019s a seizure of some materials, that doesn\u2019t mean there\u2019s a seizure of all materials or all materials relevant to a count. Sometimes there are paper ballots that are [backed up by electronic copies](https://verifiedvoting.org/verifier/#mode/navigate/map/voteEquip/mapType/ppEquip/year/2026), or vice versa. So what\u2019s really important is that the election officials have a set of reliable records they can count. The federal government may come in and say, \u201cGive us the materials,\u201d and the states are likely to say, \u201cHow about we make you copies?\u201d And that\u2019s fine. They can go off and have their copies and do whatever they want with them as long as the states are still able to count the records. It\u2019s not really clear what would happen if there\u2019s an extended lapse where the ballots are out of state custody. It\u2019s not that those ballots are immediately invalid, but there\u2019d be a fight about it. I\u2019ve been covering politics for a long time, and in the past 10 years the number of times we\u2019ve gone into terra incognita was entirely unanticipated. Which is why I have to ask certain questions that seem completely implausible. I\u2019ve been working on elections for a couple decades now. My list of things to watch out for in 2000 was long. My list of things to watch out for in every subsequent election grew longer. The last couple of years, my list of things to watch out for has spilled off the page and onto the floor. For election officials, it\u2019s a lot. They are stressed out, they\u2019re underpaid, they\u2019re overworked. But all that said, they\u2019ve got this. They\u2019re actually as prepared as I have ever seen, including dealing with things that they haven\u2019t ever seen before and should never have to be seeing. I understand why people are afraid. I understand why people think there are risks. And that\u2019s because it\u2019s not normal to have the president fostering this much concern about the democratic process and who is this unattached to the democratic process. But the good news is [the process doesn\u2019t depend on the president](https://theconversation.com/why-trump-cant-just-decree-changes-to-voting-by-mail-a-former-federal-judge-explains-how-the-presidents-executive-order-is-a-solution-looking-for-a-problem-280680). It depends on us. [Voters](https://theconversation.com/topics/voters-18527) [Election interference](https://theconversation.com/topics/election-interference-65226) [Polling places](https://theconversation.com/topics/polling-places-89171) [Election administration](https://theconversation.com/topics/election-administration-91989) [Poll workers](https://theconversation.com/topics/poll-workers-92962) [Voter intimidation](https://theconversation.com/topics/voter-intimidation-93736) [2026 midterm elections](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-midterm-elections-178375) [Justin Levitt, Loyola Law School Los Angeles](https://theconversation.com/ive-been-studying-election-integrity-for-26-years-and-heres-why-i-expect-a-normal-election-this-november-instead-of-chaos-294094) Author [Justin Levitt](https://theconversation.com/profiles/justin-levitt-2236022) Professor of Law, Loyola Law School Los Angeles Disclosure statement Justin Levitt does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.4jd9jgjvc](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.4jd9jgjvc)", "content_hash": "0a473cbe4c2aa0cc8c45a1a91064a9685ed9e33101d81230f1d6c1dd59d6c9c7", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:52Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "968", "title": "Democrats distance themselves from trans rights \u2013 close midterm races may show whether that pays\u00a0off", "url": "https://theconversation.com/democrats-distance-themselves-from-trans-rights-close-midterm-races-may-show-whether-that-pays-off-293173", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/democrats-distance-themselves-from-trans-rights-close-midterm-races-may-show-whether-that-pays-off-293173", "text": "Transgender rights have become a flashpoint in 2026 midterm races. [Brandon Bell/Getty Images News via Getty](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/pride-flag-is-seen-held-up-in-a-crowd-during-preparation-news-photo/1482464345) Democrats distance themselves from trans rights \u2013 close midterm races may show whether that pays off Published: October 12, 2026 8:21am EDT https://theconversation.com/democrats-distance-themselves-from-trans-rights-close-midterm-races-may-show-whether-that-pays-off-293173 https://theconversation.com/democrats-distance-themselves-from-trans-rights-close-midterm-races-may-show-whether-that-pays-off-293173 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article A wave of [anti-transgender legislation](https://www.aclu.org/legislative-attacks-on-lgbtq-rights-2026) has appeared in statehouses [across the country](https://www.salon.com/2024/01/06/flood-of-anti-lgbtq-bills-shows-wants-to-eradicate-trans-people-from-public-life-advocate/) over the [past few years](https://www.statista.com/statistics/1551488/anti-transgender-bills-us/?srsltid=AU7gw4UOqvhVZjihnxwGdTXg5O7J3Pon8cupFMf35XxN0arBjaYUvNJm). Now, transgender issues are a [recurring topic](https://www.axios.com/2026/10/05/democrats-transgender-sports-ads-election-kaptur) on the 2026 midterm campaign trail. As a [scholar of LGBTQ+ politics and history](https://wgss.osu.edu/people/debussy.1), I\u2019ve noticed surprising trends in how Democrats in key states [like Texas and Ohio](https://www.natesilver.net/p/nate-silver-2026-midterm-election-polls-model) talk about policies focused on transgender people, as the party vies for [control of Congress](https://www.270towin.com/2026-house-election/). With a rise in [anti-trans rhetoric from the White House](https://theconversation.com/how-the-trump-administrations-rhetoric-promotes-anti-transgender-views-285444), some Democrats are using [similar messaging](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/04/us/politics/democrats-trans-athletes-midterms.html) in an attempt to secure undecided voters. In my assessment, [this approach](https://www.thenation.com/article/politics/transphobia-midterms-ads/) could alienate [moderates, progressives and LGBTQ+ people more broadly](https://www.advocate.com/politics/2024-presidential-election-lgbtq). On the campaign trail Democratic candidates have split sharply on [transition-related care](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/some-democrats-recalibrating-approach-to-transgender-issues) and [inclusion in school sports](https://19thnews.org/2026/08/trans-athletes-democrats-messaging-amy-acton-ohio/). In Massachusetts, Rep. Seth Moulton [came out against](https://www.nytimes.com/2024/11/07/us/politics/democrats-kamala-harris.html) transgender girls competing in school sports, just days after President Donald Trump\u2019s victory in November 2024. But Sen. Ed Markey, Moulton\u2019s primary opponent for the Senate seat, took the opposite approach, [stating his unwavering support](https://19thnews.org/2026/08/ed-markey-massachusetts-transgender-rights-support/) for transgender rights. Markey [polled ahead of Moulton](https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-polls/massachusetts) throughout 2026 and won the primary by [nearly 30 percentage points](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/massachusetts-senate-results). Public opinion [data shows](https://www.umass.edu/poll/about/reports/2026-08-massachusetts-public-opinion-poll) that two-thirds of Democratic likely primary voters in Massachusetts said they would be [less likely to back a candidate](https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/08/20/congress/dems-test-trans-rights-debate-01044963) who opposes transgender rights. Other Democrats, especially in Southern and Midwestern states, are taking a more conservative approach to transgender rights. In Texas, Democratic Senate nominee [James Talarico has reversed](https://www.advocate.com/politics/national/james-talarico-trans-athletes-2677841392) his [past support](https://www.statesman.com/story/news/2021/04/21/transgender-athletes-texas-state-house-bill/7307633002/) for transgender athletes. Months after his [March primary victory](https://19thnews.org/2026/03/texas-us-senate-primary-election-results-2026/) against Rep. Jasmine Crockett, [Talarico said](https://x.com/FoxNews/status/2097777051360964890) in a [Fox News interview](https://www.texastribune.org/2026/09/22/james-talarico-trans-climate-policy-changes-election-2026/) that he would \u201coppose trans athletes playing in girls\u2019 sports if it endangers safety or fairness.\u201d [According to scholars](https://doi.org/10.1215/23289252-10440734) and [media watchdog groups like GLAAD](https://glaad.org/fact-sheet-for-reporters-transgender-participation-in-sports/), appeals to \u201csafety\u201d and \u201cfairness\u201d are common anti-trans [dog whistles](https://www.them.us/news/sophie-cunningham-transphobia) that perpetuate [misinformation about transgender athletes](https://www.aclu.org/news/lgbtq-rights/four-myths-about-trans-athletes-debunked). The shift has not resulted in a polling bump for Talarico in his race against Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton. Polls of likely voters from July to early September gave Talarico an [average lead of 3.16 percentage points](https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-polls/texas). Polls since mid-September put his [average lead at 2.66 percentage points](https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-polls/texas). Competing campaign ads Republican-aligned super PACs are also [emphasizing transgender issues](https://www.axios.com/2026/09/09/musk-pac-trans-senate-ads). Elon Musk\u2019s America PAC has [spent US$100 million](https://jp.ibtimes.com/musk-america-pac-transgender-ads-104378) on anti-trans ads in battleground states like Texas, Ohio and Alaska. [Some of these ads](https://19thnews.org/2026/09/elon-musk-pac-anti-trans-political-ads/) feature people implied to be transgender women, though it is unclear whether they were [actors or AI-generated](https://www.salon.com/2026/10/06/weve-tracked-80m-of-spending-on-almost-170-ai-generated-political-ads-this-year-its-weird-partner/). With the [prevalence of anti-trans ads](https://www.facebook.com/ads/library/?active_status=active&ad_type=all&country=US&id=999683906465685&is_targeted_country=false&media_type=all&search_type=page&sort_data%5Bmode%5D=total_impressions&sort_data%5Bdirection%5D=desc&view_all_page_id=318975057964832), Democratic campaigns are [responding in different ways](https://www.cnn.com/2026/09/27/politics/democrats-trans-attacks-messaging-midterms). Some are [veering to the right](https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/09/30/democrats-stances-trans-issues-test-how-much-lgbtq-groups-will-tolerate/) by mirroring [GOP positions](https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/democrats-tight-midwest-us-races-split-trans-rights-strategy-2026-10-08/), like transgender exclusion from school sports. Ohio offers [clear examples](https://www.advocate.com/politics/states/ohio-democrats-anti-trans-messaging) of this gamble. In June, the state\u2019s Democratic gubernatorial candidate, Amy Acton, made an [anti-trans comment](https://www.wowktv.com/news/some-progressive-ohioans-frustrated-by-amy-actons-comment-on-trans-athletes/), [referring to transgender girls](https://web.archive.org/web/20260825234225/https://www.toledoblade.com/local/politics/2026/06/30/trans-sports/stories/20260630088) as \u201cboys.\u201d This quickly [drew criticism](https://www.statenews.org/government-politics/2026-08-03/democratic-ohio-governor-candidate-faces-a-backlash-over-lgbtq-comments) from statewide [LGBTQ+ groups](https://thebuckeyeflame.com/2026/09/04/equality-ohio-changes-grade-for-amy-acton-from-pending-to-b/) and [community leaders](https://web.archive.org/web/20260722134705/https://www.toledoblade.com/local/politics/2026/07/20/dem-candidate-threatens-vote-libertarian-over-actons-position-trans-sports/stories/20260720068). In Ohio\u2019s [9th congressional district](https://ballotpedia.org/Ohio%27s_9th_Congressional_District), Rep. Marcy Kaptur\u2019s campaign has run [multiple anti-trans ads](https://rollcall.com/2026/09/24/gop-revives-anti-trans-playbook-dems-turn-page/) focusing on parental rights and transgender children in school sports, saying that the congresswoman voted to keep [\u201ctransgender ideology\u201d](https://glaad.org/gender-ideology-definition-meaning-anti-lgbt-online-hate/) out of schools. The campaign also recently [ran mailers](https://www.foxnews.com/politics/imperiled-house-democrat-gets-ripped-left-right-after-flip-flop-transgender-sports) with this message. Kaptur\u2019s county party [condemned the ads](https://www.instagram.com/p/Ddl5q5oxS0L/), saying they were \u201cdisappointed and ashamed\u201d of her campaign. When the state party\u2019s Pride Caucus shared the county party\u2019s statement on social media, the [post was deleted](https://thebuckeyeflame.com/2026/09/28/insiders-ohio-democratic-party-is-censoring-anti-trans-ads-criticism/). Pride Caucus members [accused state party leadership of censorship](https://www.advocate.com/politics/states/ohio-dems-criticism-transphobic); weeks later, [transgender leaders](https://thebuckeyeflame.com/2026/10/08/two-trans-ohioans-resign-from-the-ohio-democratic-partys-pride-caucus/) from the caucus [resigned in protest](https://www.nbc4i.com/news/politics/vice-chair-of-ohio-pride-caucus-resigns/). In contrast, former Sen. Sherrod Brown, who is running against Republican Sen. Jon Husted, has focused his campaign ads on [affordability](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rzrV75TX160), [data center regulations](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8jBuRldidV8) and [the Epstein files](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PNQA4FuBqg0) without wavering on [his support](https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/10/06/ohio-democratic-candidates-amy-acton-and-marcy-kaptur-make-anti-transgender-remarks-face-backlash/) for transgender rights, as Acton and Kaptur have. After a [misleading super PAC ad](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=774rvrxrSH0) about transgender healthcare from the GOP\u2019s [Senate Leadership Fund](https://senateleadershipfund.org/about/), Brown\u2019s campaign [released a counter ad](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/19/us/politics/sherrod-brown-transgender-ad.html) correcting the claim before pivoting to affordability. Days later, the same GOP super PAC released [another ad](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WFISHnLCmms) targeting Brown. Reporting has confirmed that the ad, which features an [adult from Texas](https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/10/06/woman-accusing-sherrod-brown-of-forcing-transgender-surgeries-isnt-from-ohio-didnt-have-procedure-as-minor/) rather than Ohio, is [factually inaccurate](https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/10/fact-check-2-lies-in-ad-accusing-sherrod-brown-on-gender-affirming-care.html) and [misleading](https://www.cnn.com/2026/10/08/politics/fact-check-republicans-deceptive-ads-on-key-democratic-candidates-stances-on-gender-surgery-for-children). Recent polling suggests [Brown is outperforming](https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/10/05/poll-dems-open-lead-in-two-ohio-races/) his fellow Democrat Acton, who is running for governor, by 2 percentage points. While these polls don\u2019t isolate the effect of a single issue, Acton has faced criticism [from the public](https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/ohio-democratic-candidates-amy-acton-003441770.html), in addition to [state](https://thebuckeyeflame.com/2026/09/04/equality-ohio-changes-grade-for-amy-acton-from-pending-to-b/) and [national](https://www.advocate.com/politics/election/hrc-warns-dems-caving-transgender) LGBTQ+ organizations. Control of Congress Amid [rising prices on fuel and food](https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/03/diesel-prices-midwest-farmers), [upticks in unemployment](https://apnews.com/article/inflation-economy-trump-iran-mortgage-unemployment-d89373805d38dc6c80f0195fd2037c3a) and a [national hiring slowdown](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/what-a-hiring-slowdown-signals-about-the-state-of-the-u-s-economy), consumer confidence in the economy has dropped to [its lowest point](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/29/consumer-optimism-slides-sharply-as-fears-escalate-over-rising-prices-and-jobs.html) in over a decade. Nationally, many Democrats are trying to emphasize [these points](https://www.kff.org/public-opinion/poll-cost-of-health-care-and-gas-top-voters-midterm-concerns-democrats-hold-significant-advantage-on-health-care-costs-and-cost-of-living/). To counter that message, some Republicans continue to [emphasize \u201cculture war\u201d issues](https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/2026/10/gop-republicans-midterms-transgender/688837/) like transgender rights. Currently, Democrats outperform Republicans on the [generic congressional ballot](https://ballotpedia.org/Ballotpedia%27s_Polling_Index:_Generic_congressional_vote) \u2013 a [polling tool](https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/20/upshot/democrats-2018-congressional-elections-polling.html) that asks voters which party they\u2019d back without naming candidates. But messaging on transgender rights may be a tipping point for midterm voters. As a [growing electorate](https://www.nbcnews.com/nbc-out/out-politics-and-policy/lgbt-voters-away-from-trump-2024-election-record-change-rcna178939) of [12% of the U.S. adult population](https://www.hrc.org/press-releases/record-breaking-lgbtq-adults-now-12-of-population-greater-than-10-in-40-of-50-states) and with a voter turnout rate of [roughly 80%](https://comcastadvertising.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/LGBTQ-Voters-Report-2024.pdf), LGBTQ+ people are a [strong voting bloc](https://theconversation.com/lgbtq-voters-in-these-4-states-could-swing-the-2024-presidential-election-239656). Recent polling indicates that two-thirds of LGBTQ+ adults said they would be [extremely to somewhat comfortable](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2026/08/11/most-americans-are-uncomfortable-with-transgender-athletes-on-sports-teams-that-dont-match-their-birth-sex/) with trans athletes competing on sports teams that don\u2019t match their sex at birth. In battleground states, anti-trans messages [could cost votes](https://www.dataforprogress.org/blog/2024/10/23/voters-prefer-candidates-who-are-supportive-of-transgender-rights-think-recent-political-ads-have-gotten-mean-spirited-and-out-of-hand) and spell defeat for Democrats who rely on them. The risk is especially high in states like Ohio and Texas, where [races are close](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/2026-midterm-election-races/) and LGBTQ+ adults are estimated to make up [more than 11%](https://www.hrc.org/resources/lgbtq-population-estimates) of the adult population. In Texas, [Talarico\u2019s lead](https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2026/oct/02/midterm-races-control-congress-charts-visuals) could be undone by depressed turnout among the Lone Star State\u2019s [2.24 million LGBTQ+ adults](https://business.houstonlgbtchamber.com/in-the-news-2/Details/more-than-2-million-texas-adults-identify-as-lgbtq-new-report-says-355283). In Ohio, home to [roughly 883,000 LGBTQ+ adults](https://thebuckeyeflame.com/2026/10/04/how-many-lgbtq-adults-are-in-ohio/), Acton\u2019s [gubernatorial race](https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/10/02/still-anyones-ball-game-bowling-green-poll-finds-statistical-tie-in-ohios-race-for-governor/) and Kaptur\u2019s [congressional district](https://dccc.org/dccc-polling-memo-oh-09-is-a-dead-heat/) race are both statistical ties. With control of Congress [up for grabs](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/early-voting-begins-in-u-s-midterm-elections-that-could-flip-control-of-congress), campaign messaging missteps could have national implications. [Democratic Party](https://theconversation.com/topics/democratic-party-1884) [Texas](https://theconversation.com/topics/texas-14161) [Political rhetoric](https://theconversation.com/topics/political-rhetoric-14897) [Political ads](https://theconversation.com/topics/political-ads-17002) [Ohio](https://theconversation.com/topics/ohio-22314) [Primaries](https://theconversation.com/topics/primaries-25319) [Transgender athletes](https://theconversation.com/topics/transgender-athletes-37174) [Transgender rights](https://theconversation.com/topics/transgender-rights-37238) [Trans rights](https://theconversation.com/topics/trans-rights-50737) [Voting blocs](https://theconversation.com/topics/voting-blocs-92290) [Anti-trans legislation](https://theconversation.com/topics/anti-trans-legislation-102032) [LGBTQI+](https://theconversation.com/topics/lgbtqi-109739) [US midterms](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-midterms-129216) [2026 midterm elections](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-midterm-elections-178375) [Dorian Rhea Debussy, The Ohio State University](https://theconversation.com/democrats-distance-themselves-from-trans-rights-close-midterm-races-may-show-whether-that-pays-off-293173) Author [Dorian Rhea Debussy](https://theconversation.com/profiles/dorian-rhea-debussy-517479) Lecturer of Women's, Gender, and Sexuality Studies, The Ohio State University Disclosure statement Dorian Rhea Debussy does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [The Ohio State University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/the-ohio-state-university-759) provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.kwu7w6hyx](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.kwu7w6hyx)", "content_hash": "07a76a8a94bbcf3e24d01368931a165810ef3d6ae6fedf665a063eebf5d0d97b", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:54Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "969", "title": "Meet the \u2018independent voters\u2019 who will likely decide which party triumphs in the 2026 midterm\u00a0elections", "url": "https://theconversation.com/meet-the-independent-voters-who-will-likely-decide-which-party-triumphs-in-the-2026-midterm-elections-292103", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/meet-the-independent-voters-who-will-likely-decide-which-party-triumphs-in-the-2026-midterm-elections-292103", "text": "Independent voter turnout in midterm elections usually lags behind that of Republicans and Democrats. [adamkaz/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/americans-voting-in-an-election-royalty-free-image/1401779241?phrase=Independent%20voters%20US&searchscope=image%2Cfilm&adppopup=true) Meet the \u2018independent voters\u2019 who will likely decide which party triumphs in the 2026 midterm elections Published: October 8, 2026 8:12am EDT https://theconversation.com/meet-the-independent-voters-who-will-likely-decide-which-party-triumphs-in-the-2026-midterm-elections-292103 https://theconversation.com/meet-the-independent-voters-who-will-likely-decide-which-party-triumphs-in-the-2026-midterm-elections-292103 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article As midterm elections near, [Democrats enjoy advantages](https://www.realclearpolling.com/polls/state-of-the-union/generic-congressional-vote) in national \u201c[generic ballot](https://www.natesilver.net/p/generic-ballot-average-2026-nate-silver-bulletin-congress-polls)\u201d polling, suggesting they may secure majorities in the House of Representative or Senate, if not both chambers. But the reality is that partisan control on Capitol Hill will be determined by just a handful of competitive House and Senate contests. The Cook Political Report currently classifies only [22 House campaigns](https://www.cookpolitical.com/ratings/house-race-ratings) and only [seven Senate campaigns](https://www.cookpolitical.com/ratings/senate-race-ratings) as toss-ups, where either the Democrat or the Republican has a genuine chance of winning. By definition, neither party enjoys a clear advantage in these contests, so [both will look](https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/6124725-trump-senate-race-risk/) to augment their base support with [the votes of independents](https://www.aa.com.tr/en/americas/independent-voters-shift-toward-democrats-ahead-of-us-midterms-poll/4048549). As [a political science scholar](https://umdearborn.edu/people-um-dearborn/julio-borquez) focused on voting and elections, I believe that in the last few weeks before Election Day, those voters will feature prominently in campaign strategies. Independents are dissatisfied Midterm congressional elections serve as a [referendum on the president\u2019s performance](https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/politics-of-congressional-elections-9781538176733/). [Presidential approval](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2026/10/04/trump-approval-rating-midterms-republicans-polls/92061673007/) polls and [surveys about the economy](https://www.courthousenews.com/voters-sour-on-trump-economy-as-democrats-open-11-point-midterm-lead-in-new-polling/) indicate that independents do not favor [President Donald Trump or the Republican Party](https://www.ap.org/news-highlights/elections/2026/where-trump-has-lost-support-with-independents-according-to-ap-norc-polling/). A [September 2026 YouGov](https://d3nkl3psvxxpe9.cloudfront.net/documents/econTabReport_5WiMaRX.pdf) survey found that only 23% of independents approve of Trump\u2019s job as president, compared with 79% of Republicans and 2% of Democrats. This continues a [steady drop in independents\u2019 approval](https://apnorc.org/projects/trump-has-lost-support-from-independents-over-the-course-of-his-second-term/) of Trump throughout his second term. Independents have also soured on Trump\u2019s handling of the economy, with 73% in a September Fox News poll saying that Trump has [made the economy worse](https://www.foxnews.com/politics/fox-news-poll-squeezed-costs-frustrated-voters-turn-democrats?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email), a sentiment also expressed by 95% of Democrats and 26% of Republicans. Independents have also registered their discontent with the war in Iran. Only 22% of independents think the [war has been worth fighting](https://globalaffairs.org/research/public-opinion-survey/7-10-americans-say-iran-war-not-worth-fighting), a view expressed by 63% of Republicans and 8% of Democrats. And 83% of independents [do not think the Trump administration has a clear plan](https://www.foxnews.com/politics/fox-news-poll-71-think-trump-administration-lacks-plan-end-iran-war) for ending the war. Across these policy questions and performance assessments, Democrats and Republicans express clear and consistent support for their party\u2019s positions and candidates. Independents, not surprisingly, stake out something of a middle ground. But on key predictors of vote choice, especially presidential approval and economic evaluations, the opinions of [independents appear to be closer to those of Democrats](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/democrats-lead-in-new-poll-as-trump-looms-over-midterms) than to Republicans. Independents\u2019 current pro-Democratic slant is not dramatic, but it could be significant in crucial toss-up contests. One prominent Republican pollster, David Winston, has sounded an alarm about \u201c[unhappy independents](https://rollcall.com/2026/06/10/unhappy-independents-will-decide-this-election/)\u201d and their threat to continued GOP control of Congress. A recent poll shows that independents are very excited about voting in the midterms. [SDI Productions/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/rear-view-of-long-line-of-unrecognizable-diverse-royalty-free-image/1495396753?phrase=Independent%20voters%20US&searchscope=image%2Cfilm&adppopup=true) How to win the independent vote In closely fought House and Senate contests, both parties need to make inroads with independent voters, which poses some challenges. First, independent voters\u2019 turnout in midterms [usually lags behind](https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/politics-of-congressional-elections-9781538176733/) that of party members. So motivating and mobilizing independents will likely require added effort. It\u2019s worth noting, however, that a recent Emerson College poll showed [70% of independents were very excited](https://emersoncollegepolling.com/september-2026-national-poll-democrats/) about voting in the midterms. That\u2019s on par with the level of enthusiasm expressed by Republicans and Democrats. A second challenge for candidates is to develop campaign messaging that can connect with independent voters. For almost any candidate [running for office](https://www.routledge.com/The-Campaign-Manager-Running-and-Winning-Local-Elections/Shaw/p/book/9781032658322), a top priority is to [mobilize the party base](https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/campaign-craft-9781440837326/). This is especially critical in toss-up contests such as the Michigan or Texas Senate races, where the base alone won\u2019t guarantee victory. But the themes that motivate the party faithful may not sit well with independents, who tend to [recoil against partisan rancor](https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/independent-politics/23F3E25D6AE3A35E94993C7D4409FA5D). For instance, Republican voters in Michigan\u2019s 10th Congressional District might applaud Mike Bouchard\u2019s pledge to combat \u201c[radical socialism](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NFLAH1_lrNM),\u201d but independent voters might find it a bit overdone. By contrast, independent voters do appear to be [responsive to the more substantive policy priorities](https://doi.org/10.1177/106591290305600404) of congressional candidates. In other words, congressional candidates, especially those in highly competitive races in [Texas](https://www.texastribune.org/2026/10/06/texas-2026-elections-competitive-races-us-congress-state-legislature/), [Nebraska](https://www.reuters.com/world/us/most-republicans-tight-midterm-distance-themselves-trump-reuters-review-finds-2026-10-07/) and [Kansas](https://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/10/07/hottest-2026-midterm-races-historically-red-kansas-becomes-toss-up-updated-daily/?streamIndex=0), will need to employ sharply partisan rhetoric to fire up their base while also offering more sober, policy-based appeals to independents. Striking that balance might be especially difficult for Republican candidates. Trump clearly wants the midterms to be [a referendum on his record](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oco_Yf1iSoA), and he wants voters to imagine that [he is on the ballot](https://www.npr.org/2026/09/23/nx-s1-5977613/trump-poll-democrats-congress). It\u2019s an effort by the White House to increase turnout among so-called [low-propensity Trump voters](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/trump-promised-to-campaign-in-midterm-battlegrounds-hes-starting-in-deep-red-states-instead). These are people who have voted for Trump in presidential elections and continue to support the president, but who often do not vote in midterms. Putting Trump center stage might well excite this segment of Trump supporters, but it might also remind independents of their deepening reservations about the president. Democratic congressional candidates will also need to be careful about balancing partisan messages to their base with more measured appeals to independent voters. But Democrats don\u2019t have to share the spotlight with the president, so their balancing acts are not as delicate as those confronting their Republican opponents. At this point in the campaign, it appears that the Trump administration\u2019s record on issues that matter to Americans is pushing independents \u2013 in some cases gently, in some cases more decidedly \u2013 in a Democratic direction. [Republicans](https://theconversation.com/topics/republicans-1524) [Democrats](https://theconversation.com/topics/democrats-1525) [Independents](https://theconversation.com/topics/independents-3383) [Polling](https://theconversation.com/topics/polling-6608) [US Congress](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-congress-7016) [Donald Trump](https://theconversation.com/topics/donald-trump-10206) [Trump administration](https://theconversation.com/topics/trump-administration-33294) [Election polls](https://theconversation.com/topics/election-polls-79425) [Independent voters](https://theconversation.com/topics/independent-voters-103710) [2026 midterm elections](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-midterm-elections-178375) [Julio Borquez, University of Michigan-Dearborn](https://theconversation.com/meet-the-independent-voters-who-will-likely-decide-which-party-triumphs-in-the-2026-midterm-elections-292103) Author [Julio Borquez](https://theconversation.com/profiles/julio-borquez-1537724) Associate Professor of Political Science, University of Michigan-Dearborn Disclosure statement Julio Borquez does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Michigan-Dearborn](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-michigan-dearborn-5841) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.xsnjsjw6y](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.xsnjsjw6y)", "content_hash": "3c5ad9ecb6534b68ffcecb923994e57b7a07ae8f3f109f87a5867dfeee0422c2", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:55Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "970", "title": "How Trump\u2019s disaster aid delays and FEMA cuts could galvanize the \u2018movable middle\u2019 in the 2026\u00a0elections", "url": "https://theconversation.com/how-trumps-disaster-aid-delays-and-fema-cuts-could-galvanize-the-movable-middle-in-the-2026-elections-294236", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/how-trumps-disaster-aid-delays-and-fema-cuts-could-galvanize-the-movable-middle-in-the-2026-elections-294236", "text": "After a deadly tornado hit communities in rural southwest Michigan in March 2026, the state\u2019s request for federal disaster aid was denied. [Bill Pugliano/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/damage-and-debris-are-seen-along-tuttle-road-following-a-news-photo/2264736348) How Trump\u2019s disaster aid delays and FEMA cuts could galvanize the \u2018movable middle\u2019 in the 2026 elections Published: October 14, 2026 8:19am EDT https://theconversation.com/how-trumps-disaster-aid-delays-and-fema-cuts-could-galvanize-the-movable-middle-in-the-2026-elections-294236 https://theconversation.com/how-trumps-disaster-aid-delays-and-fema-cuts-could-galvanize-the-movable-middle-in-the-2026-elections-294236 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article When [Hurricane Isaias](https://theconversation.com/how-hurricane-isaias-broke-through-to-become-the-first-atlantic-hurricane-in-an-extremely-quiet-2026-season-294028) hit the Florida Panhandle on Oct. 9, 2026, it was just the latest weather disaster for Americans in a year that continues to break records. The [hottest U.S. summer on record](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/news/national-climate-202608) left millions of Americans facing life-threatening [heat waves](https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jul/05/heatwave-deaths-weather). Extreme heat and drought [set the stage for wildfires](https://www.drought.gov/news/drought-summer-2026-12-maps-and-graphs-2026-09-16). Flooding from [powerful storms and days of heavy rainfall](https://www.climatecentral.org/climate-shift-index-alert/climate-change-fueling-extreme-rainfall-flooding-hitting-communities-across-US) inundated communities in several states. And now [a \u201cSuper El Ni\u00f1o](https://wmo.int/news/media-centre/wmo-prepare-el-nino),\u201d on top of [already rising temperatures](https://berkeleyearth.org/global-temperature-report-for-2025/) and [sea levels](https://www.climate.gov/news-features/understanding-climate/climate-change-global-sea-level), is expected to make extreme events worse, at least into spring 2027. In the past, states relied heavily on federal aid and expertise to respond quickly to disasters. But in communities across the U.S., people recovering from floods, storms and wildfires in 2025 and 2026 have been left waiting much longer for federal aid \u2013 if it arrives at all. Hurricane Isaias hit the Florida Panhandle on Oct. 7, 2026, amid a quiet Atlantic hurricane season. Active hurricane seasons can push nationwide disaster costs well over $100 billion. [Jen Golbeck/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Image](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/laura-dean-cleans-out-her-home-that-was-severely-damaged-news-photo/2299093316) Will there be political consequences in the midterm elections for the slow aid and lack of government assistance? The answer to that question runs through the ways the Trump administration has changed disaster response and federal disaster aid. As a professor who has [written for years about civic engagement and climate policy](https://www.danarfisher.com/), I have reason to believe that voters\u2019 frustration with slow disaster aid may show up at the polls. Cuts to FEMA hobbled federal disaster help Even though the United States [is experiencing more disasters](https://www.climatecentral.org/climate-services/billion-dollar-disasters) as the climate changes, the federal programs meant to help Americans recover are being diminished. The Federal Emergency Management Agency has faced significant [cuts to funding and staff](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/administrations-arbitrary-unlawful-cuts-fema-funding-and-staff-threaten) since the Trump administration took office in January 2025. The administration [initially talked about abolishing the agency](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/administrations-arbitrary-unlawful-cuts-fema-funding-and-staff-threaten), arguing that [states and communities could deal](https://theconversation.com/if-fema-didnt-exist-could-states-handle-the-disaster-response-on-their-own-248758) with disasters on their own. It created a plan to cut FEMA\u2019s staffing by 50%, but in September 2026, a [judge found that plan to be unlawful](https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cand.448664/gov.uscourts.cand.448664.491.0.pdf). FEMA [lost over 17%](https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-26-108427.pdf) of its trained staff members in 2025 alone, including hundreds of people who were on call to respond to disasters. Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin [continues to defend](https://federalnewsnetwork.com/workforce/2026/09/mullin-defends-fema-staffing-cuts-after-court-ruling/) the administration\u2019s goal of a leaner workforce at the agency. The Trump administration also [withheld billions of dollars](https://www.eenews.net/articles/trump-quietly-halts-money-for-preventing-disaster-damage/) meant to help states reduce their disaster risk and protect homes, hospitals and other structures from storm damage. Lawsuits forced the administration to [restore some of the funds](https://www.floods.org/news-views/fema-news/fema-ordered-to-restart-bric-program/), but many communities are [still struggling](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/administrations-arbitrary-unlawful-cuts-fema-funding-and-staff-threaten) on their own. These changes have tangible effects on how well states are able to recover from disasters and how well they can prepare for the [disaster risks ahead](https://theconversation.com/two-key-ingredients-cause-extreme-storms-with-destructive-flooding-why-these-downpours-are-happening-more-often-254123) as the climate changes. Slower aid, if it arrives at all The Trump administration is also taking longer to approve emergency funding after disasters than past administrations did and denying more of those requests. In 2025 it [denied 26% of requests](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/administrations-arbitrary-unlawful-cuts-fema-funding-and-staff-threaten) for major disaster declarations, which unlock immediate federal aid. That was up from an average of 15% over the previous two decades. An independent [analysis of FEMA data](https://andrewrumbach.com/viz/fema-dashboard.html) exposes some clear patterns in funding delays under the Trump administration. The review found that major disaster declarations have taken an average of nearly 10 weeks to be issued during the current Trump administration, up from an average of three weeks or less over the previous two decades. The administration says it is simply [conducting more thorough reviews](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/trump-is-taking-longer-to-approve-disaster-aid-and-denying-democratic-states-more-frequently) when governors make these aid requests. But people caught in disasters don\u2019t have time to wait. Major disaster aid includes individual assistance, such as temporary housing, loans and repair grants, that disaster survivors need quickly to recover. The review also found that about 85% of the requests for federal assistance from states that went for Donald Trump in the 2024 election were eventually approved, while only about half of requests have been approved in states that voted for his Democratic opponent, Kamala Harris. A separate Associated Press report in July 2026 noted that [no other president](https://apnews.com/article/trump-fema-disaster-declarations-cf3f78f5c10d431340a638f6ced9746a) had such a sharp partisan divide in approving disaster declaration requests between Republican and Democratic governors since at least 1989, when a federal law set rules for disaster determinations. Disaster failures can mobilize political moderates A handful of studies, [including mine](https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/gejmv_v1), have found that people who have experienced a disaster often have higher levels of civic engagement. In my study, civic engagement included contacting elected officials, signing petitions, attending public meetings, participating in boycotts or joining protests, among other actions. We didn\u2019t look at the connection to voting patterns, but I believe there is good reason to expect our findings to extend to voting as well. The relationship between experiencing a disaster and level of civic engagement was strongest for political moderates who are sometimes referred to as the \u201c[movable middle](https://www.macfound.org/press/grantee-news/strategies-for-deepening-engagement-with-the-moveable-middle)\u201d \u2013 they could swing in either political direction if they have reason to do so. Given the [disasters America has seen](https://www.climatecentral.org/climate-services/billion-dollar-disasters) in recent years and the sharp drop in federal assistance, I expect that political moderates who become more civically engaged will play a significant role in the midterm elections this year. How FEMA cuts play out in a crisis Several recent disasters have shown what happens when the federal government isn\u2019t prepared to respond quickly. After the devastating July 4 floods that swept through Texas Hill Country in 2025, the Department of Homeland Security, which oversees FEMA, [waited three days](https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/library/files/fema-report/) before approving funding to send federal search-and-rescue teams, a Congressional report confirmed. Other funding approvals took an average of 12 days, leaving local and state programs to [fill critical gaps in disaster systems](https://www.news-press.com/press-release/story/31020/american-youthworks-receives-national-award-for-july-4th-flood-disaster-response/). In summer 2026, Texas was hit with floods again. The president approved [assistance for some of the counties after a seven-week delay](https://www.houstonpublicmedia.org/articles/news/texas/2026/09/04/561088/federal-disaster-aid-approved-7-weeks-after-south-central-texas-floods-bexar-windstorm-assessment-begins/). Other requests for assistance were still waiting months later. Flash flooding swept through Texas Hill Country again in 2026. Some FEMA aid was eventually approved but took seven weeks. [Brandon Bell/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/sheds-are-displaced-near-an-overpass-on-the-banks-of-the-news-photo/2286551169) FEMA [denied individual and statewide assistance](https://www.michigan.gov/mspnewsroom/news-releases/2026/08/06/fema-denies-the-state-of-michigans-appeal-for-federal-aid-following-deadly-sw-michigan-tornadoes) to Michigan after severe storms and tornadoes tore up homes across several rural communities in March 2026. With highly contested Senate races looming in both states, these actions by the Trump administration could color voters\u2019 views or contribute to a lack of enthusiasm for the party in power and even increase support for the opposition. A survey conducted by Pew Research Center in May 2025 found that the majority of Americans [believe that it is a good idea](https://www.pewresearch.org/science/2025/05/29/americans-views-on-how-to-address-the-impacts-of-extreme-weather/) for the federal government to provide financial assistance to help people rebuild after disasters in place at high risk of extreme weather. These findings are relatively consistent across political parties: 60% of those who identified as Republican or leaning Republican said they believed it was a good idea, as did 68% of those who identified as Democrat or leaning Democrat. Too little, too late? Trump recently started announcing disaster aid approvals in states consequential to the control of Congress in the midterm elections, including in Wisconsin and Ohio. In some cases, he [coupled his candidate endorsements with disaster funding announcements](https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nc/charlotte/news/2026/10/08/disaster-relief-funding-and-trump-s-midterm-rhetoric). I don\u2019t believe those late announcements will move the needle in his party\u2019s favor at this late date. [Natural disasters](https://theconversation.com/topics/natural-disasters-1243) [Extreme weather](https://theconversation.com/topics/extreme-weather-3799) [Hurricanes](https://theconversation.com/topics/hurricanes-9045) [Tornado](https://theconversation.com/topics/tornado-22606) [Wildfires](https://theconversation.com/topics/wildfires-28939) [Trump administration](https://theconversation.com/topics/trump-administration-33294) [FEMA](https://theconversation.com/topics/fema-42431) [US midterms](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-midterms-129216) [US voters](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-voters-163461) [2026 midterm elections](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-midterm-elections-178375) [Dana R. Fisher, American University School of International Service](https://theconversation.com/how-trumps-disaster-aid-delays-and-fema-cuts-could-galvanize-the-movable-middle-in-the-2026-elections-294236) Author [Dana R. Fisher](https://theconversation.com/profiles/dana-r-fisher-312250) Director of the Center for Environment, Community & Equity and Professor in the School of International Service, American University School of International Service Disclosure statement Dana R. Fisher does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [American University School of International Service](https://theconversation.com/institutions/american-university-school-of-international-service-2886) and [American University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/american-university-1187) provide funding as members of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.xfu7xcemy](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.xfu7xcemy)", "content_hash": "9648b0a2a82b0cbd2b09a59ca5162406a29c93c8199b90b57cbd55189439b22e", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:56Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "971", "title": "These state supreme court races could decide abortion access and trans\u00a0rights", "url": "https://theconversation.com/these-state-supreme-court-races-could-decide-abortion-access-and-trans-rights-292881", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/these-state-supreme-court-races-could-decide-abortion-access-and-trans-rights-292881", "text": "Whoever wins state judicial races in November will not just be interpreting the law. They will be writing the next chapter of it. [Stefania Pelfini la Waziya/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/voting-booths-with-no-people-royalty-free-image/2013953851?phrase=Vote%20US%20elections&searchscope=image,film&adppopup=true) These state supreme court races could decide abortion access and trans rights Published: October 7, 2026 8:40am EDT https://theconversation.com/these-state-supreme-court-races-could-decide-abortion-access-and-trans-rights-292881 https://theconversation.com/these-state-supreme-court-races-could-decide-abortion-access-and-trans-rights-292881 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article In the heated news coverage of what\u2019s at stake in [the 2026 midterms](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-midterms-184240), state supreme court races are drawing relatively little attention. Yet these are highly significant elections. Thirty-one states are holding supreme court elections in 2026, with voters nationwide picking judges to fill more than 60 open seats. As a law professor whose [research and writing focuses on reproductive health](https://law.utexas.edu/faculty/rachel-rebouche/), I\u2019m watching these races closely. That\u2019s because in the coming years these courts will have the final word on many consequential legal fights around abortion and trans healthcare. State supreme court judges make high-stakes decisions When the [U.S. Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022](https://rewirenewsgroup.com/2022/06/24/the-day-roe-died/), ending a half-century of federal constitutional protection for abortion, it handed this major issue back to the states. As a result, in addition to prompting ballot initiatives, the court\u2019s decision in [Dobbs v. Jackson Women\u2019s Health Organization](https://www.oyez.org/cases/2021/19-1392) has also spurred litigation in state courts. These cases ask judges to discern, for example, whether state constitutions [create a right to abortion](https://rewirenewsgroup.com/2026/09/14/indiana-supreme-court-hears-religious-freedom-challenge-to-state-abortion-law/) \u2013 or, at least, [prohibit restrictions on abortion access](https://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2026/sep/28/judge-hears-arguments-in-lawsuit-challenging/). Moreover, in weighing the [legality of abortion bans](https://19thnews.org/2026/09/texas-abortion-lawsuit-ken-paxton-tierra-walker/), state judges must determine specific issues of abortion access. [Idaho\u2019s high court recently approved](https://www.newsfromthestates.com/article/idaho-supreme-court-approves-state-officials-re-drafted-abortion-ballot-initiative-language) Republican officials\u2019 rewritten wording for a ballot initiative that would repeal the state\u2019s abortion ban; advocates for the initiative argue that the language misrepresents Idaho\u2019s abortion law. In Indiana, [the state Supreme Court recently heard arguments](https://www.newsfromthestates.com/article/indiana-supreme-court-weighs-limits-religious-exemptions-abortion-ban) over whether a religious freedom law creates an exception to the state\u2019s near-total abortion ban. The plaintiffs in that case argued that their faiths allowed or required the procedure in circumstances the ban currently forbids. Increasingly, the U.S. Supreme Court has been handing questions concerning transgender rights back to states, too. In June 2025, the Supreme Court ruled 6-3 in [United States v. Skrmetti](https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/24pdf/23-477_2cp3.pdf) that Tennessee\u2019s ban on puberty blockers and hormone therapy for transgender minors does not count as sex discrimination under the U.S. Constitution. Consequently, that and similar bans only need to pass the lowest level of judicial review; they no longer have to pass the higher bar of \u201cstrict scrutiny.\u201d This means lower courts can essentially rubber-stamp the bans that state legislatures have passed. In fact, the [Skrmetti decision cleared the way](https://rewirenewsgroup.com/2025/06/24/how-the-supreme-court-blessed-anti-trans-discrimination/) for the roughly two dozen states with similar bans to keep enforcing them. Skrmetti did not, however, require any state to pass such a law. And it does not address how far the ruling extends to adults or whether it applies to other related aspects of gender-affirming care, such as insurance coverage. In other words, the people who now decide whether abortion restrictions stay on the books or whether a minor can get gender-affirming care are, in many cases, not justices sitting on the U.S. Supreme Court. In many cases, they will be state supreme court judges. State supreme court judges will decide whether abortion restrictions stay on the books and whether a minor can get gender-affirming care. [wildpixel/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/election-law-royalty-free-image/1911373147?phrase=Judicial%20races%20US%20elections&searchscope=image%2Cfilm&adppopup=true) North Carolina, Georgia, Michigan, Montana have races to watch In Montana, judicial races could change the ideological bent of the state\u2019s top court. Judicial elections in the state are technically nonpartisan, but Republicans see a chance to retake the state Supreme Court with judges they endorse. In 2023, its Democrat-endorsed majority [ruled that the state constitution protects abortion](https://law.justia.com/cases/montana/supreme-court/2023/da-22-0207-0.html) rights. It also determined that attempts to stop one class of health providers \u2013 advanced practice registered nurses \u2013 from performing abortions violated those rights. Judges who upheld restrictions affecting residents\u2019 reproductive rights will face voters this fall in [Alabama, Arizona, Florida, North Dakota, Oklahoma and Texas](https://boltsmag.org/your-state-by-state-guide-to-the-2026-supreme-court-elections/). In some of these races, abortion is a live \u2013 and legally dynamic \u2013 issue. [North Carolina\u2019s high court](https://www.nccourts.gov/courts/supreme-court) also leans heavily conservative. This year one of just two Democrats on the seven-judge panel \u2013 incumbent Democrat Anita Earls \u2013 is up for reelection. If she loses, the GOP would gain a near-total supermajority on North Carolina\u2019s Supreme Court. That outcome would likely cement the party\u2019s edge on key political matters for years to come. Michigan looks like the mirror image of North Carolina. All justices there are officially nonpartisan, but [Democrat-backed justices hold a 6-1 majority](https://www.michiganpublic.org/politics-government/2026-08-06/have-questions-about-judicial-elections-this-will-help) on the seven-member court. Chief Justice Megan Cavanagh and Justice Noah Hood \u2013 both Democrats \u2013 are up for reelection this fall. Democrats are hoping to retain their edge, in part to keep abortion protected. Voters [enshrined abortion rights directly into Michigan\u2019s Constitution](https://www.legislature.mi.gov/Laws/MCL?objectName=mcl-Article-I-28) in 2022, and the state\u2019s highest court has already interpreted that amendment to dismiss [abortion-related restrictions](https://www.wkar.org/wkar-news/2023-05-11/michigan-supreme-court-dismisses-last-of-abortion-related-cases). Ohio protects abortion, but courts will decide how much Ohio is a powerful example of what is at stake in this year\u2019s state supreme court races. Republicans hold a 6-1 majority on the state\u2019s high court, a dominant position built over successive election cycles in 2022 and 2024. In the 2026 midterms, Ohio\u2019s only Democratic state Supreme Court justice, Jennifer Brunner, is [defending her seat against a Republican challenger](https://ballotpedia.org/Ohio_Supreme_Court_elections,_2026). If Brunner loses, Ohio\u2019s highest court could become a unanimous conservative bench \u2013 one that will likely determine the outcome of high-profile cases related to both abortion access and gender-affirming care. Ohio voters [approved a constitutional amendment in 2023 protecting abortion access](https://apnews.com/article/ohio-abortion-amendment-election-2023-fe3e06747b616507d8ca21ea26485270). The amendment establishes that individuals have the right to make their own reproductive decisions. The language covers contraception, fertility treatment, continuing a pregnancy, miscarriage care and abortion up to fetal viability. It also asserts that the state generally cannot interfere with these decisions. Now, lower courts are [litigating whether restrictions on abortion access](https://www.statenews.org/government-politics/2026-08-20/both-sides-of-abortion-debate-say-latest-ohio-supreme-court-action-on-amendment-wont-be-the-last) \u2013 for instance, the state\u2019s 24-hour waiting period and ban on telehealth for medication abortion \u2013 can survive scrutiny under that amendment. The Ohio Supreme Court may eventually decide that case. In my assessment, an even more conservative Ohio court might be inclined to read the voter-approved abortion amendment as narrowly as possible. Ohio\u2019s Supreme Court is similarly poised to make big decisions on trans rights. For example, it could uphold the state\u2019s ban on gender-affirming care for transgender minors. That would reverse [a 2025 appellate ruling](https://statecourtreport.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/ohio-court-strikes-down-ban-gender-affirming-care-minors) that had struck down the law, which was passed over the veto of Republican Gov. Mike DeWine. Even if they don\u2019t change the partisan makeup of these courts, elections for state supreme court seats in places such as Ohio may matter for the scope and breadth of rulings that claw back individual rights. Or they may foretell how a conservative near-supermajority might lock in abortion and trans restrictions for a generation. Whoever wins these judicial races in November will not just be interpreting the law. In a real sense, they will be writing the next chapter of it. This story was produced in collaboration with [Rewire News Group](http://www.rewirenewsgroup.com), a nonprofit newsroom that covers reproductive health. [Abortion](https://theconversation.com/topics/abortion-1130) [Republicans](https://theconversation.com/topics/republicans-1524) [Democrats](https://theconversation.com/topics/democrats-1525) [Transgender](https://theconversation.com/topics/transgender-1693) [Abortion law](https://theconversation.com/topics/abortion-law-4639) [US Supreme Court](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-supreme-court-5362) [US law](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-law-5363) [Judges](https://theconversation.com/topics/judges-11567) [Michigan](https://theconversation.com/topics/michigan-13508) [Idaho](https://theconversation.com/topics/idaho-25675) [Indiana](https://theconversation.com/topics/indiana-26509) [Transgender rights](https://theconversation.com/topics/transgender-rights-37238) [Montana](https://theconversation.com/topics/montana-39078) [Abortion bans](https://theconversation.com/topics/abortion-bans-84873) [State law](https://theconversation.com/topics/state-law-127371) [Rachel Rebouch\u00e9, The University of Texas at Austin](https://theconversation.com/these-state-supreme-court-races-could-decide-abortion-access-and-trans-rights-292881) Author [Rachel Rebouch\u00e9](https://theconversation.com/profiles/rachel-rebouche-1389508) Professor of Law, The University of Texas at Austin Disclosure statement Rachel Rebouch\u00e9 does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ujxdqa3m4](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.ujxdqa3m4)", "content_hash": "423dce736cd375d8e8611f910ce6af57e2819f7bddf6b3af0a36c5f5cef06ee6", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:20:57Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "972", "title": "Iran war pushes diesel \u2013 the economy\u2019s lifeblood \u2013 to record high prices, with no relief on the\u00a0horizon", "url": "https://theconversation.com/iran-war-pushes-diesel-the-economys-lifeblood-to-record-high-prices-with-no-relief-on-the-horizon-292049", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/iran-war-pushes-diesel-the-economys-lifeblood-to-record-high-prices-with-no-relief-on-the-horizon-292049", "text": "Diesel fuel prices are at all-time highs in the United States. [Paul Weaver/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/sign-displays-the-prices-of-regular-gasoline-and-diesel-at-news-photo/2294723764) Iran war pushes diesel \u2013 the economy\u2019s lifeblood \u2013 to record high prices, with no relief on the horizon Published: September 17, 2026 2:45pm EDT https://theconversation.com/iran-war-pushes-diesel-the-economys-lifeblood-to-record-high-prices-with-no-relief-on-the-horizon-292049 https://theconversation.com/iran-war-pushes-diesel-the-economys-lifeblood-to-record-high-prices-with-no-relief-on-the-horizon-292049 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Diesel fuel prices in the U.S. continue to break all-time records, [hitting $6.28 a gallon](https://www.eia.gov/petroleum/gasdiesel/) as a national average on Sept. 14, 2026, and they aren\u2019t likely to come down anytime soon. Because diesel is the fuel for freight transportation, farming and construction \u2013 and is related to a key heating fuel \u2013 the effects are set to spread across the entire U.S. economy. The inflation-adjusted price of diesel in late August was [about as high as it was in 2022](https://usenergydata.org/charts/gasoline-prices/?tab=chart&fuel=diesel&time=2011..2026&areas=NUS%7ESCA) and has likely risen further. There are still three factors that we, scholars of U.S. and [global energy markets](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=DAwwVkwAAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao) and [supply chains](https://payneinstitute.mines.edu/project/jamie-webster/), see that set the stage for prices to remain high and potentially climb even further. Two of these elements \u2013 environmental protection regulations on oil refineries and [Russia\u2019s war with Ukraine](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/14/trump-ukraine-russia-diesel-prices-oil.html) \u2013 have been building tension for years. But the situation has been pushed into a crisis by the [U.S. war against Iran](https://www.wsj.com/business/energy-oil/americans-have-spent-100-billion-more-on-fuel-during-the-iran-war-377952a1), which has closed the Strait of Hormuz to a large percentage of oil and refined products shipments for more than six months. Amid that crisis, U.S. oil refineries are [making lots of money](https://financialpost.com/pmn/business-pmn/us-refiners-see-billions-in-profits-from-global-fuel-crunch). In mid-September 2026, diesel prices were up 67% over [September 2025\u2019s monthly national average of $3.75 a gallon](https://www.bts.gov/newsroom/motor-fuel-prices-september-2025-0), and they have risen sharply since. In January 2026, before the U.S. and Israel first attacked Iran in late February, the average diesel price in the U.S. was lower, [at $3.52 a gallon](https://www.bts.gov/newsroom/motor-fuel-prices-january-2026-1) \u2013 meaning that diesel prices have jumped more than 78% since the war began. In part, that\u2019s because the country, and the world, have been losing diesel refining capacity for many years and can\u2019t just ramp up production to fill the shortages. Diesel prices hit truckers, who deliver a vast range of products to consumers and markets across the nation. [Brandon Bell/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/person-pumps-diesel-at-a-pilot-gas-station-on-september-14-news-photo/2295291579) Refinery capacity drops Starting in 2006, U.S. federal regulations were phased in that required refiners to produce diesel fuel with far less sulfur than had previously been allowed. The regulations were intended to reduce [air pollution and acid rain precursors mainly from trucks and buses](https://www.epa.gov/archive/epapages/newsroom_archive/newsreleases/f8c41d888050eb7c85257188006f8f47.html). The [resulting fuel was much cleaner](https://www.farmprogress.com/management/diesel-fuel-s-successful-transition-to-ultra-low-sulfur). In 2014, the rules were applied to locomotives and marine vehicles. U.S. refineries had to install expensive equipment to comply, which took [years and billions of dollars](https://www.ogj.com/refining-processing/article/17224535/questions-linger-as-refiners-cut-us-diesel-sulfur-content). This, in turn, increased costs [between 5 and 9 cents per gallon](https://www.ezoil.com/resources-diesel-ultra-low-sulfur-diesel-basics). And the fuel [contains slightly less energy](https://www.ezoil.com/resources-diesel-ultra-low-sulfur-diesel-basics) per unit of volume \u2013 so a larger quantity of low-sulfur diesel is required to meet the demand. Only one refinery, [in Galveston, Texas](https://inspectioneering.com/news/2019-08-28/8725/texas-storage-terminal-operator-plans-to-build-its-own-refinery), has been built [in the past 50 years](https://www.opis.com/resources/energy-market-news-from-opis/us-total-refining-capacity-down-again-in-2025-after-two-refinery-shutdowns-eia/), and at 45,000 barrels per day it meets less than 0.2% of U.S. demand. In 2025, two [refineries shut down](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=67807) due to poor economic performance. And since 2020, more than a dozen others have [converted to producing renewable diesel](https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/oil-and-gas/our-insights/converting-refineries-to-renewable-fuels-no-simple-switch) to take advantage of federal and state biofuel incentives, but that conversion can cut the amount of actual fuel they produce at least in half, according to our estimates. As a result, the remaining refineries are running flat out. Crude going into refineries reached [about 17.5 million barrels a day](https://www.agbull.com/diesel-gets-a-small-reprieve-but-refinery-slack-is-nearly-gone/) in late August, and refineries were operating at nearly 98% of their maximum capacity \u2013 the highest level since 2018. Even so, [output of distillate](https://www.eia.gov/tools/glossary/index.php?id=Distillate%20fuel%20oil), which includes diesel and related fuels such as heating oil and jet fuel, was lower than in 2025. Distillate inventories held by refiners, distributors and retailers [fell to about 103 million barrels](https://rbnenergy.com/daily-posts/blog/100bbl-diesel-crack-or-how-2026-exposed-fragility-global-refining) by late August, the lowest for that point in the calendar since 1951. A global squeeze As U.S. inventories of diesel fell in August, the [country\u2019s diesel exports hit a record high](https://oilprice.com/Latest-Energy-News/World-News/US-Diesel-Exports-Hit-Record-High-as-Domestic-Stockpiles-Slide.html) as the global market sought to fill supplies normally provided by Russia. Historically the world\u2019s [second-largest diesel exporter](https://universaltrades.co/blog/top-10-diesel-exporting-countries-en/) after the United States, Russia [banned exports of diesel on July 9, 2026](https://www.spglobal.com/energy/en/news-research/latest-news/crude-oil/072726-russia-extends-gasoline-export-ban-until-end-of-2026/), to ensure supplies for its military. In September, the country extended the export ban until the end of 2026 in the wake of [Ukrainian drone strikes](https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2026/09/04/diesel-hits-record-585-gallon-iran-war-drags/) on Russian refineries. Middle Eastern refiners, also key to global diesel supplies, have scaled back production because of [disruptions from the war with Iran](https://www.reuters.com/commentary/reuters-open-interest/trumps-ukraine-energy-truce-wont-fix-global-diesel-crunch-2026-09-15/). Together, these forces mean the [supply for diesel is very tight](https://www.eia.gov/petroleum/gasdiesel/). On Sept. 15, 2026, U.S. Senate Majority Leader [John Thune proposed the U.S. ban diesel exports](https://www.morningstar.com/news/marketwatch/20260915132/top-senate-republican-floats-a-diesel-export-ban-as-prices-soar-it-might-not-work) to increase domestic availability. Refiners seek dollars Even before the crisis in the Persian Gulf, the U.S. Energy Information Administration reported several factors were pushing diesel prices upward, including [large exports to Europe](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=67184), a [cold winter in the Northeast](https://www.eia.gov/petroleum/heatingoilpropane/#itn-tabs-2) in 2025-26 and [high freight demand](https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/aeo/narrative/). With the war continuing, refinery companies are taking advantage of the low supply \u2013 and their [inability to make a whole lot more diesel](https://rbnenergy.com/daily-posts/blog/us-refiners-already-running-hard-relief-diesel-remains-elusive) \u2013 to charge [more than ever](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68104) for diesel fuel. By late August, short-term profit margins for making diesel \u2013 called \u201c[crack spreads](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/includes/crackspread_explain.php)\u201d in the industry \u2013 [surpassed $100 per barrel](https://finance.yahoo.com/energy/articles/100-diesel-cracks-signal-much-200000054.html) for the first time ever, clearly showing demand for the fuel is well outpacing supply, as well as demand for crude oil itself. What this means is that truckers, drivers and shipping companies are paying more for diesel \u2013 costs that are passed on to consumers. At the same time, [refinery companies are seeing large profits](https://financialpost.com/pmn/business-pmn/us-refiners-see-billions-in-profits-from-global-fuel-crunch). High diesel prices hit everyone The vast majority of the diesel fuel used in the U.S. is for transportation, [mostly trucking](https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/blogs/energysource/the-diesel-shock-is-here-which-us-states-will-be-hit-the-hardest). Other significant users are the railroad, agriculture, construction and marine industries. A [congressional Joint Economic Committee analysis](https://www.kttc.com/2026/09/07/diesel-prices-drive-up-harvest-costs-minnesota-iowa-farmers/) in September 2026 found that higher diesel prices added more than $1.4 billion to the cost of planting major U.S. crops in 2026 versus 2025, a 63% increase, with Illinois, Iowa and Minnesota hit hardest. Because nearly every retail item travels by truck at some point, the diesel spike also feeds slowly into broader consumer inflation. And there\u2019s one more hit coming: Heating oil, which is chemically closely related to diesel, is a [key heating fuel](https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/report/perspectives/2025/10-winterfuels/2025_winter_fuels.pdf) in the northeastern U.S. The latest projection from the Energy Information Administration expects national distillate inventories to [fall below 100 million barrels](https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/) in September and stay at that level through much of 2027. That\u2019s lower than it has been since 2021, and heating oil prices for the coming winter are [already up 30% or more](https://abc17news.com/money/cnn-business-consumer/2026/09/14/get-ready-for-a-30-jump-in-heating-oil-costs-this-winter-as-iran-war-drives-up-prices/). The Middle East conflict is having wide-reaching implications. While crude oil prices are now above $100 per barrel, it is diesel, the fuel that much of the economy runs on, that is feeling the greatest stress. And as refiners run flat out, any interruption would reduce production, as happened after a power outage at a [Mobil refinery in Illinois](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/exxon-mobils-joliet-refinery-reports-upset-due-power-outage-2026-09-14/) on Sept. 13. With falling stockpiles, even a sudden end to the Iran war is unlikely to yield significantly lower prices anytime soon. [Agriculture](https://theconversation.com/topics/agriculture-24) [Transport](https://theconversation.com/topics/transport-39) [Economy](https://theconversation.com/topics/economy-254) [Energy](https://theconversation.com/topics/energy-662) [Freight](https://theconversation.com/topics/freight-2008) [Rail](https://theconversation.com/topics/rail-2023) [Logistics](https://theconversation.com/topics/logistics-2059) [Air pollution](https://theconversation.com/topics/air-pollution-2452) [Oil refining](https://theconversation.com/topics/oil-refining-3486) [Diesel](https://theconversation.com/topics/diesel-5351) [Clean Air Act](https://theconversation.com/topics/clean-air-act-18340) [Oil industry](https://theconversation.com/topics/oil-industry-29103) [Diesel fuel](https://theconversation.com/topics/diesel-fuel-66906) [US oil industry](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-oil-industry-85815) [US agriculture](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-agriculture-95098) [Oil refineries](https://theconversation.com/topics/oil-refineries-117074) [US economy](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-economy-124249) [Trucking](https://theconversation.com/topics/trucking-137648) [Grocery prices](https://theconversation.com/topics/grocery-prices-143304) [Iran war fallout](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-war-fallout-185612) [Iran war](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-war-185765) [Morgan Bazilian, Jamie Webster, Colorado School of Mines](https://theconversation.com/iran-war-pushes-diesel-the-economys-lifeblood-to-record-high-prices-with-no-relief-on-the-horizon-292049) Authors [Morgan Bazilian](https://theconversation.com/profiles/morgan-bazilian-798291) Professor of Public Policy and Director of the Payne Institute, Colorado School of Mines [Jamie Webster](https://theconversation.com/profiles/jamie-webster-2765130) Fellow, Payne Institute for Public Policy, Colorado School of Mines Disclosure statement In addition to being a fellow at the Payne Center, Jamie Webster also works in the Energy practice at Boston Consulting Group, which assists companies around the world across all energy types. Morgan Bazilian does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.5eg7mejwm](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.5eg7mejwm)", "content_hash": "2161b409857ac4851a5df199eda3a9e12241a7ddda01cda829d69aad8d23f3af", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:00Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "973", "title": "Houthi advance boosts Iran\u2019s leverage over the US and Saudi Arabia \u2013 but may cost rebel group its legitimacy back in\u00a0Yemen", "url": "https://theconversation.com/houthi-advance-boosts-irans-leverage-over-the-us-and-saudi-arabia-but-may-cost-rebel-group-its-legitimacy-back-in-yemen-291930", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/houthi-advance-boosts-irans-leverage-over-the-us-and-saudi-arabia-but-may-cost-rebel-group-its-legitimacy-back-in-yemen-291930", "text": "Yemen\u2019s Houthi armed forces patrol the capital Sanaa on Sept. 12, 2026. [Mohammed Huwaish/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/yemens-houthi-armed-forces-patrol-as-yemeni-prisoners-loyal-news-photo/2294384190?adppopup=true) Houthi advance boosts Iran\u2019s leverage over the US and Saudi Arabia \u2013 but may cost rebel group its legitimacy back in Yemen Published: September 17, 2026 2:08pm EDT https://theconversation.com/houthi-advance-boosts-irans-leverage-over-the-us-and-saudi-arabia-but-may-cost-rebel-group-its-legitimacy-back-in-yemen-291930 https://theconversation.com/houthi-advance-boosts-irans-leverage-over-the-us-and-saudi-arabia-but-may-cost-rebel-group-its-legitimacy-back-in-yemen-291930 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article It took just a few days for Houthi forces to [seize most of Yemen\u2019s Red Sea coast](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/15/houthi-rebels-seize-red-sea-hanish-islands-saudi-oil-warning), including the [shores of the Bab el-Mandeb Strait](https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/9/13/red-sea-nations-watch-as-houthis-seize-bab-al-mandeb-strait), a crucial Red Sea maritime choke point. The offensive started on Sept. 3, 2026, with a push to cut the strategically important road linking the city of Taiz to the port of Mokha. By Sept. 11, Mokha, the town of Dhubab and the island of Mayyun, in the mouth of the strait, [were under Houthi control](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4g7vr0lngwo). The Yemeni rebel group seized [some 2,100 square miles of territory](https://abcnews.com/International/houthis-yemen-gains-put-control-strategic-strait-key/story?id=136485092), forcing [more than 100,000 people](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/15/more-than-100000-displaced-as-they-flee-violence-in-yemen-un) to flee, according to the U.N., with many making the risky journey to nearby Djibouti by boats. It is the largest shift in the front lines of Yemen\u2019s civil war since a [U.N.-brokered truce](https://news.un.org/en/story/2022/04/1116282) froze them in April 2022. Most of the coverage has interpreted the offensive [as a new lever for Iran](https://www.economist.com/middle-east-and-africa/2026/09/13/the-houthis-shock-advance-in-yemen-gives-iran-new-leverage) \u2013 a key ally of the Houthis \u2013 in its ongoing war with the United States, or a [fresh threat to Saudi Arabia](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/houthis-strike-saudi-targets-anew-talks-over-strait-hormuz-stall-2026-09-15/) and global shipping. Such framing is not wrong, but it is incomplete. The more consequential question, I believe, is what the offensive means for Yemen itself, and where it leaves both a civil war that had quietly stalemated for years and the Houthis\u2019 own unresolved problem of legitimacy. Why advance now? Years of Yemeni civil strife entered a new phase in 2014 when [Houthis seized the capital Sanaa](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-29380668) from the increasingly unpopular government of President Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi. A decade of subsequent fighting between the rebel group and a Saudi-led coalition [brought famine](https://wfpusa.org/place/yemen/) and destruction across the impoverished nation. But since 2022 the conflict has largely been stalled, with Houthis controlling much of the country\u2019s northwest and the Saudi-backed forces remaining dominant in parts of the country\u2019s south and east. So why have the Houthis advanced now? The group\u2019s military spokesman, Yahya Saree, [said it was triggered](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/iran-backed-houthis-seize-island-at-entrance-to-red-sea-threatening-saudi-oil-export) by years of Saudi aggression and blockade. Such grievances are not invented. U.N. agencies have documented real, sustained damage from [coalition restrictions on Yemen\u2019s ports and airspace](https://news.un.org/en/story/2017/11/570122) over the course of the civil war. The Houthis also trace the latest round of fighting to a [July 13 strike on the Sanaa airport\u2019s runway](https://www.nbcnews.com/world/yemen/yemen-saudi-arabia-air-strike-sanna-airport-houthi-rebels-rcna587273) by Yemeni government forces with Saudi backing. That attack forced an Iranian aircraft carrying officers of Iran\u2019s powerful [Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps](https://www.cfr.org/backgrounders/irans-revolutionary-guards) to turn back. The Houthis declared their ceasefire with Saudi Arabia over that day, [struck the airport of the Saudi city of Abha](https://apnews.com/article/yemen-houthis-airport-saudi-strikes-7bddae3006304df8bf7f89e980006b33) within hours and announced a naval blockade a week later. They then opened a missile campaign in early August, well before the ground offensive on Sept. 3. But it is hard to read the Houthis\u2019 sustained campaign and the landings on Bab el-Mandeb\u2019s islands as simple payback for an airport strike. Rather, something more deliberate appears to have been in motion. Another factor is Iran. Since Tehran [closed the Strait of Hormuz](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/why-irans-disruption-of-the-strait-of-hormuz-matters/) in March following the U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran, Saudi Arabia has routed crude oil through Bab el-Mandeb at roughly eight times the rate it did a year earlier. Houthi control of that strait hands Iran a mirror-image choke point to the one it has over Hormuz. Indeed, Yemeni security sources say a [senior Iranian commander, Abdul Reza Shahlai](https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/mena/2026/09/14/shadow-man-irgc-flight-and-a-secret-meeting-irans-hand-in-houthi-offensive-revealed/), was on the ground overseeing part of the latest operation in September. There\u2019s also a potential economic incentive for the Houthis. Since March, Tehran has been [charging vessels up to US$2 million a ship](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-24/iran-charges-some-ships-hormuz-transit-fees-for-safe-passage) for safe passage through Hormuz. The Houthis may likewise see economic leverage as a way to bolster a movement long battered by war. Forcing Riyadh back to that table Perhaps the most important factor, however, is that the Houthis reasoned conditions on the ground were increasingly favorable for an offensive. Their already fractured opposition has become even more divided in the country\u2019s south, particularly after a [Saudi rift with the United Arab Emirates in late 2025](https://www.foreignaffairs.com/united-states/real-risks-saudi-uae-feud) led the latter to withdraw its support for anti-Houthi forces in the south altogether. With the broader region engulfed in conflict and Saudi Arabia distracted, the Houthis made their move against a coastal Yemeni force that has been left exposed. First and foremost, the Houthis are a Yemeni movement whose fight has always been about power inside Yemen. As such, Houthi officials have been careful to frame the Bab el-Mandeb operation as a national, sovereign act rather than one done for its Iranian ally; they are adamant that they are [not a mere Iranian proxy](https://nationalinterest.org/blog/middle-east-watch/why-the-houthis-are-not-an-iranian-proxy). As recently as 2023, Saudi Arabia and the Houthis had been negotiating a [comprehensive nationwide ceasefire](https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/war-yemen) that would have, in part, ended Saudi restrictions and sanctions on Houthi governance. But the plan was never implemented following the [Red Sea crisis of late 2023 and 2024](https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099253002102539789/pdf/IDU10b8b59671dbc814cfc19c4a1299ff54854ba.pdf), when the Houthis ramped up attacks on commercial shipping in response to the Israeli war in Gaza. Riyadh has shown little urgency to revive the diplomatic process since. The Houthis may now be betting that escalating inside Yemen, where the cost to Saudi Arabia is high, is the surest way to force Riyadh back to that table. This time, they may believe they have leverage to receive the political recognition they have wanted all along, not just the [economic terms Saudi Arabia was considering](https://south24.net/news/news.php?nid=5481) in 2023. A Houthi-provided image of vans carrying military supplies from Saudi Arabia on Sept. 7, 2026, in Hadramaut province, Yemen. [Houthi Media Center via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/in-this-handout-screen-grab-captured-from-a-video-provided-news-photo/2294029110?adppopup=true) What the Strait operation cannot buy The seizure of the Bab el-Mandeb Strait and the ongoing fighting in the country come with serious risks for the Houthis, however. The leverage the Houthis are gaining vis-a-vis Saudi Arabia and on the battlefield in Yemen could come at the cost of their broader standing in Yemen. No government or entity has ever managed to hold both military and political supremacy over the entire territory of Yemen [in modern history](https://www.britannica.com/place/Yemen/Government-and-society). While the Houthis have achieved military supremacy in the vast majority of the northwest, they have never pursued anything resembling a political bargain with the internationally backed government or even other parties. Their movement has always prized military dominance over political inclusion. The latest coastal offensive is no exception. According to Houthi-affiliated news sources, within days of taking Mokha the group [had moved to reopen the port\u2019s customs house](https://www.yemenipress.net/archives/439895), [cut port fees and customs duties by as much as half](https://masa-press.net/2026/09/10/%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B4%D8%A7%D8%A6%D8%B1-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D9%85%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%A1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AE%D8%A7-%D8%AA%D8%AE%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%AA/) and [launched a drive to hand out 20,000 food baskets across Mokha and the neighboring coastal districts](https://www.yemenipress.net/archives/439918), while its authority in Sanaa [offered a general amnesty](https://www.ypagency.net/890001) to the fighters who had held the coast against it. A week later, its leader, Abdulmalik al-Houthi, [told southerners directly](https://www.ypagency.net/891898) that his forces were not targeting them, only the troops Saudi Arabia had brought in. That is legitimacy performed through governance after a military conquest, more public relations than policy. It certainly isn\u2019t the political bargain that could hold Yemen together for long. Moreover, a decade of Houthi rule in Sanaa suggests that they have no desire to work with others. Their modus operandi has been to rule by guns, not coalition. More broadly, seizing the Arabian Peninsula\u2019s other choke point and banning Saudi shipping through it makes the Houthis look, to much of the world and no small part of Yemen, like Tehran\u2019s instrument \u2013 whatever their own objectives actually are. And it invites the Houthis\u2019 enemies to respond with force, potentially weakening the group\u2019s capabilities. Saudi Arabia is not a neutral party waiting patiently for good sense to prevail. It has prosecuted the war in Yemen for over a decade at [enormous cost to the country\u2019s civilians](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/10/26/world/middleeast/saudi-arabia-war-yemen.html). Few in Yemen expect the Saudi-backed government to try retaking Sanaa by force, and a negotiated settlement will most likely be necessary eventually. But the Houthis have certainly now exposed themselves \u2013 and any Yemeni civilians caught up in escalation \u2013 to relentless [airstrikes by Saudi Arabia](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/15/world/middleeast/yemen-saudi-arabia-houthis-attacks.html) and its allies. For the Houthis, the gamble to advance now may backfire. It is one thing to take territory and quite another thing to hold it. Moreover, the Houthis may now find themselves controlling a strait the world wants reopened while still lacking the one thing that strait was never going to buy them: a whole country that actually consents to their rule. [Middle East](https://theconversation.com/topics/middle-east-361) [Saudi Arabia](https://theconversation.com/topics/saudi-arabia-1591) [Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-1870) [Yemen](https://theconversation.com/topics/yemen-6632) [Persian Gulf](https://theconversation.com/topics/persian-gulf-16941) [Yemen civil war](https://theconversation.com/topics/yemen-civil-war-39810) [Strait of Hormuz](https://theconversation.com/topics/strait-of-hormuz-73874) [Houthi](https://theconversation.com/topics/houthi-150903) [Bab el-Mandeb Strait](https://theconversation.com/topics/bab-el-mandeb-strait-151287) [Iran war](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-war-185765) [Mahad D. Darar, Colorado State University](https://theconversation.com/houthi-advance-boosts-irans-leverage-over-the-us-and-saudi-arabia-but-may-cost-rebel-group-its-legitimacy-back-in-yemen-291930) Author [Mahad D. Darar](https://theconversation.com/profiles/mahad-d-darar-1427854) Ph.D. Candidate in Political Science, Colorado State University Disclosure statement Mahad D. Darar does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Colorado State University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/colorado-state-university-1267) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.539emuc9p](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.539emuc9p)", "content_hash": "6d98c60fa3954d273ecb911c2b253592665855d911cedc8d356994023a744978", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:01Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "974", "title": "Rapid Houthi advances in Yemen pose massive strategic risks for Saudi Arabia in the\u00a0Gulf", "url": "https://theconversation.com/rapid-houthi-advances-in-yemen-pose-massive-strategic-risks-for-saudi-arabia-in-the-gulf-292070", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/rapid-houthi-advances-in-yemen-pose-massive-strategic-risks-for-saudi-arabia-in-the-gulf-292070", "text": "Yemen\u2019s Houthi rebels march in Sanaa, Yemen, on Sept. 10, 2026. [AP Photo](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Photos%20show%20Houthi%20rallies%2C%20fighters%20and%20daily%20life%20inside%20Yemen./7b39c4220b0945bfbeb9cbb0e4c99f09/photo) Rapid Houthi advances in Yemen pose massive strategic risks for Saudi Arabia in the Gulf Published: September 16, 2026 1:48pm EDT https://theconversation.com/rapid-houthi-advances-in-yemen-pose-massive-strategic-risks-for-saudi-arabia-in-the-gulf-292070 https://theconversation.com/rapid-houthi-advances-in-yemen-pose-massive-strategic-risks-for-saudi-arabia-in-the-gulf-292070 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article An advance by Yemen\u2019s Houthi rebels, which has included the targeting of [Saudi cities](https://www.nbcnews.com/world/middle-east/saudi-arabia-mecca-houthis-drone-iran-war-trump-oil-gas-prices-rcna598075) [and infrastructure](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/09/11/world/middleeast/houthis-yemen-red-sea-shipping-maps.html), has quickly complicated a widening Middle East war. Not only has it all but unraveled [a 2022 truce](https://osesgy.unmissions.org/en/truce-agreement-april-2022) putting a lid on Yemen\u2019s civil war \u2013 in which the Iran-aligned Houthis were fighting a Saudi-led coalition \u2013 but the seizure of territory next to the Red Sea has further threatened Saudi energy exports. On Sept. 13, Saudi Arabia and other Arab Gulf nations were due to take part in crucial Omani-hosted talks with Iran in a bid to reopen the Strait of Hormuz. But [they were called off](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/13/world/middleeast/iran-meeting-gulf-arab-states.html), seemingly over attacks by both the Houthis and Iraqi groups aligned with Tehran. The Conversation spoke with [Gulf analyst](https://www.bakerinstitute.org/expert/kristian-coates-ulrichsen) Kristian Coates Ulrichsen to help make sense of the dizzying array of intersecting news developments. What happened with the Oman talks? Periodically since the [war in Iran began on Feb. 28](https://www.nytimes.com/article/iran-war-trump-us-oil-hormuz-key-dates-events.html), 2026, and particularly since the [signing of the Iran-U.S. memorandum of understanding in mid-June](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4gy700j0eko), Omani and Iranian officials have been meeting to discuss how to resolve the blockage in the Strait of Hormuz. Oman and Iran are the two coastal Gulf States through which the waterway passes, despite Iranian rhetoric about securing and maintaining control over the strait. The challenges facing the negotiators is that Oman will need to build consensus among the other Gulf States for any agreement, as well as the United States, given that one Iranian demand is for passage to be limited to civilian vessels, which would effectively bar U.S. naval ships from the Gulf, including the U.S. Navy\u2019s 5th Fleet, which is headquartered in Bahrain. Gulf officials \u2013 and their U.S. counterparts \u2013 would strenuously resist any Iranian attempt to convert their wartime leverage over the strait into a postwar deal that formalizes and regularizes that control. The [postponement of the planned meeting in Oman\u2019s capital Muscat](https://apnews.com/article/mideast-news-iran-yemen-houthis-israel-saudi-27d276de911a983cdb1b81101ee56083) illustrates that the time is not yet right for the talks to proceed and that further dialogue is needed in order to build a consensual framework that can best enable them to succeed. How serious are the new attacks on Saudi assets? The uptick in [attacks on Saudi energy infrastructure](https://www.wsj.com/world/middle-east/yemens-houthi-militants-attack-saudi-energy-infrastructure-067fa4f7) has put in jeopardy the viability of the work-around to Hormuz that enabled the kingdom to maintain oil exports at about half their prewar levels. Saudi vessels have already started to avoid transiting the [Bab al-Mandeb](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/why-is-bab-el-mandeb-strait-so-important-2026-09-10/) Strait and taking the far longer route through the Suez Canal into the Mediterranean and around the coast of Africa. That adds weeks and associated costs to oil exports to predominantly Asian markets. While the damage to a pumping station that led to the [temporary closure of the Saudi\u2019s East-West Pipeline](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/us-energy-chief-says-saudi-arabia-oil-pipeline-should-be-back-within-days-2026-09-15/) was reportedly done by a drone launched from Iraq, the kingdom faces attacks from both its northern and southern neighbors with the range, intent and capability to hit critical energy targets. Yemeni security forces escort buses carrying released Houthi detainees as they arrive in Sanaa on Sept. 12, 2026. [Mohammed Hamoud/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/yemeni-security-forces-escort-buses-carrying-released-news-photo/2294895123?adppopup=true) Saudi officials may respond to the latest attacks by rerouting oil back to its east coast facilities on the Persian Gulf, and there has been a steady increase in the volume of oil making its way through Hormuz in recent days and weeks. This return to using the Saudi port at Ras Tanura nevertheless makes oil transportation remain vulnerable to Iranian attacks on shipping in the strait. Moreover, should the Saudis face a \u201cdouble choke point\u201d crisis whereby the Strait of Hormuz and the Bab al-Mandeb Strait are both effectively shut, there will be no safe and secure way to continue Saudi oil and other industrial exports, or the import of non-oil goods and commodities by ship. What options does Saudi Arabia have to respond? Saudi Arabia [intervened militarily in Yemen](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/mar/26/saudi-arabia-begins-airstrikes-against-houthi-in-yemen) in March 2015 at the head of an Arab coalition but quickly got bogged down in an unwinnable conflict. In fact, the major successes in ground operations against the Houthis and other adversaries in Yemen were achieved by the United Arab Emirates rather than Saudi forces. There is very little appetite in Riyadh for a new military operation in Yemen, especially since the Houthis are skilled in asymmetric combat and have far more advanced drone and other technological capabilities than they possessed during the previous rounds of conflict with Saudi Arabia. The fact that the Houthis outlasted the two-month U.S. [Operation Rough Rider](https://ctc.westpoint.edu/feature-commentary-an-assessment-of-operation-rough-rider/) between March and May 2025 indicates that bombing from the air will not force the Houthis into submission. And the rapid advance made by the Houthis to [take Mokha and other positions](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4g7vr0lngwo) in southwestern Yemen since Sept. 3 suggest that Saudi-trained forces on the ground are insufficient. Fishermen motor past a commercial vessel anchored off Yemen\u2019s coast at Bab al-Mandeb, in the straits connecting the Red Sea with the Gulf of Aden and the Indian Ocean, on Sept. 12, 2026. [AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/fishermen-motor-past-a-commercial-vessel-anchored-off-news-photo/2294750914?adppopup=true) The Saudi leadership, led by de facto ruler Crown Prince [Mohammed bin Salman](https://www.cnn.com/world/middleeast/mohammed-bin-salman-fast-facts), is instead seeking to put together a multilateral coalition of states that may be able to contribute, in different ways, to ensuring maritime and regional security in and around the Red Sea. This will include the recent signatories Pakistan and Turkey, alongside Saudi Arabia, to the [Mecca Joint Defense Agreement](https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/saudi-arabia-turkey-pakistan-sign-joint-defence-deal-amid-regional-turmoil-2026-08-07/) and may also include Egypt, which [Crown Prince Mohammed visited on Sept. 15](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/15/egypts-sisi-meets-mbs-backs-saudi-call-for-secure-red-sea-navigation). Egypt and Turkey may be able to contribute naval assets to assist with maritime patrols in the Red Sea, while Pakistan has long provided Saudi Arabia with soldiers to help protect the kingdom. How could this all affect US-Saudi relations? Saudi officials, along with counterparts in other Gulf States, [urged the U.S. and Iran](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/iran-warns-gulf-states-tell-trump-desist-or-we-hit-you-hard-2026-08-06/) to reach a diplomatic resolution prior to the launch of the war on Feb. 28 and have found themselves caught in the middle of a conflict they sought to avoid. The perception that the U.S. began the war without a \u201cPlan B\u201d has intersected with the feeling that the U.S. has done little to protect Saudi Arabia and the other Gulf States. If the White House continues to struggle to make progress in negotiating an end to the war and a resumption of unrestricted passage through the [Strait of Hormuz](https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2026/09/16/irans-maximum-leverage-strait-hormuz-has-passed/), it may be that regional states step in to make a deal of their own. The postponement of the Oman meeting suggests we are not yet at that point. Yet patience may certainly be wearing thin in Riyadh. [Oil](https://theconversation.com/topics/oil-23) [Middle East](https://theconversation.com/topics/middle-east-361) [Energy](https://theconversation.com/topics/energy-662) [Saudi Arabia](https://theconversation.com/topics/saudi-arabia-1591) [Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-1870) [Oman](https://theconversation.com/topics/oman-1982) [Yemen](https://theconversation.com/topics/yemen-6632) [Persian Gulf](https://theconversation.com/topics/persian-gulf-16941) [Persian Gulf States](https://theconversation.com/topics/persian-gulf-states-19913) [Red Sea](https://theconversation.com/topics/red-sea-36260) [Houthis](https://theconversation.com/topics/houthis-63976) [US-Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-iran-70837) [Strait of Hormuz](https://theconversation.com/topics/strait-of-hormuz-73874) [Bab el-Mandeb Strait](https://theconversation.com/topics/bab-el-mandeb-strait-151287) [Red Sea shipping](https://theconversation.com/topics/red-sea-shipping-151288) [Gulf of Aden](https://theconversation.com/topics/gulf-of-aden-153277) [Red Sea politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/red-sea-politics-153898) [Iran war](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-war-185765) [Strait of Hormuz blockade](https://theconversation.com/topics/strait-of-hormuz-blockade-187096) [Kristian Coates Ulrichsen, Rice University](https://theconversation.com/rapid-houthi-advances-in-yemen-pose-massive-strategic-risks-for-saudi-arabia-in-the-gulf-292070) Author [Kristian Coates Ulrichsen](https://theconversation.com/profiles/kristian-coates-ulrichsen-128169) Fellow for the Middle East at the Baker Institute, Rice University Disclosure statement Kristian Coates Ulrichsen does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Rice University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/rice-university-931) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.hmmsmsqam](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.hmmsmsqam)", "content_hash": "a767b088aed0fa67a99c90d8502ac3e8ec62489785ef696649e826b6ae1aa5fa", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:02Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "975", "title": "A new Houthi front and expanded Iranian attacks risk sinking Gulf nations\u2019 choke\u2011point workaround and widening shipping\u00a0risks", "url": "https://theconversation.com/a-new-houthi-front-and-expanded-iranian-attacks-risk-sinking-gulf-nations-choke-point-workaround-and-widening-shipping-risks-288218", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/a-new-houthi-front-and-expanded-iranian-attacks-risk-sinking-gulf-nations-choke-point-workaround-and-widening-shipping-risks-288218", "text": "The navy of Yemen\u2019s internationally recognized government patrols a shipping lane off the Hanish Islands in the Red Sea on July 27, 2026. [Khaled Ziad/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.co.uk/detail/news-photo/sailors-from-the-navy-of-yemens-aden-based-internationally-news-photo/2287231399?slot=33&adppopup=true) A new Houthi front and expanded Iranian attacks risk sinking Gulf nations\u2019 choke\u2011point workaround and widening shipping risks Published: August 3, 2026 8:29am EDT https://theconversation.com/a-new-houthi-front-and-expanded-iranian-attacks-risk-sinking-gulf-nations-choke-point-workaround-and-widening-shipping-risks-288218 https://theconversation.com/a-new-houthi-front-and-expanded-iranian-attacks-risk-sinking-gulf-nations-choke-point-workaround-and-widening-shipping-risks-288218 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article After a nine-month pause, Houthi militants in Yemen allied to Iran [resumed attacks on vessels](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/23/iran-war-us-trump-houthis-red-sea-oil.html) in the Red Sea on July 22, 2026, directly targeting the group\u2019s longtime foe Saudi Arabia. A week later, a floating liquefied natural gas storage facility in Egypt\u2019s Mediterranean port of Damietta [was struck](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/29/world/middleeast/ships-drone-strike-egypt.html) by suspected Iranian drones, causing a fire that spread to a nearby tanker. As [a longtime Gulf analyst](https://www.bakerinstitute.org/expert/kristian-coates-ulrichsen), I believe these two maritime attacks by Iran and its proxies signal that efforts to bypass the Strait of Hormuz \u2014 which Tehran has largely shut down since being attacked by Israel and the U.S. on Feb. 28 \u2014 is no panacea for regional peace or stability in global energy markets. Indeed, the incidents have expanded the zone of risk for shipping to two other maritime choke points: the [Bab al-Mandab Strait](https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/bab-al-mandan-red-sea-suez-shipping-crisis-houthis-gaza) between Yemen and the Horn of Africa, and the Suez Canal and pipeline links between the Red Sea and the Mediterranean. At the same time, they have put at risk Saudi Arabia\u2019s strategy of redirecting oil exports away from the Strait of Hormuz through its western ports. No longer quiet on the Houthi front The Houthis, a rebel group that captured large swaths of northern Yemen during the near-decade-long civil war in the country, have long been an irritant to the strategic plans of Gulf nations and the U.S. Yet during the first phase of the Iran war from Feb. 28, 2026, to the [U.S.-Iran memorandum](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4gy700j0eko) of understanding on June 17, the Houthi front in Yemen remained relatively quiet. This was hardly surprising. The Houthis had by then withstood months of U.S. airstrikes in early 2025 [that only ended](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/may/06/us-to-halt-bombing-campaign-against-houthis-in-yemen-trump-says) with an Oman-brokered ceasefire agreement. And the group has been hit hard by a series of Israeli strikes, including one in August 2025 [that killed](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/8/30/yemens-houthis-confirm-israeli-airstrike-killed-the-groups-prime-minister) its prime minister and nine members of his cabinet. While the strikes hit the technocratic component of the movement rather than its inner core of leaders, they underscored the Houthis\u2019 vulnerability to external threats. The renewed flare-up began in earnest in early July when a Houthi delegation flew on the Iranian airline Mahan Air from Sanaa to Tehran to attend the funeral of Iran\u2019s former Supreme Leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, who was killed on the opening day of the Iran war. It was [the first direct flight](https://nadwaaldawsari.substack.com/p/irans-new-air-bridge-to-yemen?r=16v79) from Houthi-controlled Yemen to Iran since 2015, and when the delegation attempted to return on July 12, the Saudi-backed and internationally recognized government in Yemen struck the runway in Sanaa, forcing the flight to divert to the port city of Hodeidah. Houthi supporters attend a rally in Yemen\u2019s capital against Saudi influence on July 31, 2026. [AP Photo/Osamah Abdulrahman](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/Yemen%20Mideast%20Wars/982a9ed8aed14dbda2740e32a3026434/photo) Mahan Air is under a U.S. Treasury [designation](https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-spokesperson/2026/07/united-states-sanctioning-irans-mahan-air-network-and-irgc-linked-front-company) for its links to Iran\u2019s [Islamic Revolutionary Guards Corps](https://www.cfr.org/backgrounders/irans-revolutionary-guards), and the return flight [reportedly carried IRGC personnel](https://responsiblestatecraft.org/houthi-attack-saudi-arabia/) along with components for missiles and drones. Against this backdrop, the Houthi [announcement of a naval blockade](https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/07/houthis-declare-red-sea-blockade-saudi-arabia-oil-markets-brace-impact) on Saudi Arabia amounted to the most serious escalation of Saudi-Houthi tensions since a tenuous ceasefire largely ended Yemen\u2019s civil war in 2022. It also evoked memories of years of missile and drone attacks from Houthi areas on Saudi urban and energy infrastructure during the Yemeni civil war. Those attacks led the Saudi leadership to distance themselves from the American and British airstrikes in Yemen in 2025 and [ensured that the long Houthi campaign](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/27/world/middleeast/houthis-saudi-arabia-iran-war.html) against shipping in the Bab el-Mandab Strait and Red Sea from 2023 to 2025 avoided hitting Saudi vessels. The loss of a solid Saudi plan B This de-escalation of Saudi-Houthi tensions was critical in enabling the Gulf kingdom to quickly pivot its oil exports from its east coast ports to the west coast after the [Strait of Hormuz was blocked](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/why-irans-disruption-of-the-strait-of-hormuz-matters/) in March 2026. Within days of the closure, the East-West pipeline from the Saudi oil processing facility at Abqaiq to Yanbu, on the Red Sea, was [operating at its full capacity](https://www.spglobal.com/energy/en/news-research/latest-news/crude-oil/031026-aramcos-east-west-pipeline-to-hit-full-capacity-in-next-couple-of-days-ceo) of 7 million barrels per day. This workaround allowed the Saudis to [export around 3.43 million](https://x.com/ArgaamPlus/status/2079615023362552179?s=20) barrels of oil per day in May \u2013 less than half the prewar level, but a far higher figure than neighboring oil producers such as Kuwait, which lacks any alternative to passage through Hormuz. To be sure, the workarounds put in place during the spring are not perfect \u2013 they remain within range of attack from Iran and its proxies. And both Yanbu, on the western coast of Saudi Arabia, and the United Arab Emirates\u2019 own bypass pipeline from Abu Dhabi to Fujairah were [targeted by missiles and drones](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/17/drone-sparks-fire-at-uae-oil-site-as-gulf-takes-more-hits-amid-iran-war) in the first phase of the Iran war. A vessel transits the Bab al-Mandeb Strait off the coast of southern Yemen on July 25, 2026. [Khaled ZIAD/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/vessel-transits-the-bab-el-mandeb-strait-off-the-coast-of-news-photo/2286971847?slot=90&adppopup=true) And it is these workarounds that are now at risk. Since July 20, the Houthis have [struck at least four oil tankers](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/28/world/middleeast/houthis-strike-saudi-tanker.html) in the Red Sea. In retaliation, Saudi Arabia has [carried out airstrikes](https://www.wsj.com/world/middle-east/a-truce-in-yemen-unravels-threatening-to-widen-mideast-war-d0b2b678?mod=middle-east_more_article_pos11) against Houthi targets in Yemen for the first time since the 2022 truce. Vital Saudi oil infrastructure has also come under attack from both the Houthis and from Iranian-aligned groups in Iraq, who [may have damaged](https://www.wsj.com/world/middle-east/saudi-arabia-blames-iran-backed-militias-for-attack-on-oil-facilities-eb6cf40c) the crucial processing facility at Abqaiq that acts as the nerve center for Saudi oil. Quagmires and choke points With the Saudis having also carried out [joint airstrikes with the U.S.](https://www.axios.com/2026/07/29/saudi-arabia-iraq-strikes-vance-deescalation-iran) in Iraq on July 28, the kingdom now risks getting drawn into a conflict it has sought for years to avoid. If the Houthi targeting of vessels in the Red Sea leads to similar [restrictions on shipping](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/red-sea-shipping-slows-after-houthi-attack-saudi-arabia-data-shows-2026-07-27/) in the Bab al-Mandab Strait as in the Strait of Hormuz, it would render a second regional choke point effectively inoperable. A man sits on a surfboard as a container ship and other commercial vessels appear anchored in the Strait of Hormuz off Bandar Abbas, Iran, on July 27, 2026. [Razieh Poudat/ISNA via AP](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/US%20Iran/9154c051a0c246fdad9c7dbde5004cdc/photo) That would necessitate a workaround to the workaround, as tankers divert north through the Suez Canal into the Mediterranean \u2013 [adding about four weeks](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/asian-refiners-look-suez-canal-move-saudi-oil-amid-houthi-shipping-threats-2026-07-21/) to journey times and associated costs to destinations in Asia. Even this plan B may not be secure \u2013 and this is where the significance of the drone attacks on the vessels in Egypt\u2019s Damietta becomes clear. Although the world\u2019s largest oil tankers cannot transit the Suez Canal, [they can utilize](https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/07/aramco-turns-egypt-houthis-disrupt-saudi-oil-exports-what-know) the SUMED pipeline to shuttle oil from the Egyptian Red Sea port of Ain Sokhna to the port of Sidi Kerir on the Mediterranean for onward collection. This would add logistical friction to the process of first unloading and then reloading the oil cargoes. But if shippers and insurers perceive that Egypt\u2019s Mediterranean facilities are not only within Iranian range [but also capable](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/29/fire-erupts-on-ships-at-egypts-damietta-port) of being hit, that raises the prospect of the Red Sea being restricted at both ends. Doing so would render Saudi Arabia\u2019s west coast ports as vulnerable to disruption as its east coast ports in the Gulf. The costs of an expanding conflict The first phase of the war in Iran showed how quickly assessments of risk could affect shippers and insurers in the Strait of Hormuz, and the latest attacks could threaten to do the same for the Red Sea routes as well. An expanded conflict zone could wreak havoc on energy and commodity supply chains already stretched thin by the loss of oil production in the spring. By abandoning the restraint they showed during the spring, the Houthis may be seeking leverage over Saudi Arabia to gain political and economic concessions. But the Saudis too have shown greater assertiveness in their conduct of airstrikes, which could add to regional escalatory dynamics. The result is a further layer of complexity in what has become a series of overlapping conflicts that have defied resolution and risk inflicting even greater harm on the world economy in the weeks and months ahead. [Oil](https://theconversation.com/topics/oil-23) [Diplomacy](https://theconversation.com/topics/diplomacy-79) [Foreign policy](https://theconversation.com/topics/foreign-policy-266) [Middle East](https://theconversation.com/topics/middle-east-361) [Energy](https://theconversation.com/topics/energy-662) [Conflict](https://theconversation.com/topics/conflict-1275) [Egypt](https://theconversation.com/topics/egypt-1402) [Saudi Arabia](https://theconversation.com/topics/saudi-arabia-1591) [Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-1870) [Yemen](https://theconversation.com/topics/yemen-6632) [Oil pipeline](https://theconversation.com/topics/oil-pipeline-13947) [Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC)](https://theconversation.com/topics/gulf-cooperation-council-gcc-18703) [Gulf states](https://theconversation.com/topics/gulf-states-20720) [Red Sea](https://theconversation.com/topics/red-sea-36260) [Gulf](https://theconversation.com/topics/gulf-39202) [Yemen civil war](https://theconversation.com/topics/yemen-civil-war-39810) [Houthi rebels](https://theconversation.com/topics/houthi-rebels-39811) [Houthis](https://theconversation.com/topics/houthis-63976) [US-Iran](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-iran-70837) [Strait of Hormuz](https://theconversation.com/topics/strait-of-hormuz-73874) [Middle East politics](https://theconversation.com/topics/middle-east-politics-136981) [US-Middle East](https://theconversation.com/topics/us-middle-east-152275) [Iran war](https://theconversation.com/topics/iran-war-185765) [Kristian Coates Ulrichsen, Rice University](https://theconversation.com/a-new-houthi-front-and-expanded-iranian-attacks-risk-sinking-gulf-nations-choke-point-workaround-and-widening-shipping-risks-288218) Author [Kristian Coates Ulrichsen](https://theconversation.com/profiles/kristian-coates-ulrichsen-128169) Fellow for the Middle East at the Baker Institute, Rice University Disclosure statement Kristian Coates Ulrichsen does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Rice University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/rice-university-931) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.wktxtk4vc](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.wktxtk4vc)", "content_hash": "7f5876eadf26e1a8bfd54e6c197fd63c699fe1ce7c166dcecf7b9e6db948c3c3", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:04Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "976", "title": "Amid grinding war, protesting Ukrainians still want their voices and values heard at\u00a0home", "url": "https://theconversation.com/amid-grinding-war-protesting-ukrainians-still-want-their-voices-and-values-heard-at-home-287823", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/amid-grinding-war-protesting-ukrainians-still-want-their-voices-and-values-heard-at-home-287823", "text": "Amid grinding war, protesting Ukrainians still want their voices and values heard at home Published: July 20, 2026 9:28am EDT Protestors denounce President Volodymyr Zelenskyy\u2019s decision to dismiss Defense Minister Mykhailo Fedorov at a rally in Kyiv on July 17, 2026, [AP Photo/Efrem Lukatsky](https://newsroom.ap.org/home/search?query=Fedorov&mediaType=photo) https://theconversation.com/amid-grinding-war-protesting-ukrainians-still-want-their-voices-and-values-heard-at-home-287823 https://theconversation.com/amid-grinding-war-protesting-ukrainians-still-want-their-voices-and-values-heard-at-home-287823 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Ukraine is [experiencing its largest domestic protests](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/18/ukraine-war-briefing-protests-of-fedorovs-dismissal-continue-as-zelenskyy-tries-to-repair-rift-with-poland) since Russia\u2019s full-scale invasion in February 2022. The spark to the unrest was the [summary dismissal on July 15, 2026](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/17/dismissal-ukraine-defence-minister-mykhailo-fedorov-volodomyr-zelenskyy), of the country\u2019s youthful defense minister, Mykhailo Fedorov, appointed just six months ago. Fedorov, seen as charismatic and tech savvy, was already viewed by some as a leading light for change, with a reformist message that [chimed with many Ukrainians](https://kiis.com.ua/?cat=reports&id=1616&lang=eng). So his ouster \u2013 the consequence of [a clash with Oleksandr Syrskyi](https://theconversation.com/ukraines-defence-shake-up-exposes-rift-in-zelenskys-inner-circle-287744), the head of the country\u2019s armed forces and part of the older guard \u2014 tapped into a generational clash over how best to defeat Russia. Frustrations, though, have been building in Ukraine for some time, including over [contentious recruiting methods](https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2026/03/ukraine-military-russia-war-manpower-recruitment) and corruption allegations. Indeed, the latter was the primary reason for [demonstrations in Ukraine](https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/23/europe/ukraine-corruption-agencies-protests-intl) in the summer of 2025. Wars are by nature national emergencies, during which [politics as normal is often suspended](https://www.statesofexception.org/statesofexception). Before the war and especially after 2014, Ukraine had [a lively democracy and public sphere](https://www.politybooks.com/bookdetail?book_slug=russia-and-ukraine-entangled-histories-diverging-states--9781509557363). After the 2022 invasion, Ukraine [introduced martial law](https://www.rnbo.gov.ua/en/Diialnist/7156.html), placed its television stations [under central government control](https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/citing-martial-law-ukraine-president-signs-decree-combine-national-tv-channels-2022-03-20/) and [suspended elections](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/feb/20/ukraine-elections-start-of-war-volodymyr-zelenskyy). For Ukraine, these protests raise important questions about public unity, corruption and the agility of the military-industrial establishment to fight a war now in its fifth year. Our new polling data, gathered by the [Kyiv International Institute of Sociology](https://www.kiis.com.ua/?lang=eng) (KIIS), provides important context to the current demonstrations, and insights into the wartime priorities of ordinary Ukrainians. From June 9 to 26, 2026, we surveyed 1,801 people across the country in a computer-aided-telephone survey. Here are three findings from that survey that provide a broader context to the demonstrations. 1. Anti-corruption agencies and the media need protecting Ukraine may be at war but fighting corruption at home and maintaining media freedom still matter a great deal to Ukrainians. Mindful of last summer\u2019s attempt by President Volodymyr Zelenskyy to [scrap the independence](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/aug/02/demonstrations-in-ukraine-can-never-be-more-than-a-peaceful-protest-russia) of two of Ukraine\u2019s anti-corruption agencies, we asked ordinary Ukrainians if they felt these agencies \u201cshould be safeguarded\u201d even with Ukraine at war with Russia. We also asked if they felt the same about \u201cfreedom of the press and media.\u201d Protesting Ukrainians want their voices heard. [Paula Bronstein/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.co.uk/detail/news-photo/protests-continue-on-the-streets-of-the-ukrainian-capital-news-photo/2286075215?adppopup=true) About half of Ukrainians think that anti-corruption agencies should be safeguarded a lot during the war. An additional 26% of Ukrainians said they should be safeguarded a little. Only a minority (16%) think that anti-corruption agencies should not be safeguarded at all. Likewise, Ukrainians believe in the vital role of journalism. Despite war-time media restrictions, Ukraine retains a robust online public sphere and a [free press](https://www.coe.int/en/web/kyiv/-/ukraine-improves-its-position-in-the-world-press-freedom-index-ahead-of-world-press-freedom-day). Indeed, it played a role in mobilizing Ukrainians against the unpopular dismissal of Fedorov. The protests are in keeping with our poll which finds that a strong majority (62%) of Ukrainians think that freedom of the press and the media needs to be safeguarded a lot. Only about 5% do not think it should be safeguarded at all. 2. Fighting corruption during wartime matters [Previous polling by KIIS revealed](https://kiis.com.ua/?lang=eng&cat=reports&id=1590&page=6) that the war with Russia and corruption were what Ukrainians considered the most serious problems facing the country. We decided to follow up by asking Ukrainians what they would rank first as the bigger problem: \u201cRussian aggression\u201d or \u201cgovernment corruption, including around mobilization.\u201d The results were surprising. Although the most common response was Russian aggression (49%), more than a third of respondents ranked government corruption tied to mobilization as the country\u2019s top challenge. Another 14% said Russian aggression and corruption were equal concerns. Public opinion research elsewhere reveals that many Ukrainians still optimistically [view the war against Russia](https://www.kiis.com.ua/?lang=eng&cat=reports&id=1611&page=2) as a fight for a future in which Ukraine is an established democracy within the European Union. In this sense, the values evident in the survey responses may be what makes the war meaningful for many Ukrainians. Even during an exhausting war which of late has seen [increased Russian attacks on civilians](https://ukraine.un.org/en/319320-civilian-casualties-soar-ukraine-first-half-2026-amid-escalating-attacks-and-intensifying), Ukrainians prioritize domestic reforms and democratic accountability like fighting corruption. 3. Ukrainians oppose drafting younger men Although manpower is seen as essential for Ukraine\u2019s war effort against a larger Russian adversary, the process of getting new military recruits has proved contentious. Ukrainian military authorities have sometimes used [violent methods](https://responsiblestatecraft.org/ukraine-forced-conscription/) to enforce military service, and the lowering of the draft from 27 to 25 in April 2024 was unpopular. We asked whether Ukrainians agreed that it \u201cwas time to draft all young Ukrainian men into military service.\u201d A clear majority of respondents disagreed with this sentiment, with more than a fifth objecting \u201cvery strongly.\u201d Less that 8% fully agreed. A woman passes by a wall covered with military conscription posters for Ukraine\u2019s armed forces on Aug. 5, 2025. [Pierre Crom/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.co.uk/detail/news-photo/woman-passes-by-a-wall-covered-with-military-conscription-news-photo/2227951620?adppopup=true) What this indicates to us is a strong societal consensus that Ukraine should continue to protect its adolescent and young men from military mobilization, despite [the persistent recruitment crisis](https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2026/03/ukraine-military-russia-war-manpower-recruitment) facing its military. One of the attractions of Fedorov to young people was that his preference for unmanned drones over on-the-ground combat forces offers a promise of Ukrainian victory without large-scale casualties. Syrskyi, by contrast, is associated with Soviet-style attritional warfare. Fairly or not, the latter [acquired the nickname \u201cthe butcher](https://www.politico.eu/article/oleksandr-syrskyi-ukraine-commander-in-chief-butcher-volodymyr-zelenskyy-war-russia/)\u201d some time ago. Fedorov\u2019s promise of victory through technological transformation to support drone warfare may be an illusion. But his very public attack on Ukraine\u2019s current military-industrial establishment posed a serious challenge to its legitimacy and public support. A figure with [close ties to Silicon Valley](https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/ukraine-protests-mykhailo-fedorov-ouster/2jcj8zs6d) and political ambitions, Fedorov is a disrupter in the mold of many contemporary tech figures. Whether his polices ultimately serve the cause of Ukraine at this consequential moment in its war with Russia is an open question. But the demonstrations that followed his dismissal nonetheless suggest the multitude of ways Ukrainians view democracy and freedom at home and abroad. [Democracy](https://theconversation.com/topics/democracy-619) [Government](https://theconversation.com/topics/government-723) [Corruption](https://theconversation.com/topics/corruption-1852) [Polling](https://theconversation.com/topics/polling-6608) [Ukraine](https://theconversation.com/topics/ukraine-8201) [Ukraine elections](https://theconversation.com/topics/ukraine-elections-10657) [Anti-corruption](https://theconversation.com/topics/anti-corruption-13637) [Good governance](https://theconversation.com/topics/good-governance-33730) [Volodymyr Zelensky](https://theconversation.com/topics/volodymyr-zelensky-70055) [Russia-Ukraine](https://theconversation.com/topics/russia-ukraine-72684) [Mykhailo Fedorov](https://theconversation.com/topics/mykhailo-fedorov-117477) [Russian war in Ukraine](https://theconversation.com/topics/russian-war-in-ukraine-179435) [Sarah Wilson Sokhey, University of Colorado Boulder, Gerard Toal, Virginia Tech, John O\u2019Loughlin, University of Colorado](https://theconversation.com/amid-grinding-war-protesting-ukrainians-still-want-their-voices-and-values-heard-at-home-287823) Authors [Sarah Wilson Sokhey](https://theconversation.com/profiles/sarah-wilson-sokhey-2728659) Associate Professor of Political Science, University of Colorado Boulder [Gerard Toal](https://theconversation.com/profiles/gerard-toal-507842) Professor of Government and International Affairs, Virginia Tech [John O\u2019Loughlin](https://theconversation.com/profiles/john-oloughlin-1164350) Professor of Geography, University of Colorado Boulder Disclosure statement The authors received funding from the Institute of Behavioral Science at the University of Colorado for this survey. Gerard Toal received funding from the School of Public and International Affairs in the College of Liberal Arts and Human Sciences at Virginia Tech to support this research. John O\u2019Loughlin receives funding from US National Science Foundation and the University of Colorado Boulder Partners [University of Colorado](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-colorado-boulder-733) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.m6eyp5mgm](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.m6eyp5mgm)", "content_hash": "cd3e4c57d8f98ad08a2ff675f8654f8ba9e02a0900fed674d665fed44e2fd0ef", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:05Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "977", "title": "California is taking a beating from an unusually destructive 2026 Pacific hurricane\u00a0season", "url": "https://theconversation.com/california-is-taking-a-beating-from-an-unusually-destructive-2026-pacific-hurricane-season-294207", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/california-is-taking-a-beating-from-an-unusually-destructive-2026-pacific-hurricane-season-294207", "text": "Waves crash into damaged homes in Long Beach, Calif., amid high surf generated by Tropical Storm Rachel on Oct. 11, 2026. [Mario Tama/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/in-an-aerial-view-pacific-ocean-waves-wash-into-damaged-news-photo/2299702764?phrase=california%20storm&adppopup=true) California is taking a beating from an unusually destructive 2026 Pacific hurricane season Published: October 12, 2026 3:13pm EDT https://theconversation.com/california-is-taking-a-beating-from-an-unusually-destructive-2026-pacific-hurricane-season-294207 https://theconversation.com/california-is-taking-a-beating-from-an-unusually-destructive-2026-pacific-hurricane-season-294207 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article California isn\u2019t a place most Americans associate with tropical weather, but it\u2019s taking a beating in 2026 from one of the most active eastern Pacific hurricane seasons in modern history. For the second time ever, national forecasters issued [a tropical storm watch for Southern California](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/ep18/ep182026.public.050.shtml) as Tropical Storm Rachel headed toward the region, bringing high tides, coastal flooding and drenching rain from Oct. 9-11. The first tropical storm watch was issued just [three years earlier](https://theconversation.com/tropical-storm-hilary-pounds-southern-california-with-heavy-rain-flash-flooding-211869), for Hurricane Hilary. The U.S. occasionally sees heavy rain from the remnants of Pacific storms that pass over Mexico and travel into the U.S. Southwest. But the 2026 season has been different. Three [hurricanes deluged Hawaii](https://theconversation.com/another-hurricane-just-sideswiped-hawaii-why-the-islands-usual-force-field-seems-to-be-down-in-2026-291524) as they passed close to the islands, a rare event for that state. And in September, [Hurricane Marie churned up big waves](https://www.forbes.com/sites/alisondurkee/2026/09/07/hurricane-marie-brings-ten-foot-waves-and-flooding-to-southern-california-photos/) that damaged oceanfront homes near Los Angeles before veering away from the coast. Rachel [weakened to an extratropical cyclone](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/ep18/ep182026.discus.056.shtml) before its landfall in Baja California, but it brought high waves to Southern California that [damaged more coastal homes](https://ktla.com/news/local-news/long-beach-assessing-damages-from-tropical-storm-rachel-threat-of-powerful-waves-still-not-over/) and flooded streets. Its rainfall drenched communities from San Diego to the desert Southwest and even triggered [flooding in Las Vegas](https://www.ktnv.com/news/video-rain-from-tropical-storm-rachel-causes-flooding-across-las-vegas-valley). An emergency vehicle drives through coastal floodwaters amid strong waves and high tides generated by Tropical Storm Rachel on Oct. 9, 2026, in Newport Beach, Calif. [Mario Tama/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/in-an-aerial-view-an-emergency-vehicle-drives-through-news-photo/2299437313) [I study Pacific hurricanes](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=5uEy_XoAAAAJ&hl=en). While tropical storm impacts to California are unusual, they aren\u2019t unprecedented. El Ni\u00f1o raises the risks. California\u2019s history with tropical storms Historically, Southern California has seen a few direct hits from tropical storms and numerous cases of damage from systems that remained offshore. To find the earliest known storm there, we [have to travel back all the way to 1858](https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/bams/85/11/bams-85-11-1689.pdf), when a Category 1 hurricane, simply known as the 1858 San Diego Hurricane, brushed the coast of San Diego County and brought tropical-storm-force winds to the region. San Diego of the 1850s had a fraction of the population it has today, but the storm still caused significant damage. A map of all the eastern Pacific tropical cyclone tracks since 1971 shows how few reach California. Hurricane Nora is in blue and Hurricane Hilary is in red. Each track stops when the storm loses its tropical cyclone characteristics. While weaker than the 1858 hurricane, the only tropical storm known to have made landfall on the California coast was a [1939 tropical storm that hit Long Beach](https://www.weather.gov/media/wrh/online_publications/TMs/TM-197.pdf), in Los Angeles County. It brought tropical-storm-force winds across a large swath of Southern California, [along with over 4 inches of rain in some areas](https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2019-08-21/hurricanes-here-the-cordonazo-that-lashed-los-angeles). That storm was a surprise, and the United States Weather Bureau \u2013 the precursor to the modern National Weather Service \u2013 opened a Southern California office in 1940 in response. A 2004 research paper hypothesized that if a similar storm hit the region in the mid-2000s, it would have likely caused over US$200 million in damage to the region. While a few other tropical storms in the 1970s came close to a landfall in California, all weakened or changed course, avoiding a direct hit. This includes the [remnants of Hurricane Hilary](https://theconversation.com/tropical-storm-hilary-pounds-southern-california-with-heavy-rain-flash-flooding-211869) in 2023 that were still strong enough to cause widespread flooding in Southern California and dump [10 inches of rain in the mountains](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/EP092023_Hilary.pdf). A handful of additional systems that made landfall in Baja California or stayed offshore have brought tropical-storm-force winds to the state, including [Hurricane Nora in 1997](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/EP161997_Nora.pdf). In addition, multiple recent storms over the open ocean have generated swells that damaged port and pier infrastructure in the region, including [hurricanes Marie in 2024](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/EP132014_Marie.pdf), [Narda in 2025](https://ktla.com/news/local-news/hurricane-narda-causing-high-surf-at-southern-california-beaches/) and [another Marie in 2026](https://www.youtube.com/shorts/CIE_evQTzqs). El Ni\u00f1o raises California\u2019s storm risk Several environmental factors increase the probability of tropical storms making an approach to Southern California. The most notable is El Ni\u00f1o. This [natural climate pattern](https://youtu.be/WPA-KpldDVc?si=KLh2d0eAq07Lih-0), defined by unusually warm water in the eastern tropical Pacific, [increases the frequency of storms](https://theconversation.com/2026s-el-nino-is-shaping-up-to-be-a-doozy-its-already-affecting-global-shipping-fish-behavior-and-heat-291490) in the eastern North Pacific. That\u2019s because warmer than normal sea surface temperatures in the region are necessary to support the formation and development of these storms. Storm tracks during an exceptionally active eastern Pacific hurricane season in 2026 show the remnants of Tropical Storm Rachel, No. 18, crossing into California. [NOAA](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/index.php?basin=epac) Even during El Ni\u00f1o years, the waters off the coast of Southern California and northern Baja California are [typically too cool](https://www.ospo.noaa.gov/products/ocean/sst/contour/) to [support hurricanes](https://oceanexplorer.noaa.gov/ocean-fact/hurricanes/). Still, storms moving fast enough or with enough support from upper-level winds can survive these slightly cooler temperatures, allowing the system to make an approach toward California. When Tropical Storm Rachel began its approach toward Southern California on Oct. 9, the [National Hurricane Center noted](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/ep18/ep182026.discus.051.shtml) that the sea surface temperatures were anomalously warm off the coast of Baja California and played a role in its potential to remain a tropical cyclone as it approached the coast. The big storm problem: Rain Like most eastern Pacific tropical storms and hurricanes, the primary effect on California from these systems is rainfall. Most of Southern California is arid and rarely receives rainfall during summer and early fall. Early reports showed [Rachel dumped upward of 6 inches of rain](https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=sgx&issuedby=SGX&product=RRM) in parts of Southern California, including over 1 inch at San Diego International Airport in one weekend. That inch of rain in San Diego is double the typical October precipitation there. While those amounts may seem minuscule compared to what typically comes with a tropical storm or hurricane landfall in places like Florida, they can cause flash flooding in the normally dry Southwest. The National Hurricane Center [warned of flash flooding](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/ep18/ep182026.public.053.shtml) in California and other parts of the Southwest before Rachel\u2019s landfall. Unique cases like Rachel show that Mother Nature can always go against conventional wisdom, and, given enough time and the right conditions, potentially destructive things can happen. [Oceans](https://theconversation.com/topics/oceans-183) [California](https://theconversation.com/topics/california-1445) [Extreme weather](https://theconversation.com/topics/extreme-weather-3799) [El Nino](https://theconversation.com/topics/el-nino-5638) [Tropical storms](https://theconversation.com/topics/tropical-storms-7001) [Flooding](https://theconversation.com/topics/flooding-8771) [Hurricanes](https://theconversation.com/topics/hurricanes-9045) [Pacific Ocean](https://theconversation.com/topics/pacific-ocean-12006) [Storm surge](https://theconversation.com/topics/storm-surge-17515) [Nicholas Grondin, University of Tampa](https://theconversation.com/california-is-taking-a-beating-from-an-unusually-destructive-2026-pacific-hurricane-season-294207) Author [Nicholas Grondin](https://theconversation.com/profiles/nicholas-grondin-1047350) Assistant Professor of Environmental Studies, University of Tampa Disclosure statement Nicholas Grondin does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [University of Tampa](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-of-tampa-3946) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.rvpwyaw6u](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.rvpwyaw6u)", "content_hash": "3b49a4e5da873932141860c9f1e7c30251cec02d3563780435185d3854f1b90a", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:06Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "978", "title": "How Hurricane Isaias broke through to become the first Atlantic hurricane in an extremely quiet 2026\u00a0season", "url": "https://theconversation.com/how-hurricane-isaias-broke-through-to-become-the-first-atlantic-hurricane-in-an-extremely-quiet-2026-season-294028", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/how-hurricane-isaias-broke-through-to-become-the-first-atlantic-hurricane-in-an-extremely-quiet-2026-season-294028", "text": "Hurricane Isaias just after making landfall near Destin, Fla., on Oct. 9, 2026. [NOAA](https://www.star.nesdis.noaa.gov/goes/floater.php?stormid=AL092026#homePageLink) How Hurricane Isaias broke through to become the first Atlantic hurricane in an extremely quiet 2026 season Published: October 9, 2026 8:38am EDT \u2022Updated: October 9, 2026 11:02pm EDT https://theconversation.com/how-hurricane-isaias-broke-through-to-become-the-first-atlantic-hurricane-in-an-extremely-quiet-2026-season-294028 https://theconversation.com/how-hurricane-isaias-broke-through-to-become-the-first-atlantic-hurricane-in-an-extremely-quiet-2026-season-294028 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Hurricane Isaias shattered the peace of one of the quietest Atlantic hurricane seasons on record as it grew into [2026\u2019s first Atlantic hurricane](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/refresh/graphics_at4+shtml/080328.shtml?cone) and [made landfall on the northern Gulf Coast](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/al09/al092026.update.10100129.shtml) near Destin, Fla., late on Oct. 9. The Category 2 hurricane\u2019s [storm surge reached 5.6 feet](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/al09/al092026.update.10100129.shtml) at nearby Panama City Beach as it crossed the coast. Forecasters warned of [damaging wind gusts](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/al09/al092026.discus.013.shtml) over a large portion of the southeastern U.S. and flooding rain as the storm moved inland toward the Appalachian Mountains. Isaias [tied a 1905 storm](https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/news/noaas-goes-east-satellite-monitors-hurricane-isaias) for the latest-forming first hurricane of a season on record. To understand why it was able to strengthen into a hurricane when no other storms in the first two-thirds of the 2026 season did, let\u2019s take a look at what was going on in the atmosphere. As an [atmospheric scientist](https://www.umass.edu/engineering/news/davis-paros-chair) who has studied hurricanes for years, I can tell you that wind shear and the climate phenomenon known as El Ni\u00f1o both play important roles. Hurricane Isaias faced strong winds as it moved across warm Gulf waters. The wind influence is evident in the storm\u2019s lopsided rain bands, primarily pushed to its northeast, on Oct. 8, 2026. [NOAA](https://www.star.nesdis.noaa.gov/goes/index.php) Why the 2026 season had such a quiet start Usually, Atlantic hurricane activity [hits its peak in September](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/climo/). In a typical season, the Atlantic region experiences [five hurricanes and two major hurricanes](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/climo/) by early October. Having no hurricanes [until Oct. 7](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/refresh/graphics_at4+shtml/080328.shtml?cone) is nearly unprecedented in the Atlantic hurricane record. The main culprit is likely El Ni\u00f1o. El Ni\u00f1o is [a climate pattern](https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/ninonina.html) that occurs every few years when ocean surface temperatures in the central and eastern Pacific Ocean heat up. That heat can influence weather around the world \u2013 including Atlantic hurricanes. How El Ni\u00f1o influences weather around the world, including Atlantic hurricanes. The 2026 [El Ni\u00f1o was already strong](https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/outlook/) by late summer, and forecasters expected to see it become [one of the strongest](https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso_advisory/ensodisc.shtml) El Ni\u00f1o events on record. 2 ways El Ni\u00f1o prevents hurricanes from forming When the waters of the eastern Pacific warm substantially, they create more rising air over the eastern Pacific, and with it, more [widespread thunderstorm activity](https://www.climate.gov/news-features/blogs/enso/walker-circulation-ensos-atmospheric-buddy). The net effect is a current of rising air that flows northward across portions of Central America. This flow turns toward the east in the Northern Hemisphere [because of the Earth\u2019s rotation](https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/atmosphere/what-the-coriolis-effect), and it increases the westerly winds across the tropical Atlantic. Winds near the surface blowing from the east, along with winds at higher altitudes blowing from the west, create [vertical wind shear](https://theconversation.com/what-is-wind-shear-an-atmospheric-scientist-explains-how-it-can-tear-apart-hurricanes-and-why-el-nino-ramps-it-up-213527). Wind shear makes it harder for hurricanes to form because it disrupts any deep column of moisture that tries to organize into a vortex. El Ni\u00f1o has another effect on Atlantic hurricane formation: With more rising air above the eastern Pacific warm waters, air tends to [subside over the tropical Atlantic and become dry](https://www.climate.gov/news-features/understanding-climate/el-nino-and-la-nina-frequently-asked-questions). Hurricanes need lots of moisture, so dry air makes it harder for one to form. Under normal atmospheric circulation, air in the eastern tropical Pacific sinks, leaving drier air in that region, while the tropical Atlantic has moist, rising air that can fuel hurricanes. During El Ni\u00f1os, however, that flips \u2013 the eastern tropical Pacific Ocean is wetter, creating strongly rising air and more Pacific hurricanes, while the region where Atlantic hurricanes typically form is drier and less stormy. [NOAA](https://www.climate.gov/news-features/blogs/enso/walker-circulation-ensos-atmospheric-buddy) The tropical Atlantic was hit with both vertical wind shear and dry air in 2026. Most of the [storms that managed to form](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/index.php) in the Atlantic earlier in 2026 did so to the north of the region of vertical shear and dry air, but it is rare for storms forming at those higher latitudes to become strong hurricanes. The hurricane storm tracks in 2026, through Oct. 8, show that most storms formed in northern areas. Typically, storms that become strong hurricanes form farther south and travel through the Caribbean. But dry air and wind shear made formation difficult in 2026 \u2013 Isaias is storm No. 9. [NOAA](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/index.php) How Isaias formed in these conditions So how did Isaias manage to break through? First, Isaias did not form in the deep tropics, where many of the strongest hurricanes originate. That region is still seeing plenty of wind shear. Isaias [formed over the western Gulf](https://www.yahoo.com/news/weather-news/articles/tropical-storm-isaias-formed-south-142516301.html) of Mexico (also referred to as the Gulf of America) instead. In early October, [vertical wind shear in the western Gulf weakened](https://www.atmos.albany.edu/student/abentley/realtime/standard.php?domain=northamer&variable=irro_wind) and a small region of enhanced moisture moved over the western Gulf. Both of these factors made hurricane formation more likely, and [exceptionally warm](https://theeyewall.substack.com/p/hurricane-isaias-is-strengthening) water provided fuel for the storm that formed. Isaias organized rapidly in this environment. But, as is often true with late-season hurricanes approaching the U.S., the jet stream is not far away. While Isaias strengthened, vertical shear also started increasing \u2013 not due to El Ni\u00f1o, but because the [jet stream tends to dip farther south](https://s.w-x.co/wxnext/img/SummertoFallJetStream.png) as autumn sets in. Isaias grew into a dangerous storm, but it could have been even stronger without the effects of wind shear. What\u2019s ahead for Atlantic hurricane season? The rapid emergence of Isaias is a reminder that a slow start to the hurricane season doesn\u2019t mean a quiet end. The season does not end officially until Nov. 30. Atmospheric conditions often change considerably in October from what they were in July through September as temperatures fall. So coastal regions can\u2019t let their guard down yet \u2013 the season could bring more surprises. This article, originally published Oct. 9, 2026, has been updated with the storm making landfall. [Oceans](https://theconversation.com/topics/oceans-183) [El Nino](https://theconversation.com/topics/el-nino-5638) [Hurricanes](https://theconversation.com/topics/hurricanes-9045) [Atlantic Ocean](https://theconversation.com/topics/atlantic-ocean-12005) [Jet stream](https://theconversation.com/topics/jet-stream-14987) [Florida](https://theconversation.com/topics/florida-15445) [Atmosphere](https://theconversation.com/topics/atmosphere-25442) [Atmospheric science](https://theconversation.com/topics/atmospheric-science-25900) [Atlantic hurricane season](https://theconversation.com/topics/atlantic-hurricane-season-67582) [Wind shear](https://theconversation.com/topics/wind-shear-101670) [Christopher Davis, UMass Amherst](https://theconversation.com/how-hurricane-isaias-broke-through-to-become-the-first-atlantic-hurricane-in-an-extremely-quiet-2026-season-294028) Author [Christopher Davis](https://theconversation.com/profiles/christopher-davis-2718762) Professor of Earth, Geographic, and Climate Sciences, UMass Amherst Disclosure statement Christopher Davis works for the University of Massachusetts Amherst, and the NSF National Center for Atmospheric Research. He receives funding from the State of Massachusetts, NASA and the State of California. Partners [UMass Amherst](https://theconversation.com/institutions/umass-amherst-1563) provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.symxvafhj](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.symxvafhj)", "content_hash": "bd698488ab77df65c045a9c05ed3788cb86db48a47548e88f71fc84b65341c84", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:07Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "979", "title": "Antarctica\u2019s ice loss is locked in, and extra snowfall won\u2019t save it \u2013 that\u2019s bad news for low\u2011lying coastal areas\u00a0elsewhere", "url": "https://theconversation.com/antarcticas-ice-loss-is-locked-in-and-extra-snowfall-wont-save-it-thats-bad-news-for-low-lying-coastal-areas-elsewhere-293066", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/antarcticas-ice-loss-is-locked-in-and-extra-snowfall-wont-save-it-thats-bad-news-for-low-lying-coastal-areas-elsewhere-293066", "text": "A glacier front meets the sea in Antarctica. The ice sheet holds enough water to raise global sea level by about 190 feet (58 meters). Qiyue Sun Antarctica\u2019s ice loss is locked in, and extra snowfall won\u2019t save it \u2013 that\u2019s bad news for low\u2011lying coastal areas elsewhere Published: September 30, 2026 8:47am EDT https://theconversation.com/antarcticas-ice-loss-is-locked-in-and-extra-snowfall-wont-save-it-thats-bad-news-for-low-lying-coastal-areas-elsewhere-293066 https://theconversation.com/antarcticas-ice-loss-is-locked-in-and-extra-snowfall-wont-save-it-thats-bad-news-for-low-lying-coastal-areas-elsewhere-293066 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Sea-level rise is one of the most visible signs of climate change, one that is threatening low-lying coastal communities around the world. Today, [about 1 billion people](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10196-1) live in coastal areas; about 100 million live within 1 meter (3.3 feet) of sea level, highly exposed to coastal flooding. Over the long term, when it comes to future sea-level rise, one place matters more than any other: Antarctica. The frozen continent holds enough ice to raise global sea level by [about 190 feet (58 meters)](https://nsidc.org/learn/parts-cryosphere/ice-sheets/ice-sheet-quick-facts). Yet [scientists have disagreed](https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-14-3033-2020) on a fundamental question: Will Antarctica gain more ice than it loses this century or lose more ice than it gains? In a new study published in the scientific journal [Nature Geoscience](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-026-02102-1), [we](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=LKD_TNwAAAAJ&hl=en) [looked](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=ceifbhUAAAAJ&hl=en) at hundreds of thousands of potential futures and the impact of each on the ice sheet. We found that Antarctica\u2019s ice loss this century is locked in \u2013 even if the world limits global warming to 1.5 degrees Celsius (2.7 degrees Fahrenheit), the 2015 international [Paris climate agreement\u2019s](https://unfccc.int/process-and-meetings/the-paris-agreement) most ambitious goal. Why the Antarctic ice sheet is so hard to predict The Antarctic ice sheet\u2019s future depends on a chain of events. [Greenhouse gas emissions](https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/resources/climate-change-in-data/) from vehicles, coal- and natural gas-fueled power plants and other sources around the world determine how much the ocean and air around Antarctica warms. That warming shapes how ice shelves around the continent\u2019s margins melt, and how quickly the glaciers behind them slide over the bedrock. When those ice shelves thin or collapse, studies show the [glacier flow speeds up](https://nsidc.org/learn/parts-cryosphere/ice-shelves/why-ice-shelves-matter). A European research team explains ways Antarctica is vulnerable to ice loss from the ocean and how models work. TiPACCS. Each step relies on assumptions that scientists have yet to agree on, such as [how ice shelves collapse](https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/world-of-change/larsen-b/). Different assumptions give different answers, and because each step feeds the next, the differences grow \u2013 what scientists call cascading uncertainty. As a result, research teams around the world can\u2019t agree on whether Antarctica will gain or lose ice this century. Snow is part of the puzzle. Warmer air holds more moisture, [so as temperatures rise, more snow will likely fall on Antarctica](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10912-x). In some projections, that extra snow outweighs the ice lost to the warming ocean, and the ice sheet actually grows. Auditioning possible futures A big reason for this persistent disagreement involves the cost of computing. Each ice-sheet simulation runs with just one set of assumptions for every step in the chain, from how much greenhouse gas we emit to how the ice behaves. One run can keep a supercomputer busy for days, so no team can test every plausible combination. Our team of data scientists, glaciologists and oceanographers took a shortcut. An international project called the Ice Sheet Model Intercomparison Project, or [ISMIP6](https://www.ismip.org/research/ismip6), has built a large archive of Antarctic simulations showing how the ice responds under different circumstances. We trained a physics-informed machine learning model on that archive to learn how each simulation\u2019s outcome depends on its physical assumptions, such as how easily ice slides over bedrock. The machine learning model does in a fraction of a second what took the original models days, predicting sea-level change with similar accuracy. NASA\u2019s Grace satellite has tracked ice mass loss over the years. [NASA](https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/31158/#media_group_312023) That speed let us run millions of combinations and check hundreds of thousands of possible futures against [data from satellites](https://grace.jpl.nasa.gov/mission/grace/) that weigh the ice sheet from space by sensing tiny changes in Earth\u2019s gravity. Only scenarios that were able to match what the satellites have seen since 2002 made the cut. The scenarios that did make the cut point one direction: Antarctica will [lose more ice than it gains this century](http://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-026-02102-1), even if humanity is able to [limit global warming to 1.5 C](https://www.unep.org/resources/limiting-overshoot-navigating-exceedance). That means Antarctica will [contribute more to sea-level rise](https://www.climate.gov/news-features/understanding-climate/climate-change-global-sea-level) that\u2019s already being driven by melting of the Greenland ice sheet and mountain glaciers and from warming ocean temperatures that cause water to expand. What a clearer forecast can do The biggest lessons from our study are that greenhouse gas emissions matter and [extra snowfall won\u2019t save Antarctica](http://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-026-02102-1). Every ton of greenhouse gas that humans can avoid releasing today reduces how much ice Antarctica loses this century, and the benefit \u2013 slower and less sea-level rise \u2013 lasts for hundreds of years. In a scenario with very high emissions, our results show a potential faster domino effect. A fast-warming ocean thins the ice shelves, weakened shelves break apart, and glacier ice flows into the ocean faster. Each step makes the next one easier. Together, these steps could add up to 10 inches (25 centimeters) of sea-level rise from Antarctica alone by 2100 \u2013 [enough to permanently flood the homes of more than 10 million people](https://doi.org/10.1029/2024EF005914). What different types of models help scientists understand about ice melt, and how they are connected to help predict the Antarctic ice sheet\u2019s future. On the left, how greenhouse gas emissions change the climate, including sea ice, ocean and atmospheric dynamics, which influence one another and levels of global warming. On the right, what goes into an ice sheet model, including ice shelf melt and collapse and how the ice slides. Every stage adds uncertainty. Yucheng Lin Of more than 20 physical assumptions about Antarctica, three matter most for understanding future sea-level rise: [how ice shelves respond](https://www.asoc.org/learn/melting-ice-shelves/) to a warming ocean, [how ice slides over bedrock](https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/cryosphere/glaciers/glacier-power/how-do-glaciers-move), and [how fast the ocean and air around Antarctica warm](https://www.asoc.org/learn/heating-oceans/). Gaining a better understanding of these processes would do the most to sharpen sea-level projections. What\u2019s still missing Our study is far from the final word. Some processes are missing from today\u2019s simulations, so our machine learning model can\u2019t learn about them \u2013 for example, [how ice cracks and breaks](https://www.antarcticglaciers.org/glacier-processes/structural-glaciology/), and how [rivers and lakes beneath the ice sheet](https://www.antarcticglaciers.org/glacier-processes/glacial-lakes/subglacial-lakes/) change over time. Any of these could change our understanding of how quickly Antarctica\u2019s ice reaches the sea and whether coastal communities have enough time to adapt. What our study does offer is a much faster way to test combinations of assumptions that are too costly to simulate one by one. That provides a clearer basis for the decisions coastal communities around the world are already having to make. [Research](https://theconversation.com/topics/research-26) [Climate change](https://theconversation.com/topics/climate-change-27) [Ice](https://theconversation.com/topics/ice-1071) [Antarctica](https://theconversation.com/topics/antarctica-1189) [Global warming](https://theconversation.com/topics/global-warming-2768) [Sea level rise](https://theconversation.com/topics/sea-level-rise-6790) [Coastal flooding](https://theconversation.com/topics/coastal-flooding-8519) [Flooding](https://theconversation.com/topics/flooding-8771) [Ice sheets](https://theconversation.com/topics/ice-sheets-24915) [Antarctic ice sheet](https://theconversation.com/topics/antarctic-ice-sheet-70594) [Ocean science](https://theconversation.com/topics/ocean-science-109310) [Yucheng Lin, City University of Hong Kong, Robert Kopp, Rutgers University](https://theconversation.com/antarcticas-ice-loss-is-locked-in-and-extra-snowfall-wont-save-it-thats-bad-news-for-low-lying-coastal-areas-elsewhere-293066) Authors [Yucheng Lin](https://theconversation.com/profiles/yucheng-lin-2767294) Assistant Professor of Energy and Environment, City University of Hong Kong [Robert Kopp](https://theconversation.com/profiles/robert-kopp-275595) Professor of Earth Sciences, Rutgers University Disclosure statement Yucheng Lin receives funding from City University of Hong Kong (project number 9610793). Robert Kopp receives funding from the U.S. National Science Foundation (NSF) and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA). Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) Languages [Portuguese](https://theconversation.com/perda-de-gelo-na-antartica-ja-e-inevitavel-e-isso-e-uma-ma-noticia-para-as-areas-litoraneas-293309) English DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.77g3yh4g3](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.77g3yh4g3)", "content_hash": "0a7b3ef2756744bed78a46326061829b87f6c56754263ea8db1cb50da9232fcf", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:09Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "980", "title": "As cities prepare for 2026\u2019s super El Ni\u00f1o, some crucial blind spots can\u2019t be\u00a0ignored", "url": "https://theconversation.com/as-cities-prepare-for-2026s-super-el-nino-some-crucial-blind-spots-cant-be-ignored-290787", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/as-cities-prepare-for-2026s-super-el-nino-some-crucial-blind-spots-cant-be-ignored-290787", "text": "An affordable-housing crisis and a growing homeless population led Los Angeles to declared a shelter crisis during the 2015-16 El Ni\u00f1o. [David McNew/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/fernando-lopez-carries-possessions-along-the-los-angeles-news-photo/498136120?adppopup=true) As cities prepare for 2026\u2019s super El Ni\u00f1o, some crucial blind spots can\u2019t be ignored Published: September 21, 2026 8:38am EDT https://theconversation.com/as-cities-prepare-for-2026s-super-el-nino-some-crucial-blind-spots-cant-be-ignored-290787 https://theconversation.com/as-cities-prepare-for-2026s-super-el-nino-some-crucial-blind-spots-cant-be-ignored-290787 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article This year\u2019s El Ni\u00f1o has the potential to be one of the [strongest in living memory](https://www.bbc.com/weather/articles/c3ekg93vjz9o), and the evidence is showing up around the world. The U.S. just saw its [hottest summer on record](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/news/national-climate-202608) in the Lower 48 states. Drought is [slowing shipping in the Panama Canal](https://theconversation.com/2026s-el-nino-is-shaping-up-to-be-a-doozy-its-already-affecting-global-shipping-fish-behavior-and-heat-291490). The Pacific is experiencing an [exceptionally busy hurricane season](https://theconversation.com/a-second-hurricane-just-sideswiped-hawaii-why-the-islands-usual-force-field-seems-to-be-down-in-2026-291524), while the [Atlantic is unusually quiet](https://substack.com/home/post/p-215680819). Those are just a few early signs. Forecasters warn that this El Ni\u00f1o could bring [extreme heat, destructive storms and flooding](https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/09/1168265) to many parts of the planet through early 2027. Storm waves crash onto seaside houses at Mondos Beach, Calif., on Jan. 12, 2016, during a strong El Ni\u00f1o. [Mark Ralston/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/el-nino-generated-storm-waves-crash-onto-seaside-houses-at-news-photo/504713230?adppopup=true) I am a [researcher who works on climate and disaster resilience](https://www.climatepolicylab.org/bethany-tietjen), and I study how governments prepare for climate disasters and help to identify what they are missing. Community planning for El Ni\u00f1o often starts by identifying infrastructure that\u2019s vulnerable to extreme weather. What can be harder to address are the needs of the populations most at risk, including low-income communities and older adults who may need help preparing their homes and themselves for extreme conditions. As communities prepare for the rising risk of extreme weather, they need to be aware of the blind spots. What is El Ni\u00f1o and what\u2019s at risk? El Ni\u00f1o is part of a [natural climate pattern](https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/ninonina.html) that appears every few years when temperatures in the eastern Pacific Ocean rise. Its effects can [shift weather patterns](https://theconversation.com/2026s-el-nino-is-shaping-up-to-be-a-doozy-its-already-affecting-global-shipping-fish-behavior-and-heat-291490) away from what is considered normal. Typically wet regions can get unusually dry, while dry areas can experience extreme rainfall that causes flooding. Global average temperatures also tend to rise during El Ni\u00f1o conditions, leaving people facing [dangerous heat, both indoors](https://theconversation.com/heat-waves-can-leave-homes-dangerously-hot-even-for-young-healthy-adults-272025) [and outdoors](https://theconversation.com/extreme-heat-is-taking-a-toll-on-firefighters-as-wildfires-stretch-crews-to-the-limit-290571). What is El Ni\u00f1o? On the U.S. West Coast, for example, the [combination of El Ni\u00f1o conditions with king tides and rising sea levels](https://www.ppic.org/blog/supercharged-sea-levels-this-winter-will-forecast-the-future/) could lead to more coastal flooding, erosion and damage to homes and infrastructure. In the past, [flood damage and landslides](https://www.conservation.ca.gov/cgs/landslides/el-nino) from heavy rain during El Ni\u00f1o have shut down highways and affected public services long after a storm has passed. In winter, El Ni\u00f1o typically shifts weather patterns across the U.S. However, every El Nino is different \u2013 conditions during one El Ni\u00f1o may be very different from the next. [NOAA](https://www.noaa.gov/news-release/el-nino-forms-expected-to-strengthen-say-noaa-forecasters) Preparing for the [strong El Ni\u00f1o expected in late 2026](https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso_advisory/ensodisc.shtml) and early 2027 means ensuring that critical infrastructure is able to withstand flooding, temperature extremes and other severe weather conditions. That means inspecting flood control and drainage systems, trimming trees that could knock down power lines during high winds, and reinforcing or maintaining infrastructure such as [dams](https://www.smdp.com/county-outlines-el-nino-storm-preparations-as-forecasters-warn-of-historic-season/), [stormwater channels](https://www.ci.oceanside.ca.us/Home/Components/News/News/1042/14), [roads and bridges](https://www.sandiego.gov/elnino). Aging infrastructure, in particular, can be a problem. [Old levees and dams](https://infrastructurereportcard.org/cat-item/dams-infrastructure/), for example, can leave a community vulnerable to catastrophic flooding, as the Seattle area saw in December 2025 when [flooding from heavy rainfall broke levees](https://theconversation.com/west-coast-levee-failures-show-the-rising-risks-from-americas-aging-flood-defenses-272556) in multiple communities. Repairing and replacing large-scale infrastructure like this is expensive and time-consuming. Too often, by the time it fails, [repairs had been discussed for years](https://theconversation.com/heavy-rain-on-snow-is-testing-aging-dams-across-michigan-and-wisconsin-this-is-the-future-in-a-warming-world-281221) but not completed. Where we\u2019re finding gaps in disaster planning Infrastructure is only part of the challenge. Preparing a community for El Ni\u00f1o also means preparing people for what could be unprecedented conditions. A study I worked on with researchers at Tufts University reviewed climate adaptation and disaster preparedness in several high-risk communities in the United States. We [identified gaps](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2024.102882) in the policies, resources and institutional capacity available to help communities adapt to rising risks. Crews inspect damage to a Green River levee in the Seattle suburbs on Dec. 15, 2025. Thousands of people were urged to evacuate during a series of atmospheric river storms, and the National Guard was sent to monitor and reinforce several levees considered at risk. [AP Photo/Manuel Valdes](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/ExtremeWeatherWashington/d6e74ec36c664ee88b4a43c6abb0b7c5/photo) In particular, we found that governments often focus heavily on physical risks while giving too little attention to [social vulnerability](https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/111729). Vulnerable populations, including people experiencing homelessness, older adults and people with disabilities, often face [higher risks and greater challenges in preparing for disasters](https://theconversation.com/evacuating-in-disasters-like-hurricane-milton-isnt-simple-there-are-reasons-people-stay-in-harms-way-240869). People with limited mobility, for example, may have [difficulty evacuating quickly or getting into shelters](https://www.ready.gov/people-disabilities). Individuals with limited financial resources might have [fewer options for relocating or for making home improvements](https://www.povertylaw.org/article/what-natural-disasters-reveal-about-racism-and-poverty-2/) in preparation for extreme weather. Someone experiencing homelessness may not have safe shelter. In the Los Angeles area, homeless people often set up camp in normally dry riverbeds. But those areas are at high risk of flooding during El Ni\u00f1o. Heavy rains during the 2015-16 El Ni\u00f1o swept away this camp\u2019s mattresses. [Genaro Molina/Los Angeles Times via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/felipe-flores-lopez-bed-floats-away-as-he-looses-his-news-photo/503500210?adppopup=true) It is often easier for governments to identify and invest in physical infrastructure than to invest in policies that address complex social vulnerabilities like these. For example, building a seawall can be [tied to a specific project or funding stream](https://www.nlc.org/article/2026/07/16/where-do-cities-get-most-infrastructure-funding-municipal-bonds/). Reducing social vulnerability, on the other hand, may require coordination across housing, healthcare, emergency management and social services. And it typically requires sustained investments over time. Yet, preparing successfully for extreme weather often requires considering housing and shelter needs, healthcare access and support services. After a wildfire or major flooding, people may need [temporary rental assistance or affordable housing](https://theconversation.com/rents-rise-faster-after-disasters-but-a-federal-program-can-help-restrain-excesses-247307) to avoid displacement. Older adults or people with disabilities may need [transportation to shelters or assistance](https://theconversation.com/evacuating-in-disasters-like-hurricane-milton-isnt-simple-there-are-reasons-people-stay-in-harms-way-240869) getting medications and healthcare. Many of these needs [can be difficult to address](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2024.102882) because the underlying vulnerabilities are deeply entrenched. Issues such as [affordable housing shortages](https://apnews.com/article/affordable-housing-shortage-poorest-us-80fab2a0fb645e4d629b0ef7e3c2b496), poverty or difficulty accessing healthcare cannot be solved by a single disaster-preparedness policy. But they do [shape how communities are able to recover](https://theconversation.com/in-many-of-appalachias-flood-ravaged-areas-residents-have-little-choice-but-rebuild-in-risky-locations-240429) from extreme weather. Identifying gaps before extreme weather strikes A community\u2019s preparedness often improves after an event, when the gaps become obvious. The challenge is identifying those gaps before a disaster occurs. In Los Angeles, emergency managers are using a good method to meet that challenge in 2026: They are carrying out [scenario exercises](https://laist.com/news/climate-environment/la-county-emergency-managers-rush-to-prepare-for-el-nino) that bring together county departments and community disaster management coordinators to practice how they would respond to an El-Ni\u00f1o-driven extreme weather event. These exercises are designed to identify gaps in existing plans and figure out what might be needed if severe storms, flooding or mudslides occur. [Scenario exercises](https://www.planning.org/knowledgebase/scenarioplanning/) allow people involved in disaster response to work through a hypothetical event before it happens. Heavy downpours in October 2015 caused mudslides that trapped vehicles on Interstate 5 north of Los Angeles. The storm was early in the season for El Ni\u00f1o damage and served as a warning of the risks to come. [Mark Ralston/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/police-cars-drive-past-vehicles-that-are-stuck-on-a-road-news-photo/493001850) In both the [San Francisco Bay Area](https://www.kqed.org/news/12097546/el-nino-could-bring-a-wet-wild-winter-how-can-you-and-your-home-be-ready) and in [Durham County, North Carolina](https://dconc.gov/Engineering-and-Environmental1/Documents/Sustainability/July-August-2026-Durham-County-Sustainability-Blog.pdf), officials are talking publicly about the risks to encourage residents to prepare. Bay Area officials are stressing the importance of following emergency management notifications and weather forecasts, particularly for individuals living close to the water or on hillsides that could face flash flooding and landslides. In [Oceanside, California](https://www.ci.oceanside.ca.us/Home/Components/News/News/1042/), city officials convened an El Ni\u00f1o working group that included representatives from their public works, engineering, fire and police departments to coordinate planning, communications strategies and the monitoring of the San Luis Rey River. Broader lessons The challenge of preparing for El Ni\u00f1o in the face of uncertainty reflects some of the broader issues humanity faces when preparing for [future climate changes](https://theconversation.com/climate-change-is-intensifying-the-water-cycle-bringing-more-powerful-storms-and-flooding-heres-what-the-science-shows-187951). Keeping up with infrastructure maintenance, scenario planning ahead of heat or storm seasons, understanding the needs of vulnerable populations and having plans in place to meet them can help prevent a severe storm from becoming a major disaster for the community. [Housing](https://theconversation.com/topics/housing-376) [Disability](https://theconversation.com/topics/disability-1181) [Health care](https://theconversation.com/topics/health-care-2204) [ENSO](https://theconversation.com/topics/enso-2467) [Extreme weather](https://theconversation.com/topics/extreme-weather-3799) [El Nino](https://theconversation.com/topics/el-nino-5638) [Homelessness](https://theconversation.com/topics/homelessness-10211) [Older adults](https://theconversation.com/topics/older-adults-33768) [Emergency response](https://theconversation.com/topics/emergency-response-39472) [Disaster preparedness](https://theconversation.com/topics/disaster-preparedness-52989) [El Ni\u00f1o Southern Oscillation](https://theconversation.com/topics/el-nino-southern-oscillation-61187) [Bethany Tietjen, Tufts University](https://theconversation.com/as-cities-prepare-for-2026s-super-el-nino-some-crucial-blind-spots-cant-be-ignored-290787) Author [Bethany Tietjen](https://theconversation.com/profiles/bethany-tietjen-1384556) Research Fellow in Climate Policy, The Fletcher School, Tufts University Disclosure statement Bethany Tietjen does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Tufts University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/tufts-university-1024) provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.94c44ja3e](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.94c44ja3e)", "content_hash": "18ad7b37e3954a16572407a9b0a49526e3ba4819fddbecfeb9cc573e6b62ab63", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:10Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "981", "title": "Why the nor'easter\u2019s storm surge was so high, and what it says about the El Ni\u00f1o winter\u00a0ahead", "url": "https://theconversation.com/why-the-noreasters-storm-surge-was-so-high-and-what-it-says-about-the-el-nino-winter-ahead-293082", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/why-the-noreasters-storm-surge-was-so-high-and-what-it-says-about-the-el-nino-winter-ahead-293082", "text": "A nor'easter\u2019s storm surge swept into restaurants and homes in Sea Bright, N.J., in late September 2026. [AP Photo/Andres Kudacki](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/APTOPIX%20NJ%20Extreme%20Weather/1c2e87ae3c2f45e39974bd1c5edeac05/photo) Why the nor'easter\u2019s storm surge was so high, and what it says about the El Ni\u00f1o winter ahead Published: September 30, 2026 8:44am EDT https://theconversation.com/why-the-noreasters-storm-surge-was-so-high-and-what-it-says-about-the-el-nino-winter-ahead-293082 https://theconversation.com/why-the-noreasters-storm-surge-was-so-high-and-what-it-says-about-the-el-nino-winter-ahead-293082 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article As a [powerful nor\u2019easter](https://apnews.com/video/a-powerful-noreaster-pummels-the-shoreline-in-the-eastern-u-s-causing-travel-disruption-abcf6e7b21094627b22b09e1787c3887) hit the U.S. Atlantic Coast in late September 2026, the ocean poured into coastal neighborhoods from North Carolina to New York. Water from the storm surge and heavy rain filled streets and surrounded homes as near-hurricane-force winds knocked out power. It may have [even uncovered a shipwreck more than a century old](https://www.npr.org/2026/09/27/nx-s1-5982785/nantucket-shipwreck-uncovered-by-noreaster). Atlantic City, New Jersey, saw its highest water level since Superstorm Sandy in 2012, as the storm surge [washed into houses and vehicles and eroded protective beach dunes](https://whyy.org/articles/noreaster-flooding-new-jersey-2026/). I [study storm systems](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=kXsr-UMAAAAJ&hl=en). People may be wondering if the nor\u2019easter flooding is a prelude for what to expect as a [strong El Ni\u00f1o](https://theconversation.com/2026s-el-nino-is-shaping-up-to-be-a-doozy-its-already-affecting-global-shipping-fish-behavior-and-heat-291490) continues to build this winter and as sea level rises in the future. To understand the risks, let\u2019s break down what caused the water to rise so high. A computer model forecasts the nor'easter\u2019s winds in late September 2026, showing how they pushed water onshore. Brian Tang, NOAA images The storm surge Several factors go into high water levels, and they combined to worsen flooding during the nor\u2019easter. [Storm surge](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/surge/) is the piling up of water caused by strong winds pushing water onshore. As the nor\u2019easter began to take shape, gale-force winds began pushing water over hundreds of miles of the Atlantic toward the coast. As the storm strengthened and its pressure dropped, the winds grew stronger and this push of water intensified. Fences and sand dunes lie in ruin along Maryland\u2019s Ocean City Beach on Sept. 28, 2026. [Finn Gomez/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/fences-and-sand-dunes-lay-in-ruin-along-ocean-city-beach-on-news-photo/2297616261?adppopup=true) There are four basic factors that determine the surge height at any given location: the strength of the onshore wind, the size of the storm, the shape of the coastline and the depth of the ocean. The September 2026 surge was highest where the water was directed into shallow bays and inlets. In those areas, the push of water becomes concentrated into a small area with nowhere to go but onto land. Some of the largest surges during the nor\u2019easter were in [Raritan Bay in New Jersey](https://www.nj.com/weather/2026/09/repeated-flooding-is-what-set-weekend-noreaster-apart-from-sandy-experts-say.html) and Delaware Bay. The slow movement of the nor\u2019easter up the East Coast resulted in a large, sustained surge. High tides raised the flood risk Inundation \u2013 the actual water level above the ground \u2013 depends on both the storm surge and the tide. The greatest flooding occurs when the peak storm surge coincides with high tide. Worse yet is when the high tides are higher than normal, such as around a full moon. The [gravitational pull](https://www.britannica.com/video/How-does-the-Moon-affect-the-tides/-320503) of [both the Moon and the Sun](https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides06_variations.html) combine around a full moon to make the tides larger. That\u2019s what happened during the height of the nor\u2019easter. The phenomenon of tides is primarily driven by the Moon\u2019s gravitational influence on the ocean. [NOAA Education](https://www.noaa.gov/education/resource-collections/ocean-coasts/tides) Additionally, the long duration of the nor\u2019easter allowed inundation to occur over multiple high tides, resulting in repeated rounds of coastal flooding. The role of sea-level rise Another important factor for inundation is sea-level rise. Sea-level rise is occurring because the planet is warming. That melts glaciers, [sending more water into the oceans](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-026-08088-0), and [causes the ocean to physically expand](https://courses.ems.psu.edu/earth107/node/1502). Sea-level rise is occurring faster in parts of the U.S. East Coast compared with other areas, primarily due to something called [post-glacial rebound](https://www.whoi.edu/press-room/news-release/why-is-sea-level-rising-higher-in-some-places-along-u-s--east-coast-than-others/). The weight of the Laurentide ice sheet that covered large parts of North America thousands of years ago pushed down the land below it, while causing the land around its edges to rise \u2013 including along the mid-Atlantic seaboard. As the ice melted, those edges that had lifted slowly sank again. Arrows represent the direction and magnitude of relative sea-level change along the U.S. East Coast. [NOS/NOAA/CO-OPS](https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/trends-and-extremes/main-product.html?mapTab=station&amp;tab=trends&amp;station-id=8534720&amp;plot=relative-sea-level-change-ntde&tab=trends) In Atlantic City, [sea level is rising](https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/trends-and-extremes/main-product.html?mapTab=station&tab=trends&station-id=8534720&plot=relative-sea-level-change-ntde) at a rate of [about 2 inches (50 millimeters) every decade](https://njclimateresourcecenter.rutgers.edu/resources/nj-sea-level-rise-reports/). A couple of inches can mean the difference between water staying out of one\u2019s house or pouring in. Even if nor\u2019easters don\u2019t become stronger in the future, they will become more damaging because sea level is already higher, and more places will experience flooding. More to come this El Ni\u00f1o winter? With one of the strongest El Ni\u00f1os in recent memory already [affecting weather patterns](https://theconversation.com/2026s-el-nino-is-shaping-up-to-be-a-doozy-its-already-affecting-global-shipping-fish-behavior-and-heat-291490) around the world, is this early-season nor\u2019easter a sign of things to come? It\u2019s difficult to say. One study found that strong nor\u2019easters along the East Coast are [more likely during El Ni\u00f1o years](https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/clim/21/1/2007jcli1705.1.xml). Computer model seasonal forecasts suggest an [increased chance of above-normal precipitation](https://charts.ecmwf.int/products/seasonal_system5_standard_rain?area=NAME&base_time=202609010000&stats=tsum&valid_time=202612020000) along the East Coast during the winter. This suggests moisture-laden storms with heavy precipitation may be more likely to move up the coast with damaging storm surges. However, every El Ni\u00f1o is different, and there are few past El Ni\u00f1os close to the strength expected this time to compare with. A family carries their belongings through the water of a flooded road during a nor'easter storm on Sept. 26, 2026, in Point Pleasant, N.J., where a voluntary evacuation had been issued. [Ryan Murphy/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/family-carries-their-belongings-through-the-water-of-a-news-photo/2296750646?adppopup=true) The best advice this El Ni\u00f1o winter is to be proactive in preparing and monitoring the weather. Check your insurance policies to understand your coverage, particularly if you are in a flood-prone area. Have an [emergency supply kit](https://theconversation.com/what-goes-into-a-disaster-kit-and-go-bag-heres-a-checklist-for-wildfires-hurricanes-and-other-storms-168638) and make sure its contents are refreshed. Should a big storm threaten, pay attention to the weather forecast from trusted sources and listen to the advice of your community\u2019s emergency managers to keep everyone safe. [Oceans](https://theconversation.com/topics/oceans-183) [Sea levels](https://theconversation.com/topics/sea-levels-1049) [Extreme weather](https://theconversation.com/topics/extreme-weather-3799) [El Nino](https://theconversation.com/topics/el-nino-5638) [Sea level rise](https://theconversation.com/topics/sea-level-rise-6790) [Tropical storms](https://theconversation.com/topics/tropical-storms-7001) [Storms](https://theconversation.com/topics/storms-8517) [Atlantic Ocean](https://theconversation.com/topics/atlantic-ocean-12005) [Coastal towns](https://theconversation.com/topics/coastal-towns-13095) [Coastal](https://theconversation.com/topics/coastal-21741) [Brian Tang, University at Albany, State University of New York](https://theconversation.com/why-the-noreasters-storm-surge-was-so-high-and-what-it-says-about-the-el-nino-winter-ahead-293082) Author [Brian Tang](https://theconversation.com/profiles/brian-tang-1539057) Professor of Atmospheric Science, University at Albany, State University of New York Disclosure statement Brian Tang receives funding from the National Science Foundation, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, and the Center for Western Weather and Water Extremes at Scripps Institution of Oceanography at UC San Diego. Partners [University at Albany, State University of New York](https://theconversation.com/institutions/university-at-albany-state-university-of-new-york-1978) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.f3enedpm7](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.f3enedpm7)", "content_hash": "ad14263f326cd59497d20aeb79a7b3cdfb30fa07723cbc6852e839a302413880", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:11Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "982", "title": "Using light to control brain cells \u2013 neuroscientist Karl Deisseroth explains how he made his Nobel\u2011winning discovery", "url": "https://theconversation.com/using-light-to-control-brain-cells-neuroscientist-karl-deisseroth-explains-how-he-made-his-nobel-winning-discovery-293926", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/using-light-to-control-brain-cells-neuroscientist-karl-deisseroth-explains-how-he-made-his-nobel-winning-discovery-293926", "text": "Optogenetics involves the use of light to alter the activity of neurons. [Westend61/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/illustration/glowing-fiber-optics-on-brain-scan-royalty-free-illustration/2287907473) Using light to control brain cells \u2013 neuroscientist Karl Deisseroth explains how he made his Nobel\u2011winning discovery Published: October 15, 2026 8:16am EDT https://theconversation.com/using-light-to-control-brain-cells-neuroscientist-karl-deisseroth-explains-how-he-made-his-nobel-winning-discovery-293926 https://theconversation.com/using-light-to-control-brain-cells-neuroscientist-karl-deisseroth-explains-how-he-made-his-nobel-winning-discovery-293926 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The [2026 Nobel Prize in physiology or medicine](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2026/press-release/) honored [Karl Deisseroth](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2026/deisseroth/facts/), [Peter Hegemann](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2026/hegemann/facts/) and [Georg Nagel](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2026/nagel/facts/) for their work developing optogenetics \u2013 a technique that allows researchers to control the activity of neurons with light. The Conversation Weekly podcast talked to Karl Deisseroth, a neuroscientist at Stanford University, about his journey developing optogenetics and its potential to treat a wide range of neuropsychiatric conditions. What follows is an edited transcript of the podcast. Take us back to the early 1990s. You\u2019re training to be a doctor and you start a rotation working with psychiatric patients. What questions about the brain did that raise for you? Both basic neuroscience (people asking how the brain works) and clinical neuroscience (doctors trying to help people with brain disorders) have faced a very similar problem, which is the brain is quite mysterious and quite hard to study. It\u2019s billions of fragile, transparent, very fast-acting cells, buried within the skull and doing very complex things. We don\u2019t have a good model for how the brain works, unlike the heart, which is very [obviously a pump](https://theconversation.com/engineering-mini-human-hearts-to-study-pregnancy-complications-and-birth-defects-226883). We don\u2019t actually know what the brain\u2019s trying to do. A lot of neuropsychiatric treatments are [not as effective as they could be](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2021.08.018), and developing new ideas has been hard. The treatments we had were [not grounded in a solid understanding](https://theconversation.com/im-part-of-the-team-writing-the-next-bible-of-psychiatry-for-the-first-time-the-dsm-will-use-biology-to-understand-mental-disorders-288064) of the cells involved. What are the cells actually doing? What are they trying to do? This was a problem that held back treatment. When I was in psychiatry training in the early 2000s, and also as a medical student in the late 1990s, I was very much impressed with the magnitude of the challenge. You went on to get a Ph.D. in neuroscience, and then you set up a lab at Stanford trying to understand how to control particular cells in the brain. What were the first experiments you did? I have a background in molecular biology, and I knew how to work with genes. So I was motivated to see if we could use genetics to make a treatment or an intervention that targets one kind of cell. Every cell has genes that tell it what to do. Different cells in the brain, just as other parts of the body, can be genetically targeted because different cells have different jobs. A cell that is a [dopamine neuron](https://theconversation.com/dopamine-is-a-brain-chemical-famously-linked-to-mood-and-pleasure-but-researchers-have-found-multiple-types-of-dopamine-neurons-with-different-functions-211985) has all the machinery needed to make the hormone dopamine. It uses different genes than a cell that makes the hormone serotonin, so they can be distinguished from each other. And so the thought was maybe I could have a way of controlling just the dopamine cells or just the serotonin cells by inserting a specific gene into them. But I didn\u2019t know what would work well, so I tried inserting different types of genes into neurons. Inside this flask is the unicellular algae that played a critical role in the development of optogenetics. [Tobias SchwarzSCHWARZ/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/german-scientist-darius-rauch-holds-a-flask-containing-news-photo/2298094993) Some of the genes I tried were quite conventional; they were genes from mammals that I thought might affect the activity of neurons. But one was a [gene from a single-celled algae](https://theconversation.com/how-pond-algae-led-to-a-nobel-prize-and-a-new-way-to-study-the-brain-293579) \u2013 a microbe that converts light to energy. This gene coded for a light-activated protein called channelrhodopsin-2 that Peter Hegemann and Georg Nagel identified a few years earlier. And I thought it was worth a try. It was the least likely to work, but why not try it? And amazingly, that was the one that worked. The genes that were lower risk didn\u2019t work, and this gene from a single-cell microbe actually did. Walk us through what you did. You inserted the gene into rat cells, right? I had a preparation of cultured rodent neurons in a dish \u2013 basically a 24-well plate that let me look at many different conditions at the same time in different wells of this plate. This let me test side by side the conventional mammalian genes and this gene from the algae. So the first step was to get the genes into the cells. Then I exposed some plates of cells to light, while others I did not. I used a test to see which cells had activated. When I looked across the cells in the screen, I found that the ones that had the algae gene and had gotten light were showing this marker of activation, whereas the others had a much lower signal. That was a really thrilling moment because it showed that this gene from a photosynthetic single-celled organism \u2013 almost as far as you can possibly be from mammals evolutionarily \u2013 was working beautifully in fragile mammalian neurons. It was a moment of a lot of pressure and complexity, but also delight. Listen to Karl Deisseroth on [The Conversation Weekly podcast](https://theconversation.com/nobel-laureate-karl-deisseroth-on-how-light-is-changing-what-we-know-about-the-brain-293925). You went on to name your method optogenetics \u2013 the ability to control specific nerves using light in the brain \u2013 and you soon went on to using it to control living mice in various ways. What was it like in those first few years of working with live animals? The [first experiment](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2021.08.018) [was in 2004](https://doi.org/10.1038/nn1525), but we didn\u2019t show that it could [control the behavior of freely moving mammals](https://doi.org/10.1523/JNEUROSCI.3578-07.2007) until 2007. So it took almost three years. But that first moment in 2007 was quite uplifting because that showed we had gone a big, big chunk of the way toward making it work. This was an experiment that was very visible and easy to understand. The right side of the brain controls the left side of the body and attention to the left side of the world. We put in a fiber optic into the right part of a mouse motor cortex \u2013 the part that controls movement \u2013 and when we flipped the switch, the mouse immediately began going left in circles. And then as soon as we turned off the laser light, it stopped and just looked up at us, as if nothing had happened. That, coupled with the first experiment \u2013 those were transcendent moments. What changed in your understanding of the brain in the moment you were able to switch particular neurons on and off? We understood that the technique would probably work to help us understand the brain better at that moment. And then we immediately started applying it to all sorts of different cells where things were mysterious \u2013 where nobody knew or couldn\u2019t prove exactly what one cell type or another was doing in perception and cognition and action. We were able to find cells that were involved in [hunger](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0866-8) [and](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aan6747) [thirst](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aav3932) \u2013 basic survival drives. We were able to identify cells and pathways controlling [aggression and defensiveness](https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09736), [parenting](https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13307), how all the [different parts of anxiety](https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12018) come on together and go away together, [memory](https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13294), [motivation](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41593-022-01229-9). The general applicability became clear over the subsequent years. Researchers are now using optogenetics in attempts to restore sight in people with visual impairment. How does that work? In 2010, two of my colleagues in Europe were interested in delivering one of these light-activated proteins to human eye samples \u2013 specifically the [retina of the eye](https://www.aao.org/eye-health/anatomy/retina-103) \u2013 which worked very well. So they did careful studies in monkeys and in people. And in 2021, just five years ago, they published a paper showing they could [confer light sensitivity back](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-021-01351-4) to the retinas of a person who was completely blind from a neurodegenerative disease called [retinitis pigmentosa](https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/retinitis-pigmentosa). That\u2019s what we call [direct optogenetics](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41593-025-02097-9). It\u2019s somewhat complicated because you have to deliver the gene and light to the person. Karl Deisseroth, left, and his wife, Michelle Monje-Deisseroth, were at home when they were notified of his Nobel win. [AP Photo/Noah Berger](https://newsroom.ap.org/detail/APTOPIX%20Nobel%20Medicine/7bac147740db414b9a0fdb05382eed43/photo) And going back to your psychiatric patients, who you still see in the clinic today, how do you hope optogenetics will be able to help them? There\u2019s a much easier and more broadly impactful avenue that we call [indirect optogenetics](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41593-025-02097-9), which uses the knowledge that comes from studying which cells matter in a symptom and then designing medications that target those cells. This is something we\u2019ve really lacked in neuropsychiatry for a long time. Indirect optogenetics is making strong headway for [autism and schizophrenia](https://www.fiercebiotech.com/biotech/how-nobel-winning-optogenetics-went-pond-potential-treatments-vision-loss-and-schizophrenia). There are trials that are moving to their final stages for those disorders. My particular clinical specialty is autism, and I see the difficulties that come with irritability and anxiety in these patients. And I can see optogenetics leading to new medications that will help. [Neuroscience](https://theconversation.com/topics/neuroscience-427) [Brain](https://theconversation.com/topics/brain-1544) [Nobel Prize](https://theconversation.com/topics/nobel-prize-1570) [Optogenetics](https://theconversation.com/topics/optogenetics-6523) [Neurons](https://theconversation.com/topics/neurons-9368) [Neuropsychiatry](https://theconversation.com/topics/neuropsychiatry-10916) [Q+A](https://theconversation.com/topics/q-a-100901) [TC global health](https://theconversation.com/topics/tc-global-health-189737) [2026 Nobel prize in physiology or medicine](https://theconversation.com/topics/2026-nobel-prize-in-physiology-or-medicine-193977) [Nobel Prize 2026](https://theconversation.com/topics/nobel-prize-2026-194011) [Karl Deisseroth, Stanford University](https://theconversation.com/using-light-to-control-brain-cells-neuroscientist-karl-deisseroth-explains-how-he-made-his-nobel-winning-discovery-293926) Author [Karl Deisseroth](https://theconversation.com/profiles/karl-deisseroth-2770199) D. H. Chen Professor, Professor of Bioengineering and of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Stanford University Disclosure statement Karl Deisseroth has received funding from the NIH and the Howard Hughes Medical Institute. He was the founder of MapLight Therapeutics, a biopharmaceutical company. Partners [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.cvt5vsvx6](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.cvt5vsvx6)", "content_hash": "dc0c35226735171e9bb7e88c18e7f9c17b0ea44506bdfbb10d496dfaa1fe6f0d", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:12Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "983", "title": "A molecule\u2019s \u2018handedness\u2019 can determine whether it\u2019s an effective drug \u2013 4 examples of pharmaceuticals where chirality\u00a0matters", "url": "https://theconversation.com/a-molecules-handedness-can-determine-whether-its-an-effective-drug-4-examples-of-pharmaceuticals-where-chirality-matters-293909", "final_url": "https://theconversation.com/a-molecules-handedness-can-determine-whether-its-an-effective-drug-4-examples-of-pharmaceuticals-where-chirality-matters-293909", "text": "A molecule\u2019s \u2018handedness\u2019 can determine whether it\u2019s an effective drug \u2013 4 examples of pharmaceuticals where chirality matters Published: October 8, 2026 2:46pm EDT Henri Kagan of France and Kenso Soai from Japan won the 2026 Nobel Prize in chemistry for their work on chiral molecules. [Jonathan Nackstrand/AFP via Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/heiner-linke-chair-of-the-nobel-committee-for-chemistry-news-photo/2298426507?phrase=chemistry%20nobel&adppopup=true) https://theconversation.com/a-molecules-handedness-can-determine-whether-its-an-effective-drug-4-examples-of-pharmaceuticals-where-chirality-matters-293909 https://theconversation.com/a-molecules-handedness-can-determine-whether-its-an-effective-drug-4-examples-of-pharmaceuticals-where-chirality-matters-293909 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The 2026 Nobel Prize in chemistry concerns a property called chirality, which may sound like an abstract science term but is actually all around you, all the time. Picture a spiral galaxy rotating in space, or follow the outline of a snail\u2019s shell as it spirals clockwise to the tip. In each case, you can draw a mirror image of the spiral that won\u2019t overlap with the original picture. You can even see this property on your own body: Hold out your hands, palms up. Your hands are mirror images of one another, yet however you turn them, you can never lay your right hand exactly on top of your left with both palms facing the same way. All of these are [examples of chirality](https://www.chemistryviews.org/details/ezine/10567177/What_is_Chirality/). Chiral objects have a mirror image that cannot be superimposed directly on them. This concept shows up in geometry in the shapes of organisms and, most consequentially, in the molecules that make up living things. This week, the [2026 Nobel Prize in chemistry](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2026/press-release/) went to [Henri Kagan](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2026/kagan/facts/) and [Kenso Soai](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2026/soai/facts/) for their work on how chemistry can favor one chiral version, or \u201chand,\u201d over the other. Why handedness matters [As chemists](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=0LnzexcAAAAJ&hl=en) who [design and measure](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=4lgltlQAAAAJ&hl=en) chiral molecules, we work with this mirror-image problem every day. Molecular handedness [has far-reaching implications](https://theconversation.com/many-drugs-have-mirror-chemical-structures-one-helpful-and-the-other-harmful-a-nobel-winning-discovery-allows-chemists-to-make-either-on-demand-293914), and you can see why when you consider how chiral objects interact with one another in daily life. A regular claw hammer is an achiral, or nonchiral, tool. It works identically whether you hold it in your left hand or right hand. But a pair of scissors is often made to favor a right-handed user. If you are left-handed and have used right-handed scissors, you know the result: Squeezing them with your right hand pulls the blades inward against each other, while squeezing them with the left pushes the blades outward, so they fold the paper instead of cutting it. Scissors are chiral tools designed to interact with the human hand, which is also chiral. Some molecules have \u2018mirror images,\u2019 which may act in different ways in chemical reactions. [DariaRen/iStock via Getty Images Plus](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/colorful-stereoisomers-royalty-free-image/1148425363) Nearly every protein on Earth is [built from one mirror-image form](https://www.science.org/content/article/breakthrough-could-explain-why-life-molecules-are-left-or-right-handed) of each amino acid, and DNA and RNA from one form of sugars. DNA assembles to form helices of one, and only one, chirality. Similarly, snail shells almost always spiral in the same direction for every snail species. This omnipresent single-handedness of every known living thing is [called homochirality](https://cen.acs.org/biological-chemistry/origins-of-life/closer-understanding-origins-biological-homochirality/104/web/2026/05). When chemists make chiral molecules without adding any external chiral agents, they get a [racemic mixture](https://chem.libretexts.org/Ancillary_Materials/Reference/Organic_Chemistry_Glossary/Racemic_Mixture): an even 50:50 blend of both hands. So how did the primordial soup that formed life on Earth, which presumably had this exact balanced chemistry, end up one-handed? The Nobel Prize This question remained open until the groundbreaking discoveries honored by the 2026 Nobel Prize in chemistry, awarded to Kagan and Soai. In the 1980s, Kagan found that a catalyst, a helper that speeds up a reaction, does not have to be entirely made of molecules with the same handedness to make a purely single-handed product. In some reactions, a partly pure catalyst yielded a product purer than the catalyst itself. In the 1990s, Soai found a molecule that makes copies of itself and favors its own hand over its mirror-image version. Start with a 51:49 imbalance, and after a few rounds the mixture is overwhelmingly one-handed. Later versions of the reaction amplified an imbalanced mixture of just 0.00005% to better than 99.5%. While the Soai reaction is a laboratory system and not a recreation of early Earth, it shows one possibility for how life may have chosen one-handedness. Handedness in health Because life evolved under homochirality, the human body is a highly chiral environment. Cell receptors and enzymes are built from single-handed proteins. Consequently, a pharmaceutical molecule enters the body like a key looking for its lock. If a drug is administered as a racemic mixture of both hands, one mirror image may fit the receptor perfectly, while its mirror twin doesn\u2019t. This twin can trigger unintended side effects, or it may fit as a key into an entirely different enzyme and disrupt your body\u2019s usual processes. Throughout medical history, controlling chirality has meant the difference between therapeutic breakthroughs and medical tragedies. Chemists label the two mirror-image forms with letters, D and L for sugars and R and S for most drugs, so the letter tells you which version you\u2019re holding. Four chiral molecules in particular can illustrate why handedness matters in medicine. The first is thalidomide. Sold in the late 1950s as a 50:50 mixture to treat insomnia and morning sickness, [thalidomide was widely prescribed](https://theconversation.com/explainer-what-is-thalidomide-8327) in Europe, Canada and Australia. After its manufacturer withdrew it in 1961, [more than 10,000 children](https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-15536544) were found to have been born with severe malformations, in which their limbs were shortened or failed to form. Thalidomide, originally prescribed for morning sickness, led to birth defects. A [1979 study in mice](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/583234/) pointed to thalidomide\u2019s [S-form as the culprit](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3614593/). But thalidomide\u2019s molecules are chemically flexible, meaning they can convert between the two forms while inside your body. Even a purely single-handed version would not have been safe. Another example is ibuprofen. Ibuprofen was developed at the British company Boots in the 1960s and has become one of the most [widely used pain relievers](https://theconversation.com/how-do-painkillers-actually-kill-pain-from-ibuprofen-to-fentanyl-its-about-meeting-the-pain-where-its-at-173804) in the world. Ibuprofen\u2019s [S form does the work](https://www.news-medical.net/health/Ibuprofen-Chemistry.aspx), blocking enzymes that produce chemical signals for pain and swelling. The R-form is largely inactive and does nothing against these signals. Luckily, [the body corrects the mix](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1352228/): An enzyme in your body converts a share of the R-form into the S-form, which is why a pill containing both forms works anyway. Ibuprofen pills sold over the counter contain both mirror-image forms of the drug, but your body converts the wrong-handed molecules into the kind it can use. [Photo by Kevin Carter/Getty Images](https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/kirkland-signature-ibuprofen-tablets-are-displayed-for-sale-news-photo/2287825360?phrase=ibuprofen&adppopup=true) A third example is L-DOPA. In 1969, neurologist Oliver Sacks [gave L-DOPA to patients](https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2024/09/30/coming-alive) who had spent decades nearly motionless after the [encephalitis epidemic](https://asm.org/articles/2025/december/encephalitis-lethargica-a-mysterious-neurological) of the early 20th century. Many patients woke up, spoke and moved in the episode later told in his book and the film \u201c[Awakenings](https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0099077/?_ssoLoop=1).\u201d The effect proved largely temporary, but the drug remains a mainstay of Parkinson\u2019s treatment. In Parkinson\u2019s disease, neurons producing a [chemical messenger called dopamine](https://theconversation.com/dopamine-is-a-brain-chemical-famously-linked-to-mood-and-pleasure-but-researchers-have-found-multiple-types-of-dopamine-neurons-with-different-functions-211985) degenerate, and the dopamine itself cannot cross [from the bloodstream into the brain](https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/24931-blood-brain-barrier-bbb). L-DOPA can, and enzymes in the brain then convert it into dopamine. Its mirror image, D-DOPA, is not a fit for that enzyme, so it does not make the transition. However, making only the L-form at scale was a challenge. In the 1970s, [William Knowles](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2001/knowles/facts/) at Monsanto solved this issue using a special chiral catalyst. This work [earned him a share](https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2001/knowles/lecture/) of the 2001 Nobel Prize in chemistry. A final example is glucose. Corn or grape sugar is commonly known as glucose, but the term actually refers to specifically one form, called the D-enantiomer, of this sugar. Cells run on D-glucose. The first [step of glycolysis](https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/glycolysis), the chain of reactions cells use to pull energy out of sugar, [uses an enzyme](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/biology/hexokinase-enzyme/) that recognizes only that form. Its mirror image, L-glucose, has the same physical properties, such as melting point, appearance and sweet taste. Yet, cellular enzymes cannot process it, so it supplies no calories and does not raise your blood sugar. In the 1980s, researchers [explored it as a low-calorie sweetener](https://doi.org/10.1038/221555a0) for people with diabetes, but high production costs made it financially unviable. Beyond biology Handedness matters well beyond the body. Chiral molecules emit and absorb light that is [circularly polarized](http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/phyopt/polclas.html), meaning it twists to the left or right as it travels. Researchers are [developing displays](https://cen.acs.org/materials/photonics/path-creating-polarized-OLED-displays/103/web/2025/12) that produce this type of light directly, which could be used to make screens that work in very bright environments, such as direct sunlight. The same property is useful for security. A [tag made with chiral molecules](https://www.technologynetworks.com/applied-sciences/news/scientists-create-uncrackable-coded-tags-with-dyed-liquid-crystals-to-prevent-counterfeiting-390281) can look like an ordinary mark in normal light but reveal a hidden pattern through a filter that passes only left-twisting or right-twisting light. This property could make it harder to counterfeit. Chirality also shapes how electrons move. Electrons have a [built-in property called spin](http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/spin.html), which you can picture as a tiny compass needle pointing up or down. When electrons pass through chiral molecules, one type of spin can get through more easily than others. Because spin can carry information, controlling it [could lead to electronics](https://doi.org/10.1002/smll.202203015) that waste less energy and to new quantum devices, although scientists are still debating how the effect works. Kagan\u2019s and Soai\u2019s work showed how a faint asymmetry between two chiral forms can grow into a decisive preference. Chemists are learning to build that preference into materials, not just medicines. [Chemistry](https://theconversation.com/topics/chemistry-730) [Nobel Prize](https://theconversation.com/topics/nobel-prize-1570) [Chemical reactions](https://theconversation.com/topics/chemical-reactions-5308) [Chirality](https://theconversation.com/topics/chirality-28530) [Nobel Prize in Chemistry](https://theconversation.com/topics/nobel-prize-in-chemistry-44351) [Organic chemistry](https://theconversation.com/topics/organic-chemistry-74686) [Isomers](https://theconversation.com/topics/isomers-121470) [Stereoisomers](https://theconversation.com/topics/stereoisomers-124934) [Nobel Prize 2026](https://theconversation.com/topics/nobel-prize-2026-194011) [Cedric Schaack, Mallory Green, Wake Forest University](https://theconversation.com/a-molecules-handedness-can-determine-whether-its-an-effective-drug-4-examples-of-pharmaceuticals-where-chirality-matters-293909) Authors [Cedric Schaack](https://theconversation.com/profiles/cedric-schaack-2770043) Assistant Professor of Chemistry, Wake Forest University [Mallory Green](https://theconversation.com/profiles/mallory-green-2770044) Assistant Professor of Chemistry, Wake Forest University Disclosure statement Cedric Schaack receives funding from the National Science Foundation (Award #2541093) and the American Chemical Society Petroleum Research Fund (70454-DNI4) Mallory Green does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners [Wake Forest University](https://theconversation.com/institutions/wake-forest-university-1709) provides funding as a member of The Conversation US. [View all partners](https://theconversation.com/us/partners) DOI [https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.quy44jwhn](https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.quy44jwhn)", "content_hash": "eacc2042ed50939ec48d69814576e6e6682da590a1d486e7bc36051a50c33b12", "domain": "theconversation", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:21:13Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "984", "title": "Troops kill two suspected kidnappers, rescue victim, recover N10m ransom in Kano", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/troops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/troops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano/", "text": "[Metro](https://dailypost.ng/metro/)Troops kill two suspected kidnappers, rescue victim, recover N10m ransom in KanoPublished on October 17, 2026By [Muhammad Bukar](https://dailypost.ng/author/muhammad-bukar/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/troops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano/comments/)Troops of 3 Brigade, Operation MESA, Kano, have rescued a kidnapped victim, neutralised two suspected kidnappers and recovered N10 million in ransom during an operation in Falgore Forest, Doguwa Local Government Area (LGA) of Kano State.According to Major Babatunde Zubairu of the Army Directorate of Information, the operation followed the abduction of Alhaji Kabiru Aliyu on October 11, 2026, at Fanyabo Village, Falgore Forest, Doguwa LGA, Kano State. The victim was reportedly returning from Yandoka Market in Kaduna State at about midnight when the incident occurred.The kidnappers subsequently contacted the victim\u2019s family in Yan Taburmi Village, Falgore Forest, demanding a ransom for his release.Acting on credible intelligence, troops of 3 Brigade, in conjunction with personnel of the Nigeria Police Force, the Department of State Services (DSS) and local hunters, mounted an ambush in the area.Recommended[2027: No vacancy in Aso Rock, North will vote 100 per cent for Tinubu - Kano Gov, Yusuf](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/)[COAS charges troops to sustain offensive against terrorists](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/coas-charges-troops-to-sustain-offensive-against-terrorists-assures-improved-welfare-2/)[Nurse dies during asthma attack at Ogun psychiatric hospital as colleague alleges oxygen failure](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/nurse-dies-during-asthma-attack-at-ogun-psychiatric-hospital-as-colleague-alleges-oxygen-failure/)The operation resulted in the neutralisation of two suspected kidnappers and the successful rescue of the victim, while other members of the kidnapping syndicate fled towards Falgore Forest.During a subsequent search of the area, the troops recovered N10 million reportedly paid by the victim\u2019s family as ransom. The recovered money was subsequently returned to the victim and his family.The Commander of 3 Brigade, Nigerian Army, Brigadier General Saka Jimoh, commended the troops for their alertness, professionalism and fighting spirit.He reaffirmed the brigade\u2019s commitment to sustaining pressure on criminal elements and preventing further security breaches in the area.The brigade commander also urged members of the public to continue providing credible and timely information to security agencies to support ongoing efforts to maintain peace and security in Kano State.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Troops%20kill%20two%20suspected%20kidnappers%2C%20rescue%20victim%2C%20recover%20N10m%20ransom%20in%20Kano%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Ftroops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano%2F) FacebookYou may like[Police arrest three men over alleged homosexual acts in Oyo](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/oyo-police-arrest-three-men-over-alleged-sodomy/)[2027 elections: Kano police warn against fake news, boast of robust security](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-elections-kano-police-warn-against-fake-news-assure-robust-security/)[Gunmen kill three police officers in Zamfara](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/bandits-kill-three-policemen-in-zamfara-night-attack/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/troops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano/comments/)", "content_hash": "33fe20d6c8c04c15bb3afb014480dec57e291afde54bb7043cd6dfee74cc11cd", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:28Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "985", "title": "COAS charges troops to sustain offensive against terrorists", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/coas-charges-troops-to-sustain-offensive-against-terrorists-assures-improved-welfare-2/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/coas-charges-troops-to-sustain-offensive-against-terrorists-assures-improved-welfare-2/", "text": "[News](https://dailypost.ng/hot-news/)COAS charges troops to sustain offensive against terroristsPublished on October 17, 2026By [John Gabriel](https://dailypost.ng/author/john-gabriel/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/coas-charges-troops-to-sustain-offensive-against-terrorists-assures-improved-welfare-2/comments/)The Chief of Army Staff (COAS), Lieutenant General Waidi Shaibu, has charged troops of the Joint Task Force North-East, Operation HADIN KAI, to sustain the operational momentum against Boko Haram and Islamic State West Africa Province (ISWAP) terrorists while maintaining the highest standards of discipline, resilience and professionalism in the ongoing counter-terrorism operations.In a statement by Colonel Appolonia Anele, Acting Director of Army Public Relations, the COAS gave the charge on Saturday, 17 October 2026, while addressing officers and soldiers at the 212 Tank Battalion Parade Ground, Maiduguri, Borno State, during an operational assessment visit to the North-East theatre.Lieutenant General Shaibu commended the troops for their courage, sacrifices and sustained offensive operations, which have significantly degraded terrorist capabilities and contributed to improved security across the region.He urged them to consolidate the gains recorded, maintain operational vigilance and intensify efforts towards the decisive defeat of terrorism.Recommended[Veteran journalist Dele Cole dies at 86](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/)[Peter Obi questions why Nigeria did not declare World Poverty Day public holiday](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/)[Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/)He emphasised that discipline, combat readiness and unwavering commitment remained critical to operational successThe Army Chief reminded the troops of the nation's confidence in their ability to restore lasting peace and stability to the North-East.General Shaibu assured personnel of sustained attention to their welfare, including the provision of modern combat equipment, improved accommodation, adequate medical support and timely troop rotation to enhance morale and operational effectiveness.\"You must remain disciplined, resilient and focused. The nation is proud of you and looks up to you for the final defeat of terrorism,\" the COAS charged.Earlier, the Army Chief received a comprehensive operational briefing from the Theatre Commander, Joint Task Force North-East, Operation HADIN KAI, Major General Ademola Ajose, and sector commanders. The briefing highlighted the prevailing security situation, operational successes, emerging challenges and strategies for sustaining pressure on terrorist elements across the theatre.In a demonstration of his commitment to personnel welfare, Lieutenant General Shaibu also interacted with wounded soldiers, commending their courage and sacrifices in defence of the nation. He assured them of continued medical attention, rehabilitation and institutional support.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=COAS%20charges%20troops%20to%20sustain%20offensive%20against%20terrorists%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Fcoas-charges-troops-to-sustain-offensive-against-terrorists-assures-improved-welfare-2%2F) FacebookYou may like[Troops kill two suspected kidnappers, rescue victim, recover N10m ransom in Kano](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/troops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano/)[Wrong choices of politicians kept Nigeria like this - Ex-NCDC boss, Ihekweazu charges students not to lose hope](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/)[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/coas-charges-troops-to-sustain-offensive-against-terrorists-assures-improved-welfare-2/comments/)", "content_hash": "0c90b5cf492406a519501da2b829b9f27f837fff35c61b392edf5f919cc55566", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:30Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "986", "title": "2027: Makinde's camp names 12 states that will stop Tinubu [FULL LIST]", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-makindes-camp-names-12-states-that-will-stop-tinubu-full-list-3/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-makindes-camp-names-12-states-that-will-stop-tinubu-full-list-3/", "text": "[Politics](https://dailypost.ng/politics/)2027: Makinde's camp names 12 states that will stop Tinubu [FULL LIST]Published on October 17, 2026By [Matthew Atungwu](https://dailypost.ng/author/matthew-atungwu/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-makindes-camp-names-12-states-that-will-stop-tinubu-full-list-3/comments/)The camp of Allied Peoples Movement, APM, presidential candidate, Seyi Makinde, has vowed that its principal will defeat President Bola Tinubu in 12 battleground states in the 2027 presidential election.The camp made the vow in a statement by its Director of Strategic Communications, Richard Ihediwa, on Saturday.It said President Tinubu\u2019s planned visits to the states will not change growing opposition to his re-election.The states where it said Makinde will defeat Tinubu are Rivers, Oyo, Kaduna, Plateau, Borno, Enugu, Bauchi, Sokoto, Delta, Kano, Adamawa and Lagos, as well as the Federal Capital Territory, FCT.Recommended[2027: No vacancy in Aso Rock, North will vote 100 per cent for Tinubu - Kano Gov, Yusuf](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/)[Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/)[2027: G-100 coalition reveals when opposition will pick single candidate to face Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/)The group blamed the alleged resentment on the economic hardship Nigerians are facing under Tinubu\u2019s administration.It accused the President\u2019s policies and members of his inner circle of worsening public discontent through political arrogance.According to the group, the Tinubu administration has spent trillions of naira on wasteful programmes and projects while ordinary Nigerians continue to struggle.\u201cMr President should know that his visit to these states, if he eventually does, will not in any way change the direction or the determination of the people to vote him out.\u201cWe challenge President Tinubu not to have a change of mind but visit the states to face the people,\" it said.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=2027%3A%20Makinde's%20camp%20names%2012%20states%20that%20will%20stop%20Tinubu%20%5BFULL%20LIST%5D%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2F2027-makindes-camp-names-12-states-that-will-stop-tinubu-full-list-3%2F) FacebookYou may like[Troops kill two suspected kidnappers, rescue victim, recover N10m ransom in Kano](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/troops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano/)[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)[2027: NDC candidate raises alarm over alleged plot to remove party from ballot in Lagos](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-ndc-candidate-raises-alarm-over-alleged-plot-to-remove-party-from-ballot-in-lagos-2/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-makindes-camp-names-12-states-that-will-stop-tinubu-full-list-3/comments/)", "content_hash": "0e07dbe319b12e2362ee4e84449c4ee0cc3e40ac8d1a9e04ef41238a6e62b805", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:31Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "987", "title": "2027: Tinubu 1000 years wiser than opposition - Atiku's ex-ally, Showunmi", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-tinubu-1000-years-wiser-than-opposition-will-defeat-them-atikus-ex-ally-showunmi/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-tinubu-1000-years-wiser-than-opposition-will-defeat-them-atikus-ex-ally-showunmi/", "text": "[Politics](https://dailypost.ng/politics/)2027: Tinubu 1000 years wiser than opposition - Atiku's ex-ally, Showunmi Published on October 17, 2026By [Seun Opejobi](https://dailypost.ng/author/seun-opejobi/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-tinubu-1000-years-wiser-than-opposition-will-defeat-them-atikus-ex-ally-showunmi/comments/)Segun Showunmi, a former ally of the African Democratic Congress, ADC, presidential candidate, Atiku Abubakar, has said President Bola Tinubu is one thousand years wiser than all the opposition and he will defeat all those vying for the presidency in 2027.Showunmi made the remark while backing Tinubu's stance that he will not visit all states to campaign.Speaking in Abuja, the president had said he would focus on governance because there are bills to pay.Reacting, Showunmi faulted critics and opposition members berating Tinubu over his remark of being absent from campaign rallies.Recommended[2027: Makinde's camp names 12 states that will stop Tinubu [FULL LIST]](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-makindes-camp-names-12-states-that-will-stop-tinubu-full-list-3/)[2027: No vacancy in Aso Rock, North will vote 100 per cent for Tinubu - Kano Gov, Yusuf](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/)[Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/)Addressing a press conference on Saturday, Showunmi said: \u201cThe President said he won't attend all of the rallies and of course the children of hate have latched onto that.\"In the build up to 2023 when they were filling stadia and they were saying absolutely nothing apart from calling the name of their party and getting their friends to answer, the man told them at that time that town hall is different from ballot blue, they were laughing, did he not defeat them?\"Now he has told them that we are going to use science and technology, I have work to do and can't leave the nation's work undone and be jumping up and down the street to say hello, we have built our structure on how polling units will work and governors will be in charge.\"Instead of them going to figure out how to build structures, they are coming to ask me how he will not be there. You check a candidate out mostly so that he can be recognized.\"When a candidate has achieved optical recognition across board, he can design his campaign in a way that it's a little bit more efficient and a little bit more easy for him.\"Do you even like that kind of criss-crossing that they will be littering the whole place, whereas, they are talking to themselves, that guy called President Tinubu is a thousand years wiser than them and I guarantee you that he's going to defeat them again.\"Tinubu must not go around the country for campaigns. He is adopting a smarter approach. All these campaign rallies by political parties are just making noise on the streets, nothing meaningful.\u201dShare this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=2027%3A%20Tinubu%201000%20years%20wiser%20than%20opposition%20-%20Atiku's%20ex-ally%2C%20Showunmi%20%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2F2027-tinubu-1000-years-wiser-than-opposition-will-defeat-them-atikus-ex-ally-showunmi%2F) FacebookYou may like[2027: G-100 coalition reveals when opposition will pick single candidate to face Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/)[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)[2027: NDC candidate raises alarm over alleged plot to remove party from ballot in Lagos](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-ndc-candidate-raises-alarm-over-alleged-plot-to-remove-party-from-ballot-in-lagos-2/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-tinubu-1000-years-wiser-than-opposition-will-defeat-them-atikus-ex-ally-showunmi/comments/)", "content_hash": "38c2e6c92cee7be3e8cb2cb624ecf763d94438099e9fbadd8bc30ef25a6da260", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:32Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "988", "title": "Ballon d'Or 2026: He's ahead - Constantin Eckner names player to win award", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/ballon-dor-2026-hes-ahead-constantin-eckner-names-player-to-win-award/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/ballon-dor-2026-hes-ahead-constantin-eckner-names-player-to-win-award/", "text": "[Sport](https://dailypost.ng/sport-news/)Ballon d'Or 2026: He's ahead - Constantin Eckner names player to win award Published on October 17, 2026By [Don Silas](https://dailypost.ng/author/don-silas/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/ballon-dor-2026-hes-ahead-constantin-eckner-names-player-to-win-award/comments/)Football pundit and commentator, Constantin Eckner, has backed Bayern Munich striker Harry Kane to win the Ballon d'Or this year.According to Eckner, Kane is ahead of Barcelona's Lamine to win the Ballon d\u2019Or due to his brilliant performances.\u201cYes. I see him [Harry Kane], pretty much ahead of Lamine Yamal [in the race for the 2026 Ballon d'Or], I think it will be a race between these two,\" Eckner said of Kane, as quoted by Casino Guru via Bavarian Football Works.\"It might be Harry Kane\u2019s time, I think he would be the safe winner.\"Recommended[EPL: Iwobi's goal earns Fulham first win of season](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/epl-iwobis-goal-earns-fulham-first-win-of-season/)[Turkey: Two top players Galatasaray want to sign in January transfer window revealed](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/turkey-two-top-players-galatasaray-want-to-sign-in-january-transfer-window-revealed/)[LaLiga: I don't want new players at Real Madrid - Mourinho](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/laliga-i-dont-want-new-players-at-real-madrid-mourinho/)Kane netted more than 70 goals for club and country last season and also won the Bundesliga title with Bayern Munich.The former Tottenham Hotspur captain also won the European Golden Boot and led England to win a bronze medal at the World Cup.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Ballon%20d'Or%202026%3A%20He's%20ahead%20-%20Constantin%20Eckner%20names%20player%20to%20win%20award%20%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Fballon-dor-2026-hes-ahead-constantin-eckner-names-player-to-win-award%2F) FacebookYou may like[NPFL: Abdallah upbeat Ranchers Bees will recover from poor start](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/npfl-abdallah-upbeat-ranchers-bees-will-recover-from-poor-start/)[2026 U-17 WWC: Poland coach eyes winning start against Flamingos](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2026-u-17-wwc-poland-coach-eyes-winning-start-against-flamingos/)[LaLiga: I would have left Real Madrid if I was Vinicius Jr \u2013 Mourinho](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/laliga-i-would-have-left-real-madrid-if-i-was-vinicius-jr-mourinho/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/ballon-dor-2026-hes-ahead-constantin-eckner-names-player-to-win-award/comments/)", "content_hash": "15f29760b623862045f5257aeb017030e3c0e301a0ab5d845fd9399f2fda9cd8", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:34Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "989", "title": "2027: No vacancy in Aso Rock, North will vote 100 per cent for Tinubu - Kano Gov, Yusuf", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/", "text": "[Politics](https://dailypost.ng/politics/)2027: No vacancy in Aso Rock, North will vote 100 per cent for Tinubu - Kano Gov, YusufPublished on October 17, 2026By [Matthew Atungwu](https://dailypost.ng/author/matthew-atungwu/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/comments/)Kano State Governor Abba Yusuf has declared that the North will vote for President Bola Tinubu 100 per cent in the 2027 general elections.Yusuf made the declaration on Saturday during the unveiling of the Arewa for Asiwaju (A4A) national headquarters in Abuja.He referred to a purported mutual agreement reached between the North and the South in 1999, which, according to him, provides for two consecutive terms each for the North and the South.According to him, the arrangement was previously honoured during the two terms of the late President Muhammadu Buhari.Recommended[2027: G-100 coalition reveals when opposition will pick single candidate to face Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/)[Troops kill two suspected kidnappers, rescue victim, recover N10m ransom in Kano](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/troops-gun-down-two-kidnappers-rescue-one-recover-n10m-ransom-in-kano/)[2027: NDC candidate raises alarm over alleged plot to remove party from ballot in Lagos](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-ndc-candidate-raises-alarm-over-alleged-plot-to-remove-party-from-ballot-in-lagos-2/)\"Arewa will support the president of the Federal Republic of Nigeria 100%. If I may recall, our leaders are here. Our elders in politics are here.\"In 1999, there was a mutual agreement between the North and the South, that the North will be allowed to go for two consecutive terms in office.\"Based on that, in the last dispensation, President Buhari of blessed memory held sway for two consecutive terms, meaning for eight years.\"We don't have any cause to now violate that promise, even though not constitutionally, but I can say for constitution of common sense. Common sense dictates that we have to respect this mutual agreement.\"I therefore, on behalf of all the Northern states, on behalf of all democrats, on behalf of all patriotic Northern states, we want to assure Tinubu of our full support. 100%. He has done exceedingly well,\" Governor Yusuf said.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=2027%3A%20No%20vacancy%20in%20Aso%20Rock%2C%20North%20will%20vote%20100%20per%20cent%20for%20Tinubu%20-%20Kano%20Gov%2C%20Yusuf%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2F2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2%2F) FacebookYou may like[2027: Tinubu an absentee President not fit for re-election - Kenneth Okonkwo](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/tinubu-cannot-meet-nigerians-directly-kenneth-okonkwo/)[2027: ADC faction disowns Atiku, rejects alliance with G-100](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/atiku-under-pressure-as-adc-faction-rejects-alliance-with-g-100/)[2027 elections: Kano police warn against fake news, boast of robust security](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-elections-kano-police-warn-against-fake-news-assure-robust-security/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/comments/)", "content_hash": "a5de854dd044cc620f82488ce9adba654b657c1fba8af8841d2c57070ef729d9", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:35Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "990", "title": "Veteran journalist Dele Cole dies at 86", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/", "text": "[News](https://dailypost.ng/hot-news/)Veteran journalist Dele Cole dies at 86Published on October 17, 2026By [Nana Ismail](https://dailypost.ng/author/aisha-ahmad/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/comments/)Veteran journalist and diplomat Ambassador Dele Cole has died at the age of 86.Cole reportedly passed away on the morning of Saturday, 17 October 2026, according to family sources.He was a former managing director of the Daily Times, one of Nigeria\u2019s leading newspaper organisations. He later served the country as ambassador to Argentina and Brazil.Born on August 4, 1940, in Abonnema, in the Kalabari Kingdom of present-day Rivers State, Cole had a long academic journey that took him to institutions in Nigeria, New Zealand and the United Kingdom.Recommended[Peter Obi questions why Nigeria did not declare World Poverty Day public holiday](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/)[Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/)[Wrong choices of politicians kept Nigeria like this - Ex-NCDC boss, Ihekweazu charges students not to lose hope](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/)He began his early education at St Bartholomew\u2019s School in Enugu, where he studied from 1946 to 1953. He later attended Baptist High School in Port Harcourt between 1954 and 1958 before proceeding to Ibadan Grammar School, where he studied from 1959 to 1960.In 1962, Cole travelled to New Zealand to further his education at the University of Otago in Dunedin, where he remained until 1965.He continued his studies at the University of Cambridge in the United Kingdom from 1966 to 1970. He subsequently became a fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge, from 1970 to 1973 and earned a doctorate in history.Cole\u2019s career in the media included his appointment as a non-executive director of the Daily Times in 1975. He later rose to become the newspaper\u2019s managing director, helping to establish his reputation in Nigeria\u2019s journalism industry.Beyond journalism, he also played a role in public service and international relations. Between 1999 and 2007, he served as Special Adviser on International Relations to former President Olusegun Obasanjo.His diplomatic career also saw him represent Nigeria as ambassador to Brazil and Argentina, strengthening his involvement in the country\u2019s foreign relations.Cole was also active in politics during the 1990s, when he was associated with the Social Democratic Party (SDP).Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Veteran%20journalist%20Dele%20Cole%20dies%20at%2086%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Fveteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86%2F) FacebookYou may like[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)['Give us pension increase before you leave office' - Ogun retired principals beg Gov Abiodun](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/give-us-pension-increase-before-you-leave-office-ogun-retired-principals-beg-abiodun/)['We will protect valid votes in Kogi LG election\u2019 \u2013 KOSIEC Chairman](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/kogi-lg-poll-kosiec-vows-ro-protect-valid-votes/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/comments/)", "content_hash": "376facb58617252ce76e708214b2f0b4d6cf8a4e8171fb186d973f9c80ffd028", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:37Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "991", "title": "Peter Obi questions why Nigeria did not declare World Poverty Day public holiday", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/", "text": "[News](https://dailypost.ng/hot-news/)Peter Obi questions why Nigeria did not declare World Poverty Day public holidayPublished on October 17, 2026By [Olanrewaju Rasheedat](https://dailypost.ng/author/olarewaju-rasheedat/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/comments/)The presidential candidate of the Nigeria Democratic Congress, NDC, Peter Obi, has questioned why Nigeria did not declare the International Day for the Eradication of Poverty a public holiday, saying the occasion should have been used for national reflection on rising poverty.Obi made the remarks in a post marking the international day, arguing that millions of Nigerians are struggling to afford food and healthcare.He cited figures in his post showing that Nigeria\u2019s poverty rate rose from 40.1 per cent in 2019 to 56.2 per cent in 2023, with the rate projected to reach 63 per cent in 2026.\u201cWhat meaning does economic growth have when citizens are getting poorer, hungrier, and more insecure?\u201d Obi asked.Recommended[Veteran journalist Dele Cole dies at 86](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/)[Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/)[Wrong choices of politicians kept Nigeria like this - Ex-NCDC boss, Ihekweazu charges students not to lose hope](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/)He called for better management of public resources and increased investment in education, healthcare, agriculture and small businesses to address the situation.Obi added that poverty should not define Nigeria\u2019s future, insisting that a country where citizens can meet their basic needs and live with dignity is possible.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Peter%20Obi%20questions%20why%20Nigeria%20did%20not%20declare%20World%20Poverty%20Day%20public%20holiday%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Fpeter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday%2F) FacebookYou may like[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)['Give us pension increase before you leave office' - Ogun retired principals beg Gov Abiodun](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/give-us-pension-increase-before-you-leave-office-ogun-retired-principals-beg-abiodun/)['We will protect valid votes in Kogi LG election\u2019 \u2013 KOSIEC Chairman](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/kogi-lg-poll-kosiec-vows-ro-protect-valid-votes/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/comments/)", "content_hash": "cd6aebe3ba06dc371e6e1588ce01fef518a132b0bc1255b3b2fa8ddc14bdc20a", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:38Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "992", "title": "Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/", "text": "[News](https://dailypost.ng/hot-news/)Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams TinubuPublished on October 17, 2026By [Francis Ugwu](https://dailypost.ng/author/francis-ugwu/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/comments/)The presidential candidate of the Nigeria Democratic Congress, NDC, Peter Obi, has criticised Nigeria\u2019s economic situation, saying economic growth is meaningless if citizens continue to become poorer.Obi made the remark in a post on X on Saturday, marking the International Day for the Eradication of Poverty. He argued that rising poverty levels showed that the country was moving backwards despite its abundant human and natural resources.He cited figures indicating that Nigeria\u2019s poverty rate rose from 40.1 per cent in 2019 to 56.2 per cent in 2023, with the rate projected to reach 63 per cent in 2026.According to him, millions of Nigerians are struggling to afford food and access healthcare despite government claims of economic growth.Recommended[Veteran journalist Dele Cole dies at 86](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/)[Peter Obi questions why Nigeria did not declare World Poverty Day public holiday](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/)[2027: G-100 coalition reveals when opposition will pick single candidate to face Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/)\u201cWhat meaning does economic growth have when citizens are getting poorer, hungrier, and more insecure?\u201d he asked.Obi said progress should be measured by improvements in citizens\u2019 living conditions rather than economic growth figures alone.He also suggested that Nigeria should have declared Saturday a public holiday to encourage national reflection on the country\u2019s growing poverty crisis.\u201cPerhaps today should have been declared a public holiday, not for celebration, but for national reflection on how a country so richly endowed with human and natural resources has allowed poverty to overcome it,\u201d he said.The former Anambra State governor maintained that poverty was not Nigeria\u2019s destiny, arguing that competent leadership and prudent management of public resources could help reverse the trend.He called for increased investment in education and healthcare, as well as support for agriculture and small businesses, as measures to address poverty in the country.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Economic%20growth%20meaningless%20if%20citizens%20are%20getting%20poorer%20%E2%80%93%20Peter%20Obi%20slams%20Tinubu%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Feconomic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu%2F) FacebookYou may like[Wrong choices of politicians kept Nigeria like this - Ex-NCDC boss, Ihekweazu charges students not to lose hope](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/)[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)['Give us pension increase before you leave office' - Ogun retired principals beg Gov Abiodun](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/give-us-pension-increase-before-you-leave-office-ogun-retired-principals-beg-abiodun/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/comments/)", "content_hash": "c0e90ada78f0d853f23613549c8c0d4dadb3790eebe08b08d8a2cc46ca416555", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:39Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "993", "title": "2027: G-100 coalition reveals when opposition will pick single candidate to face Tinubu", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/", "text": "[Politics](https://dailypost.ng/politics/)2027: G-100 coalition reveals when opposition will pick single candidate to face TinubuPublished on October 17, 2026By [Matthew Atungwu](https://dailypost.ng/author/matthew-atungwu/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/comments/)The G-100 Coalition says opposition parties seeking to unite against President Bola Tinubu in the 2027 presidential election may unveil a single candidate just days or weeks before the poll.The convener of the group, Salihu Lukman, disclosed this during an appearance on Channels Television\u2019s Hard Copy programme.Lukman, a former National Vice Chairman of the APC, said opposition parties were committed to picking a consensus candidate.\u201cI can tell you categorically, and my confidence about that comes from what I see in the leadership of the parties: they are committed and they are working, taking all the steps necessary.Recommended[2027: No vacancy in Aso Rock, North will vote 100 per cent for Tinubu - Kano Gov, Yusuf](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-no-vacancy-in-aso-rock-north-will-vote-100-for-tinubu-kano-gov-yusuf-2/)[Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/)[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)\u201cSo it could be two months, some weeks to the election, or a few days to the election; they will come out and tell Nigerians this is the direction, and everybody will queue,\u201d Lukman said.The G-100 was set up to encourage opposition parties to rally behind one presidential candidate who could challenge the ruling APC in 2027.The move followed an all-opposition summit held in Ibadan in April, where participants agreed to pursue a consensus candidate.According to Lukman, the process has expanded beyond the six political parties that initially signed on, with efforts underway to bring more parties into the alliance.He explained that political parties and their presidential candidates had taken over the process from the G-100 and were now negotiating directly to build the trust needed to reach an agreement.\u201cWhen the parties owned the process we had started and took it beyond, took it out of our hands and began to engage each other based on the need to develop the kind of confidence they need to trust one another,\u201d Lukman said.He said the lack of trust among opposition parties and their presidential candidates remained one of the biggest obstacles to forming a united front ahead of the 2027 election.Lukman said a summit held on August 31 agreed to establish five working groups to handle different aspects of the proposed alliance, including the selection of a single presidential candidate.According to him, each participating party would nominate members to the relevant working group through its presidential candidate or party leadership, and the representatives would operate under agreed terms of reference.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=2027%3A%20G-100%20coalition%20reveals%20when%20opposition%20will%20pick%20single%20candidate%20to%20face%20Tinubu%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2F2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu%2F) FacebookYou may like[2027: NDC candidate raises alarm over alleged plot to remove party from ballot in Lagos](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-ndc-candidate-raises-alarm-over-alleged-plot-to-remove-party-from-ballot-in-lagos-2/)[2027: Tinubu an absentee President not fit for re-election - Kenneth Okonkwo](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/tinubu-cannot-meet-nigerians-directly-kenneth-okonkwo/)[2027: ADC faction disowns Atiku, rejects alliance with G-100](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/atiku-under-pressure-as-adc-faction-rejects-alliance-with-g-100/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-g-100-coalition-reveals-when-opposition-will-pick-single-candidate-to-face-tinubu/comments/)", "content_hash": "54f860c8fb6c351aed748f9557488e53d30a0fb4370245b4d43da0fe0c472224", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:41Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "994", "title": "'Opposition parties may name joint candidate days before 2027 election\u2019 - Lukman", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/opposition-may-pick-consensus-candidate-days-before-2027-election-lukman/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/opposition-may-pick-consensus-candidate-days-before-2027-election-lukman/", "text": "[Politics](https://dailypost.ng/politics/)'Opposition parties may name joint candidate days before 2027 election\u2019 - LukmanPublished on October 17, 2026By [Nana Ismail](https://dailypost.ng/author/aisha-ahmad/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/opposition-may-pick-consensus-candidate-days-before-2027-election-lukman/comments/)Former Director-General of the Progressives Governors Forum, Salihu Lukman, says opposition parties could wait until a few days before the 2027 general election before announcing a joint presidential candidate to challenge the ruling All Progressives Congress (APC).Lukman, who convenes the political pressure group G-100, said opposition parties were committed to reaching an agreement and were working to prevent their votes from being divided among several candidates.Speaking on Channels Television\u2019s Hard Copy programme on Saturday, he expressed confidence that the parties would eventually agree on one candidate despite disagreements and a lack of trust among their leaders.\u201cI can tell you categorically, and my confidence about that comes from what I see in the leadership of the parties: they are committed and they are working, taking all the steps necessary,\u201d he said.Recommended[2027: Atiku, Peter Obi, Adebayo, others to state position on opposition coalition against Tinubu \u2013 Lukman](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/2027-atiku-peter-obi-adebayo-others-to-make-public-statements-on-opposition-coalition-against-unseat-tinubu-lukman/)[2027 Presidency: Lukman reveals details of coalition talks with Atiku, Peter Obi to unseat Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/14/2027-presidency-lukman-reveals-details-of-coalition-talks-with-atiku-peter-obi-to-unseat-tinubu/)[2027: Why opposition parties should unite behind single presidential candidate \u2013 Lukman](https://dailypost.ng/2026/08/30/2027-why-opposition-parties-should-unite-behind-single-presidential-candidate-lukman/)He added that the announcement could come months, weeks or even days before the election.\u201cSo it could be two months, some weeks to the election, or a few days to the election, they will come out and tell Nigerians this is the direction, and everybody will queue,\u201d Lukman said.He explained that discussions had moved beyond the six political parties initially involved, with efforts being made to bring more parties into the proposed alliance.G-100 emerged from efforts to unite opposition parties following an opposition summit in Ibadan in April 2026. Participants at the meeting agreed to work towards presenting a common presidential candidate against the APC.However, Lukman said political parties and their presidential candidates had since taken over the process, allowing them to negotiate directly and build the trust required to sustain an alliance.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text='Opposition%20parties%20may%20name%20joint%20candidate%20days%20before%202027%20election%E2%80%99%20-%20Lukman%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Fopposition-may-pick-consensus-candidate-days-before-2027-election-lukman%2F) FacebookYou may like[2027: Osun election shows how opposition parties can remove Tinubu \u2014 Lukman](https://dailypost.ng/2026/08/17/2027-osun-election-shows-how-opposition-parties-can-remove-tinubu-lukman/)[2027: I want to see Tinubu, APC defeated -Lukman](https://dailypost.ng/2026/07/31/2027-i-want-to-see-tinubu-apc-defeated-lukman/)[2027: Dumping ADC with Obi will diminish you \u2013 Lukman warns Kwankwaso](https://dailypost.ng/2026/05/03/2027-dumping-adc-with-obi-will-diminish-you-lukman-warns-kwankwaso/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/opposition-may-pick-consensus-candidate-days-before-2027-election-lukman/comments/)", "content_hash": "8dd4420ae6878c8b7b5b38bbf1b123e253f4dca3238e3b51981245242a012c97", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:42Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "995", "title": "Wrong choices of politicians kept Nigeria like this - Ex-NCDC boss, Ihekweazu charges students not to lose hope", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/", "text": "[News](https://dailypost.ng/hot-news/)Wrong choices of politicians kept Nigeria like this - Ex-NCDC boss, Ihekweazu charges students not to lose hopePublished on October 17, 2026By [John Owen Nwachukwu](https://dailypost.ng/author/john-nwachukwu/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/comments/)A former Director-General of the Nigeria Centre for Disease Control, NCDC, and current Assistant Director-General of the World Health Organisation, WHO, Chikwe Ihekweazu, has urged Nigerians, particularly students, not to lose hope in the country despite its challenges.Ihekweazu made the call in Abuja on Saturday during the matriculation ceremony of Miva Open University, stating that Nigeria\u2019s current situation reflected the choices made by political leaders and their priorities, urging students to acquire education not only for personal success but also to contribute to national development.\u201cWhatever you do, please do not lose hope in our country. The future we want will not be built by resources alone. It will be built by educated, engaged, and courageous citizens,\u201d he said.The WHO official expressed concern over the millions of children out of school and the poor conditions of many schools and universities, noting that such challenges were not inevitable but resulted from decisions about how the country\u2019s resources were allocated.Recommended[Veteran journalist Dele Cole dies at 86](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/veteran-journalist-dele-cole-dies-at-86/)[Peter Obi questions why Nigeria did not declare World Poverty Day public holiday](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/peter-obi-questions-why-nigeria-did-not-declare-world-poverty-day-public-holiday/)[Economic growth meaningless if citizens are getting poorer \u2013 Peter Obi slams Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/economic-growth-meaningless-if-citizens-are-getting-poorer-peter-obi-slams-tinubu/)According to him, political leaders must prioritise education and public welfare over personal comfort and excessive displays of power.\u201cWhether to build roads or to invest in education, whether to travel in convoys of 50 cars or to fix our universities, these are not benign choices,\u201d he said.Ihekweazu argued that education empowers citizens to question leadership decisions, demand accountability and develop alternatives capable of moving Nigeria in a different direction.He urged the students not to see education merely as a pathway to personal comfort, but as an opportunity to develop the judgment, confidence and social consciousness needed to transform society.\u201cPlease do not consume education only as a route to personal success. Use it to develop the judgment, the confidence, and the social consciousness to serve others and transform our society,\u201d he said.He added that Nigeria\u2019s future would not be handed to its citizens but would have to be built through deliberate efforts and collective responsibility.Drawing lessons from history, Ihekweazu recommended the book Why Nations Fail by Daron Acemoglu and James Robinson, explaining that societies often stagnate when political and economic power is concentrated in the hands of a few privileged individuals.He noted that European societies had also experienced periods of aristocratic dominance, monopolies and restricted opportunities before gradually moving towards more inclusive systems.According to him, wider access to education helped people acquire the knowledge and confidence to challenge existing structures and demand more inclusive societies.\u201cYour education must therefore be more than a passport to personal comfort. It should help you to understand the institutions around you, question what does not work, challenge injustice, create alternatives and widen the opportunity for others,\u201d he said.He reiterated that education should ultimately empower individuals to improve not only their own lives but also the lives of others.\u201cThe purpose of education is not simply to change what you know; it is to expand what you\u2019re capable of doing with your life and in the lives of others,\u201d he said.He urged the students to recognise their responsibility in shaping Nigeria\u2019s future, insisting that the country belonged not only to political officeholders but also to younger generations whose decisions would influence its development.\u201cNigeria does not belong only to presidents, governors, ministers, or those who hold power today. It also belongs to you,\u201d he said.He added that the future would be shaped by what the students learnt, what they built, what they refused to accept, whom they chose to serve and the opportunities they created for others.The Chancellor of the institution, Sim Shagaya, added, \"Our nation lost a lot of talents because in our analogue are made students to abandon their lives, family and work in order to study,\" adding that studying from home have changed the narrative.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Wrong%20choices%20of%20politicians%20kept%20Nigeria%20like%20this%20-%20Ex-NCDC%20boss%2C%20Ihekweazu%20charges%20students%20not%20to%20lose%20hope%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F17%2Fwrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope%2F) FacebookYou may like[Tinubu explains FG\u2019s removal of universities from IPPIS](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/why-we-freed-varsities-from-ippis-recruitment-waivers/)['Give us pension increase before you leave office' - Ogun retired principals beg Gov Abiodun](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/give-us-pension-increase-before-you-leave-office-ogun-retired-principals-beg-abiodun/)['We will protect valid votes in Kogi LG election\u2019 \u2013 KOSIEC Chairman](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/kogi-lg-poll-kosiec-vows-ro-protect-valid-votes/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/17/wrong-choices-of-politicians-kept-nigeria-like-this-ex-ncdc-boss-ihekweazu-charges-students-not-lose-hope/comments/)", "content_hash": "bda26ca2582496a01d0b740f655d6dc505bfee5ab1be7e6d023e41ec8e2b7b15", "domain": "dailypost", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:43Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "996", "title": "Building Genuine connections with people on the internet using the CallMeChat App", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/16/building-genuine-connections-with-people-on-the-internet-using-the-callmechat-app/", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/16/building-genuine-connections-with-people-on-the-internet-using-the-callmechat-app/", "text": "[Sponsored](https://dailypost.ng/sponsored/)Building Genuine connections with people on the internet using the CallMeChat AppPublished on October 16, 2026By [Daily Post Staff](https://dailypost.ng/author/daily-post-staff/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/16/building-genuine-connections-with-people-on-the-internet-using-the-callmechat-app/comments/)Building Genuine Connections with People on the Internet Using the CallMeChat App You can have 1,000 contacts and still have nobody to call on a rough Tuesday. It happens more than we admit. The internet made reaching people easy, but it never made knowing them easy. This guide explains how to network and build genuine connections online, and where to find friends online without feeling fake or pushy. Likes Are Not Friendships Scrolling feels social, but mostly it isn\u2019t. You see a hundred faces, tap a heart, move on. Nothing really happens to you, and nothing happens to them. A real bond needs back-and-forth. A reply you didn\u2019t expect. A joke that lands. A pause that nobody rushes to fill. That\u2019s the raw material of friendship, and a feed rarely gives you much of it. Where Online Friendships Actually Begin According to Pew Research Center, 57% of teens had made at least one new friend online as early as 2015. Adults follow the same path, only more quietly. A shared hobby, a game, a niche forum, a local group.Recommended[One university. Many possibilities. Your future starts at GOUNI!](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/16/one-university-many-possibilities-your-future-starts-at-gouni/)[One Home, One Connection: How LG ThinQ Is Making Life Smarter for Nigerians](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/16/one-home-one-connection-how-lg-thinq-is-making-life-smarter-for-nigerians/)[Global expectations soar as Healing Streams returns for final 2026 edition](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/15/global-expectations-soar-as-healing-streams-returns-for-final-2026-edition/) The pattern is always similar. Something small in common opens the door, and curiosity does the rest. If you want to find friends online, start with what you already love doing, then look for people who love it too. They\u2019re out there, and many of them are hoping someone will say hello first. How to Network and Build Genuine Connections Without the Cringe Networking has a bad reputation because many people treat it like a trade. Collect a contact, ask for a favor, disappear. Everyone can smell that from a mile away. The better way is plain curiosity. Ask what someone is working on, what made their week, what they\u2019d do with a free Saturday. Then follow up. A short message two days later, an article you thought they\u2019d like, a quick \u201chow did the interview go?\u201d That tiny act of remembering is what turns a contact into a person who\u2019s glad to hear from you. Where to Find Friends Online Different places give you different things, so it helps to know what each one offers: Interest communities, such as forums, Discord servers, and subreddits built around one hobby Learning groups, like language exchanges, book clubs, and coding circles Local meetup pages that move between screens and real streets Live video chat, which suits the evenings when you want a real conversation tonight, not next week Mix two or three. Variety protects you from the dry spells every single place has. Why Video Builds Trust Faster Than Text Text hides a lot. Tone gets lost, jokes fall flat, silence feels cold. A smile, a laugh, even a half-second hesitation tells you more in five minutes than fifty messages ever could. That\u2019s why moving from typing to talking is the biggest single step in learning how to network and build genuine connections online. This is a good time to mention CallMeChat. It\u2019s an online video chat where you can talk to new people in real time. The main reason to try https://callmechat.com/ is the low barrier to entry, as everything is anonymous and there\u2019s no unnecessary stress from worrying about your reputation and trying to build it. No polished captions, no perfect photos. You just show up and see who\u2019s there. Keep Safety Simple Genuine doesn\u2019t mean careless. Don\u2019t share your home address, workplace details, or anything about money with someone you\u2019ve just met. Trust is earned slowly, and that\u2019s perfectly fine. If a conversation feels off, end it. You don\u2019t owe anyone your time. Turning One Good Chat into a Lasting Connection When a conversation goes well, say so. \u201cI enjoyed this. Want to talk again next week?\u201d It\u2019s direct, a little brave, and almost always welcome. Then build a rhythm: a weekly call, a playlist swap, a movie you both watch and argue about afterward. Habits That Help You Find Friends Online Pick two or three places and stay there. Being a familiar face in a small community beats being a stranger in twenty. People remember the person who replies, thanks others, and asks follow-up questions. Offer something, too. Answer a question, share a useful link, cheer for someone\u2019s small win. Generosity is the oldest networking trick there is, and it still works on a screen. Try a mix: one group built around a hobby, one around your profession, and one video-based space where live talk happens. Common Mistakes to Avoid A few traps catch almost everyone at the start: Talking only about yourself Expecting instant best-friend status Quitting after one awkward chat Spreading yourself across ten platforms at once Awkward is normal. Nearly everyone feels it in the first minute, and it fades faster than you would think. A Quick Reality Check About Expectations Not every chat will go well. Some will be short, some awkward, a few plain boring. That\u2019s normal, and it isn\u2019t a verdict on you. You only need a handful of good ones to change your week. The same Surgeon General advisory notes that lacking social connection can raise the risk of early death about as much as smoking up to 15 cigarettes a day. A few awkward minutes seem like a fair price. Final Thoughts Loneliness is common, but it isn\u2019t permanent. Be curious, move to video when you can, follow up, and give relationships the hours they need.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Building%20Genuine%20connections%20with%20people%20on%20the%20internet%20using%20the%20CallMeChat%20App%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F16%2Fbuilding-genuine-connections-with-people-on-the-internet-using-the-callmechat-app%2F) Facebook You may like[Stockity: Digital Trading Platforms Gain Ground as More Users Explore Financial Markets Online](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/15/stockity-digital-trading-platforms-gain-ground-as-more-users-explore-financial-markets-online/)[OPay named Digital Bank and Financial Inclusion Leader at BAFI 2026](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/15/opay-named-digital-bank-and-financial-inclusion-leader-at-bafi-2026/)[Renewed Hope Cities: Tinubu\u2019s Housing Initiative draws over 100,000 Nigerians as projects expand nationwide](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/15/renewed-hope-cities-tinubus-housing-initiative-draws-over-100000-nigeri