y.ng/todays-paper/) [BD Fx Live exchange-rate tracking and currency analysis for the Nigerian market.](https://currency.businessday.ng/) [Podcasts & Shows BusinessDay Television and our audio shows, for when you'd rather listen than read.](https://businessday.ng/podcast/) [BD Conferences Priority access and subscriber pricing on our conferences and industry events.](https://conferences.businessday.ng/) \u201cNigeria's business leaders make decisions every day with real money and real consequences behind them. Our job is to make sure they're making those decisions with the facts, not the noise.\u201d \u2014 The BusinessDay Editors Choose your plan Need 20+ seats? [Talk to our corporate sales team \u2192](https://businessday.ng/corporate-subscription/) Questions Can I cancel anytime? Yes. Cancel any time from My Account \u2192 Subscribe \u2014 no phone call, no email required. You'll keep access until the end of your current billing period. Is my payment information secure? Yes. Payments are processed by our payment provider; BusinessDay never stores your card details directly. Can I read on my phone? Yes. Your subscription works on any browser, on any phone, tablet or computer \u2014 there's no separate app to install. What's the difference between Individual and Corporate plans? Individual plans are for a single reader. Corporate plans cover a team under one organization account, managed from the Team tab in My Account. Do free newsletters require a subscription? No \u2014 our free newsletters are open to everyone. A subscription adds unlimited article access, BD Pro analysis, the daily e-paper and ad-free reading on top.", "content_hash": "2d111786200fe9bbd7806d7311f6ea413b79fe75c82453cee8d1ff51bd4ddc55", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:23Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1026", "title": "Personal Income Tax Calculator", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/", "text": "Share Advertisement In this article [Tax Guide (\u20a6) \u2013 Effective Jan 2026](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#tax-guide-%e2%82%a6-effective-jan-2026) [2026 Nigeria Personal Income Tax Guide (Disclaimer & Assumptions)](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#2026-nigeria-personal-income-tax-guide-disclaimer-assumptions) [Disclaimer](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#disclaimer) [How the Calculator Works](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#how-the-calculator-works) [Tax Bands Used in This Calculator (Effective January 2026)](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#tax-bands-used-in-this-calculator-effective-january-2026) [Rent Relief (New Provision)](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#rent-relief-new-provision) [Example](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#example) [Final Note](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/tax-calculator/#final-note) Tax Guide (\u20a6) \u2013 Effective Jan 2026 Annual Income (\u20a6): Annual Rent Paid (\u20a6): (Used to compute rent relief: lower of 20% of rent or \u20a6 500,000) Estimate Tax 2026 Nigeria Personal Income Tax Guide (Disclaimer & Assumptions) Disclaimer This calculator is provided for informational and educational purposes only.It gives a rough estimate of potential tax obligations under Nigeria\u2019s Personal Income Tax (Amendment) Act, 2025, expected to take effect from January 1, 2026. Actual tax liabilities may differ depending on: Individual circumstances Employer payroll computations Approved allowances, reliefs, or deductions Implementation details from the Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) or state tax authorities Always consult a qualified tax adviser or the FIRS for official guidance before making financial decisions. How the Calculator Works This tool estimates your annual personal income tax based on your gross annual income and annual rent paid (for rent relief).It applies the progressive tax bands announced under the 2025 reform. Here\u2019s how it works step-by-step: Input your total annual income (\u20a6). Optionally input your annual rent (\u20a6) \u2014 used to calculate rent relief. The calculator: Deducts your rent relief (the lower of 20% of rent paid or \u20a6 500,000). Applies the 2026 tax bands to the remaining taxable income. Displays your estimated tax and net income after tax. Tax Bands Used in This Calculator (Effective January 2026) Taxable Income Range (\u20a6)Tax RateNotesUp to \u20a6 800,0000%Tax-free thresholdNext \u20a6 2.2 million15%Middle-income bandNext \u20a6 9 million18%Upper-middle incomeNext \u20a6 13 million21%Higher income bracketNext \u20a6 25 million23%Wealth bracketAbove \u20a6 50 million25%Top income earners Rent Relief (New Provision) Taxpayers can deduct a rent relief from their taxable income, calculated as the lower of: 20% of annual rent paid, or \u20a6 500,000 Example If your annual income is \u20a6 5,000,000 and you pay \u20a6 1,200,000 yearly in rent: Rent relief = \u20a6 240,000 (20% of \u20a6 1.2 m) Taxable income = \u20a6 4,760,000 Tax is then calculated progressively using the bands above. Your net income is displayed after deducting estimated tax. Final Note This calculator is designed to help individuals and businesses get a quick sense of how the 2026 tax structure may affect take-home pay or salary planning. Related News [Businesses risk overpaying tax as new rules expose outdated practices](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/businesses-risk-overpaying-tax-as-new-rules-expose-outdated-practices/) [CBN survey shows multiple taxation top business constraint despite reforms](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cbn-survey-shows-multiple-taxation-top-business-constraint-despite-reforms/) [NRS seals Kaduna Electric over unpaid taxes amid N456.5bn debt](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nrs-seals-kaduna-electric-over-unpaid-taxes-amid-n456-5bn-debt/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [BusinessDay](https://businessday.ng/author/bdtech/) Nigeria's leading finance and market intelligence news report. Also home to expert opinion and commentary on politics, sports, lifestyle, and more Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "72f504fa4d9b0d9a669a227d2d70e8e7f25803e13d1ab3979b316e0fb1a5c671", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:26Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1027", "title": "Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/", "text": "Share Advertisement \u2026says exchange-rate stability boosts prospects for long-term naira financing Bangkok|| The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) is seeking deeper financial cooperation with Chinese banks and institutions as improving exchange-rate stability creates opportunities to attract more long-term investment into the domestic economy. Olayemi Cardoso, CBN governor, said the apex bank was looking to strengthen collaboration with Chinese financial institutions, particularly in payment systems, cross-border transactions and knowledge-sharing, while exploring opportunities to attract greater investment from Asian markets into Nigerian financial instruments. Speaking Saturday at the Nigerian delegation\u2019s closing press briefing at the 2026 International Monetary Fund and World Bank Group Annual Meetings in Bangkok, Thailand, Cardoso said the planned engagements in China would move beyond high-level discussions to identify practical areas of cooperation. The planned China visit follows Cardoso\u2019s recent engagements in Singapore, which formed part of Nigeria\u2019s broader effort to deepen financial and economic ties with Asian markets, and reflects the CBN\u2019s push to strengthen institutional cooperation, explore opportunities for cross-border financial collaboration and attract more Asian investment into Nigerian markets. \u201cWe are basically interested in seeing how we can deepen collaboration between institutions, Chinese banks, Nigeria Central Bank, and others, and the payment system in particular,\u201d he said. China\u2019s payment infrastructure offers potential lessons for Nigeria, Cardoso said, adding that the CBN was interested in peer reviews and exchanges between the two countries\u2019 institutions. He disclosed that the initial discussions had taken place at leadership level during the Annual meetings, with more detailed engagements expected during the planned visit to China. \u201cSo the first engagement we had here was an engagement at the top. And then once we go to China, people will be rolling up their sleeves and coming down to brass tacks as to what makes sense and what doesn\u2019t,\u201d Cardoso said. The CBN governor also pointed to the potential for Chinese and other Asian investors to increase their exposure to Nigerian financial instruments, noting that investment from the region had remained limited despite the substantial liquidity available in its markets. He said Nigeria\u2019s improved macroeconomic stability could help change that pattern by reducing the currency risks that previously discouraged long-term investment. \u201cThe chances of getting long-term Naira capital have increased astronomically as a result of the stability that we are finding in markets,\u201d Cardoso said. He cited the narrowing gap between Nigeria\u2019s official and parallel foreign-exchange rates as evidence of the improvement, saying the premium, which had previously reached about 60 percent, had declined to around 3 to 4 percent. The reduction in exchange-rate distortions, he said, had eased a major obstacle to financial institutions seeking to develop long-term naira-denominated financing products. Cardoso also highlighted the banking sector\u2019s recapitalisation exercise as part of efforts to strengthen Nigeria\u2019s financial system and improve its capacity to support economic growth in an exercise which raised N4.65 trillion, with just over 70 percent of the funds coming from domestic investors. Cardoso said the CBN\u2019s responsibility was to maintain monetary and price stability, while development finance institutions should channel capital into productive sectors of the economy. He identified the Africa Finance Corporation (AFC), which he now chairs, the Bank of Industry (BOI) and the Development Bank of Nigeria (DBN) as institutions expected to play a greater role in mobilising financing as market conditions improve. The governor said the CBN would work more closely with the AFC to strengthen its capacity to mobilise resources in Nigeria and across Africa. Cardoso also stressed the importance of policy consistency and transparency in sustaining investor confidence, saying international investors closely monitor economic developments and policymakers\u2019 decisions. His comments come as Nigeria seeks to convert recent improvements in macroeconomic indicators into stronger investment and economic growth. Headline inflation fell to 14.63 per cent in September 2026, while external reserves have risen above $55 billion, according to figures Cardoso cited in earlier remarks. The CBN governor said consolidating those gains would require sustained policy discipline, stronger institutions and closer cooperation with international partners. He expressed optimism that stronger financial ties with China and other Asian economies could open up new channels for investment, trade financing and payment-system cooperation. Related News [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) [17 firms pay shareholders N1.13trn in dividends in H1](https://businessday.ng/market-intelligence/article/17-firms-pay-shareholders-n1-13trn-in-dividends-in-h1/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Hope Moses-Ashike](https://businessday.ng/author/hope-ashike/) Hope Moses-Ashike is an Associate Editor, Banking and Finance, with more than a decade of experience reporting on Nigeria\u2019s financial\u2026 Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) [Food, energy costs threaten Nigeria\u2019s disinflation gains, CPPE warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/food-energy-costs-threaten-nigerias-disinflation-gains-cppe-warns/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "e35ea949a9c7f01fdb30a9e0a6e24311735cb4c55ae78ef876195501b88da185", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:27Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1028", "title": "Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/", "text": "Share Advertisement Bangkok|| Nigeria risks renewed pressure on the naira and capital flight if the government reverses its economic reforms to restore fuel subsidies, Taiwo Oyedele, finance minister and coordinating minister of the economy, warned, defending fuel price discounts offered by the Nigerian National Petroleum Company (NNPC) as a commercial strategy rather than a government-funded subsidy. Oyedele, speaking at a press briefing to conclude the IMF-World Bank Annual Meetings in Bangkok, Thailand, said reversing the reforms could undermine investor confidence, trigger capital outflows and reverse gains made in stabilising the foreign exchange market. He warned that even the suggestion that the government was considering a return to fuel subsidies could unsettle investors, prompt Nigerians to move their savings into foreign currencies and put additional pressure on the naira. \u201cEven Nigerians, the elites, the middle class, the high net worth, will move away from Naira. They will start buying dollars,\u201d he said. Oyedele added that capital outflows, combined with rising domestic demand for foreign currencies, could create a damaging cycle of instability in the foreign exchange market. \u201cThey start moving against Naira. So capital is exiting. Nigerians are moving against the Naira. This is the perfect combination for the storm, for the devastating impact,\u201d he said. The minister also rejected suggestions that fuel price discounts offered through NNPC Retail amounted to a return to subsidies, arguing that the arrangement reflected normal commercial practices in which businesses reduce their profit margins to attract customers and increase sales. \u201cI do not know which part of the world you live in. It\u2019s called retail marketing,\u201d he said, explaining that businesses routinely offer discounts to boost sales and build customer loyalty. \u201cWhen they say buy one, get one free, that\u2019s commercial. If they say take 10 percent off, that\u2019s commercial,\u201d he added. According to Oyedele, NNPC retail could increase sales volumes by offering more competitive prices, potentially recovering some of the reduced margins through higher sales over time. His comments come as the Federal Government seeks to defend its economic reforms amid persistent pressure over the cost of living and questions about whether the policies will be sustained ahead of the 2027 general elections. The removal of petrol subsidies in 2023 and the liberalisation of the foreign exchange market have formed key pillars of the government\u2019s reform programme, which officials say is intended to improve fiscal sustainability, strengthen market confidence and establish a more stable macroeconomic environment. However, the reforms have also increased pressure on households and businesses through higher fuel and transportation costs, fuelling calls for additional government intervention to cushion the impact. Oyedele said concerns about a possible reversal of the reforms had featured repeatedly in his engagements with investors during the annual meetings, with international investors seeking assurances that Nigeria would maintain its policy direction. He argued that restoring fuel subsidies could jeopardise the progress made in stabilising the economy, particularly if investors interpreted such a move as a retreat from the government\u2019s commitment to fiscal discipline and market-based pricing. A renewed loss of confidence, he warned, could encourage investors to withdraw capital while prompting domestic savers to seek protection against possible naira depreciation by buying dollars, increasing pressure on the foreign exchange market. Conversely, he said sustained reforms could help lower inflation, reduce borrowing costs and support stronger economic growth by creating a more predictable environment for investment and business expansion. The government is targeting economic growth of 7 per cent, while Oyedele has argued that bringing inflation down to low single digits would help create the conditions for single-digit interest rates. Lower inflation and borrowing costs, he said, would improve access to credit for businesses, encourage investment and support faster expansion across productive sectors of the economy. For the administration, the challenge is to maintain investor confidence and preserve the gains from its reforms while convincing Nigerians that the longer-term benefits of economic stability and growth will outweigh the immediate costs of adjustment. Related News [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) [17 firms pay shareholders N1.13trn in dividends in H1](https://businessday.ng/market-intelligence/article/17-firms-pay-shareholders-n1-13trn-in-dividends-in-h1/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Hope Moses-Ashike](https://businessday.ng/author/hope-ashike/) Hope Moses-Ashike is an Associate Editor, Banking and Finance, with more than a decade of experience reporting on Nigeria\u2019s financial\u2026 Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) [Food, energy costs threaten Nigeria\u2019s disinflation gains, CPPE warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/food-energy-costs-threaten-nigerias-disinflation-gains-cppe-warns/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "c6fae723f980ac15e6f733e8a355770164363fcdb0aa07366093dd4f008ae226", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:28Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1029", "title": "No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/", "text": "Share Advertisement \u2026FG targets 7% growth, sees faster expansion than IMF forecast Nigeria has ruled out a return to fuel subsidies and multiple exchange rates, with Finance Minister Taiwo Oyedele insisting that the government will not reverse the policies despite rising petrol prices and mounting pressure on households and businesses. Oyedele gave the assurance at the Nigerian delegation\u2019s closing press briefing to mark the end of the 2026 Annual Meetings of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank Group in Bangkok, Thailand, on Saturday. \u201cLet me be clear: there will be no return to fuel subsidies or multiple exchange rates,\u201d the minister said. He was responding to concerns over the rising cost of living and renewed debate about the possibility of restoring fuel subsidies ahead of Nigeria\u2019s 2027 general elections. The minister also clarified that the recent petrol discount at Nigerian National Petroleum Company Limited (NNPC) retail outlets does not amount to a return to government-funded subsidies. According to him, the discount is a time-bound commercial decision by NNPC to reduce its retail profit margin and provide temporary relief to Nigerians, rather than a subsidy financed with public funds. \u201cThe recent retail margin discount on fuel is a time-bound commercial decision by the NNPC to reduce margins and support Nigerians; it is not a subsidy paid from public funds,\u201d he said. The clarification comes amid persistent concerns over the impact of higher petrol prices on transportation, food prices, business operating costs and household purchasing power following the removal of fuel subsidies in 2023. The government has maintained that restoring broad-based subsidies would place additional pressure on public finances and risk undermining the gains from its economic reforms. Earlier in October, Oyedele warned that returning petrol prices to their pre-reform levels could cost Nigeria more than N20 trillion annually, arguing that the financial burden would compete with spending on essential public services and development priorities. He also acknowledged during the annual meetings that the sequencing of fuel subsidy removal and foreign exchange market liberalisation had been fast, recognising the hardship associated with the reforms. At the closing briefing in Bangkok, however, the minister reiterated that the government would not return to the previous subsidy regime or multiple exchange rates. The position leaves the government facing the challenge of sustaining market-based pricing while addressing the burden of high energy costs on households and productive businesses. The IMF has also advised Nigeria against restoring blanket petrol subsidies, recommending targeted assistance to vulnerable households instead. The Fund has argued that broad-based fuel subsidies are fiscally expensive and often disproportionately benefit higher-income consumers. For the Nigerian government, the challenge now is to ensure that economic reforms deliver more tangible benefits to citizens without reversing policies it considers necessary for long-term fiscal stability. FG targets 7% growth as Oyedele challenges IMF projections Beyond ruling out a return to subsidies, Oyedele said the government was targeting economic growth of 7 percent, arguing that Nigeria could expand faster than the IMF\u2019s projections as fiscal reforms complement monetary policy measures. The minister said the Fund\u2019s growth projections had historically underestimated Nigeria\u2019s eventual performance, adding that the government was developing plans to accelerate expansion across industries from 2027. \u201cIf you look back, even while we were in the middle of reforms, every year the projections of the IMF have always turned out to be too conservative. So this year will not be different,\u201d he said. \u201cFrom next year, what we are trying to do is to accelerate growth. We will see that it is cutting across all our industries. And we are not saying it because it is nice to say it. We are saying it because we have a plan.\u201d Oyedele said the government would complement the work of the monetary authorities with fiscal measures designed to tackle persistent inflationary pressures and improve conditions for businesses. \u201cWe expect growth to be much higher than what the IMF is projecting for Nigeria. Our target is to get to 7 percent growth rate,\u201d he said. He identified food, energy and logistics costs as key sources of inflationary pressure, saying the government was working to address the constraints to bring inflation down to low single digits. According to him, lower inflation would create the conditions for interest rates to decline to single-digit levels, potentially reducing borrowing costs and encouraging investment. \u201cIf we are able to tackle inflation to low single digits, not just single digits, low single digits, it will provide the conditions for a single-digit interest rate environment,\u201d Oyedele said. He illustrated the potential benefits of lower borrowing costs by noting that businesses currently face high lending rates that constrain their capacity to expand. \u201cImagine that prime lending is close to 30 percent. Your top businesses are growing at 30 percent, and you are still growing at 4.5 percent. Just imagine what is possible when the borrower is in single digits,\u201d he said. The minister added that exchange-rate movements were no longer exerting the same inflationary pressure as before, pointing to the naira\u2019s improved performance as one of the developments supporting the government\u2019s economic outlook. Cardoso backs policy consistency to sustain investor confidence Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) Governor Olayemi Cardoso, speaking at the same briefing, said sustaining investor confidence and attracting long-term capital would depend on the government\u2019s willingness to maintain its economic reform course. Cardoso said Nigeria had made considerable progress in restoring macroeconomic stability and should avoid reversing the difficult measures already implemented. \u201cI honestly think that the answer to that is a lot easier than it may sound. And I think the answer, quite frankly, is just stay the course,\u201d he said. \u201cWe have come a very long way. We have shown that it can be done and that stability has finally returned to our economy. And, of course, we have to build on that.\u201d The CBN governor said the government should not undermine the reforms that had helped restore confidence in the economy. \u201cThe last thing we are going to do is play around with all the difficult reforms that we have done. So we will stay the course,\u201d he said. Cardoso also linked improved exchange-rate stability to better prospects for long-term naira financing, noting that the gap between the official and parallel market exchange rates had narrowed substantially. \u201cAt one point in time, the premium between the official rate and the black market rate was about 60 percent. And today, it\u2019s down to about 3 or 4 percent,\u201d he said. He said the reduction in exchange-rate uncertainty had strengthened the prospects for attracting long-term domestic capital and made it easier for development finance institutions to channel resources into productive investment. \u201cThe chances of getting long-term naira capital have increased astronomically as a result of the stability that we are finding in markets,\u201d Cardoso said. He explained that the CBN\u2019s responsibility was to maintain macroeconomic and price stability, while development finance institutions should take a greater role in mobilising resources for development. \u201cThe Central Bank\u2019s responsibility is to ensure stability and to ensure macroeconomic price stability. And in the long term, not just in the short term, but into the long term, bring down inflation and ensure price stability,\u201d he said. Cardoso\u2019s comments reinforce the government\u2019s position that maintaining the reform framework is essential to sustaining investor confidence, while Oyedele\u2019s growth target highlights the administration\u2019s expectation that fiscal and monetary measures will eventually translate into faster economic expansion. Related News [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [17 firms pay shareholders N1.13trn in dividends in H1](https://businessday.ng/market-intelligence/article/17-firms-pay-shareholders-n1-13trn-in-dividends-in-h1/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Onyinye Nwachukwu](https://businessday.ng/author/onyinye-nwachukwu/) Onyinye Nwachukwu is the Abuja Bureau Chief of BusinessDay, overseeing coverage across Abuja and Northern Nigeria. With more than two\u2026 Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [Food, energy costs threaten Nigeria\u2019s disinflation gains, CPPE warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/food-energy-costs-threaten-nigerias-disinflation-gains-cppe-warns/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "d996d9ec88580bda55cbdf2497ec943614bdf9e46feb692e78f2be278c484183", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:29Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1030", "title": "Five years after N900m e-ticketing rollout, racketeering claims persist", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/five-years-after-n900m-e-ticketing-rollout-racketeering-claims-persist/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/five-years-after-n900m-e-ticketing-rollout-racketeering-claims-persist/", "text": "Share Advertisement Passengers cite difficulties booking at official fares as NRC blames scammers, limited train capacity and weaknesses in station access control. Five years after the Federal Government introduced a N900 million electronic ticketing system to curb corruption and improve accountability in railway operations, fresh allegations of ticket racketeering on the Abuja-Kaduna Train Service are renewing questions about whether the platform has made it easier for passengers to obtain tickets at official prices. The controversy followed claims by Faizah Raji, an Instagram blogger who also shared her experience on X, that she could not secure a ticket through the official booking portal but was offered one by an agent for N7,000, against the official adult fare of N3,600 she cited. The alleged N3,400 difference highlights the potential cost to passengers of relying on unofficial intermediaries, although the account does not independently establish how the ticket was obtained or whether railway personnel were involved. The Nigerian Railway Corporation has denied involvement in ticket racketeering, attributing the problem partly to scammers exploiting excess demand and weaknesses in station access control. It also says private concessionaires, rather than the corporation directly, operate the electronic ticketing platforms. The latest claims come two years after the NRC announced an investigation into allegations of ticket racketeering involving officials on the Abuja-Kaduna route. The findings of that probe, including whether disciplinary or enforcement action followed, remain unestablished from the information available for this report. However, the renewed allegations have brought attention back to whether the electronic ticketing system, introduced in January 2021 to reduce revenue leakages and opportunities for corrupt practices, has sufficiently addressed the problems it was designed to solve. In her account, Raji said the official portal typically opens for bookings around 6 a.m., but prospective passengers increasingly encounter messages indicating that tickets are sold out or unavailable shortly after the platform opens. She said she attempted to book a ticket for her intended journey at 6 a.m. but was unsuccessful, prompting her to contact an agent whose number she had obtained. According to her, the agent requested N7,000 for a ticket against the regular adult fare of N3,600. Raji argued that the price difference created an opportunity for intermediaries to profit from passengers struggling to obtain tickets through official channels. At the difference of N3,400 per ticket, an agent selling 10 tickets at the alleged price would collect N34,000 above the equivalent official fares, assuming all tickets were genuine and sold at those prices. The actual profit, however, would depend on the agent\u2019s costs and how the tickets were obtained. \u201cThis is a parallel market,\u201d Raji alleged, questioning why passengers were struggling to access tickets through the official platform while intermediaries appeared able to offer them at higher prices. She also raised concerns about reports of passengers boarding trains without valid tickets, arguing that such practices, if established, would point to weaknesses in ticket verification and boarding controls. The claims have not independently established that NRC officials were involved in the alleged resale arrangements or that the corporation deliberately withheld tickets from the official portal. In a statement issued in response to the viral video, signed by Callistus Unyimadu, chief public relations officer of the NRC, the corporation said Raji had acknowledged that management warned prospective passengers to purchase tickets only through approved channels. The corporation said that although her experience might be correct, her conclusions about the involvement of the NRC and its management were misplaced. It maintained that passengers who patronise unauthorised ticket agents against its warnings do so at their own risk and that the corporation should not be held liable for losses arising from such transactions. The NRC also clarified that it does not directly operate the electronic ticketing platforms, which are managed under public-private partnership arrangements. According to the corporation, SecureID/TPS handles ticketing for the Abuja-Kaduna Train Service, Global Software Digital Solutions (GSDS) manages ticketing for the Lagos-Ibadan Train Service, while Fane Warri-Itakpe Ticketing Solutions Ltd. handles the Warri-Itakpe service. The corporation said the concessionaires were approved by the Infrastructure Concession Regulatory Commission and the Federal Executive Council before 2023. The NRC attributed part of the ticketing challenge to rising passenger demand, saying demand for train travel had exceeded available supply by more than tenfold, creating a significant imbalance between the number of passengers seeking seats and the capacity available. It also said scammers had devised ways of obtaining multiple National Identification Numbers (NINs) to purchase tickets for resale to unsuspecting passengers. Following the 2022 train bombing, the government introduced the NIN requirement for ticket purchases as part of efforts to improve passenger identification and reduce ticket racketeering, according to the corporation. The NRC said it subsequently introduced a second verification process in 2025, requiring passengers to present personal identification cards when boarding, in addition to the initial verification of the NIN used to purchase their tickets. \u201cThe NRC ticketing and verification system is integrated live with the NIMC database. Our system at Idu, Kubwa in Abuja, and at Rigasa in Kaduna, has the capacity to pull and display the picture of the person whose NIN number was used in purchasing the ticket from NIMC database. \u201cThat is the first point of the two way verification. At this point the individual with a third party ticket will definitely be discovered and refused boarding. This capacity also requires a good Access Control System at the station. \u201cThe Rigasa/Kaduna station being a hurriedly adopted station do not have an acceptable Access Control System thereby occasionally allowing agents of scammers to overwhelm security operatives. To check this, in 2025 NRC introduced the MM2 airport system requiring second verification at boarding with a personal ID card,\u201d the statement added. The NRC explained that the system at Idu and Kubwa stations in Abuja and Rigasa station in Kaduna could retrieve the photograph associated with a passenger\u2019s NIN. The corporation, however, acknowledged weaknesses in access control at Rigasa station, saying the facility had been hurriedly adopted and did not have an acceptable access-control system, occasionally allowing scammers\u2019 associates to overwhelm security operatives. It said the additional identification checks were introduced to address such vulnerabilities, while the electronic ticketing companies had been directed to improve access to their platforms. The corporation further declared zero tolerance for ticket racketeering, warning that staff found culpable or involved in colluding with offenders would be dealt with summarily. Despite the NRC\u2019s explanation, passengers commenting on the controversy have called for a more comprehensive investigation into the alleged resale of tickets and the possible involvement of railway personnel. \u201cThe corporation shall continue to ensure quality customer service at all times, across all our operation areas as we are determined to ensure that the train becomes a first choice alternative by discerning travellers across the country,\u201d the corporation added One social media user, L\u2019akerz, argued that the corporation should not dismiss the underlying concerns simply because the blogger\u2019s allegations against management might have gone too far. The user called for undercover investigations to identify those involved and stronger safeguards against ticket hoarding and resale at inflated prices, stressing that accountability and lasting solutions should take precedence over protecting the corporation\u2019s image. Similarly, Muyideen Nagim said the difficulty of obtaining tickets had pushed some passengers towards third-party sellers. \u201cWhether you admit it or not, this ticket racketeering is true and very shameful. To get a ticket is mostly through 3rd parties. Investigate & bring erring staff to book,\u201d he said, adding that he had resorted to road travel because of the difficulty of securing tickets. Another commenter, Rotimi, urged the NRC to take responsibility for resolving the problem, calling for a more comprehensive solution that would ensure passengers were electronically identified. Other users also alleged that similar ticketing difficulties occurred on the Lagos-Ibadan Train Service, suggesting that concerns about access to tickets might extend beyond the Abuja-Kaduna corridor. These comments, however, do not independently establish the scale of the problem across the network. The renewed allegations have revived questions about the outcome of the investigation announced in 2024. At the time, the NRC, through Yakub Mahmood, its deputy director of public relations, acknowledged receiving complaints from passengers who were experiencing difficulties obtaining tickets online and announced an investigation into allegations of racketeering involving some officials on the Abuja-Kaduna service. The corporation had assured passengers that the situation would be addressed and that measures would be taken to resolve challenges with the electronic ticketing platform. However, details of the findings of that investigation, including whether any officials were disciplined or prosecuted, have not been established from the information currently available. The latest controversy also raises questions about the performance of the electronic ticketing platform introduced in January 2021. The N900 million system was designed to improve efficiency, reduce physical contact between passengers and railway personnel, strengthen accountability and curb revenue leakages associated with manual ticket sales. The Abuja-Kaduna railway itself was developed as part of a wider infrastructure project. The Federal Government said the nine train stations along the corridor were included in an all-inclusive project contract worth $874 million awarded to China Civil Engineering Construction Company (CCECC). Yet the continuing complaints over access to tickets suggest that expanding railway infrastructure and introducing digital booking systems may not, on their own, eliminate opportunities for fraud if ticket allocation, verification and enforcement remain vulnerable. For commuters, the immediate concern is the ability to obtain tickets at official prices without having to depend on intermediaries. For the NRC and its concessionaires, the challenge is to demonstrate that tickets released for sale are allocated transparently, that suspicious transactions can be detected and that passengers who report irregularities receive a credible response. The next steps will depend partly on whether the NRC publishes the findings of its earlier investigation and provides verifiable data on ticket supply, booking demand, complaints, enforcement actions and the effectiveness of the additional identity checks introduced in 2025. Until those questions are answered, the dispute over the Abuja-Kaduna service remains a test of whether Nigeria\u2019s investment in electronic railway ticketing has translated into the transparency and ease of access promised to passengers. Related News [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) [Nigeria\u2019s biggest problem is poverty, not religion, Makinde tells christians](https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigerias-biggest-problem-is-poverty-not-religion-makinde-tells-christians/) [Aiyedatiwa inaugurates Ondo APC campaign council, rallies support for Tinubu\u2019s 2027 re-election](https://businessday.ng/news/article/aiyedatiwa-inaugurates-ondo-apc-campaign-council-rallies-support-for-tinubus-2027-re-election/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "465d337c1be7decbd6762fa9402f1550a30c3c6764cfba6a76bd91867e8e62ad", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:32Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1031", "title": "Dangote to list 10% of Ethiopian fertiliser business on Africa\u2019s youngest stock exchange", "url": "https://businessday.ng/africa/article/dangote-to-list-10-of-ethiopian-fertiliser-business-on-africas-youngest-stock-exchange/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/africa/article/dangote-to-list-10-of-ethiopian-fertiliser-business-on-africas-youngest-stock-exchange/", "text": "Share Advertisement Aliko Dangote, president and chief executive of Dangote Group, has pledged to list 10 percent of the conglomerate\u2019s Ethiopian fertiliser business on the Ethiopian Securities Exchange (ESX), giving local investors an opportunity to own a stake in one of the country\u2019s largest planned industrial projects. Read also:[Africa\u2019s oldest stock exchange eyes Dangote Group shares through depositary receipts](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/africas-oldest-stock-exchange-eyes-dangote-group-shares-through-depositary-receipts/) Dangote announced the plan at the group\u2019s investor roadshow in Addis Ababa, as the Nigerian industrialist seeks to expand investment in Ethiopia and encourage African investors to finance businesses operating across the continent. \u201cThe investment in Ethiopia has no limit,\u201d Dangote said. \u201cWhatever we make here, we will bring more and more here.\u201d The group\u2019s biggest undertaking in Ethiopia is a fertiliser manufacturing complex in Gode, in the Somali Region. Dangote Group and Ethiopian Investment Holdings signed a shareholders\u2019 agreement in August 2025, giving the Nigerian conglomerate a 60 percent stake in the project and the Ethiopian state investment holding company the remaining 40 percent. Construction was officially launched in October 2025. Initially estimated to cost $2.5 billion, the planned investment rose to more than $4 billion in May 2026 after the project\u2019s scope expanded and additional infrastructure requirements were included. Designed to produce three million metric tonnes of urea annually, the facility is expected to supply Ethiopia\u2019s agricultural sector and potentially serve neighbouring markets. Its planned infrastructure includes fertiliser blending and packaging facilities, alongside dedicated electricity generation capacity. The investment could help reduce Ethiopia\u2019s reliance on imported fertiliser, which exposes the country to international price movements, transport costs and foreign exchange pressures. However, the scale of any savings will depend on actual production, operating costs, debt obligations and how much of the output is sold locally. The project could also establish a new export-oriented manufacturing business if production exceeds domestic demand and the group secures buyers in regional markets. Ethiopia\u2019s agricultural sector is the principal market for fertiliser, with demand shaped by cultivated land, input prices, government procurement programmes and farmers\u2019 access to finance. Read also: [Dangotes $16 billion Lamu refinery clears legal hurdle in Kenya](https://businessday.ng/news/article/dangotes-16-billion-lamu-refinery-clears-legal-hurdle-in-kenya/) Gode\u2019s location in the Somali Region is also significant because the area\u2019s natural gas resources have long been viewed as a potential foundation for industrial development. Natural gas is a key feedstock for conventional urea production, used to make ammonia before it is converted into fertiliser. Reliable gas supplies, electricity, water and transport links will therefore be important to the project\u2019s operating costs and long-term viability. Dangote has also discussed energy and transport infrastructure investments, including a pipeline connection involving Djibouti and Ethiopia, as well as other regional projects in Tanzania. These plans form part of the group\u2019s wider effort to secure the energy supplies and infrastructure needed to support its manufacturing operations. Beyond Ethiopia, Dangote said the group planned to expand the capacity of its Lagos refinery to 1.4 million barrels per day and invest in 10,000 megawatts of power generation across Africa. He also said the group expected to generate $36 billion in revenue by the end of 2026 and aimed to repay all its debt by October. Ethiopian officials welcomed the proposed listing, describing it as a step towards linking African capital markets and allowing local investors to participate in the growth of businesses operating on the continent. Ahmed Shide, speaker of the Ethiopian parliament, described the initiative as a model of \u201cAfrican enterprises financed by Africans\u201d. He highlighted Dangote\u2019s fertiliser investment and the potential benefits of stronger links between African financial markets, while thanking the group for opening the opportunity to Ethiopian investors. Dagmawit Shifera said the offer would give qualified Ethiopian investors an opportunity to participate and support greater integration between African capital markets. Read also: [Dangote Group, Botswana Exchange laud NGX Group for driving cross-border collaboration](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/) Representatives of the Ethiopian Securities Exchange also thanked Dangote for choosing the local market, describing the move as a sign of confidence in Ethiopia\u2019s capital market and a boost to its financial sector. If implemented, the proposed listing would allow eligible Ethiopian investors to own a stake in the group\u2019s local fertiliser business, while giving Dangote another potential source of capital within Africa. The final level of participation will depend on the listing structure, regulatory approvals and investor eligibility. The plan comes amid broader efforts to mobilise African savings to finance the continent\u2019s industrial development. For Ethiopia, the potential benefits extend beyond a stock market listing: the fertiliser complex could reduce import dependence and create export opportunities, provided the project secures reliable inputs, reaches its planned production capacity and operates at competitive costs. Related News [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Faith Omoboye](https://businessday.ng/author/faithomoboyed04gmail-com/) Faith Omoboye is a foreign affairs correspondent with background in History and International relations. Her work focuses on African politics, diplomacy, and global governance. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Angola\u2019s fuel subsidies spending projected to hit $4 billion in 2026, official says](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/angolas-fuel-subsidies-spending-projected-to-hit-4-billion-in-2026-official-says/) [West African investors identify $10bn in projects ahead of regional investment summit](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/west-african-investors-identify-10bn-in-projects-ahead-of-regional-investment-summit/) [Here are six African countries extending Dangote IPO beyond Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/here-are-six-african-countries-where-investors-can-access-the-dangote-ipo/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "481e162ac8b83806da0bc28d5487365a8bd79a995e8fffb34fd45e75537452fa", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:33Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1032", "title": "$3.5bn deal signed in Port Harcourt for petrochemical company in Bonny", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/3-5bn-deal-signed-in-port-harcourt-for-petrochemical-company-in-bonny/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/3-5bn-deal-signed-in-port-harcourt-for-petrochemical-company-in-bonny/", "text": "Share Advertisement A memorandum of understanding (MoU) has been signed for a $3.5 billion petrochemicals project in Bonny, Rivers State, between the project promoters, a Chinese engineering company and a local landowner. The proposed project will occupy 500 hectares of land owned by Sene Kalada, a Wari Alabo (house chief), in Bonny. The MoU was signed in Port Harcourt by Frank Okafor, representing Best Option Chemical Limited, Lee Edwards of CNCEC, and Kalada. Chamberlain Peterside, director-general of the Rivers State Investment Promotion Agency (RSIPA), facilitated and witnessed the agreement. CNCEC is expected to provide 80 percent of the project financing, with the balance to come from other partners through land contributions and other inputs, according to the project promoters. Best Option Chemical Limited, described as a subsidiary of Best Option Petro-Meto in Spain, will coordinate engagement with regulatory authorities, including the Nigerian National Petroleum Company (NNPC), and host communities, while CNCEC will serve as the financial partner, Okafor said. Read also: [Villagers flee, hospitalised as oil explosion rocks Eleme in Rivers](https://businessday.ng/news/article/villagers-flee-hospitalised-as-oil-explosion-rocks-eleme-in-rivers/) Feasibility studies have been completed, and physical construction could begin within nine months, the promoters said. The plant is expected to produce methanol and other petrochemical products for the domestic market and export. The project, which may witness physical construction in less than nine month according to the promoters, is expected to produce methanol and other petrochemical products that would be consumed locally and for export. The proposed investment is expected to generate jobs during construction and operations, potentially adding to Rivers State\u2019s petrochemicals industry, which includes Indorama\u2019s facilities in Eleme. On why Bonny was selected over Ahoada and Onne, the promoters said its access to gas, a key feedstock for the proposed plant was responsible for the decisoin. Bonny hosts major oil and gas operations, including Nigeria LNG, which processes natural gas for export. The investors said the concentration of hydrocarbon companies in the area made it attractive for the project. Okafor also cited the planned road link to Bonny as a factor in the decision, saying improved road access would reduce dependence on water transport. Peterside said RSIPA had facilitated discussions involving Onne, Ahoada and Bonny before the investors settled on the island. He attributed the investment interest to improved political stability and confidence in the state\u2019s security environment. He said the agency would continue to support the project and ensure transparency as the promoters work towards further agreements. Peterside described the MoU as an initial step in the investment process, with additional agreements expected as the project advances. Related News [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) [Nigeria\u2019s biggest problem is poverty, not religion, Makinde tells christians](https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigerias-biggest-problem-is-poverty-not-religion-makinde-tells-christians/) [Aiyedatiwa inaugurates Ondo APC campaign council, rallies support for Tinubu\u2019s 2027 re-election](https://businessday.ng/news/article/aiyedatiwa-inaugurates-ondo-apc-campaign-council-rallies-support-for-tinubus-2027-re-election/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "501f9a17073aadba0f0f94f8cd870df356f81329070d6953d5bc18f38ebc8414", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:34Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1033", "title": "Nollywood data void leaves investors in N2.7trn sector flying blind", "url": "https://businessday.ng/life-arts/article/nollywood-data-void-leaves-investors-in-n2-7trn-sector-flying-blind/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/life-arts/article/nollywood-data-void-leaves-investors-in-n2-7trn-sector-flying-blind/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigerian filmmakers spend hundreds of millions of naira on productions, but the lack of a standardised system for tracking budgets, revenue, and ownership rights makesSubscribe to keep reading GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Anthony Udugba](https://businessday.ng/author/anthony-udugba/) Anthony Udugba is a seasoned entertainment business journalist at BusinessDay Media, boasting over four years of experience in the creative industry. With a proven track record of delivering insightful analysis and in-depth coverage, he leverages industry data, expert opinions, and stakeholder insights to craft compelling stories that shed light on the dynamic creative ecosystem. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) [Patrick Dele Cole: The diplomat who stood up to power and helped reshape Nigerian journalism](https://businessday.ng/life-arts/article/patrick-dele-cole-the-diplomat-who-stood-up-to-power-and-helped-reshape-nigerian-journalism/) [EWA launches grant pool to fund African creative businesses](https://businessday.ng/life-arts/article/ewa-launches-grant-pool-to-fund-african-creative-businesses/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "35a20f8bc6f20b1102045c8e78ed8710b8895e49ef59a244efa6dd046ed36557", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:36Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1034", "title": "Nigeria has seed innovation to \u2018nourish tomorrow \u2019, but distribution lags", "url": "https://businessday.ng/agriculture/article/nigeria-has-seed-innovation-to-nourish-tomorrow-but-distribution-lags/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/agriculture/article/nigeria-has-seed-innovation-to-nourish-tomorrow-but-distribution-lags/", "text": "Share Advertisement In Kaduna, Musa Abdullahi, a 55-year-old farmer, harvested 3.5 tons of wheat per hectare last dry season. The previous year, on the same plot, heSubscribe to keep reading GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Josephine Okojie-Okeiyi](https://businessday.ng/author/josephine-okojiebusinessdayonline-com/) Josephine Okojie-Okeiyi is a journalist with over five years\u2019 reporting experience. She writes on industry, agriculture, commodities, climate change, and environmental issues. She is fellow of Thomson Reuters Foundation and Bloomberg Media Initiative for Africa. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Lagos rice mill creates 2,000 jobs with 2.5m bag capacity](https://businessday.ng/agriculture/article/lagos-rice-mill-creates-2000-jobs-with-2-5m-bag-capacity/) [Edo, WACOT renew Auchi fertiliser plant partnership to drive food security](https://businessday.ng/agriculture/article/edo-wacot-renew-auchi-fertiliser-plant-partnership-to-drive-food-security/) [The business of turning Nigeria\u2019s indigenous vegetables into industry](https://businessday.ng/agriculture/article/the-business-of-turning-nigerias-indigenous-vegetables-into-industry/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "e4868845a2b81243940f179981cde18caa95b501c48c5ab55fde8a6185c98bb8", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:40Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1035", "title": "EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare", "url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/", "text": "Share Advertisement Erling Haaland bagged a brace as Manchester City edged Ipswich Town 3-2 to maintain their perfect start to the Premier League season, while Liverpool fought back to a 2-1 victory over Brentford. City extended their winning run to seven matches, the first time in the club\u2019s Premier League history, and moved six points clear at the top of the Premier League table. Phil Foden opened the scoring for the Citizens before Daizen Maeda equalised for Ipswich. Haaland then netted a brace to ensure Man City took all three points despite a late fightback from the visitors. Read Also: [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) The result also saw Manchester City join Chelsea, who achieved the feat in 2005/06, and Liverpool, who did so in 2019/20, as the only clubs to win their opening seven games of a Premier League season. The victory came amid uncertainty surrounding the club following a verdict in its Premier League financial rules violations. Fans booed the Premier League anthem before kick-off and waved inflatable bananas, reviving a tradition associated with the club in the 1980s. City, however, maintained their focus, with Haaland\u2019s finishing proving decisive in a closely contested encounter. Liverpool rally to beat nine-man Brentford Liverpool had to work harder for their points, coming from behind to defeat Brentford 2-1 at the Gtech Community Stadium after a match marked by two home-team dismissals. Brentford took the lead in the 44th minute when Keane Lewis-Potter met Igor Thiago\u2019s cross with a looping header that beat Liverpool goalkeeper Alisson Becker. The hosts appeared well placed to protect their advantage, but their task became more difficult in first-half stoppage time when Yehor Yarmoliuk was sent off for what referee John Brooks described as a \u201cheadbutting action\u201d against Liverpool defender Jeremy Jacquet. Liverpool made their numerical advantage count after the break. Victor Munoz restored parity in the 62nd minute, cutting inside before finishing with his left foot after being released by Florian Wirtz\u2019s pass. Alexander Isak then put Liverpool ahead eight minutes later, finishing clinically after Bradley Barcola headed down Alexis Mac Allister\u2019s cross. Brentford\u2019s hopes of salvaging a point faded further in stoppage time when Vitaly Janelt received a second yellow card for a foul on Liverpool substitute Rio Ngumoha, leaving the hosts with nine men. The two results underlined the fine margins of the title race, with City maintaining their winning momentum and Liverpool demonstrating their ability to recover from difficult situations away from home. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Anthony Nlebem](https://businessday.ng/author/anthony-nlebem/) Head of Sports at BusinessDay Media, a seasoned Digital Content Producer, and FIFA/CAF Accredited Journalist with over a decade of sports reporting.Has a deep understanding of the Nigerian and global sports landscape and skills in delivering comprehensive and insightful sports content. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [Fury vs Joshua projected to generate over N34bn in ticket sales](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-projected-to-generate-over-n34bn-in-ticket-sales/) [EPL: Neto brace fires Chelsea into top four as Martinez suffers head injury](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-neto-brace-fires-chelsea-into-top-four-as-martinez-suffers-head-injury/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "e3c56f764cac6f0f1e258e0a37b218e9b599c1f526d152083a9449e6aa7ff0fe", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:41Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1036", "title": "Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win", "url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria international [Alex Iwobi](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobi-goal-inspires-fulham-victory-at-craven-cottage/) scored the decisive goal as Fulham secured a hard-fought 1\u20130 victory over Hull City at Craven Cottage on Saturday, earning their first win of the 2026/27 Premier League season. The Super Eagles midfielder struck in the 33rd minute to end Fulham\u2019s six-match winless run and hand manager \u00c1lvaro Arbeloa his first Premier League victory since taking charge in July. The result lifted Fulham\u2019s points tally to five, moving them off the foot of the table and out of the relegation zone, while Hull remained ninth. Fulham took the lead in the 100th meeting between the sides in all competitions after Hull twice failed to clear their lines. Sander Berge collected the loose ball and slid a pass into Iwobi\u2019s path on the edge of the penalty area. Read Also: [Iwobi makes Premier League appearance history](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobi-makes-premier-league-appearance-history/) Iwobi drove a low shot towards the bottom-right corner. The effort took a deflection off Lucas Herrington and slipped beneath Hull goalkeeper Konstantinos Tzolakis to put the hosts ahead. The goal proved decisive in a closely contested encounter, with Hull struggling to create clear-cut chances. Mohamed Belloumi dragged a second-half effort wide, providing one of the visitors\u2019 few notable opportunities. The victory offered relief for Arbeloa, whose side had struggled to turn promising performances into wins during their opening league fixtures. However, Fulham finished the match with 10 men after Ryan Sessegnon received a straight red card in added time for a challenge on Robinio Vaz. Sessegnon\u2019s studs caught the back of Vaz\u2019s calf from behind, leaving the hosts to see out the closing moments a man down. Despite the late setback, Iwobi\u2019s strike secured three valuable points for Fulham as they began their push to move away from the relegation zone. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Anthony Nlebem](https://businessday.ng/author/anthony-nlebem/) Head of Sports at BusinessDay Media, a seasoned Digital Content Producer, and FIFA/CAF Accredited Journalist with over a decade of sports reporting.Has a deep understanding of the Nigerian and global sports landscape and skills in delivering comprehensive and insightful sports content. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Fury vs Joshua projected to generate over N34bn in ticket sales](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-projected-to-generate-over-n34bn-in-ticket-sales/) [EPL: Neto brace fires Chelsea into top four as Martinez suffers head injury](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-neto-brace-fires-chelsea-into-top-four-as-martinez-suffers-head-injury/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "6bef9269680c16192bb2bfb107c077eee2ddc689adc296dcda7da1eba09c2cdf", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:42Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1037", "title": "World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/", "text": "Share Advertisement The African Democratic Congress (ADC) has challenged President Bola Tinubu to explain a $12.4 billion gap in Nigeria\u2019s external accounts identified by the World Bank, describing the discrepancy as a major accountability question amid the country\u2019s worsening cost-of-living crisis. Bolaji Abdullahi, ADC National Publicity Secretary, in a statement on Sunday said the gap, equivalent to about N16.5 trillion, was too significant to be dismissed as an accounting discrepancy, noting that it represented almost a quarter of Nigeria\u2019s N68.32 trillion 2026 federal budget. The party cited the World Bank\u2019s October 2026 Nigeria Development Update, which reported that Nigeria recorded a $12 billion current-account surplus and $6.2 billion in net financial inflows in the first half of 2026. Despite these inflows, the report identified a $12.4 billion gap. The ADC said the World Bank attributed part of the discrepancy to oil export earnings that were not repatriated, while noting that the return of export proceeds remained limited partly because Nigerian crude had been pledged against long-term loans. \u201cThe scale is staggering. This unexplained gap is larger than the entire current-account surplus for the same period. It is about 5.6 times the $2.2 billion Nigeria attracted in foreign direct investment,\u201d the opposition party said. The party accused the Tinubu administration of demanding sacrifices from Nigerians without providing sufficient accountability for the country\u2019s resources. \u201cPresident Tinubu welcomed this report. He cannot celebrate one part of the report and stay silent on a gap bigger than the surplus that he and his government are celebrating. \u201cSacrifice cannot be compulsory for everyday citizens while accountability remains optional for the government that is in charge of our collective resources,\u201d the ADC added. The ADC called on the federal government to publish a comprehensive reconciliation of the $12.4 billion gap, disclose all oil-backed loans and pledged crude commitments, and provide updated net foreign reserve figures. It also demanded evidence that increased revenues were translating into improved essential services, including healthcare, education and electricity. \u201cPresident Tinubu must open the books. Nigerians have sacrificed enough. We deserve answers,\u201d the party said. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [Fury vs Joshua projected to generate over N34bn in ticket sales](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-projected-to-generate-over-n34bn-in-ticket-sales/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) [Nigeria\u2019s biggest problem is poverty, not religion, Makinde tells christians](https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigerias-biggest-problem-is-poverty-not-religion-makinde-tells-christians/) [Aiyedatiwa inaugurates Ondo APC campaign council, rallies support for Tinubu\u2019s 2027 re-election](https://businessday.ng/news/article/aiyedatiwa-inaugurates-ondo-apc-campaign-council-rallies-support-for-tinubus-2027-re-election/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "a486ed7488fbcc1b2c5ba51f98c158482c2b595c29a1d7d09234f4356de969d7", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:43Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1038", "title": "Fury vs Joshua projected to generate over N34bn in ticket sales", "url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-projected-to-generate-over-n34bn-in-ticket-sales/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-projected-to-generate-over-n34bn-in-ticket-sales/", "text": "Share Advertisement [Tyson Fury and Anthony Joshua](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/joshua-fury-cardiff-favoured-over-wembley-for-200m-heavyweight-clash/) heavyweight bout is projected to generate over N34 billion (\u00a326 million) in ticket sales alone, putting the all-British contest among the highest-grossing boxing events in history. The rivals are scheduled to meet on December 11 at Cardiff\u2019s Principality Stadium in a fight expected to draw significant commercial interest, with UFC chief Dana White, who says he is promoting the bout, projecting a record-breaking gate. White, in a chart with Sky Sports, described the contest as a landmark event for British boxing. \u201cFor our generation, two of the biggest, most famous heavyweights in boxing. And not even arguable that it\u2019s the biggest UK fight ever between two British heavyweights,\u201d he said. Read Also: [Fury vs Joshua fight sells out 80,000 tickets in 60 minutes](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-fight-sells-out-80000-tickets-in-60-minutes/) The projected \u00a326 million gate would place Fury vs Joshua fourth on the list of boxing\u2019s highest-grossing events by ticket revenue, according to the figures reported for the contest. It would also set a new benchmark for British boxing if the projection is achieved. The reported demand has underlined the commercial appeal of the long-awaited encounter. All 80,000 tickets were reportedly sold in under an hour after general sales opened, with more than 500,000 fans said to have joined the online queue. Ticket prices ranged from \u00a388.50 for the cheapest seats to \u00a313,128, including booking fees, for the most expensive standard tickets. Fury and Joshua dominate Britain\u2019s biggest boxing gates The projected gate would put Fury and Joshua\u2019s showdown at the top of the British boxing list, ahead of two other major events involving the heavyweight rivals. Anthony Joshua\u2019s fight against Daniel Dubois reportedly generated a \u00a314 million gate, while Tyson Fury\u2019s contest against Dillian Whyte brought in an estimated \u00a312 million. The projected \u00a326 million total for Fury vs Joshua would therefore exceed both figures, reinforcing the financial significance of a contest that has taken years to arrange. However, the gate represents only one component of the event\u2019s potential commercial value. Broadcasting rights, sponsorship agreements, and other commercial partnerships could generate additional revenue, although those figures have not been included in the ticket-sales projection. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Anthony Nlebem](https://businessday.ng/author/anthony-nlebem/) Head of Sports at BusinessDay Media, a seasoned Digital Content Producer, and FIFA/CAF Accredited Journalist with over a decade of sports reporting.Has a deep understanding of the Nigerian and global sports landscape and skills in delivering comprehensive and insightful sports content. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [EPL: Neto brace fires Chelsea into top four as Martinez suffers head injury](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-neto-brace-fires-chelsea-into-top-four-as-martinez-suffers-head-injury/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "a160ecaf5b1c88774e41925b77d4df1adfc311dfe8f47da951a3e4a9ee7b5368", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:45Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1039", "title": "Transcorp Hotels\u2019 nine-month profit rises 5% despite revenue decline", "url": "https://businessday.ng/companies/article/transcorp-hotels-nine-month-profit-rises-5-despite-revenue-decline/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/companies/article/transcorp-hotels-nine-month-profit-rises-5-despite-revenue-decline/", "text": "Share Advertisement Transcorp Hotels Plc reported a 4.8 percent increase in profit for the nine months ended September 2026, supported by lower operating costs and higher finance income, even as revenue declined and borrowing costs more than doubled. The hospitality group\u2019s unaudited financial statements show that profit after tax rose to N15.54 billion from N14.82 billion in the corresponding period of 2025, while revenue fell 2.6 percent to N69.46 billion from N71.30 billion. Operating profit declined to N24.36 billion from N24.71 billion, while profit before tax edged up to N22.53 billion from N22.40 billion, reflecting the contribution of finance income to the group\u2019s overall earnings. Third-quarter earnings come under pressure The company\u2019s performance weakened in the third quarter, with profit after tax falling 18.6 percent to N5.00 billion in the three months ended September 2026, compared with N6.14 billion a year earlier. Revenue, however, increased 2.7 percent to N25.04 billion from N24.37 billion, while gross profit rose 4.6 percent to N19.34 billion. The increase in revenue was not sufficient to offset higher operating expenses, which climbed 30 percent to N9.97 billion from N7.67 billion. Consequently, operating profit declined 13.1 percent to N9.56 billion from N11.00 billion. Finance costs increased almost threefold to N2.85 billion from N957.69 million, while unrealised foreign exchange losses rose to N349.16 million from N61.99 million. Although finance income increased to N2.47 billion from N195.82 million, profit before tax still fell 13.2 percent to N8.83 billion from N10.18 billion. Rooms remain the biggest revenue driver Revenue from rooms remained Transcorp Hotels\u2019 largest income source, generating N46.29 billion in the nine months to September, although this was slightly below the N46.65 billion recorded in the corresponding period of 2025. Food and beverage revenue also declined, falling 8.3 percent to N19.27 billion from N21.02 billion. Together, the two categories accounted for the overwhelming majority of the group\u2019s revenue, underscoring its dependence on accommodation and food services. Other revenue streams delivered mixed results. Event centre hall rental income declined to N981.52 million from N1.15 billion, while shop rental revenue was broadly unchanged at N1.36 billion. Service charge income increased to N690.64 million from N396.82 million, while recreation services revenue rose to N443.25 million from N312.46 million. Secretarial services income also increased to N63.66 million from N32.41 million. The company operates in Nigeria\u2019s hospitality market, providing accommodation, meeting rooms and leisure facilities. Its portfolio also includes the Transcorp Centre, a 5,000-capacity venue commissioned in 2025 to host conferences, corporate events and social gatherings. Borrowings surge to N54.96bn One of the most significant changes in the financial statements is the sharp increase in interest-bearing borrowings. Total borrowings rose to N54.96 billion as of September 30, 2026, from N10.52 billion at the end of December 2025. This represents an increase of approximately 422 percent. The increase was driven largely by a N42.92 billion loan, which the financial statements list at N49.19 billion at the reporting date, alongside an existing N12.75 billion loan. The newly listed loan carries an interest rate of 22 percent and a maturity date of March 17, 2031. The existing N12.75 billion loan carries a 10 percent interest rate and matures on May 31, 2029. The company recorded N5.91 billion in finance costs during the nine months, up from N2.80 billion a year earlier. Interest on debt accounted for N5.51 billion of the 2026 figure, compared with N1.40 billion in the previous corresponding period. Finance income, however, increased significantly to N4.46 billion from N520.68 million, largely reflecting interest on intercompany loan receivables, which contributed N4.19 billion. Operating cash flow turns negative Net cash used in operating activities stood at N15.93 billion in the nine months to September 2026, reversing from a positive N18.82 billion in the corresponding period of 2025. Cash generated from operations was negative N11.27 billion, compared with positive N21.79 billion a year earlier, while income tax payments increased to N4.66 billion from N2.97 billion. The company also spent N5.92 billion on investing activities, including N6.28 billion on property, plant and equipment. However, financing activities generated N27.02 billion, supported by N44.48 billion in borrowing proceeds. The group also paid N13.32 billion in dividends during the period. Consequently, cash and cash equivalents increased by N5.17 billion to N20.34 billion at September 30, 2026. Assets expand as liabilities increase The group\u2019s total assets rose to N206.82 billion at September 2026, from N159.91 billion at the end of December 2025. Property, plant, and equipment increased to N127.69 billion from N124.86 billion, while trade and other receivables surged to N50.53 billion from N11.00 billion. Total liabilities increased to N109.27 billion from N64.68 billion over the same period, reflecting the increase in borrowings and other obligations. Shareholders\u2019 equity stood at N97.55 billion, compared with N95.23 billion at the end of 2025. The company closed its last trading day on Friday at N290.50 per share on the Nigerian Stock Exchange (NGX). Transcorp Hotels began the year with a share price of N170.90 and has since gained 70 percent on that price valuation, ranking it 31st on the NGX in terms of year-to-date performance. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Chinwe Michael](https://businessday.ng/author/chinwe-michael/) Chinwe Michael is a Nigerian business journalist and media professional with over 4 years of experience covering companies, financial markets, banking, fintech, economic policy, financial inclusion, and corporate affairs. She currently leads the Companies & Markets desk at BusinessDay, where she drives coverage of Nigeria\u2019s corporate and capital markets, translates complex financial data into accessible stories, and mentors younger journalists. Prior to that, she was the head of the financial inclusion/fintech desk. With a background in banking and finance and extensive experience in media and communications, Michael has developed expertise in financial journalism, economic analysis, investigative reporting and market intelligence. Her work examines how monetary policy, corporate performance, capital flows, technology, and regulatory decisions affect businesses, investors, and households. She was selected from about 3,000 applicants as one of 20 journalists for Cohort 4 of the MTN Media Innovation Programme, gaining exposure to new approaches to journalism, technology, and media innovation. Her international journalism experience and engagement with professionals across Russia, Dubai, Saudi Arabia, and others in different sectors have further broadened her understanding of global communication and development. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [LCFE bets on Commodities Exchange for transparent petroleum pricing, market efficiency](https://businessday.ng/companies/article/lcfe-bets-on-commodities-exchange-for-transparent-petroleum-pricing-market-efficiency/) [UBA wins court ruling in N156.03bn debt dispute with Stallion Group](https://businessday.ng/companies/article/uba-wins-court-ruling-in-n156-03bn-debt-dispute-with-stallion-group/) [Sage Grey Finance secures investment-grade rating from Agusto](https://businessday.ng/companies/article/sage-grey-finance-secures-investment-grade-rating-from-agusto/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "3206fe4527c959298074175c411e824d90deff48681cbe0c05790be92323954f", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:46Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1040", "title": "Nigeria handles nearly half of Gulf of Guinea vessel calls-NIMASA", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigeria-handles-nearly-half-of-gulf-of-guinea-vessel-calls-nimasa/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigeria-handles-nearly-half-of-gulf-of-guinea-vessel-calls-nimasa/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria accounted for 46 percent of port calls in the Gulf of Guinea in 2025, the Nigerian Maritime Administration and Safety Agency (NIMASA) said on Friday. Dayo Mobereola, director general of NIMASA, told this to stakeholders at a meeting in Lagos to mark World Maritime Day while listing the agency\u2019s contributions in the past year. Port calls are recorded when a vessel arrives at a port to load or discharge cargo, bunker, or carry out other port-related activities. In 2025, 4,477 vessels called at Nigerian seaports, according to Nigerian Ports Authority records. [The Gulf of Guinea](https://www.google.com/url?opi=89978449&q=https://businessday.ng/maritime/article/nigeria-urges-eu-to-extend-gulf-of-guinea-security-support/%3Famp&sa=U&ved=2ahUKEwiziNLDysCXAxU3V0EAHdNbPKkQFnoECFIQAQ&usg=AOvVaw0zFNwcV0Dyu0_QaOkoQWRW), which comprises 20 commercial seaports, accounts for 25 percent of maritime traffic in Africa. The region offers access to Europe, the South and North American continents and is the primary access route to and from Nigeria and Angola, Africa\u2019s first and second largest oil producers. The figures stirs optimism for Nigeria\u2019s maritime industry players. Investors are calling it an opportunity to scale. \u201cIt shows you how big and significant we are,\u201d Aminu Umar, president of the Nigerian Chamber of Shipping, told BusinessDay. \u201cIt means that we already are at an advantageous position, which makes it easy for us to scale.\u201d Umar, whose company operates its own ships, said Nigeria\u2019s domestic market has created enormous demand that the country can ride to become a regional maritime authority. \u201cWhat we need is to make our ports a perfect environment for becoming the hub for the rest of the nations because we already have the significant volume. So it\u2019s easy for us to scale to have them come here, and from here go to the other nations.\u201d Cargo throughput at Nigeria\u2019s ports in 2025 rose to 129.3 million metric tonnes from 103.6 million metric tonnes in 2024. Ivory Coast, the second-highest in the region, recorded 54.37 million. However, Nigeria\u2019s busiest ports, built for its colonial-era trade needs, have struggled to meet the current appetite of global trade. As ships got bigger, ports got deeper and modernised to accommodate them, but Nigeria has not caught up. Upgrades to its major ports have stalled for decades, conceding regional and transhipment authority to neighbours. \u201cOur cargoes, as well as those of other countries, have to be transshipped through other locations, such as Morocco, Las Palmas, Egypt or Fujairah. If we develop the infrastructure to accommodate larger vessels, they can come here, discharge cargoes destined for the Gulf of Guinea, and then distribute them to other countries,\u201d Umar said. \u201cIf we create a place or an environment by investing in our new infrastructure, creating deep seaports, then we can accommodate bigger vessels,\u201d he added. The federal government plans to build deep seaports across the nation\u2019s coastline states to strengthen its maritime capacity. [Read also: DP World signs MoUs for $7bn Ogun deep seaport, economic zone](https://www.google.com/url?opi=89978449&q=https://businessday.ng/maritime/article/dp-world-signs-mous-for-7bn-ogun-deep-seaport-economic-zone/%3Famp&sa=U&ved=2ahUKEwjf67H8y8CXAxU0RZ8JHVwOFtQQFnoECCUQAQ&usg=AOvVaw3k6JjDOgpP5FkqLNCLisZf) Currently, the Lekki port in Lagos is Nigeria\u2019s only deep seaport. In 2025, the port received the largest ships on average, with a Gross Registered Tonnage (GRT) of 55,712. It had the only record of [transshipment](https://www.google.com/url?opi=89978449&q=https://businessday.ng/maritime/article/nigeria-enters-regional-transshipment-market-with-lekki-port-expansion/%3Famp&sa=U&ved=2ahUKEwjbzZLJzMCXAxWeaUEAHekdLJAQFnoECCMQAQ&usg=AOvVaw34fdNI1_nsijnVx5BaJU0i) among Nigeria\u2019s ports in the first half of 2026. \u201cThe other ones which may be deeper will come. And from there, it will open up Nigeria as a major hub for cargoes,\u201d Umar said. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Bethel Olujobi](https://businessday.ng/author/bethel-olujobi/) Bethel Olujobi is the Trade Correspondent at BusinessDay. He brings scarce insight into Nigeria's maritime policies and the changing currents of global commerce defining the country's position in international trade. Before this, he reported on foreign relations, migration and sports for BusinessDay. He holds a Bachelor's degree in Mass Communication, and is an alumni of the CNN Academy and the Financial Times News School. He is certified by Reuters and Google. He is based in Lagos, Nigeria. For pitches, email: [[email protected]](https://businessday.ng/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection) Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) [Nigeria\u2019s biggest problem is poverty, not religion, Makinde tells christians](https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigerias-biggest-problem-is-poverty-not-religion-makinde-tells-christians/) [Aiyedatiwa inaugurates Ondo APC campaign council, rallies support for Tinubu\u2019s 2027 re-election](https://businessday.ng/news/article/aiyedatiwa-inaugurates-ondo-apc-campaign-council-rallies-support-for-tinubus-2027-re-election/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "a6afdd427d75b13f7bf160d514d174c9eb25a346f9e1f3700f3efc5dc6105dd4", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:47Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1041", "title": "EPL: Neto brace fires Chelsea into top four as Martinez suffers head injury", "url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-neto-brace-fires-chelsea-into-top-four-as-martinez-suffers-head-injury/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-neto-brace-fires-chelsea-into-top-four-as-martinez-suffers-head-injury/", "text": "Share Advertisement Pedro Neto scored twice as Chelsea sealed a crucial 2-0 win over Everton at Hill Dickinson Stadium, moving into the Premier League\u2019s top four despite a head injury to goalkeeper Emi Martinez. Neto opened the scoring four minutes into the second half, controlling Jorrel Hato\u2019s low cross before firing past Jordan Pickford. He completed his brace in the 11 minutes of added time, calmly slotting into the bottom corner after Everton\u2019s wall blocked Jordan Henderson\u2019s free-kick. The result lifted Chelsea above Everton into the top four, while the match was overshadowed by a serious-looking head collision involving Martinez and Everton defender Jarrad Branthwaite. The Argentine goalkeeper received lengthy treatment before being taken to hospital. After a slow start, the contest intensified when Everton midfielder Harrison Armstrong launched into a tackle on Josh Acheampong, triggering the first of five yellow cards in the opening half. The challenge lifted the home crowd and helped Everton enjoy their strongest spell of the game. However, Thierno Barry missed an opportunity to give the hosts the lead, directing a header from Jack Grealish\u2019s cross too close to Martinez. Chelsea struggled to create clear chances early on, with their first significant threat arriving four minutes before half-time when Moises Caicedo struck the crossbar from a well-worked corner. Everton were also frustrated when Morgan Rogers escaped a second booking shortly before the break following a heavy challenge on England teammate James Garner. Chelsea capitalised after the interval, with Neto breaking the deadlock to put the visitors in control. Martinez\u2019s injury briefly interrupted Chelsea\u2019s momentum, with Belgian goalkeeper Mike Penders replacing him for his first Premier League appearance. Penders had little to do as Everton struggled to threaten, managing only a few desperate crosses. Chelsea had not kept a Premier League clean sheet in 22 matches stretching back to January, but Everton offered limited attacking resistance after Martinez\u2019s departure. At the other end, Pickford produced a fine save to deny Joao Pedro before Neto sealed the points with his second goal. The result strengthened Chelsea\u2019s push for a Champions League place, while Everton were left to rue missed opportunities and their inability to respond after conceding early in the second half. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Anthony Nlebem](https://businessday.ng/author/anthony-nlebem/) Head of Sports at BusinessDay Media, a seasoned Digital Content Producer, and FIFA/CAF Accredited Journalist with over a decade of sports reporting.Has a deep understanding of the Nigerian and global sports landscape and skills in delivering comprehensive and insightful sports content. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [Fury vs Joshua projected to generate over N34bn in ticket sales](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-projected-to-generate-over-n34bn-in-ticket-sales/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "1b41f6505b55a03a65207ef78267a4a8f1b420e735bfcfc5eddc6938219ce521", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:49Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1042", "title": "Mourinho: I\u2019d have left Real Madrid if I were Vinicius Jr", "url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/mourinho-id-have-left-real-madrid-if-i-were-vinicius-jr/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/sports/article/mourinho-id-have-left-real-madrid-if-i-were-vinicius-jr/", "text": "Share Advertisement Jose Mourinho has said he would have forced an exit from Real Madrid last summer if he were Vinicius Junior, citing the repeated abuse the Brazilian winger has endured from opposition supporters. Vinicius has faced regular racist abuse since joining Madrid in 2018. He was also sent off during the club\u2019s win over Villarreal last weekend for pulling an opponent\u2019s hair and received a four-match suspension, ruling him out of the Clasico against Barcelona on October 25. \u201cHe\u2019s a fantastic boy; tremendous credit to him for the fact that he keeps living and surviving and still having a smile,\u201d Mourinho told reporters. \u201cIf it were me in his place, probably I wouldn\u2019t be here. In this last summer I would have forced an exit to a league, a country, or somewhere some rival clubs and stadiums respect him.\u201d Vinicius, however, signed a new contract with Real Madrid in the summer, extending his stay at the club until 2032. Mourinho said the Brazilian\u2019s loyalty to Real Madrid and his belief that the situation could improve influenced his decision to stay. \u201cBecause he loves Real Madrid, for loving the club and the city, for believing that things can always improve, he renewed his contract, and he keeps on smiling,\u201d Mourinho said. \u201cNo one or nobody can, well, influence him negatively, yes, because he\u2019s human, but take away his joy of living and working and being with his teammates, no, no one can do that.\u201d Mourinho substituted Vinicius during Madrid\u2019s 4-3 Champions League win at Roma on Wednesday, with the winger still working to regain the form that helped him win the competition twice under Carlo Ancelotti. The manager also welcomed the length of Vinicius\u2019s suspension, saying he had feared that figures within the Spanish football federation\u2019s disciplinary committee might impose an additional one or two matches. Real Madrid face Sevilla on Sunday as they seek to avoid falling further behind La Liga leaders Barcelona, who visit Real Betis on Saturday. Barcelona have won all their league matches this season and hold a six-point lead over fourth-placed Real Madrid. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Anthony Nlebem](https://businessday.ng/author/anthony-nlebem/) Head of Sports at BusinessDay Media, a seasoned Digital Content Producer, and FIFA/CAF Accredited Journalist with over a decade of sports reporting.Has a deep understanding of the Nigerian and global sports landscape and skills in delivering comprehensive and insightful sports content. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [Fury vs Joshua projected to generate over N34bn in ticket sales](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/fury-vs-joshua-projected-to-generate-over-n34bn-in-ticket-sales/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "ce50bcc004b1ccec7896e6a386f8fcb92e601b6751efaeaf9f017173ba2ba108", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:50Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1043", "title": "\u201cLogistics\u201d has become a convenient word for theft in Nigeria", "url": "https://businessday.ng/pro/article/logistics-has-become-a-convenient-word-for-theft-in-nigeria/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/pro/article/logistics-has-become-a-convenient-word-for-theft-in-nigeria/", "text": "Share Advertisement Language is a powerful mirror of culture. In Nigeria, everyday speech is wonderfully vibrant, with new words c