astructure, healthcare systems, and public health policies. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [17 firms pay shareholders N1.13trn in dividends in H1](https://businessday.ng/market-intelligence/article/17-firms-pay-shareholders-n1-13trn-in-dividends-in-h1/) [World Bank\u2019s October 2026 assessment and Fitch\u2019s upgrade to a positive outlook: Has Nigeria turned the corner?](https://businessday.ng/backpage/article/world-banks-october-2026-assessment-and-fitchs-upgrade-to-a-positive-outlook-has-nigeria-turned-the-corner/) [\u201cLogistics\u201d has become a convenient word for theft in Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/pro/article/logistics-has-become-a-convenient-word-for-theft-in-nigeria/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "f6b53276d77c446cbae16e85dcf2192e045714a3f24dd913c789e3b278189441", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:52Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1045", "title": "Lowest inflation since 2025 yet to ease Nigerians\u2019 cost of living", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/lowest-inflation-since-2025-yet-to-ease-nigerians-cost-of-living/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/lowest-inflation-since-2025-yet-to-ease-nigerians-cost-of-living/", "text": "Share Advertisement \u2026remains highest rate among African peers Nigeria\u2019s inflation rate has slowed to its lowest level since the National Bureau of Statistics overhauled its methodology inSubscribe to keep reading GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Oluwatobi Ojabello](https://businessday.ng/author/oluwatobi-ojabello/) Oluwatobi Ojabello, PhD, is a dynamic and multi-dimensional Assistant Editor for Economy and Markets with over two years of professional\u2026 [Wasiu Alli](https://businessday.ng/author/wasiu-alli/) Wasiu Alli is a business, economics cum data journalist with strong expertise covering macro trends, capital markets, government policies, corporate\u2026 Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "479c19483caf9684f7aad99ea1364f0817a3051fd9e4a39be573436ec73b7a62", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:54Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1046", "title": "Expert warns export curbs could hurt farmers without policy consistency", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/expert-warns-export-curbs-could-hurt-farmers-without-policy-consistency/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/expert-warns-export-curbs-could-hurt-farmers-without-policy-consistency/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria\u2019s restrictions on agricultural exports could hurt the farmers and processors they are meant to protect unless government policy remains consistent and safeguards are inSubscribe to keep reading GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) [Nigeria\u2019s biggest problem is poverty, not religion, Makinde tells christians](https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigerias-biggest-problem-is-poverty-not-religion-makinde-tells-christians/) [Aiyedatiwa inaugurates Ondo APC campaign council, rallies support for Tinubu\u2019s 2027 re-election](https://businessday.ng/news/article/aiyedatiwa-inaugurates-ondo-apc-campaign-council-rallies-support-for-tinubus-2027-re-election/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "6a9b3dbde6ba16f067edb971ef1d3b22111ed286813ee8ae85ab8bd7729cf1a9", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:56Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1047", "title": "Nigeria inflation falls to 14.63% as food prices retreat, core pressures rise", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-inflation-falls-to-14-63-as-food-prices-retreat-core-pressures-rise/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-inflation-falls-to-14-63-as-food-prices-retreat-core-pressures-rise/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria\u2019s inflation rate fell to 14.63 percent in September as food prices declined sharply, offering some relief after months of elevated price pressures. But theSubscribe to keep reading GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Oluwatobi Ojabello](https://businessday.ng/author/oluwatobi-ojabello/) Oluwatobi Ojabello, PhD, is a dynamic and multi-dimensional Assistant Editor for Economy and Markets with over two years of professional journalism experience. He delivers authoritative, data-driven coverage of fiscal policy, financial institutions and capital markets, using clear analysis to explain Nigeria\u2019s most complex economic developments. His work focuses on macroeconomic policy, financial stability and corporate performance, turning technical issues into accessible narratives that inform both experts and everyday readers. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "d371017b7da6e1d293f5602ea9bc7fa426697989326d70e878081b1d64539e67", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:29:57Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1048", "title": "Cardoso highlights Nigeria\u2019s reform gains as IMF leaders debate global economic resilience", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/cardoso-highlights-nigerias-reform-gains-as-imf-leaders-debate-global-economic-resilience/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/cardoso-highlights-nigerias-reform-gains-as-imf-leaders-debate-global-economic-resilience/", "text": "Share Advertisement Olayemi Cardoso, governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) says Nigeria\u2019s monetary and foreign-exchange reforms have strengthened the country\u2019s ability to withstand external economic shocks, while creating opportunities to attract investment and diversify foreign-exchange earnings. Cardoso stated this on Thursday during a high-level discussion at the International Monetary Fund (IMF) Annual Meetings in Bangkok, where global economic leaders examined the risks confronting the world economy and the reforms required to sustain growth. The session, Debate on the Global Economy: Forging Resilient and Dynamic Economies for the Future, featured IMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva, Eurogroup President and Greek Finance Minister Kyriakos Pierrakakis, Thailand\u2019s Deputy Prime Minister and Finance Minister Ekniti Nitithanprapas, and Argentina\u2019s Economy Minister Luis Caputo. Moderated by Lisa Abramowicz, a journalist with Bloomberg the discussion focused on fiscal discipline, economic diversification, regional integration and investment as countries contend with inflation, rising borrowing costs and geopolitical uncertainty. Cardoso noted that Nigeria\u2019s experience over the past three years had reinforced the importance of consistent policies and decisive reforms in restoring economic stability and market confidence. He recalled that the country had faced a severe foreign-exchange crisis, with net external reserves falling to approximately $3 billion at one point and outstanding foreign-exchange obligations exceeding $7 billion. The situation required significant monetary and foreign-exchange adjustments, including aggressive interest-rate increases, the harmonisation of foreign-exchange windows and measures to improve transparency and market functioning. Cardoso said these actions, alongside wider economic reforms, helped restore investor confidence, clear verified foreign-exchange obligations and strengthen reserves to their highest level in 18 years. Reas also: [CBN and finance ministry pitch $78.5bn infrastructure pipeline at IMF-World bank meetings](https://businessday.ng/news/article/cbn-and-finance-ministry-pitch-78-5bn-infrastructure-pipeline-at-imf-world-bank-meetings/) He explained that Nigeria\u2019s inflation experience differed from those of many other countries because foreign-exchange and subsidy reforms had created additional price pressures when much of the world was easing monetary policy. He said those conditions informed the CBN\u2019s aggressive tightening programme in 2024 and its subsequent decision to reduce the benchmark interest rate in September 2026. Cardoso noted that the adjustment was necessary to address weaknesses in monetary policy transmission and was absorbed without major market disruption because investors understood the Bank\u2019s policy direction. He also highlighted the CBN\u2019s efforts to reduce Nigeria\u2019s dependence on oil-related foreign-exchange inflows through increased diaspora remittances. \u201cWhen you are going through a crisis, it forces you to be creative,\u201d he said. According to Cardoso, the Bank engaged Nigerians abroad and international financial institutions to identify obstacles to remitting funds through formal channels. The engagements informed changes to existing processes, including digital improvements, while a committee chaired by the Governor worked with commercial banks to implement the initiative. He said monthly inflows increased from approximately $200 million to $600 million during the first year of the intervention, before reaching nearly $950 million in July 2026. The CBN\u2019s reported July figure of $947 million brought the country closer to its target of attracting $1 billion in monthly remittances through formal channels. Cardoso said the experience demonstrated the potential for practical institutional reforms to expand foreign-exchange inflows and strengthen the country\u2019s external position. On economic diversification, he cited the development of domestic refining capacity, particularly the Dangote refinery, as an important step towards greater value addition and reduced dependence on exporting raw materials. The Governor also pointed to renewed international market recognition, including Nigeria\u2019s return to FTSE Russell\u2019s frontier-market classification and the inclusion of Nigerian government bonds in a J.P. Morgan emerging-market benchmark. His remarks came as Georgieva warned that the global economy\u2019s resilience was increasingly under pressure from elevated debt, geopolitical disruption, inflation and growing disparities between countries benefiting from artificial intelligence investment and those being left behind. The IMF Managing Director said governments needed to rebuild financial buffers and strengthen economic fundamentals rather than assume that recent global growth would continue uninterrupted. Other panellists offered perspectives on the same challenge. Pierrakakis highlighted the importance of fiscal discipline and deeper European market integration, while Thailand\u2019s Nitithanprapas described investments in regional energy networks, digital infrastructure and skills development. Caputo outlined Argentina\u2019s fiscal reforms and investment incentives, emphasising the importance of predictability and sound economic management in attracting private capital. Asked about Nigeria\u2019s role in encouraging investment across Africa, Cardoso said larger economies had a responsibility to demonstrate the value of sound economic policies and sustained reform. He pointed to improving international market assessments as evidence of the progress Nigeria had made, while emphasising the need to maintain the country\u2019s policy direction. \u201cWe need to stay on that course,\u201d he said, adding that demonstrating results was more important than making declarations. Cardoso\u2019s contribution situated Nigeria\u2019s recent monetary reforms and efforts to diversify foreign-exchange inflows within the wider international discussion on how countries can strengthen economic resilience, attract productive investment and withstand future global shocks. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) [Nigeria\u2019s biggest problem is poverty, not religion, Makinde tells christians](https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigerias-biggest-problem-is-poverty-not-religion-makinde-tells-christians/) [Aiyedatiwa inaugurates Ondo APC campaign council, rallies support for Tinubu\u2019s 2027 re-election](https://businessday.ng/news/article/aiyedatiwa-inaugurates-ondo-apc-campaign-council-rallies-support-for-tinubus-2027-re-election/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "f8941ba9e4f4a26d570349f189f4494a13b9c39e545dd27eaa742249fafa2c1f", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:00Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1049", "title": "CBN and finance ministry pitch $78.5bn infrastructure pipeline at IMF-World bank meetings", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/cbn-and-finance-ministry-pitch-78-5bn-infrastructure-pipeline-at-imf-world-bank-meetings/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/cbn-and-finance-ministry-pitch-78-5bn-infrastructure-pipeline-at-imf-world-bank-meetings/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria is seeking $78.5bn in [private capital to finance 65 projects across critical economic](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/foreign-reserves-figure-corrected-after-cbn-admits-website-glitch/) sectors as the federal government ramps up efforts to attract investors, expand productive capacity, and create jobs. Taiwo Oyedele, Finance Minister and Coordinating Minister of the Economy, alongside Olayemi Cardoso, Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) Governor, presented the investment opportunities at the ongoing IMF-World Bank Annual Meetings in Bangkok. The projects span oil and gas, electricity, manufacturing, mining, transportation, agriculture, technology, healthcare, and urban development, according to details published on the Federal Ministry of Finance Projects Nigeria investment portal. The government estimates that the proposed investments could generate 366,763 jobs, positioning the initiative as part of its broader strategy to stimulate economic growth through private-sector financing. Oyedele stated that the government has established a dedicated Nigeria Deal Room to provide prospective investors with project information, financing requirements, and direct access to relevant government authorities. The energy sector accounts for the largest share of the investment pipeline with 25 projects requiring funding, while industry, manufacturing, and mining account for another 17. Transport and maritime infrastructure feature eight projects, agriculture and agro-processing account for six, and technology, healthcare, and urban services comprise the remaining nine. Among the proposed investments is Project Reliaxx, a crude oil evacuation and midstream infrastructure platform requiring [$1.7bn to address infrastructure bottlenecks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/how-cbn-rebuilt-mpc-credibility-after-nigerias-currency-crisis/) in the oil industry where evacuation constraints impact production and revenue generation. Other key opportunities include the proposed Lagos Greenline Railway requiring $1.29bn, the Multipurpose Industrial Platforms Limited ammonia and fertiliser project with an estimated funding requirement of $2.69bn, and the $1.25bn Premium Steel and Mines integrated steel upgrade. The portfolio also features the $355.6m PELIA Flare-Gas AI Data Centre, a $105m agro-processing and cold-chain export hub in Giri, Abuja, and the New Port City development with an estimated capital requirement of $1.6bn. Oyedele noted that the government plans further reforms to tackle investment obstacles, including electricity pricing, transmission constraints, weak agricultural productivity, and regulatory inefficiencies. He identified lower inflation, reduced borrowing costs, and better coordination between fiscal and monetary policies as essential conditions for sustained economic growth. The minister also highlighted the Economic Development Incentive, which grants tax credits to eligible businesses under Nigeria\u2019s reformed tax framework. Cardoso emphasised that reducing inflation remains vital to protecting household purchasing power, particularly for low-income Nigerians. He highlighted improvements in the capital market and banking system, noting that banks raised approximately N4.6tn during the recapitalisation exercise, with about 72% sourced from the domestic capital market. The CBN governor stated that Nigeria is targeting economic growth of 7% by 2027-2028, alongside higher oil production and increased infrastructure investment. The country plans to raise crude oil production to three million barrels per day and increase its infrastructure stock to 75% of Gross Domestic Product. Cardoso added that stronger external reserves have enhanced the nation\u2019s capacity to withstand economic shocks. Meanwhile, the [World Bank Group stated it is helping mobilise up](https://businessday.ng/interview/article/many-developers-still-do-subscriber-financing-despite-cbn-rate-cut-olaniyan/) to $5.79bn in private financing for Nigeria in fiscal year 2026, with programs expected to support approximately 850,000 direct and indirect jobs. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [kenneth Athekame](https://businessday.ng/author/krapeemyahoo-com/) Athekame Kenneth is a politics, economy, and finance reporter whose work is anchored in sharp investigative storytelling. He brings analytical depth to every piece, drawing on a strong academic foundation that includes a degree in Economics, an MBA in International Trade, and a minor in Petroleum Economics from Lagos State University, Ojo. His reporting blends rigorous research with a keen eye for hidden truths, delivering stories that illuminate power, policy, and the forces shaping everyday lives. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) [Nigeria\u2019s biggest problem is poverty, not religion, Makinde tells christians](https://businessday.ng/news/article/nigerias-biggest-problem-is-poverty-not-religion-makinde-tells-christians/) [Aiyedatiwa inaugurates Ondo APC campaign council, rallies support for Tinubu\u2019s 2027 re-election](https://businessday.ng/news/article/aiyedatiwa-inaugurates-ondo-apc-campaign-council-rallies-support-for-tinubus-2027-re-election/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "0d73cb6e56a2c85aaadcbbafab855b30d3e9ef506e5b09f425c2965f9737a50f", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:02Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1050", "title": "IMF backs Nigeria\u2019s monetary easing, says inflation fight remains unfinished", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-backs-nigerias-monetary-easing-says-inflation-fight-remains-unfinished/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-backs-nigerias-monetary-easing-says-inflation-fight-remains-unfinished/", "text": "Share Advertisement The International Monetary Fund (IMF) has backed Nigeria\u2019s decision to ease monetary policy, saying the country has made significant progress in reducing inflation, although the fight against rising prices remains unfinished. The Fund said Nigeria\u2019s decision to begin normalising interest rates was appropriate after a period of high inflation and elevated borrowing costs, while cautioning that the country\u2019s monetary policy stance remains tight. Zeine Zeidane, director, African Department, IMF, made the remarks while responding to questions during the release of the Fund\u2019s October 2026 Regional Economic Outlook Update for Sub-Saharan Africa at the ongoing IMF and World Bank Group Annual Meetings in Bangkok, Thailand. \u201cIn the case of Nigeria, they have made significant progress toward lowering inflation, because it has been coming from a high level, and they have a high interest rate,\u201d the official said. \u201cThey decided to normalise the monetary policy to go to something to lower that interest rate, which is appropriate.\u201d Zeine Zeidane, however, stressed that monetary conditions remained restrictive, indicating that the easing of interest rates does not mean the inflation challenge has been fully resolved. Read also: [IMF warns poor tax design raises business input costs by 8.9%](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-warns-poor-tax-design-raises-business-input-costs-by-8-9/) The comments come as policymakers weigh the need to support economic activity against the risk that renewed increases in energy, transport and other import costs could undermine progress in controlling inflation. In its regional outlook update, the IMF warned that rising fuel and transport costs, alongside more expensive fertiliser, could put renewed pressure on consumer prices and food security across sub-Saharan Africa. The Fund projected median inflation in the region to rise from 3.4 percent in 2025 to 4.3 percent in 2026 and five percent in 2027. The renewed inflation risks could complicate monetary policy decisions, particularly for countries facing higher import bills and limited fiscal capacity to cushion households against rising living costs. The IMF said monetary tightening might be necessary in many countries to prevent temporary price increases from persistent inflation, while exchange-rate flexibility could help absorb external shocks and preserve foreign exchange reserves where countries\u2019 exchange-rate regimes permit. Nigeria has undertaken major economic reforms, including foreign exchange market liberalisation and the removal of petrol subsidies, alongside tight monetary policy aimed at restoring macroeconomic stability. Read also: [IMF warns Nigeria against return to costly petrol subsidies](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-warns-nigeria-against-return-to-costly-petrol-subsidies/) These measures have helped strengthen the country\u2019s policy framework, although the impact of reforms on household welfare and the cost of living remains a key concern. The IMF\u2019s position suggests that while lower interest rates could provide some relief to borrowers and support economic activity, the pace of further monetary easing would need to take account of inflation developments and risks to price stability. The Fund also emphasised the importance of protecting vulnerable households through targeted support rather than broad-based subsidies, which can impose significant costs on public finances. It argued that governments should direct limited resources towards those most in need while preserving fiscal stability and productive investment. For Nigeria, the policy challenge is to sustain the progress made in reducing inflation without undermining the broader recovery or allowing renewed price pressures to erode household purchasing power. The IMF\u2019s remarks did not prescribe a specific interest-rate decision for the Central Bank of Nigeria, but underscored the need to balance economic recovery with continued vigilance against inflation. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "16b807fc9cbe7e93031316a23e0ded829e8ba52fc32155c6f4fc9fdeec10ff3a", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:03Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1051", "title": "IMF warns poor tax design raises business input costs by 8.9%", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-warns-poor-tax-design-raises-business-input-costs-by-8-9/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-warns-poor-tax-design-raises-business-input-costs-by-8-9/", "text": "Share Advertisement \u2026Says better tax systems can boost growth without sacrificing revenue Poorly designed tax systems can raise business input costs by as much as 8.9 percent in the most exposed sectors of emerging markets and developing economies, undermining productivity, investment and economic growth, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) has warned. The Fund said distortions arising from value-added tax (VAT) exemptions and the inability of businesses to recover taxes paid on production inputs can increase operating costs, with the additional burden cascading through supply chains and eventually reaching consumers. In its October 2026 Fiscal Monitor, launched at the ongoing IMF and World Bank Group Annual Meetings in Bangkok, Thailand, the Fund said governments could strengthen economic growth without sacrificing revenue by improving the design and implementation of their tax systems. The Fiscal Monitor was presented at a press briefing involving Rodrigo Vald\u00e9s, director of the IMF\u2019s Fiscal Affairs Department; Era Dabla-Norris, deputy director of the department; Davide Furceri, Division chief in the department; and Pavis Devahasadin, Communications officer at the IMF. The report noted that how governments raise revenue matters as much as how much they collect, warning that poorly designed taxes can discourage investment, distort business decisions and weaken the productive base needed to sustain future revenue generation. According to the IMF, VAT-related distortions can add between 2.3 percent and 8.9 percent to input costs across broad industry groups in the 26 economies studied, depending on the level of exposure to the tax distortions. The upper end of the range represents costs in the most exposed sectors and should not be interpreted as an economy-wide average. The Fund explained that businesses generally recover VAT paid on production inputs, but exemptions and non-registration can break the credit chain, turning the tax into an additional production cost. When a business cannot reclaim VAT paid on its inputs, the unrecovered amount increases its costs. These higher costs can then be passed to other businesses along the supply chain, ultimately contributing to higher prices for consumers. The report said eliminating the production and consumption distortions associated with existing VAT systems could generate annual welfare gains of up to 0.8 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in the economies studied, with an average gain of 0.26 percent. The estimates assume that the reforms preserve overall revenue while allowing businesses to fully recover input VAT and applying the tax more uniformly to final consumption. The IMF argued that reducing such distortions would enable businesses to use resources more efficiently, lower production costs and improve competitiveness, including for exporters that can be disadvantaged when input taxes cannot be recovered. The report\u2019s findings come amid mounting fiscal pressures confronting governments globally, with public debt historically high and projected to exceed 100 percent of global GDP before 2030. At the same time, real interest rates on government debt remain above pre-pandemic levels, while spending pressures and geopolitical tensions continue to complicate fiscal management. Global growth is also expected to remain subdued at about three percent over the medium term, making it more difficult for governments to strengthen public finances without undermining economic activity. The IMF said tax reforms could help address this challenge by reducing the economic costs associated with existing tax systems rather than relying exclusively on higher tax rates or additional revenue measures. Beyond VAT, the Fund identified corporate income taxation as another source of investment distortions, noting that typical corporate income taxes increase the cost of capital by an average of 14.7 percent in advanced economies and 19.2 percent in low-income developing countries. These taxes increase the pre-tax return that investment projects must generate to become commercially viable, potentially discouraging businesses from undertaking projects that would otherwise proceed. The report estimated that reforms allowing firms to deduct investment costs immediately could increase the long-term capital stock by 6.4 percent in advanced economies and 8.2 percent in low-income developing countries. Such reforms could also leave GDP between 2.1 percent and 2.7 percent higher, according to the Fund\u2019s illustrative estimates. The IMF stressed, however, that improving the treatment of investment costs does not necessarily require governments to lower statutory corporate income tax rates. Instead, tax systems could be redesigned to reduce the burden on marginal investments while preserving the taxation of economic rents, or returns above what is required to make an investment viable. The Fund also highlighted the importance of tax administration, arguing that complex registration, filing, payment and refund procedures impose disproportionate costs on small and young businesses. Better tax administration can improve compliance, reduce uncertainty and free up resources that businesses could otherwise devote to production, investment and expansion. The report cited evidence associating stronger tax administration with tax revenue equivalent to about 1.7 percentage points more of GDP when comparing countries at the 67th and 33rd percentiles of an operational-strength index. It added that more efficient administration could help governments raise revenue without changing statutory tax rates, while reducing reliance on more distortionary tax instruments. The Fiscal Monitor\u2019s analytical chapter also examined the effects of tax systems on employment, finding that revenue-neutral reforms to employment income taxation could raise employment rates by 1.2 percentage points in emerging market and developing economies and 0.8 percentage point in advanced economies, with the largest gains among lower earners. The report further cautioned that tax incentives favouring particular firms or business sizes can discourage expansion, encourage underreporting of sales and prevent businesses from reaching more productive scales. It recommended carefully targeting incentives towards activities with clear economic benefits, such as research and development, rather than assuming that preferential tax treatment automatically generates additional investment or innovation. The report\u2019s central message is that governments can improve the contribution of taxation to economic growth by reducing distortions in tax design, strengthening administration and ensuring that businesses and workers retain appropriate incentives to invest, produce and participate in the labour market. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Hope Moses-Ashike](https://businessday.ng/author/hope-ashike/) Hope Moses-Ashike is an Associate Editor, Banking and Finance, with more than a decade of experience reporting on Nigeria\u2019s financial system and broader economy. She closely tracks market movements, monetary policy decisions, company disclosures, regulatory actions, economic indicators, and global developments, and interprets what they mean for businesses, investors, policymakers, and households. Her reporting helps readers understand complex issues such as inflation trends, foreign exchange market dynamics, interest rate decisions, bank performance, and investment risks. She also covers major international events and periodically travels to Washington, D.C., to report on the World Bank/IMF Spring and Annual Meetings. Her dedication to financial journalism has earned her multiple recognitions and invitations to high-level professional development programmes. She is an alumna of the International Visitors Leadership Programme (IVLP) in the United States and holds an Advanced Financial Journalism Certificate from the Press Association Training in London, UK. Her other notable achievements include completing the Lagos Business School CMC Programme, the Bloomberg Media Africa Initiative Programme, and a Master Class in Journalism at Rhodes University in South Africa. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "0ea86d32c684e5ac246475b9845c9a045a3278fd1e7504d985ab88bbe829347b", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:04Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1052", "title": "IMF warns Nigeria against return to costly petrol subsidies", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-warns-nigeria-against-return-to-costly-petrol-subsidies/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/imf-warns-nigeria-against-return-to-costly-petrol-subsidies/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria should resist calls to restore broad petrol subsidies and instead channel relief to vulnerable households, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) has warned. The IMF said that restoring fuel subsidies are costly, disproportionately benefit higher-income consumers and risk worsening fiscal pressures amid rising borrowing costs. Fuel subsidies or measures preventing prices from adjusting fully to an energy shock had cost countries between 1 percent and 1.5 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in 2026, Rodrigo Vald\u00e9s, director of the IMF\u2019s fiscal affairs department, said on the sidelines of the IMF and World Bank meeting in Bangkok, Thailand, on Wednesday. \u201cProtecting people is not the same as protecting the price of fuels,\u201d Vald\u00e9s said. \u201cIt\u2019s first fiscally very costly, and second, if you look at the pattern of consumption, it\u2019s easy to see that that subsidy is readily regressive in many places,\u201d he added. Vald\u00e9s made the comments following a question on how the Washington-based fund could recommend allowing energy prices to reflect scarcity when higher fuel prices in Nigeria could raise transport costs, worsen inflation and deepen poverty, particularly when the government was facing calls to restore petrol subsidy ahead of an election year. The IMF director urged governments to improve social safety nets and household data to identify people who need assistance rather than re-introducing fuel subsidies that are costly and unsustainable. Nigeria recently introduced a 30-day petrol discount at state-owned oil company, Nigerian National Petroleum Company (NNPC) Limited retail stations, with priority for public transport operators, in a bid to bring respite to transporters against rising fuel prices. The petrol cap, though Taiwo Oyedele, minister of finance, explained that the NNPCL would forego its retail profit margin to absorb the difference, analysts and economists have widely argued that it is a \u201cback door\u201d subsidy, especially as Africa\u2019s most populous nation faces a crunch election in less than 100 days. Leading opposition figures including former vice president Atiku Abubakar, the presidential candidate for the African Democratic Congress (ADC) and former Anambra governor Peter Obi, the presidential candidate of the National Democratic Congress (NDC) have equally vowed to restore petrol subsidies and fight corruption in what experts say could hurt the Nigerian economy more and dissipate the gains of the hard won reforms, including a now stable naira, cooling inflation and quickening growth. Davide Furceri, a division chief in the fund\u2019s fiscal affairs department, said governments could redirect some of the resources spent on blanket subsidies towards vulnerable consumers. \u201cFor example, you mentioned transportation costs. Yeah, gasoline could go up, and perhaps bus fees may be subsidised in a way that is a much better policy at the end of the day than just a blanket subsidy for everybody,\u201d Furceri said. He also suggested alternatives such as school feeding programmes, geographical targeting and assistance for vulnerable farmers. Furceri acknowledged that some countries lacked household registries and digital payment systems needed to deliver targeted support effectively. However, he said governments could adopt alternative approaches in the short term while developing stronger systems. He recommended that assistance should be limited, targeted and temporary. Furceri explained that fuel subsidies could worsen global energy pressures by preventing demand from adjusting when supplies were constrained. \u201cWhen you subsidise, you don\u2019t allow, at the end of the day, demand to adjust,\u201d he said. \u201cAnd the reality is that we have less fuels in the world economy. That means that someone else has to adjust more, and the market produces a higher price for that.\u201d The IMF official said subsidies were not only costly and regressive but could also have spillover effects on the global economy. Related News [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Wasiu Alli](https://businessday.ng/author/wasiu-alli/) Wasiu Alli is a business, economics cum data journalist with strong expertise covering macro trends, capital markets, government policies, corporate earnings and comparative economics analysis. Alli turns raw data into trends that not only tells compelling stories but nudges investors to make valued and informed decisions. He\u2019s an alumnus of Lagos State University and trained at Lagos Business School. He formerly heads the Companies and Markets desk at BusinessDay where he writes and supervises the production of well researched articles on earnings updates, corporate sectoral comparisons, market intelligence as well as interviews with C-suite executives. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Cardoso targets deeper financial ties with Chinese banks](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/cardoso-targets-deeper-financial-ties-with-chinese-banks/) [Nigeria risks Naira crisis, public finances if fuel subsidy returns, Oyedele warns](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigeria-risks-naira-crisis-public-finances-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele-warns/) [No return to fuel, FX subsidies, says Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/no-return-to-fuel-fx-subsidies-says-oyedele/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "525746fd72bbc7e1cf58dc41e26063498b41886af2d68c31d90c61fb2cfcd16c", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:05Z", "language": "en"}]}